COTW_MOVE_NOTES = {
  "andy_bogard": {
    "214acac": "A regular hadouken that disappears before going fullscreen. Has THE shortest recovery of any slow fireball in the game to compensate. EX version is a slower close range orb with 3 hits that's always plus, good in combos and for oki. All versions have a shockingly large hitbox and are very active, will often catch jumping opponents. A fireball designed for midrange usage rather than long-range zoning, but it makes up for disappearing with its short recovery. This lets Andy both throw it out while maintaining the very real threat of an anti-air if the opponent doesn't jump on prediction, and also use it as a way to get + frames on oki or when spaced out. The slow moving nature of both versions especially helps with this application, with the A version being better for offense and B for neutral, as it goes further in the same active time. The EX version is kind of a different beast, as it completely lacks ranged beyond the space right in front of Andy, so it is most used by far as a combo tool. Beyond that, its neutral usage is restricted to keepout, where its admittedly large, disjointed, and very active hitbox comes in handy. On offense it's best used as an oki tool on account of its active frames, giving Andy some very easy-to-use projectile setplay. While there are no true strings into 214AC, it's a frametrap from 214BD > D, and plus with no pushback, so some conditioning can make it an effective pressure reset.",
    "214bdbd": "A mobility tool with followup attacks. Nicknamed \"Demon Flip\", a move with the same nickname belonging to Street Fighter's Akuma. B and D versions travel a set distance based on button used, it's not possible to cross up with 214B unless the opponent moves under you. 214BD always tracks the opponent, ensuring any followup will hit regardless of screen position. Funny Flip. All have their applications in offense and neutral and the meterless ones will generally be more used for the latter, however an empty flip is mostly only used on oki to bait reversals in specific setups or go for a throw mixup.",
    "214bdbda": "Command grab. Metered version whiffs on crouchers, but can be mixed up with other options to open people up. Tends to beat high dodges and ill-timed DP brake anti-airs, but cannot hit airborne opponents. All versions are a hard knockdown, metered version can be cancelled into other EX moves on hit for a combo. Similar to Flip D, it's a bit challenging to use the meterless versions as mixup; you need the opponent to not react with a DP or to not have one at all in order to push any kind of mix between this and Flip B. However, this move is similarily good in neutral since it just straight up beats high dodge and even some late brakeable DP's, since they're not immune to throws. If the opponent relies on these options and isn't ready to commit a fully invicible reversal to anti-air, this can be a very rewarding way to get in on them, since it's a hard knockdown. To that end, doing the appropriate meterless version is better, as EX actually does less damage but has the same knockdown. EX Flip Grab is more rewarding on hit if you have resources however, and it can actually be a mixup thanks again to Flip C. And while it will lose to crouch, it will beat anti-airs all the same, so it can be a more brainless tool to accomplish what the meterless ones do, and potentially nuke their HP while at it.",
    "214bdbdb": "Fastest followup, can be plus on block depending on timing and tends to trade with or beat defensive air-to-airs. Very good if timed to be meaty, but it's easily reaction DP'd with no way to mix it up meterlessly. Has significant landing recovery, so it can't be made safe against most reversals. Primary hit followup, with no significant difference between versions. Puts a massive hitbox in front of Andy that's basically always going to keep him safe if it connects, so it's good as a safety net when doing 214B in neutral to bait out responses. It will usually beat or favorably trade with people trying to air-to-air in response to the flip, though the hitbox doesn't go as high as it looks, so some can jump over it. Gives a massive launch on air counterhit, which again, isn't too uncommon, and on grounded hit it's also Andy's highest damage starter. It can be very plus as a meaty hit, and it's also an overhead, but good luck trying to take advantage of that when so much of the cast can DP an obviously close flip at no cost or risk and it cannot be mixed up with a low hit on the same frame.",
    "214bdbdc": "Similar to B but hits behind Andy, making it very niche on 214B. 214BD > C instead teleports behind the opponent, which is useful to make anti-airs whiff and for sideswitch routes. A dedicated crossup attack, but you still need to make sure you actually go over the opponent with your meterless flips since it has no front hitbox, similar to Dong's j4C. Has very similar frame data to Flip B, so it's usually safe, this time with the added utility of letting you cross up anti-air DP attempts and give people a bad input. When timed well it can even make a successful anti-air whiff entirely, though these setups aren't airtight or rewarding unless a DP is actually baited. Naturally this is best on 214D, but even from 214B it's a good way to hit someone dashing under you to make Flip A and B whiff. Also it's not an overhead but that literally does not matter. 214BD > C is the FT2 killer. Fullscreen teleport with a quick startup; while it's not unreactable, it's hard to anti-air this consistently even despite the lengthened startup. Can cancel into j214AC if it connects, which is a combo on hit and a frametrap string on block that only loses to vertical reversals. Very strong option for neutral, and on offense it's an on-demand DP bait when timed right. Like the other followups, if timed late enough it won't come out, but the teleport will actually still happen, which can catch people off guard.",
    "214bdbdd": "Hilariously active low slide. A very powerful meaty, and can easily be spaced in neutral to be safe or plus. In meaty setups, Andy can do an empty flip to bait reversals intended to beat slide. Can be mixed up with B, an overhead, but this is fuzzy blockable or DP'able. While it is a low out of Demon Flip, this move is really more of a harrass tool and a + on block meaty than any kind of mixup, as it's not possible to hit this on the same frame as Flip B, and even on timings that come close, a DP beats both AND empty flip. In setups that have the slide hit meaty however, not only is it a good starter and reliably +, Andy is usually going to be able to empty flip and bait reversals, do a different meaty, or simply throw the opponent, so this is probably meterless slide's best application. Ironically, EX slide is actually more niche, as it's less + on block and a weaker starter and cannot be spaced in neutral, though with the added threat from EX flip's other options, it will probably more frequently catch people off guard as a low. Even if the opponent can react, Flip C makes a reaction DP risky, so it will likely just get blocked, where it has excellent blockstun for Rev Cancels, if not for a raw connect.",
    "236236ac": "Only the C version is invincible and can be braked. Neither version has exceptional range, but they're both easy to combo into. 236236A is generally Andy's best use of meter, it has good damage and corner carry. 236236C is maybe Andy's best reversal and still an okay combo ender. 236236C can be braked halfway through. Links into cl.D on counter hit or wild punish. Your best friend for meter usage. 236236A is fast, does high damage and always leads to an identical knockdown, so Andy can always get his preferred setups from it, so long as the spacing is right. Even midscreen, the corner carry is huge and you still get to run oki after. For all of this, it's the best in basically every situation where invulnerability isn't needed, and is still preferred as a guard cancel. 236236C is significantly rarer to see, as not only does it do less damage for its cost, but also has a worse knockdown. Its main selling points are being Andy's best metered reversal (or at least the most reliable), and being brakable halfway through, leading to a combo, which has its situational uses. For max damage punishes this just isn't the way to go, but as a starter (usually as a reversal), it keeps the opponent grounded when braked, letting Andy followup with a full heavy to heavy confirm for over 50% damage in S.P.G. so long as he has Rev to spare, off of what is a really strong reversal. Also, these combos refund some of its cost in the process, so they're straight up more economic than the full super. Interestingly, Andy can perform a \"pseudo-236236C\" by doing 236AC~6C > 623AC > 214AC, or 236AC~6C > 623AC~AB for the braked version. This visually resembles 236236C, but deals less damage and fills up the REV gauge instead of depleting meter. Hilariously, this means he can \"perform\" this super twice in one combo.",
    "236236bd": "Both version are invincible. Due to the slow startup, neither version is particularly good as a wakeup reversal, though they can definitely work. They both hit quite high and far, which actually gives them some utility in neutral as fireball punishes and they are preferred in a few pickups. 236236B has a situational use as a combo ender due to giving better frame advantage than 236236A and thus enabling much better mid screen okizeme in exchange for a little damage. The two versions are almost entirely identical with 236236D simply being higher damage with a better hitbox. Not great as wakeup supers, but if you want to gamble regardless, this is your main option. Considering the slow startup but enormous hitbox, both of these supers are actually better used as long range anti-airs, whiff punishes, or anti-fireball options.",
    "236236rev": "Resets the Rev meter to 0%, even during Overheat. Only available during S.P.G. Very weird compared to most Hidden Gears, usually won't hit on its first active frame on grounded opponents which can hurt it as a reversal. Hidden Gear with an unusual attack animation and hitbox, which ironically makes it one of the most limiting ones in the game. Frequently not hitting on the first frame and its strange hitbox make it unreliable as a reversal, and its knockdown is fairly weak as well, so the best you'll get is f.C as a late meaty or 214C to poke from range, which are unrewarding and potentially unsafe. Suffice to say, your meter is generally better spent on 236236A, however when you do use this as a combo ender, combined with Andy's monstrous punish damage, you are likely winning the round off that combo alone.",
    "236acac": "Has variable startup and duration depending on distance from the opponent or brake timing. Straight dashpunch. 236A is a safe pressure ender, albeit one that leaves Andy very close to the opponent for a throw mixup. As a combo tool it's kind of bad; it ends juggles with low damage, and leaves grounded targets far from Andy and +1, unless it's the starter and CH, where it links to light normals for mostly low damage. This makes it much more useful as a guard cancel, where it's a fast option with an easy input that will frequently counterhit, and its followup combo's low damage is less relevant. It's also very quick to whiff with good mobility, although dashing is still usually better. 236C has worse frame data and better range, but it's a rekka. It's a decent ender with good corner carry, and an okay neutral tool, but it's unsafe so its followups need to be represented on block, which themselves are unrewarding and weak. 236AC is is basically a better 236C; better on block with higher reward that can be confirmed, so it's much better in neutral. It's also a core combo extender with good base damage and easy combos from it, very frequently used in stronger combos.",
    "236bdbd": "All versions are mostly combo tools with decent corner carry. 236D and 236BD can be braked to make them safe on block or start a juggle. Advancing launcher. While 236D has some uses as a frametrap with decent reward on hit, generally all 3 versions are combo tools. 236A will usually end your combo unless it hits high enough, but is a good cancel from f.B for a knockdown. On brake, both the D and BD versions do an absolutely pitiful 20 damage, making it so a few routes are literally better off not including them at all, though 236BD generally has no reason to be braked, and its full version is much stronger, and 236D brake is one of Andy's most used combo tools regardless.",
    "236cac6c": "Hitconfirmable followup and combo ender/extender Used as a combo tool since 236C > D cannot be juggled into, and it has little use otherwise. There is generally no reason not to use it after 236AC on hit, so go wild.",
    "236cac6d": "Frame trap tool to complement 236C/AC > C that always knocks down. Soft knockdown and cannot be combo'd from. Has a longer delay window than its counterpart, so it's the main one to use on block as a frametrap or true string low, made safer after 236AC by the threat of a cancel to a Rev Art that can frametrap or move Andy out of the way.",
    "2a": "Has decent range, good for confirms and is one of Andy's better mashing options at 5 frames.",
    "2b": "Undoubtedly Andy's best light, superior reach with decent returns on a low make it good for footsies and pressure. Usually chained into more lights, but all of them will actually just whiff a majority of the time unless you're point blank. Is a single hit confirm into 236236A, which can occasionally make it more threatening from range. Combined with dash, this is Andy's one light that's actually very good. While not the greatest, the ability to confirm into different enders depending on resouces available and preferred reward does give Andy an above average low presence in footsies and pressure. Oddly, this is a light normal that is for some reason a single hit confirm to one of Andy's super (and it's the one you'd want to combo into anyway). It's not easy to do, especially in netplay, but this restores much of the close range threat that Andy normally has to his ranged hits.",
    "2bcor2drev": "Mimmicks Andy's DP, but has worse frame data than 5DRev. Rarely used.",
    "2c": "Andy's most reliable poke, solid frame data and very good reward at all ranges. While its range leaves some to be desired, it's still a good whiff punish tool. Combos from close D feint as a double heavy confirm. This move is really good. Its range isn't anything too broken, but it's enough to contend with a lot of characters in footsies, and it doesn't have a very long total duration, making it kind of annoying to whiff punish on reaction. 0 on feint, which Andy can take advantage of in many ways with spacing traps, and on hit it's confirmable to supers and many party starter specials. Its only weakness is the lack of added reward on counterhit, as you can't connect another far heavy of any kind, and your lights probably won't reach,",
    "2crev": "Workable as an anti-air, generally best against hops. Not bad as a counterpoke, but its range isn't anything crazy.",
    "2d": "Okay range, but highly unsafe, as sweeps tend to be. Generally not a great poke unless precisely spaced. Combos from Counter Hit far D, but has almost the exact same range as 2C, which is more rewarding.",
    "56bcor56drev": "Andy's fastest feint and the one used in his normal framedata.",
    "5crev": "Standard overhead with a subtle animation, hard to space for better frames, much more doable when used as a meaty. Andy's main high/low mixup option. While that sounds bad, the good thing is that its animation does make it more likely to hit than most just by being hard to notice. It has little use in neutral due to having mediocre range and being hard to space for better advantage, but it's okay against characters like Ken.",
    "623acac": "A version is a meterless reversal with decent range and startup C version is a core combo extender and an anti air if braked EX version is a damaging extender even without brake A and C versions recover 6 frames slower on block and whiff Resident DP. Works basically as any other; great anti-air, Guard Cancel option and reversal in 623A specifically. Also a central combo tool, and on account of its limited initial range, 623C Brake will often require a microdash to use midscreen, but this is a minor optimization that you can avoid by just doing dash > 623AC instead, which is much easier to connect and only slightly weaker. 623C is generally better if braked, but 623AC loses out on damage and cancel options, so it's only recommended to Brake for safety.",
    "cla": "Very standard and unremarkable, used in confirms into DP.",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. cl.AC first hit is cancellable by any follow-ups. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender, during cl.AC only the second hit is REV Blow cancellable. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : Same move as close C but the frame data and damage are different. Still two hits which makes seriously using this string on block almost impossible. cl.AC > C : Far C follow up, different frame data and damage. D ender : Far D follow up, different frame data and damage, very unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Same move as his Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV), +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Same move as his 2D and nearly the same framedata, meaning it's very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "Standard 5B, on the faster side at 5 frames. Good pressure tool, as c.B's tend to be. 5 frames and +3 means it's probably your most reliable option to reset pressure, and it's a frametrap and confirm into c.C, which itself is plus on feint cancel. Combos naturally to 236B, so you'll often get it in place of f.B when going for Dash > 2B5B > 236B, which won't make the route drop.",
    "clc": "Two hits but plus on feint cancel. Combos from close B. First hit is super cancellable to counter a Rev Blow. Actually a very good normal on offense for a few reasons. Two hits and being plus means it's easy to confirm from, to a degree that you can react to it being a counterhit and cancel to Feint > 2C instead of 236D for a more optimal route. It's also plus on feint while not being too easy to Hyper Defense against, and the two hits make it easy to counter Rev Blow on reaction. Lastly, it's pretty fast at 6 frames, so it not only links from c.B, it's also a frametrap from it and just generally a decent button after a dash, granted it is horrible to whiff.",
    "cld": "+4 on block, +8 on hit with feint cancel. Has a hitbox that frequently anti-airs hops, making it good for dashing in and resetting pressure. Combos into itself (requires a micro walk or microdash) when counterhit into a feint. Andy's main close Heavy. Perfect for pressure at +4, and serves as a confirm to 2C as Andy's main heavy route. The hitbox helps cover early jumps or hops, so there's slightly less risk to using it against someone looking to bait a throw.",
    "fa": "Uniquely +1 despite being a far A. This is fun but ultimately not very useful, as none of Andy's frametrap normals reach at this range. This normal is kind of okay, but there is almost no reason to ever use it outside of niche blockstrings, where 236AC is a frametrap from it. 6 frames is pretty bad for a far A, as it won't give you a 5 framer as abare when out of c.A range, and the +1 is useless, as you can't frametrap from it without a cancel.",
    "fb": "Combos naturally into 236B or Command Combo > Rev Blow for a knockdown, so it's a go to-for light chains. Despite that, its range is pretty bad, and it will frequently fall out of ranged confirms. On CH or WP, combos to 236AC/BD. On top of its fast startup and high base damage, it's good for quick punishes. Andy's most frustrating normal to use. f.B seriously lacks range, however some of Andy's most useful confirms rely on this normal to do its job, which it often won't and just fall out instead. It has quite a bit of recovery, so it's even risky as a poke, but at 5 frames you can still pick up some punishes with it, as it reliably combos into 236BD and sometimes 236AC on Wild Punish or Counterhit.",
    "fbfd": "Rev Blow ender only available in S.P.G.",
    "fc": "Insanely long advancing normal, but you WILL die for whiffing it. Not special-cancellable but confirms into any of Andy's supers, links to c.C and lights on counterhit. -4 means it's usually safe unless JD'd or when against a character with a 4 frame 2A that reaches. A normal arguably belonging to Billy retrofitted for Andy. One of THE longest normals in terms of actual reach and still decently fast despite that, and with no reliable long range poke, Andy definitely appreciates this. Downsides are utterly imploding on whiff as it has a very lengthy recovery period while moving you towards the opponent and into their reach, and being unrewarding by itself, and often even on counterhit with no Super to cancel into depending on reach.",
    "fd": "Whiffs on crouchers, doesn't have great range, isn't fast and only combos into super without a Counterhit or WP. Vacuums on hit and links to 2C and 2D on Counterhit Does anti-air rising targets and pulls them down towards you. A strong advancing whiff punish button with a unique vacuum effect which leads to either a solid strike throw mix-up on normal hit or a massive counterhit confirm. Has less recovery and is a bit faster than far C along with being better at catching people in the air. Not strictly better as it has less range and whiffs on crouchers but still a solid button.",
    "j214acac": "All versions halt Andy's jump momentum, EX version sends him backwards after the attack. Meterless versions can only be done from a neutral or forward jump/hop but can be very plus. EX version can be done from a backjump or even backdash , usually safe or plus. Behaves similar to its grounded couterpart, but moves diagonally down and has recovery until landing. While that is a big weakness, and both meterless versions are usually unrewarding on hit, this move makes Andy almost impossible to consistently anti-air. From a neutral jump, Andy can do a failling button to catch people dashing under the fireball, while succesfully doing the fireball itself will usually let him land and pressure a distant opponent while beating anti-airs. This in conjunction with 214A/B makes some characters have to work overtime to get in against Andy, and he can punish risky approaches very hard. When spaced, both meterless version are good as reversal safe meaties, though they're hard to consistently set up. EX again is quite different, and also shockingly ignorant. It's doable from a backjump or backdash, sends Andy further back, and with how enormous its hitbox is it will rarely ever fail to let Andy safely land while retreating. It also does good damage, can lead to combos depending on height, and cancelling it into Rev Blow is a hard knockdown on hit, safe on block, and the opponent can't even guard cancel against it, while being dirt cheap. Shares its oki applications with 214BD, however it also works as a shimmy when done from a backdash or as a TK, where it can reliably lead to combos with Super.",
    "ja": "Good air-to-air hitbox and a decent jumpin. Has a Command Combo and is cancellable to specials/Rev Blow on full jumps. Andy's most useful air-to-air, as it's fast and he can use j.214AC and Rev Blow to turn it into damage and a hard knockdown, and even meterless you can at least get an extra hit on counterhits with the command combo, or check air JD from the opponent. Beyond that, and aside from jumpin tick throws, not used for much.",
    "jajc": "Only available from full jumps.",
    "jb": "Crossup button It's what it says on the box. The crossup hitbox is surprisingly poor, but still mostly reliable; its jumpin hitbox is much more generous.",
    "jc": "Heavy air-to-air with slow startup. Basically entirly inferior to j.D with lower damage and a similar hitbox that's worse for jumpins, but it does hit higher, so it's sometimes preferred.",
    "jd": "Best jumpin and safejump normal, also a decent air-to-air. Best all-rounder air normal, and yet another of Andy's numerous air-to-air options. Despite appearances, it's also just about his best jump in. Though shallow, the activity makes it easy for it to be plus enough to combo off. Very risky to use this for throw punishes."
  },
  "b_jenet": {
    "214acac": "B. Jenet swipes her dress forward for a big hit, any version on counter hit launches the opponent high into the air. A version : A safe ender unless used very close. cl.A > 214A is a frametrap and far A/B > 214A is a safe blockstring if far enough. You can delay it a bit to frametrap. C version : Slower, unsafe but deals more damage, B. Jenet advances during this version so don't use it on block or you'll be punished. AC version : Can be safe if spaced but B. Jenet advances(More than the A version). However it deals a lot of damage and can be REV cancelled. Also Far C > 214AC is a frametrap.",
    "214bdbd": "B and BD go up to hop height, while D goes up to jump height. D and BD can be cancelled into any air move on brake(An air normal makes them land faster). BD version is arguably B.Jenet's most powerful and important move. Braking it allows Jenet to set up an extremely oppressive high,low,grab mix up that is not reactable. B. Jenet jumps and does an axe kick, though it looks like one, no part of this move is an overhead. On brake D and BD ver. can be used to create a crossup or overhead mixup, while the B version can be used to try to land a quick low or even throw. Breaking the BD version needs some fast execution due to the extremely fast start up, but it is crucial to be able to break it consistently as a B.Jenet player so practice in training mode as needed.",
    "236236ac": "Both versions are invincible. C version starts and travels faster and deals more damage. B. Jenet flies close to the ground, pummeling the oppoenent with a barrage of kicks and torpedoes. A good super for corner carry and can punish some projectiles. Be aware that both versions are invincible only during startup, you can be hit by a projectile(Even a grounded one) during active frames or later.",
    "236236bd": "B Version is your highest damage ender. Landing it in the corner after a mid-air juggle( usually after a 214AC->214BC juggle) will give an extremely easy auto-timed safe jump by buffering a jump right after the super finishes the animation D Version can be braked before the last hit(Kiss), allows B. Jenet to extend her combo. D Version is faster, goes higher, deals more damage and has better advantage on hit. Slower startup than 236236P, B. Jenet flies off the ground and does a series of rising spinning attacks and ends with a high damaging kiss.",
    "236236rev": "Resets the Rev meter to 0%, even during Overheat. Only available during S.P.G. Jenet does a big shooting kick, starts really fast and reaches a good vertical and horizontal distance for a kick. Animated super, meaning the hit advantage doesn't change depending on where you hit your opponent.",
    "236acac": "A version travels nearly 30% of the screen and stays active a small moment before disappearing. C and AC version travel full screen. AC version has a vacuum effect on block(And it's positive), meaning it's her best REV special to REV cancel on block. B. Jenet fires a projectile grounded tornado that travels the screen with a decent speed. A version : Frametraps when used after far C, but the opponent can jump. It can also bait some DPs at max range, it can be a good tool to use for meaty setups. C version : Slower startup than the A version, meaning it cant be used as a frametrap, but safer on block and travels full screen. AC version : Same startup as A so it can frametrap when used after far C but again the opponent can jump. Positive and has a vacuum effect on block, allows B. Jenet to frametrap with close C/Far C. Can be cancelled into any REV grounded move, meaning you can cancel it into 214AC(Brake) and create a left/right mixup.",
    "236bdbd": "Every version has air invulnerability. D and BD can be braked before the spinning follow up. BD can be Rev cancelled into Harrier Bee during the second part of this move. Jumping knee followed by a spinning move that lifts B. Jenet off the ground, can be used to anti-air or against moves with air properties. B version : The fastest B. Jenet special, the startup is short but it is very unsafe on block. Works really well as a combo ender but only give a soft knockdown. D version : Slower but the brake version is a really good combo extender and is also safe on block(-3) but leaves Jenet really close to the opponent. BD version : Slower than B ver. but faster than D, braking it causes a hard knockdown and makes it positive on block, meaning B. Jenet can continue her pressure. The brake version is a combo extender while the follow-up can be both ender or an extender if REV cancelled into Harrier Bee.",
    "2a": "A fast poke with decent range, it's really nice to chain it into itself or into far A.",
    "2b": "Quick crouching low, a nice starter for her light combos. 2B ~ far A allows her to combo into 623AC~P. You can get the far A either with a slight delay at close range or with the right spacing. Any other light that follows 2B will make 623AC~P whiff.",
    "2bcor2drev": "Slower than the standing feint but has the same animation as the startup of her projectile move(236P). Meaning it can be used to bait people.",
    "2c": "Her longest range normal, cancellable and safe if feinted, a bit slow but still an amazing tool.Can be buffered into safe specials to start pressure or convert off a stray hit. In patch 1.7.2, 2C combos into 236D without a counterhit",
    "2crev": "Cannot be cancelled on block Wins against jump attacks, standing normals and REV Blows, a really nice move for anti-airing that leads into a combo in counter hit/punish situations.",
    "2d": "B. Jenet's big sweep, hits low but only causes a soft knockdown and is not cancellable.",
    "4ab": "Side switch and hard knockdown, less advantage than the forward throw and the opponent lands closer(but still far).",
    "56bcor56drev": "B. Jenet's fastest feint and the one used in her normal framedata. B. Jenet does the Sonic finger pose. Because this one has less recovery than the down feint, you have to use this one when you feint to cancel a normal.",
    "5crev": "B. Jenet's overhead that wins against low attacks and throws, the later you hit, the more frame advantage you get. Can lead into combo if you hit the opponent really late.",
    "623acacp": "Every version loses to lows, cross-ups and projectiles, it only works at the 3rd frame meaning it loses against meaty attacks. A/C version need to parry a move to automatically trigger a 1F follow-up. AC can also trigger the follow-up manually by pressing any punch button(But the startup is slow). Light normals can combo into 623AC > P follow-up if close enough. On counter hit it launches the opponent high into the air allows B. Jenet to start a combo. B. Jenet's counter, can parry a lot of things(Even REV Blow), it's not a real reversal but it can be used against frametraps. The parry has a long active window but be aware it will cause a counter hit if hit during start-up and wild punish if hit in the recovery frames, use it at your own risk.",
    "ab": "Hard knockdown, but throws the opponent really far away. In the corner, she can set up a meaty overhead by whiffing a stand D and then immediately going into the overhead.She can also set up an extremely plus meaty fireball by doing a 2B then immediately going into 236A.",
    "cd": "The follow up won't come out on whiff or counter hit(Allows B.Jenet to start a combo). DO NOT WHIFF THIS The fastest Rev Blow in the game by a large margin and completely unreactable in most situations, so it can very easily break through common frame traps. Its speed combined with its rising hitbox makes it viable as an anti-air as well, where it's unblockable by airborne opponents and safe against ones that land in time. Arguably the best Rev Blow in the game and Jenet's best feature.",
    "cla": "A nice poke that can be used for anti-air or to start a light combo.",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : Same move as far C but the frame data is different and it deals more damage. cl.AC > C : Far C follow up, the frame data is different and the damage is much higher(It even deals more than cl.AC). D ender : A new move only available during her combination attack, very unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Same move as her Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV) but one frame slower with +2F advantage on hit and block. 2D ender : Deals a bit more damage than her classic 2D but has the same frame data in general, meaning it's very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "Links into close C on hit and frametraps on block. A good close poke that is plus both on hit and guard.",
    "clc": "Jenet's fastest close range heavy, really fast and allows jenet to become +1 on block with a feint In patch 1.7.2 this move was nerfed heavily. Due to being plus +1 only, B.Jenet CANNOT frame trap into far C anymore and frame trapping with lights doesn't lead to a combo due to the bad range on her lights. Jenet's best pressure buttons post 1.7.2 are her close B and her close D.",
    "cld": "Starts B.Jenet's pressure and gameplan Links into any light. Forces the opponent to stand. On counter hit you can feint and micro walk to connect a Close C. Massive plus frames on block but doesn't link into a heavy on normal hit Far C frame traps after a blocked Close D feint into a massive combo to catch mashers Two hit and has a bit less pushback than close C. Because it's slower and two hit, it can mess up the just defend timing and win against non-invul guard cancel but it also means the opponent can hyper defend the second hit. A flaw of this move is that it doesn't combo naturally into Far C.",
    "fa": "Really good poke, cancellable and can hit crouching opponents. An amazing tool to end your light combo into a special/super.",
    "fb": "Nearly the same as far A, with less range but a bit more damage.",
    "fc": "Far C is a really good poke, has nearly the same range as far A but with a better reward on hit and is safe on block if feinted.",
    "fc46c": "Far C follow-up, cannot be used on whiff. On hit, it increases scaling: Better for short combos, worse for long combos (especially with REV) than far C alone. On block, this increases your opponent\u2019s pushback and REV meter, but beware of hyper defense.",
    "fd": "Reaches vary far and hits crouching opponents, super cancellable but not special cancellable. Can be super cancelled to combo an airborne opponent on counter hit.Can be a siutational anti air for certain jump angles with high reward, but it doesn't have anti air invulnerablity. Overall, a move that doesn't have many uses except and shouldn't be used much.",
    "j2bdbd": "B Version start with a small hop in the air. D and BD version start at a diagonal angle. B and D can be only used during a neutral or forward jump. BD can be used during any jump and even a backdash. B. Jenet glides during startup, can be followed by a series of kicks.",
    "j2bdbdk": "1st of the kick follow-ups. Against a grounded opponent, you will have more advantage the closer to the ground you are when hitting them.",
    "j2bdbdkx2": "The 2nd kick, nearly same as the previous version. You can use the second attack to improve your advantage after hitting the opponent high with the first attack.",
    "j2bdbdkx3": "The BD version launches a grounded opponent on hit, can combo into 236B or a super if she's close enough to the ground. 3rd of the kick attacks, pretty much the same as the previous version except that the BD version causes a knockdown. Same thing as the second kick, use the three kicks to be closer to the ground and increase your advantage.",
    "j2bdbdkx4": "BD version sends the opponent higher on hit, allows B. Jenet to combo in the air or if she's close to the ground. Last follow-up, very unsafe on block, every version knocks down the opponent on hit.",
    "j6b": "Diagonal dive kick, depending on the spacing it can be safe and can allow B. Jenet to start a combo on counter hit.",
    "ja": "Whiffs against crouching opponents. Really fast, good for air to air and can be cancelled by j.2K for a combo on counter hit.",
    "jab": "Hard knockdown and side switch with the opponent. An air throw that seems to hit really far vertically but adds a lot of landing recovery on whiff.",
    "jb": "Crossup tool, can also be used has an air to air, a bit slower than j.A but can hit a bit farther horizontaly.",
    "jc": "Only the first hit is overhead Double slap attack, must be done at low height to hit crouching opponents.",
    "jcd": "Only available during S.P.G, her air REV blow that causes a hard knockdown and crumple on counter hit. Safe if used close to the ground.",
    "jd": "Really good jump-in attack, with a good vertical hitbox."
  },
  "billy_kane": {
    "214acac": "Billy extends his staff upward at an angle. Long reaching anti-air; does not hit grounded opponents. No invulnerability, but combos from the High Dodge Attack for anti-air combos. A version will only air reset against a jumping opponent, but juggles properly in combos. C version launches on Counter Hit. Basically the same move as 236A/C, but angled upward to hit airborne opponents. It doesn't hit grounded opponents at all. It's difficult to use this move on reaction to a jumping opponent, but it's possible thanks to its faster startup; however you'll often combo into this rather than anti-air with it. If you combo into this in the corner, you can almost always continue the combo afterward, usually into a 214B/D or super. REV (A+C) Version: Hits twice and launches slightly higher, even if you don't do its followup. You will always want to do the followup, though, for the sake of combos.",
    "214bdbd": "Billy swings his staff at the opponent up to three times. The first hit is +7 on Counter Hit, allowing you to link into 2A. A brand-new special move to Billy, the B version of this rekka chain is one of only two special moves that Billy can combo into from lights. In many cases, it\u2019s Billy\u2019s optimal light combo ender due to the better okizeme, higher damage from three lights, and combo reliability. Using the first hit on its own doesn't do much, but it's safe when spaced and its combo potential on Counter Hit allows you to beat mashing. The D version is very outclassed by the B/BD versions and Billy's other special moves, so you'll almost never use it. REV (B+D) version: Combo filler that easily combos some of from Billy's other REV specials, it does higher damage than meterless rekkas, is surprisingly juggle friendly, and can easily go into Billy's other REV moves on hit or block. It also has stronger conversions on Counter Hit, linking to close C.",
    "214bdbd46k": "Second rekka's follow-up. The meterless versions are quite unsafe, but if delayed they become a frametrap that's +8 on counterhit, allowing for full close C/D combos. REV (B+D) Version: +2 on block and often leaving opponents just out of range of their abares, this sets up spacing traps nicely, as long as you don\u2019t mash out the third hit on accident.",
    "214bdbd46k46k": "Final rekka's follow-up, and the one that knocks down on hit. If you somehow manage to delay trap the last hit it launches on Counter Hit. Though it's punishable by the whole cast, the high pushback of the final hit can create a knowledge check where you can potentially create a spacing trap if the opponent doesn't know how to punish it. REV (B+D version) : Combos to 214AC, 623AC, 236BD and REV Blow midscreen, and all of the above plus 236AC in the corner.",
    "214cac46p": "Billy makes the end of his extended staff explode. C and REV Suzume Otoshi follow-up. Depending on distance/height and stage position, it is possible to combo after this move, especially after the AC version. REV (A+C) Version: Does more damage and launches opponents slightly higher, improving your combos afterward. It leads to a powerful combo if you can hit it, and like 236AC, it juggles to 623AC, though you'll lose some hits. Almost always juggles to 236AC, 623AC and 236236B/D no matter the height or distance.",
    "236236ac": "The infamous wheel of death. Billy spins his staff as he conjures a ring of flames, then slings that ring at the opponent. Hits behind Billy as well; even when this happens, he will still launch them forward. All hits nullify projectiles, even other projectile supers like Terry\u2019s Power Geyser. Does not reach from the max range of close C. The final hit launches on Counter Hit, but has additional scaling. A very strong reversal super and, when in range, devastating combo ender. The forward-moving fire ring can catch opponents off guard if they\u2019re too far out to block and punish, but a well-timed jump-in will go over this. A Version : Does high damage and is fast for a basic super, has great okizeme, works in all corner juggles. All-around amazing super that you will want to use in combos, sometimes as a reversal, or even as an anti-air under the right circumstances. Its range is a bit of an issue. However, in some juggles, such as after 236BD, you can either do 623AC into this move to score damage at the cost of REV. If you don\u2019t want to spend REV, you can cancel dash into this super from certain juggles by using the dash macro, which is done by inputting 236Rev > 236A. C Version: Deals a bit more damage and has better startup, which makes it work in more combos. However, it\u2019s worse than the A version in almost every other way. The range is identical. Unless you need the extra damage to close out the round, it\u2019s better to stick to the level 1 version, keeping your extra bar just in case.",
    "236236bd": "Billy lifts his opponent up with his staff, then jumps after them and covers them in his staff's flames. Has way better corner carry and range than his other supers, at the cost of damage. Only the D version is invincible on startup. If it hits an extended limb, the opponent will fall out as Billy goes through the whole animation, leaving him wide open for a punish on the ground. This never happens in combos. A very long-reaching super that is quite combo friendly. It combos from all ranges of your lights, including the max range of far A. B Version: Easily combos from light normals and various juggles, such as after 236BD and 214AC. Its superior corner carry combined with the range makes it ideal as your combo super midscreen, even if it doesn\u2019t deal as much damage. This is Billy\u2019s best Zero Frame super by far unless you have Hidden Gear. Its range guarantees it reaches opponents no matter how they\u2019ve spaced their move. On block, only one hit connects against crouching opponents, but the super is extremely unsafe regardless of guard. D Version: Reaches absurdly far (almost 2/3 screen) while becoming fully invincible on startup. This makes it a very strong projectile punish in addition to comboing from even more than the B version, such as single-hit confirming far C or some 214P juggle routes. You can Brake this super, but you'll need to spend a lot of REV to make up the damage (although you get to build some meter back in doing so). The super Brakes automatically on block, but it\u2019s still very unsafe.",
    "236acac": "Billy extends his staff directly forward at the opponent. A super long-reaching poke that outranges most characters' jump ranges. All versions lose to High Dodge Attack and are considerably punishable if jumped. A version will only air reset against a jumping opponent, but juggles properly in combos. Beware that while the follow-ups to C and AC versions are mostly safe on block, characters with long range specials can punish using Guard Cancels. This move right here is why you play Billy. A massively forward-reaching poke that hits opponents from well beyond their reach. Use this move to aggravate, infuriate, and really get under the skin of your opponent. You can end a lot of combos with this as well, to push the opponent all the way back out and make them work their way back in - against this very move, perhaps. Just beware of its glaring flaws, which are its excessive recovery on whiff and the fact that High Dodge Attack beats it; you can, however, feint this move to see how your opponent will deal with it, whether they try to jump, Guard Cancel or High Dodge Attack. The A version is faster and does more damage but is a standalone poke, and the C version is slower and weaker on its own but allows you to follow it up with one of two options, as seen below. REV (A+C) Version: Hits three times and has the speed of the A version with the follow-ups of the C version. You can juggle with it only once in a combo, but the follow-ups can juggle only once each. You should almost always do the follow-ups with the AC version, since cancelling them into other REV arts allows you to start your offense from half-screen and beyond.",
    "236bdbd": "Billy pole-vaults upward, kicking the opponent up with him, then comes back down spinning his staff. D version is Billy's only brakable special move. For all versions, the gap between jumping up and landing is punishable by running under Billy and hitting from the opposite side. REV version can be canceled into j.REV Blow. The classic pole vault. The B version combos from lights, and it\u2019s optimal from a two-light sequence, but from three or more, the first hit will whiff, causing you to lose damage. On block, it's terrible because the opponent can run under while Billy is airborne and punish from the other side. The D version combos from heavies and has a brakable hit that launches the opponent, allowing for a full combo; unlike brakable DPs, it adds no additional scaling. Its high pushback on block also makes it tough to punish. REV (B+D) Version: Launches the opponent even if you don't brake it. Thanks to this, almost every REV combo Billy has will go into this move, and from there he can end in 214C, super, or even more REV moves like another 623AC if he feels like it. Braking the BD version is a whopping +5 on block, and on hit, combos into D brake, launching the opponent twice for an even higher launch. Braking the BD version still lets you use the rest of the move later in the combo.",
    "236cac4p": "Billy uses the end of his staff to pull the opponent a short distance toward himself. Both versions are safe on block. Vacuum on hit and block, but doesn\u2019t knock a standing opponent down. The alternate, safer follow-up to 236C/AC, which lets you push a strike/throw mix on the opponent when you hit it grounded. Although the meterless version is minus on block, it sets up spacing traps nicely for Billy using his superior reach. Ending combos with the staff pull gives you fantastic okizeme that lets you pressure all four wakeup rolls simultaneously. REV (A+C) Version: Pulls the opponent slightly further than the C version (depending on distance) and has slightly better frame advantage. Its main use is to pull opponents hit at a distance into a full combo using REV Accel. It consistently combos to 623AC on both grounded and airborne opponents, but if it hits an airborne opponent too high, it can pull them up and over your 623AC, so in those instances, do another REV move instead (like 214AC).",
    "236cac6p": "Billy makes the end of his extended staff explode. C version is really unsafe on block, and can be punished by some super moves at any range (such as Terry's Buster Wolf). A knockdown followup to 236C that does generous damage, on top of fantastic okizeme if the opponent is cornered, especially if you use the AC version. It\u2019s possible to confirm this followup when seeing the 236C hit, or using the followup below instead to stay safe by the frames. REV (A+C) Version: Retains its damage with a slightly higher launch on hit. Up close and in the corner, it combos to 214AC and 236BD, which both lead to some frighteningly high damage. From far away, it gives a slightly better knockdown than the meterless version, but the pull followup gives better oki and more REV accel options.",
    "2a": "Chains into every light normal except itself. Combos into 236B at tip range, making it an extremely important normal. Also combos into 214B rekkas for less damage, better knockdown? and extra hitconfirm opportunity. A crouching poke, slightly faster than far A but doesn't have quite the range. You can chain light normals into and out of it, but you can't chain it into itself. Thus it is also used as filler in light confirms and blockstrings.",
    "2b": "Billy's fastest non-proximity normal. A quick, low-hitting starter for his light combos. Can be chained into from close A. The universal low-hitting light combo starter. It chains into his other lights, basically does what you expect a 2B to do. It also just happens to be Billy\u2019s fastest non-proximity attack. As a safe low, it's good to press against opponents trying to walk out of throw range, since you're plus on hit, and its speed and low properties make it difficult to Just Defend against.",
    "2bcor2drev": "Mimics Senpuu Kon(623P).",
    "2c": "Wild Punish +20f. A special-cancellable anti-air. Unlike High Dodge Attack, it also cancels on block. Counter Hitting an airborne opponent combos to 214A/C/AC. Billy's late anti-airs are trash, being limited to this normal and his High Dodge Attack. Unlike HDA, 2C also special cancels on block, forcing the opponent to Just Defend multiple times when jumping in. It doesn't have high invincibility, however, so timing it as an anti-air is more precise. On Wild Punish, you get dash up into close D, just like when far D Wild Punishes.",
    "2crev": "Cannot be cancelled on block. Wins against jumping attacks, standing normals and REV Blows. Combos starting with it have additional scaling. Sadly, this is your best anti-air. However, the slowdown you get when beating moves with it allows you to confirm your special cancels so you don't whiff something on the air reset from a normal hit.",
    "2d": "Short range on the first hit makes it hard to punish blocked sweep with your own. On a Wild Punish, puts the opponent in an OTG state, allowing you to hit them with an extra low attack (universal for all sweeps). It looks like it hits twice, but only hits once. That said, the second hit is good for tagging opponents who stop blocking low, as well as giving extra pushback to make punishing it much more difficult.",
    "4ab": "Can be followed by a dash-jump C for a safejump (vs 5f reversals).",
    "5bcor5drev": "Mimics Sansetsu Kon Chuudan Uchi(236P). All three of Billy\u2019s feints have the same frame data, so they\u2019re identical in combos.",
    "5crev": "Universal overhead, wins against lows and throws. Triggers a slowdown if it meets either of these. On Counter Hit or Wild Punish, links to close D. This is Billy's only grounded overhead, but a fantastic one at that. On top of opening up crouching opponents, it leaves you close and plus enough for your feint cancel BnB on Counter Hit or Wild Punish, so whenever you successfully call out a low or throw with it, it can lead to massive damage. Hitting it meaty also allows you to combo from it by linking to 2B. Despite its slower startup than most LDAs, the animation can make it rather tricky to react to, thus it ends up forming a major part of Billy's offense, especially when the opponent is cornered.",
    "623acac": "Billy twirls his staff around. Each version deflects projectiles of equal strength, including the AC version. Can be held indefinitely, but is easily countered by using low attacks (or Rev Blow). Regular, non-held version recovers slightly faster after a successful deflect. While the AC version is plus on block, it can only be REV cancelled at the third or last hits, making it very susceptible to Hyper Defense. AC version will combo after almost every REV cancel, no matter the state of the opponent. Meterless 623P is pretty funny. It destroys incoming fireballs while being deceptively tricky to get around, so it can be a daunting move for an unprepared opponent to deal with. You can end longer corner combos in meterless 623P for an air reset that can force strike/throw at the cost of damage; in this scenario, you can also hold it and make them land into it for added chip damage, which may checkmate some Overheated characters depending on the matchup and resources available to them, but it\u2019s usually better to combo into a real knockdown. REV (A+C) Version: This is one of Billy's best REV moves, especially in combos, as plenty of things combo into and out of it, including REV pull (236AC~4P). It also has the unique property of not having a juggle limit, so it can be used multiple times in combos, limited only by your REV meter and the number of ways to juggle into it. When hitting an airborne opponent, it juggles consistently to 236236A/C and Hidden Gear. Outside of combos, it's also an advancing attack that's plus on block, but its high number of hits make it vulnerable to Just Defense/Hyper Defense + Guard Cancels. Great to abuse an opponent in Overheat with, however.",
    "6a": "Billy does a spin towards the opponent. Advances with two hits. Covers about the same space as Far C, while the forward movement slips under jump-in attempts. Goes over grounded projectiles (except for Geese\u2019s Reppuu Kens). Safe on block unlike 6B, but doesn't reach as far. Beware that both hits jail, so if the 1st gets blocked the 2nd can be punished by a Hyper Defense Guard Cancel. An advancing command normal that is quite annoying with its two advancing, jailing hits, general safety, ability to high-profile some grounded fireballs, and annoying voice clip. It's dangerous to whiff, however, so try not to. After many of Billy's hard knockdowns - particularly REV Blow - you can frame-kill with this move, advancing forward all the while. In some cases, you will still have enough time afterward to shimmy the opponent on their wakeup.",
    "6b": "Billy pole-vaults feet-first at the opponent. Goes very far, but is unsafe on block and loses to High Dodge Attack. Goes over low attacks and projectiles. Space this move to make it safe, perhaps even plus. Its abundant active frames make some meaty setups possible. Great for punishing grounded fireballs like ones from Rock or Terry, but otherwise your main goal with this move is not to hit it, but use it to reposition. For example, if the opponent full jumps, a 6B will go clean under them while maintaining the distance you had before. Another way to use this move is on okizeme, either to close space after an anti-air 214P or push offense when it's adequately spaced. If it's not spaced or if it whiffs, though, it's quite easy for the opponent to punish.",
    "6bcor6drev": "Mimics Suzume Otoshi(214P).",
    "ab": "Can be followed by a dash-jump C for a safejump (vs 5f reversals).",
    "cla": "Can be chained into from 2A, and chains into other light normals. Billy's combination attack is accessed from this normal, and is his optimal meterless combo from it by damage. When it hits point blank, you get a four-light sequence (cl.A 2B 2A 5A) into 236236K or Hidden Gear. Billy's fastest normal, which has generous confirming ability into light specials, supers, or his Combination Attack. Like other close A's, it's plus on block. Its low hitbox for a close A makes it weak as an anti-air, so it can't be used as a hop check.",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. cl.AC, cl.ACC, cl.ACCD are REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : First hit of the auto-combo, can only be canceled into a follow-up or an ender. cl.AC > C : Follow-up that can be canceled into any ender or REV Blow. A simple way to deal more damage, increase the REV, or reduce the opponent\u2019s guard bar(Overheat). D ender : Just a mid ender, soft knockdown on hit and unsafe on block but it's REV Blow cancellable and combos on hit. With REV Blow, this route is the only way for Billy to score a hard knockdown from lights without spending a super. With or without REV blow, this is Billy's optimal meterless combo from close A starter by damage. 6D ender : Overhead ender, +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Two hit low ender and same move than his 2D, so it's very unsafe on block. For some reason, this move does a ton of REV damage.",
    "clb": "Billy's most advantageous normal on block without feint cancel, allowing tick throws. Links to 2B on normal hit, allowing Billy to confirm into light strings on reaction. Links to either close heavy on Counter Hit when close. A powerful move in Billy's offense, close B gives you light strings on hit while allowing you to push a mixup on block, thanks to being +3 with little pushback. Its link to close C on Counter Hit can only be done way up close, but it's the only way to confirm into Billy's REV moves from a light normal.",
    "clc": "Slower than close D, but can be used at longer range thanks to close C\u2019s superior proximity range. This normal is essentially just combo filler, since it links reliably from close D feint cancel. With that said, you should familiarize yourself with the move's proximity range, as knowing it will save you from accidentally doing far C and dropping your combos when you're trying to link into it.",
    "cld": "The faster but shorter of the close heavies. Forces stand on hit. Its great advantage on block, epsecially when feint cancelled, allows you to set up frame traps and dash pressure. If you feint cancel this move on Counter Hit, you can dash into a second close D, but it's tight to time. Billy's best button on offense by a country mile, as long as you feint cancel it, being ridiclous on both hit and block. You pretty much always want to feint cancel it, there's little reason not to; On hit when feint cancelled, links into close C, which leads into all your strongest combos. It also frame traps to close C on block. You can really run up a turtling opponent\u2019s REV gauge by doing close D feint cancel on block, then dashing it up and doing it again. Each time they block, they are left in a horrid situation where anything they press likely doesn't reach any buttons you press afterward. However, do it too much and the opponent can check it with their own close normals.",
    "fa": "Billy's longest-ranged light attack. Consistently combos when chained into from other light normals. No longer special cancellable as of Season 2. This normal is very good at poking, reaching well beyond the typical range of most other characters. Billy can confirm a super at its max range.",
    "fb": "Has shorter range than crouch A, but with pretty much the same frame data. Good for cancelling to meterless specials due to more damage and less pushback, letting it link consistently to 214B. On Counter Hit, combos to 623AC or 236D. Make sure you chain into this to get the most damage out of your light strings.",
    "fc": "Wild Punish +12 (+22 if feint cancelled). The longest-ranged special-cancellable move in the game. Has a large hurtbox, so loses to High Dodge Attack even when the opponent is far away. On Wild Punish, you can feint cancel to link into a second far C. AWFUL on whiff. Easily Wild Punished if jumped over. A huge normal that reaches beyond half screen and even special cancels. The range and threat of high damage and potentially pulling the opponent into a mixup via special cancel ensure that this normal will be on the opponent's mind a lot; just so much as pressing it can intimidate your opponent into looking for ways to get around it. But whatever you do, don\u2019t whiff this move. If it gets jumped over, the opponent can easily Wild Punish you. It also gets beaten by High Dodge Attack. Due to this, it\u2019s best to keep this move to where you know it will make contact.",
    "fd": "Wild Punish +21f. Despite appearances, does not whiff on crouch. Can be used in spacing traps against jabs, since Billy's hurtbox doesn't expand before the strike. Massive Wild Punish starter; you can dash into cl.D from any range. This move\u2019s good range and nice hitbox angle make it a suffocating normal to press in advantage - especially when the opponent is cornered. Since Billy's hurtbox doesn't move forward until after the attack comes out, it is very tough to challenge, even from the air. You will most often use this move in shimmies, however, due to its HUGE reward when it hits as a Wild Punish. When it does, Billy has enough time to dash and do his close D combos, including the Feint Cancel BnB. Landing this is your best opportunity to dump REV into a big combo, as you can easily do upwards of 600 damage with a super.",
    "ja": "Air-to-air with good speed-size ratio. Cancels into Rev Blow. Doesn't combo but can be used to blow up air Just Defends or the opponents j.Rev Blow. The hop version does less damage. Control the air with this move. It's fast and reaches far, making it perfect when meeting the opponent in the air. If you make contact with the opponent's jumping REV Blow, you can cancel this move into your own to stop it.",
    "jb": "Can cross up. Part of the j.B,D command combo. This move\u2019s speed and hitbox make it surprisingly good as a retreating hop aerial, particularly because it has much less of a problem hitting grounded opponents than j.A.",
    "jbjd": "Only available from full jumps. Can be used for jump-ins or air-to-airs. When you land j.B on an airborne opponent, you might as well go through with the target combo, just in case the j.B Counter Hits. You have nothing else to do after j.B and going through with the target combo helps you set up a left-right mixup on their landing.",
    "jc": "Massive hitbox. A horizontally far-reaching jump attack for pre-emptively controlling space or exceptionally far reaching jump-ins. It's practically impossible to deal with if spaced unless the opponent has a hard read.",
    "jd": "This button is your main jump-in. It deals more damage and comes out faster than j.C."
  },
  "blue_mary": {
    "214acac": "placeholder 214A/C : placeholder 214AC : placeholder",
    "214acac6p": "All versions result in a non-EX Quick Step (even the AC version). Cannot be cancelled into Quick Step: D. Same as the original: Forward Quick Step that can be cancelled into all follow-ups except Quick Step: D.",
    "214bdbd": "Blue Mary\u2019s dodge move is even throw-invulnerable during some of its fully invulnerable frames. Both versions can be cancelled into her command grab follow-up 214B/D : placeholder 214BD : placeholder",
    "214bdbd46p": "Values in brackets apply when used after a Quick Step > Real Counter.",
    "214or236acac2k": "placeholder",
    "214or236acac3k": "Cannot be braked on whiff.",
    "214or236acac4k": "placeholder",
    "214or236acac6k": "placeholder",
    "236236ac": "Mary grabs the opponent and gives them a back-breaking submission from behind. Both versions have invincibility on startup. Doing this super during Quick Step gives you a special version that combos properly on opponents in hitstun with no loss in damage (other than the scaling). Opponents can jump the basic version after the super flash, but not the Redline version. This grab super has fast startup, shockingly high damage, and multiple ways to land it. Its very quick startup makes it great at punishing some moves that are otherwise safe, interrupting frame traps, and stopping guard point moves (like REV Blows), making it scary on defense. Unlike other grab supers, however, you can even combo into it; when you do this super from a Quick Step, you can grab opponents in hitstun, giving you a confirm with very little scaling. Since it\u2019s only one hit, using it right before an opponent enters S.P.G will wipe out the vast majority of their health within it, and you can make them guess for the rest of it with the super\u2019s great okizeme. While the Redline version takes away the opponent\u2019s ability to jump it on reaction, the minimal extra damage makes dumping both bars into it pointless. Just stick to the basic version in combos.",
    "236236bd": "Mary jumps to knee the opponent, then giant swings them in the other direction. Both versions have generous invincibility on startup. Switches sides on hit. Becomes an unblockable air grab if it doesn\u2019t hit on the early active frames. Taking into account her other command grab super - if used as a reversal, this forces her opponent into a quick reaction check to either block or jump in order to not get hit. The Blue Mary classic. This super is your strongest combo ender when dumping REV into your special moves, usually as a cash-out or off of a successful mix-up via REV Accel. It\u2019s important to remember that it switches sides on hit. Since comboing into it will cause you to lose the corner, you have to change up your entire combo or just give up on the super entirely if you want to keep opponents cornered. On the flipside, that makes it extra scary as a reversal; if it works when you\u2019re cornered, suddenly they\u2019re the ones trapped and you can start your own offense. Try not to use the Redline version as a reversal, as Mary\u2019s supers are too strong to dump both bars just for the extra 90 damage. It\u2019s fine to close out a round with it, as it\u2019s your highest damage ender without Hidden Gear.",
    "236236rev": "Mary delivers a lunging elbow, triggering a cinematic on hit. Only available during S.P.G. Resets the Rev meter to 0%, even during Overheat. Hits from half-screen, making some attacks punishable exclusively by this move. Mary\u2019s Hidden Gear is her strongest combo ender, and works in all the same combos as M. Dynamite Swing while avoiding the side switch. It\u2019s also effective as a reversal, with the added bonus of resetting Mary\u2019s REV meter on hit. Ironically, her Hidden Gear\u2019s poor okizeme often makes it less desirable to use than even her basic supers unless it will kill, especially if she needs to make a comeback. However, the REV reset may prove invaluable in giving you back access to your REV specials to put the pressure on later in the round.",
    "236acac": "Frame advantage on Quick Step cancels",
    "236acac4p": "All versions result in a non-EX Quick Step (even the AC version). Cannot be cancelled into Quick Step: A. Same as the original: Backward Quick Step that can be cancelled into all follow-ups except Quick Step: A \u2014 So no infinite loops, sadly.",
    "236acac6p": "placeholder",
    "236bdbd": "placeholder 236B : placeholder 236D : Plus on block if the opponent crouches. 236BD : Always plus on block.",
    "236dbd46k": "Even though the move list says this follow-up requires a 4/6K motion, it is also possible to do perform it using 2K. This is probably to make the Quick Step -> Slicer follow up more intuitive to do.",
    "26bcor26drev": "Blue Mary's fastest feint and the one used in her normal framedata. Mimics Real Counter.",
    "2a": "HORRIBLE range for a 2A. This ruins what would otherwise be a great button. Your most reliable and highest damage ender is far B > D > D Command Combo. That\u2019s it. Combos to 623D brake up close, but this will scale your combo hard.",
    "2b": "A pretty decent 2B. Use it to stop opponents from walking away from your Quick Step mix. Your main low abare. Easily converts into 5B command combo at most ranges.",
    "2c": "Stubby, yet highly important pressure tool. Wild Punish +18 (+24 if feint cancelled) Massive confirm window into any Slicer, including from Quick Step. Combos into itself on Counter Hit when feint canceled.",
    "2crev": "Cannot be cancelled on block. Wins against standing normals, jump attacks and REV Blows. It's really really small. Use it sparingly.",
    "2d": "Pretty average sweep. On Wild Punish, puts the opponent in an OTG state. Your highest damage follow-up is REV Blow, but outside of S.P.G, you can follow up with a second 2D or any version of Straight Slicer.",
    "4ab": "This throw catapults opponents a pretty substantial distance, so it\u2019s really difficult to push a mix-up afterward. However, against cornered opponents, safe jump setups do exist, and Real Counter still dodges wakeups.",
    "5bcor5drev": "Mimics the startup of Spin Fall. Crosses up when close. This isn't mix, but it can be used to screw up someone's DP reaction.",
    "5crev": "Universal overhead, wins against lows and throws. On Counter Hit or Wild Punish, links to 2C. Safer as an overhead than 6B, but doesn\u2019t give a combo outside of Counter Hit or Wild Punish.",
    "623bdbd": "Only the D version allows a manual follow-up, while BD triggers it automatically if not braked. 623B : A very fast DP that beats air attacks clean. Not invincible on startup except to air attacks. 623D : One of the slower brakeable DPs in the game at 9 frames but launches the opponent unusually high. Usually followed with a charged (i.e. non-dash) D Straight Slicer, which can be juggled into any of her non-command grab supers, or a 623B. Mary can even combo into a ground REV blow off of this due to the juggle height. The braked version of this move recovers 9 frames slower on whiff. 623BD : Cannot be braked on whiff and is air unblockable.",
    "623d46k": "Even though the move list says this follow-up requires a 4/6K motion, it is also possible to do perform it using 2K. This is probably to make the Quick Step -> Slicer follow up more intuitive to do.",
    "6b": "Quick overhead. Your best option after this is 2B > 5B > command combo. Vertical Arrow is prone to whiffing at most ranges, and light slicer doesn't grant a hard knockdown.",
    "ab": "Two forward dashes after a successful throw gets you point blank and +3, perfect for pushing Mary\u2019s deadliest mix-ups. This setup also perfectly times Real Counter to dodge every wakeup reversal, except for a few supers.",
    "cd": "A worse-than-average REV Blow when used raw. It really struggles to hit anyone in the air, the hitbox is not good. Travels super far forward before coming out, making it easy to whiff punish and easier to grab. Hits OTG, and is in fact Mary\u2019s most damaging OTG by a mile when it\u2019s available. Use it when your sweep Wild Punishes.",
    "cla": "Chains into close B at point blank range, which is crucial for her REV Quick Step combos.",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. cl.AC first hit is cancellable by any follow-ups. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : First hit of the auto combo, cancellable by any follow-ups. cl.AC > C : A second hit cancellable by any ender, a nice way to increase the damage or your opponent REV meter. Unsafe on block and can be punished if the auto-combo was started too close to the opponent. D ender : Far D follow up, different frame data and damage, very unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Safest ender, Same move as her Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV) but +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Same move as her 2D, meaning it's very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "Terrifying normal on Blue Mary's kit that grants high damage on hit and fantastic pressure on block. Links into close C at point blank range, which is crucial for her REV Quick Step combos. Links into 2C otherwise.",
    "clc": "Mary swings her hand upwards in a slicing motion, hitting twice. Wild Punish +21 (+27 if feint cancelled). A very important button for Mary and key to many of her combo routes. If cancelled into a REV Quick Step, Mary will be +5 on hit +0 on block , allowing a link into either cl.A or 2A > clB. On a counter hit, you can combo into clB and link into another clC off that. You must confirm the counter hit into either clB or clC, because the extra counter hit pushback will cause the usual clA > clB link to turn into clA > fB. Links into 2C after a feint cancel. Second hitbox is surprisingly high, will catch attempted hops and jumps with ease.",
    "cld": "Mary strikes the opponent in the midsection with a knee. Wild Punish +23 (+28 if feint cancelled) Forces stand on hit Links into itself on a feint-cancelled counter hit, but usually requires a difficult dash cancel or microwalk. Cancelling into 236AC makes this link much easier, at the cost of REV. +2 on block when canceled with 236AC.",
    "fa": "It's unimpressive.",
    "farbd": "Mary spins around and does a high side kick with her other foot The 2nd kick is largely similar to her far D but with a worse hitbox that will not hit crouching opponents even if close. It is +3 on block for some reason. However, if the initial far B hits, the 2nd kick will hit crouching opponents. It can also be slightly delayed, allowing extra time for hit-confirms or the ability to mess up an opponent's JD/HD timing.",
    "fardd": "This is the Command Combo of all time. Only comes out if far D hit or was blocked. While it helps to have your pokes do more damage, this Command Combo has a lot of problems. It\u2019s always unsafe on block, you\u2019re never in throw range on hit, and doing it means you lose out on far D\u2019s excellent Counter Hit or Wild Punish conversions. It\u2019s good for when far D Counter Hits an airborne or backdashing opponent, but that\u2019s about it.",
    "fb": "Mary does a straight side kick. Reliably combos into D > D Command Combo, unless it hits an extended hurtbox.",
    "fc": "Long ranged, but pretty terrible framedata wise. Wild Punish +19 (+23 if feint cancelled). Converts into 236AC > clB on counterhit. Mary can usually get a run up clD on feint cancel if wild punish, even from far away. Reliably punishes most sweeps. This is a risky button to use, but since it's your largest mid you have to use it eventually. Decent to counterhit fish with if your timing is good. Nonetheless, having a bad long ranged button is better than having no long ranged buttons at all.",
    "fd": "Mary does a high roundhouse kick. Wild Punish +24. Misses crouching opponents unless very close. Mary's best Wild Punish button with the best combination of range and speed.",
    "hold46bdbd": "Mary slides across the screen at quick speed. NOT A LOW B version : placeholder D version : placeholder BD version : placeholder",
    "hold46dbd46k": "Even though the move list says this follow-up requires a 4/6K motion, it is also possible to do perform it using 2K. This is probably to make the Quick Step -> Slicer follow up more intuitive to do.",
    "ja": "Mary\u2019s fastest air normal, with an available Command Combo follow-up on Counter Hit or Wild Punish.",
    "jajb": "One of those air Command Combos that sets up a left-right mix-up on the opponent\u2019s landing, and off of Mary\u2019s fastest air normal at that. Can only be done during a full jump. Hitting it on an airborne opponent while descending allows you to or Quick Step under the opponent to go to the other side. Slightly delaying the dash fakes this cross-under and keeps you same side.",
    "jb": "Great downward-reaching normal to start combos or pressure. Can cross-up",
    "jc": "Fantastic pre emptive air to air/pressure tool. Grants a knockdown on hit, which you can actually convert from in most cases with 623D brake. Ignorant hitbox, but very slow. However, unlike most other normals that put the opponent into a juggle state, it has an extra active frame and does more damage compared to em. As such, it hits crouchers a lot easier than the others. Given its range, this makes Mary's jC an incredibly strong callout air to air, and a superbly powerful part of her offense.",
    "jcd": "Mary somersaults in the air before lunging with an outstretched arm. If it hits, she transitions into a flying arm bar, pulling her opponent into the ground and snapping the arm. Does not go into the hitgrab if it hits an airborne opponent, but the full damage of the REV Blow will instead be on the first hit. Also does not go into the hitgrab on counter hit, but does not deal full damage on grounded opponents.",
    "jd": "Long, but shallow jD. Doesn't see too much use over jC, but it does have incredible range, offering occasional utility."
  },
  "chun_li": {
    "214bdbd": "Chun-Li does an aerial cartwheel and lands with a leg drop. All versions can be braked with AB, which causes her to land without attacking. Can be used to punish fireballs preemptively. +6 on a Counter Hit or Wild Punish, allowing links into 5B, but pushback makes this unreliable. Has no problems linking into Hidden Gear in the same situation. B version reaches about slightly more than half screen and can hit from round start position. D version goes up a higher arc and reaches about 3/4 of the screen. BD version is mostly identical to the D version but is faster and is an actual overhead. All versions can OTG but both D and BD versions do more damage than a sweep (3D). This is useful after a wild punish 3D to squeeze out slightly more damage.",
    "236236ac": "Chun-Li fires off a massive, stationary Kikoken. Reversal super that leaves Chun-Li extremely open if jumped.",
    "236236bd": "Let's go Justin All versions can be jump cancelled on the last launching kick. Chun-Li always jumps forward no matter which jump direction is inputted. B version is slow and is unable to combo from many juggle routes (regular dp brake, EX SBK into EX Air Kicks, to name a few) D version is slightly faster and can be braked on the upwards launching kick. Often does less damage in longer combos compared to Kikosho but has much greater mixup potential going for air stomps resets. Adds 20% scaling to the combo on hit.",
    "236236rev": "Costs 2 bars and only avaiable during S.P.G., displayed damage is with S.P.G. bonus. Resets the Rev meter to 0%, even during Overheat.",
    "236acac": "Chun-Li fires a mid-height fireball that travels horizontally across the screen. Regular versions are very unsafe on block and should not be used at very close distances. A version is slow and stops just short of full screen. C version is fast and fizzles out even sooner, traveling about 2/3's screen width. AC version is as fast as the C version and doesn't dissipate while traveling.",
    "236bdbd": "Chun-Li's signature flurry of kicks. B version combos from any light and is safe on block. D version can only combo from 3C, 4C and high evade. Launches the opponent, which leads to very high damage. BD version the REV combo starter of choice and combos from 5C, 2C or 6A.",
    "26bcordrev": "Feints the startup of Kikoken (fireball). The main feint used for combos.",
    "2a": "Chains into all of her lights, including itself A standard 2A. It has a slightly shorter range and lower damage than 5A, but you can use it to build charge for SBK, her optimal light combo ender. Keep in mind that 3A is her overhead input. You do not want to be inputting the 21-frame overhead accidentally during close-range abare or frame traps.",
    "2b": "A quick low starter that is as fast as Chun\u2019s other lights Can chain into any light normal, but if you chain it into itself, your only combo afterward is a super Unlike the modern SF games, does not cancel to special moves Having the same startup as her A buttons, this normal is just as good to mash or pressure with, since it hits low. You can chain it into itself for repeated lows to stop the opponent from walking away from your offense, but since it\u2019s minus on hit and block, opponents might mash on you after seeing it.",
    "2c": "Can link from 5C but has the very annoying tendency to miss if 5C doesn't hit from point blank. In most cases it is safer to cancel straight from 5C since both moves combo into the same special moves. Can also link from 5B with the same pitfalls Occasional linking woes aside, Chun\u2019s 2C is an incredible normal. On top of going into all of her special moves on hit, it\u2019s also fast for a non-proximity heavy, has good range, and easily frame traps from other light normals. The downside is that it\u2019s always minus on block, and she has no good cancels to push pressure without spending a ton of REV, but at the very least, it\u2019s safe.",
    "2crev": "Beats High/Mid, can be used as an anti-air. Combos into 236D on a grounded hit which can be easily confirmed into if it triggers a slowdown on a grounded opponent. Otherwise, combos into 623D brake on a Counter/Wild Punish anti-air. However, missing this confirm against an empty jump leaves Chun-Li punishable.",
    "2d": "A long-ranged low starter The only special cancels that combo from long range are her fireballs and 623BD, meaning you will have to commit to your cancels from it In-engine, low starters like this are quite powerful, stopping your opponent from simply walking away from your offense and forcing specific block directions for Just Defense. Because of this, Chun\u2019s 2D is one of her best moves on offense and in footsies. The issues with 2D, however, are the relatively weak cancels from it, no semblance of a single-hit confirm, and being horrible on block on its own. Thus you\u2019ll be forced to commit to specific special cancels, and you\u2019ll need to spend more REV to get good damage from it.",
    "3a": "Chun-Li leans into an overhead strike. Links into 5A or 2A on hit, with 5A doing 4 more damage. Can be made up to +7 on hit if hit meaty, allowing stronger links.",
    "3b": "Chun-Li jumps over the opponent and turns around to land on the other side with a kick. A cross-up only move. Cannot hit opponents in the corner. Unlike Street Fighter, you cannot combo after this move without Counter Hit or Wild Punish. Airborne for most of its duration, but not immediately, so meaty throws still beat it.",
    "3c": "Chun-Li swipes upward twice from a crouching position. One of only two normals which can combo into 236D, which is key to dealing high meterless damage, especially in the corner. The startup of this move is too slow to combo from any regular jump in without a Counter Hit or Wild Punish.",
    "3d": "Chun-Li goes for the ankles with a thrust kick. This is Chun-Li\u2019s sweep. For a sweep, it\u2019s got good range. Although it\u2019s definitely punishable, it can be spaced to avoid some characters\u2019 meterless punishes. On Wild Punish, causes an OTG state that allows for a second 3D or any version of Hazanshu.",
    "4ab": "Better oki than her forward throw on paper, but the distance at which she throws them makes pressuring them on wakeup rather awkward. She can meaty the opponent\u2019s wakeup with a fireball, but certain reversal supers will beat it.",
    "4c": "Chun-Li\u2019s infamous double palm. One of only two normals which can combo into 236D, which is key to dealing high meterless damage, especially in the corner. If she doesn't have enough time for a jump D, this will be her optimal DP punish starter when cancelled into a forward/down feint. Interestingly, causes a knockdown on a Wild Punish, which may be a reference to this move crush countering in SFV. This is generally suboptimal and Chun-Li is better served doing 6C or 6D to start a Wild Punish.",
    "5a": "Chains into all of her lights, including itself (best chain option is usually 5B) Leads into her Combination Attack, but at a certain range, the Combination Attack will whiff Good hitbox for checking hops Chun has no 4-frame normal, so you will have to settle for this when mashing. That said, it\u2019s a really good button to mash, chaining into itself with little pushback. Chain this into 5B when possible.",
    "5a5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during 5AC or 5ACC. 5AC, 5ACC, 5ACCD are REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with 5A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. 5AC : First hit of the auto-combo, can only be canceled into a follow-up or an ender. 5AC > C : Follow-up that can be canceled into any ender or REV Blow. A simple way to deal more damage, increase the REV, or reduce the opponent\u2019s guard bar(Overheat). D ender : Just a mid ender, soft knockdown on hit and unsafe on block but it's REV Blow cancellable. 6D ender : Overhead ender, +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Low ender and same move than his 3D, so it's very unsafe on block.",
    "5b": "Quite plus on hit and block, especially compared to her other lights You can link this to 2C at most ranges (except when chaining 2+ lights into this) The range is deceptively long This goofy-looking normal, taken straight from 3rd Strike, is a core part of her offense in this game. It\u2019s very plus on hit and block, has surprising range, and even links to her heavies with the right combo routes. Chun gets a lot of mileage out of this move with its linking and cancel options, especially since you can chain into it.",
    "5bcordrev": "Feints the startup of Hundred Lightning Kicks. Is much slower but looks very convincing and threatening.",
    "5c": "Extremely good on hit and block; links to 2C, 6A, etc. The range isn\u2019t great, so some links into it will whiff depending on your route All-around fantastic button up close. It\u2019s fast, leads to GREAT damage, and is plus with not a whole lot of pushback \u2014 making it great for combos and pressure. Its only downside is its range, which is solved by Chun\u2019s other normals.",
    "5crev": "Beats Lows and Throws. Outclassed by 3A as an overhead because it doesn't lead to anything on regular hit. Also outclassed by 6D as a low/throw crush as it gains low/throw invulnerability slower and has lower damage potential.",
    "5d": "Wild Punish +22 Has a tiny bit of forward movement on startup Whiffs on crouching opponents Chun\u2019s dedicated Wild Punish button. Its startup isn\u2019t great, but the quick recovery (for a heavy normal) makes up for it. Try not to overuse this normal when fishing for whiff punishes, though, as opponents can duck it and punish.",
    "623or22bdbd": "Placeholder",
    "6a": "Chun-Li punches with both hands. Links from stand C and works from most ranges where stand C hits. Compared to 2C, it has more range, but is a frame slower and does slightly less damage. On hit, combos to all the REV moves that 2C combos into.",
    "6b": "Chun-Li does a quick thrust kick. Absurdly long range for its speed, at just 7f startup. Poke with fairly low commitment. Can be used to confirm into Supers in ranges where 2C doesn't reach after 5B.",
    "6c": "Chun-Li\u2019s infamous, super-far-reaching Heavy Punch from SFV/SF6. Chun-Li\u2019s longest ranged normal. +22 on Wild Punish, leaving enough time for Chun-Li to dash in for a combo at most ranges.",
    "6d": "Chun-Li does a leaping crescent kick of sorts. A low crush that dodges lows much more quickly than her low evade. It also has the added benefit of being a valid Wild Punish heavy which doesn't scale like evasion moves, leading to very high damage. First 2 active frames whiff on crounching characters. Advantage on hit or block will therefore vary by 2 Frames is the opponent is crouching.",
    "8ja": "A two-hit attack. The hits combo into each other on Counter Hit. Fast and decently ranged, this is an interesting air normal that is mainly effective at stopping jumps out of the corner. It has trouble hitting grounded opponents, though.",
    "8jb": "Chun-Li\u2019s fastest air normal. Lacking in range, but makes up for it with its absurd active frames A good panic button in air-to-air exchanges due to it being Chun\u2019s fastest air normal. When used on offense, you can use its active frames to your advantage, but not great if you\u2019re going for damage.",
    "8jd": "Can cross up. This air normal has a big hitbox on both sides of Chun-Li. It\u2019s best used after a knockdown in the corner, where you can pressure every wakeup roll option simultaneously with it.",
    "ab": "Doing a forward dash after this throw sets up an autotimed meaty 6D, giving this throw terrifying okizeme.",
    "hold28bdbd": "Chun-Li's highest damage special that can combo from lights, but hampered by its charge input. B and BD versions combo from any light while D can only combo from 5B.",
    "j236bdbd": "Chun-Li performs her Lightning Kicks while slowly descending to the ground. BD version has Chun-Li recover much more quickly upon landing, which reliably combos into either version of Kikosho (236226A/C) if it hits an airborne opponent.",
    "j2b": "Chun-Li stomps on the opponent while in the air. Can be cancelled into any other air move (including normals) on any of the 3 hits. The 3rd stomp hitting a grounded opponent spikes them into the floor for a soft knockdown.",
    "ja": "Cancels to j.236K and REV Blow. On Counter Hit, both cancels will combo properly. Solid air-to-air normal. It\u2019s quite fast and gives you cancel options to up your reward in air battles. Cancelling to REV Lightning Kicks on Counter Hit gives you a juggle to Kikosho on the ground.",
    "jab": "Yet another strong air-to-air option, especially since this one can\u2019t be Just Defended. In the corner, this sets up a meaty 3A overhead, which gets much stronger links.",
    "jb": "Both long-ranged and can cross up. The preferred cross-up jump in. It\u2019s got some decent range, too, making it the swiss army knife of Chun\u2019s air attacks.",
    "jc": "Chun-Li does a straight punch in the air. Unlike almost any other air normal in the game, this does not cause a regular flip out or a knockdown. Instead, the opponent is knocked horizontally backwards and falls much more quickly than with standard air normals. The opponent is also left in a juggle state until they reach the ground, after which they will return to a grounded standing state (despite appearing to be in an air reset animation). A weird air normal that you can combo out of even on normal hit, despite appearances. This is to ensure the target combo follow-up works properly, but you can also pick up juggles on the ground from it, such as her supers.",
    "jcjc": "Works from her air stomps, but causes an air reset state and cannot be comboed after.",
    "jd": "Chun-Li\u2019s preferred jump-in normal. When done low enough to the ground, you can easily confirm 4HP into your strongest combos."
  },
  "cristiano_ronaldo": {
    "214acac": "Forward advancing stance with different followups. REV version can dribble REV Ball on the floor (e.g 214BD > 214AC), useful in combos.",
    "214bd": "B and D version have same frame data but change how high the Ball goes. Setplay tool. Less useful in the corner because you can't do crossups, but still a great meaty option (up to +16 as it can beat out some meterless reversals and reset the situation. 214BD is useful in the corner as it bounces on the opponent twice and can intercept meterless reversals, especially when using jumps midscreen. 214BD creates a REV Ball with different distance The button can be held in the D and REV version to make the Ball go higher and/or further.",
    "214pa214ppa": "Manually stops stance. Can be used as a throw pressure tool to catch people asleep on the wheel.",
    "214pb214ppb": "Does a running slide, -9 at worst with ways to space it to be safer. REV Version launches the opponent upward for followup REV Arts and is his strongest damaging launcher among all his REV Arts in a vacuum. Can be useful as a canned mixup with 214AC~D on block. Still unsafe so you'll need to REV cancel on block.",
    "214pc214ppc": "Forward advancing shoulder tackle. Safe on block but you lose your turn. Being +1 on hit at close range allows you to go for a strike/throw mixup. On Counter Hit, it is +6, which can convert into Close C from anywhere. REV Version launches opponent on hit, and is his most versatile launcher among his REV Arts, comboing into 236BD or 623AC easily. It is also +1 on block which grants you the same mixup, and Counter Hit grants a launching combo.",
    "214pd214ppd": "18f overhead that knocks down on hit. Can catch people looking for Slide Tackle off guard. Unsafe on block, only the REV version is cancellable to REV Blow to be made safe. REV Version ground bounces on air hit. Mostly a knowledge check and can get you killed if the opponent knows how to fuzzy guard both high and low follow ups",
    "236236a": "General close-range super that doesn't advance very far. Useful as a reversal on a hard read and the best 1 bar ender for damage.",
    "236236b": "Fullscreen projectile super. Beats out almost any other non-super projectile, making this a strong answer to win fireball wars. Does less damage than Stellar Strike Shot, but grants better frame advantage; preferred ender when looking for a ball setup in the corner.",
    "236236c": "Slightly faster than Level 1, now being as fast as Light DP. Still a good reversal and general 2 bar meter dump when you're not in SPG.",
    "236236d": "Stronger version of Level 1 that does a bit more damage. Still less damage than his other supers.",
    "236236rev": "Cinematic super of Ronaldo doing his legendary free kick. Note that Ronaldo and the opponent side swap on hit and are brought back to the center of the stage. SIUUUUUUU!",
    "236bddb": "Low-ground projectile. 236D advances Ronaldo a bit and shoots further. REV version will beat out non-REV projectiles and can bounce back to Ronaldo on contact with opponent for combos or continued pressure. Is also a hard knockdown on any connect , so you can just randomly win neutral by landing this once. The REV version is unaffected by juggle limits for some reason, so it can be looped into itself when hit at a sweetspot that lets it hit twice on a juggled opponent. This leads to disgusting damage if you have the execution.",
    "2a": "Standard crouch jab mash button, -1 on block but can cancel into itself and other A buttons for potential frame advantage. Barely moves the Ball forward.",
    "2b": "Cancellable into itself. Faster than Far B, and Far A after two of these can be +4~5. Can also cancel into Far B in a light chain to confirm into 623A. Barely knocks away Ball.",
    "2c": "Somewhat stubby range, but it can confirm into REV Arts for full combos. Key normal for feint combos from Close B/D, as well as a frametrap from Close D > Feint. Shoots the Ball forward at a low angle.",
    "2crev": "Moves him forward. 12f somewhat short-ranged anti-air that's useful to counter specific moves or short hops. Shoots Ball at a curved angle slowly.",
    "2d": "Advancing slide kick. Doesn't shift hurtbox Horrible on block, but it's good to catch people who keep walking forward/back without crouchblocking. Shoots the Ball near ground level.",
    "4ab": "Puts a lot of space between you and opponent. Shooting a Ball may seem good, but strong players will take advantage of a free chance to Just Defend it for resources.",
    "4bd": "Ronaldo starts shuffling backwards at a slightly slower speed than his backwards walking speed and allows him to do 2 new followups.",
    "4bdhp": "value in [] are for crouch blocking opponent. 16f header. Has ??f airborne frames, but has no Wild Punish bonus. Shoots the Ball forward.",
    "4bdlp": "8f forward tackle. -7 on block. Not worth using raw. Sends the Ball flying forwards towards the enemy.",
    "5crev": "Beats Lows and Throws. 24f standing overhead, and thanks to Ronaldo's damage output it's potentially insanely rewarding. Can catch people asleep at the wheel crouchblocking Ball mix, and it's easily spaced or timed to be up to +2 on block, which is more than any other Low Dodge for absolutely no reason.",
    "623acac": "Values in [] note break cancels. Light version is invincible to everything, Heavy and REV are invincible to air attacks. Light version mainly used as meterless reversal, while Heavy and REV are better as anti-airs or combo extenders/enders. Despite their invincibility, all versions lose to High Dodge Attack, as Ronaldo is considered airborne for all of their active frames. The REV version, when braked, has extra recovery on whiff",
    "ab": "Grants you Ball oki in the corner to do basically whatever you want.",
    "ball": "Integral part of Ronaldo's kit which has a variety of uses. For the regular Ball, if it is juggled once it can bounce off the opponent one additional time before disappearing. This cannot be done multiple times to extend the Ball's lifespan. REV Ball bounces back on contact with opponent which can be hit back using normals. Can only can be bounced off the opponent twice total with the lifespan not being extendable. 214BD can setup a REV Ball as well but has special properties which causes KD on hit.",
    "cd": "Forward advancing REV Blow. Can be used at the end of REV Art combos for immense corner carry and hard knockdown.",
    "cla": "General +1 button that can chain into itself or Crouch A.",
    "clb": "+3 light on its own that can chain into itself and other lights.",
    "clc": "Safe at -3 with feint. Linked into from a lot of normals on Counter Hit, but also a faster frametrap option than cl.D. Links into itself on Counter Hit.",
    "cld": "+4 with feint. Ronaldo's best meaty with feint, but is prone to being REV Guarded out after. Crouching C followup is a 4f frametrap which can be cancelled for more pressure. On Counter Hit, this move can link into Close C (which can feint link into Crouching C).",
    "fa": "Very unique move. Ronaldo does a chest bump that moves him forward. Has lots of active frames, so it can be up to +6 on block or link into other buttons on hit (including cl.C). Has head hitbox, so it can preemptively anti-air some light buttons. Despite animation, it is not airborne. Juggles the Ball up and slightly forwards.",
    "fb": "General close range poke that's fully safe on block. Useful at the end of light chains for confirming into 623A for soft knockdown. Also useful as a frame trap in light chains on block. Juggles the Ball slightly up into the air, one of the most frequently used normals for this purpose.",
    "fc": "Belligerent advancing, airborne mid. Use this. Similar to Far A, but it actually is airborne for much of its animation, meaning it will beat lows, throws, and ground projectiles, while being very difficult to interrupt, since Ronaldo will just flip out. Easily spaced to be plus on block, or plus enough on hit to link into c.C, or even 2C. Will always link to c.C on Counterhit or Wild Punish for amazing damage. Great whiff punish normal, and yet it only has 13 frames of recovery on top of being prone to win trades, so it's actually kind of difficult to reliably whiff punish. Shoots the Ball at a slight downward angle.",
    "fd": "Longest range poke, but also prone to whiff punishes. Also unsafe up close at -5 as most characters can punish with Close/Far A. Like Far C, has ??f of airborne frames that can Wild Punish. Like Far C, this can also link into cl.C on Counter Hit for a full combo. Shoots the Ball at a slightly downward angle.",
    "ja": "General air jab. Useful for contesting slower buttons in the air. Juggles the Ball a little bit forward. Likely a foul, however, since he's using his elbow.",
    "jac": "Second hit doesn't connect with opponent on hit, strictly shifts this as a tool to shoot the Ball from long range. Best used from 214[D].",
    "jad": "Like Command Combo 1, second hit doesn't connect with opponent. Shoots Ball at a relatively lower angle.",
    "jb": "Has a hitbox on his back knee that can crossup, making this normal good for Ball setplay. Juggles the Ball backwards.",
    "jc": "Most damaging jump-in, but a bit shorter range than Jumping D. It's relatively longer recovery only allows it to combo into Close B for combos. Mostly useful from hop or hard reads. Shoots the Ball diagonally downward towards the enemy.",
    "jcd": "General aerial REV Blow. Can be used after 623AC to add some extra damage, but not as relatively useful for corner carry.",
    "jd": "Farthest reaching but also slowest air normal. Useful from longer range jumps and can combo into Close D for combos for slightly more damage. Shoots the Ball diagonally downward towards the enemy."
  },
  "duck_king": {
    "214ac46a": "Duck does a quick spin, hitting three times. It's a true blockstring if cancelled immediately from Beat Rush Breakin'. Can be delayed to either frametrap or blow up attempts at Hyper Defense.",
    "214ac46a46a": "Duck does a parody of Kenshiro's super and punches with both fists. Regular versions requires Hype State.",
    "214ac46c": "Duck hops and does a vertical elbow drop.",
    "214ac46d": "Duck does two thrusting kicks low to the ground, which hits low.",
    "214ac46d46d": "Duck crawls down low towards the opponent, dealing minimal damage. Regular versions requires Hype State.",
    "214ac46d46d46d": "Duck transitions from his crawl into a handstand, kicking the opponent.",
    "214acac": "Duck's new special move where he dashes forwards with the opponent. This can be followed up with various moves. OVERHEAT : The move's travel distance is drastically reduced. Duck also can no longer perform any follow ups.",
    "214bdbd": "Duck leaps forwards with an extended knee. OVERHEAT : Duck stumbles slightly if this move misses, leaving him more open than usual. D version is enhanced during Hype State, allowing extra juggles and having a much shorter startup. It is important to be mindful of Duck's REV during a combo, as the D version loses its enhanced properties when Overheat, which could cause unexpected combo drops.",
    "214c46b": "Duck tumbles forward into a handstand, grabs the opponent with his legs and throws them towards his back. Regular versions are improved during Hype State, dealing more damage and allowing it to combo from Beat Rush Breakin'.",
    "214c46c46c": "Duck turns around and body checks the opponent with his back. Regular versions requires Hype State.",
    "236236ac": "Duck grabs his opponent, kicks them into the air and performs his signature flurry of kicks while doing a windmill. Command grab super with long reach, reaching slightly further than his far B. Can be jumped after the super flash since it isn't frame one, however it is an amazing punish tool due to its speed and range. Has a hidden input done with 4123692A that does 20 extra damage and is a true frame 1 grab. The 2 frame startup combined with its range allows Duck to punish many moves reaction, such as DP brakes. The Redline version is a frame 1 command grab that cannot be jumped post super flash. Same hidden input can be used for extra damage.",
    "236236bd": "In order to continue the super, press any of the 4 attack buttons shortly each hit in the sequence. Failing to do so will cause the super to be cut short with a weak hit. Can hit from slightly under round start distance. Conincidentally, the rhythm for each button press can be synchronised to Duck's COTW theme, at 144BPM.",
    "236acac": "Duck curls up into a ball in the air and flies downwards diagonally towards the opponent. OVERHEAT : Like the ground version, Duck stumbles and falls if this move misses, which leaves him catastrophically vulnerable. C version requires Hype State to perform the Ground Finisher follow up.",
    "2cc": "Duck headbangs low to the ground. Shifts Duck forwards slightly, slightly extending 2C's range. Continuing to mash C extends this move indefinitely. Can be done on whiff. Counts as a valid Wild Punish heavy.",
    "2d2d": "Duck follows through his spinning sweep into a windmill, kicking upwards. An odd attack, only usable if 2D hits. Only combos from a Wild Punish 2D, dealing more damage than simply following up with another 2D. Doing so allows the opponent to roll in exchange for slightly more damage. If done on an opponent without a Wild Punish, Duck will be left at -18 after their short roll.",
    "4123692ac": "The Ignition Gear version is even faster at 1 frame, making it unjumpable after the super flash.",
    "44d": "Duck kicks backwards without turning. Cancellable from Long Goodbye from frame 17 onwards. Has a very short hitbox and does not count as a Wild Punish heavy. These combined attributes makes this move extremely poor as a whiff punish tool and is hardly worth using over simply walking back and using far C.",
    "623bdbd": "Duck does a quick headspin before kicking upwards in a spiral. OVERHEAT : The heavy version has additional landing frames if not braked. The light version also loses its invulnerability, like every other meterless invincible reversal. C version requires Hype State to perform the Air Finisher follow up.",
    "66b": "Duck does a knee slide that doesn't hit. This move can go through the opponent if close enough. Doesn't switch sides on a cornered opponent.",
    "66d6d": "Requires Hype State",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. cl.AC first hit is cancellable by any follow-ups. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender, during cl.AC only the second hit is REV Blow cancellable. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : First hit of the auto combo, cancellable by any follow-ups. cl.AC > C : A second hit cancellable by any ender, a nice way to increase the damage or your opponent REV meter. Unsafe on block and can be punished if the auto-combo was started too close to the opponent. D ender : Far D follow up, different frame data and damage, very unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Safest ender, Same move as his Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV) but +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Same move as his 2D, meaning it's very unsafe on block.",
    "fc46c": "Duck throws out both arms in a peace sign pose of sorts. Cancellable on any of the 4 active frames far C hitting which is a fairly tight confirm window. Combos into REV Blow on hit. Also juggles into 623B in the corner."
  },
  "gato": {
    "214acorholdacafterpiercingfangorholdacafterforwardthrow": "Gato turns his back to the opponent, becoming unable to block but gaining access to a new set of moves Stance with 5 follow-ups: A stance cancel (A) A counter that catches highs, mids and lows, and nullifies projectiles (B) A series of 3 strikes (C) A command grab (D) A dash cancel (66) At 12f is the earliest you can go into these follow-ups. Stance cancel (A) and dash cancel (66) exit the Lull stance; after all the other follow-ups, you go back to the Lull stance. The dash cancel is distinct from a regular forward dash as it is completely inactionable throughout its duration Gato can move around while his back is turned to reposition himself, but only laterally",
    "214bd": "Gato's infamous demon flip, an essential part of what makes him so overwhelming. B version is a great cancel option from an anti-air high dodge, allowing you to react to a counter hit with Whirling Fang (214K > B) or remain safe/plus with Vortex Fang (214K > A) in case of a flipout. D version can be braked at -2. Very useful in frametraps.",
    "236236a": "Provides a poor knockdown as it leaves Gato very far from the opponent, even in the corner Generally outclassed by Dragon Fang(236236B) in most situations, however Freeze Fang is much easier to combo into, giving it a niche as a round closing option following a 2B or a 6A",
    "236236b": "Has a sweet spot close to Gato, signaled by the slightly more dramatic hitstop on the first hit and a zoom-in on the last hit Failing to hit the sweet spot causes the last hit to whiff, resulting in less knockdown advantage and significantly worse damage Gato's preferred way to spend one bar as it gives him a better knockdown than 236236A while also dealing more damage",
    "236236c": "Gato stuns the opponent with a palm strike before he leaps onto them, twists their neck with his thighs and transitions into a vengeful spinning frankensteiner. Identical range to the A version Provides an even worse knockdown than the A version Looks very cool Can be braked on the first hit",
    "236236d": "Drills the opponent into the heavens with a flying vertical kick, charged with the ferocity of a coiled dragon. One frame faster than the B version, allowing it to be used in even lower juggles",
    "236236rev": "Gato lunges out with the back of his hand. If it hits, he will discombobulate the opponent with a series of blows before he turns them around and blasts their chest with a two-handed palm thrust, bursting their heart and lung meridian with a deadly dose of chi. Does 75 damage in chip unless JD'd or REV guarded Generally what Gato wants to spend 2 bars on if the opportunity presents itself and he wants to cash out, as he has no double level 1 combo routes",
    "236ac": "Less safe and less good than it usually is in other games. A version frametraps from lights, C version from lights and heavies (except for cl.5C). A version is still an excellent tool as it is safe from buttons at max spacing (easy to get in pressure with lights) and easily converts from lights in neutral. A and REV Art version both travel the same distance. C version covers farther distance, but due to its lack of safety, sees use mostly as a combo tool.",
    "2a": "Gato's non-proximity 5f jab for mashing out of sticky situations Can reliably chain into f.B for a super confirm or less reliably into f.A for a small combo with 236A ~ 6P",
    "2b": "Main low for mix-ups and catching retreating opponents Chains into f.B even at tip range",
    "2c": "A shorter version of f.C that is 1f faster Due to the priority system, 2C will beat an opponent's A and B normals outright rather than trade should they hit on the same frame As such, [cl.C > Feint, 2C] will frametrap 5f light buttons, but wont cause a counter hit",
    "2crev": "Beats Highs and Rev Blows Special cancellable on hit only Exceptionally good High Dodge Attack with a solid hitbox and lengthy upper body invuln",
    "2d": "Gato's furthest reaching normal, despite the somewhat deceptive animation, and a fairly long sweep in general",
    "2drev": "Mimics the startup of f.5D Also resembles the startup of cl.5D, and the startup of Wind Fang (Anything that raises his leg)",
    "4ab": "Can't transition into Lull like forward throw Much weaker knockdown in general despite having more frames to work with, especially mid-screen",
    "5crev": "Beats Lows and Throws Hits Mid unlike most other Low Dodge Attacks Becomes overhead when done as the 6D ender in Gato's Combination Attack while retaining its fast 17f startup",
    "5drev": "Mimics the startup of his cl.5C cl.5A > 5D+REV mimics the startup of the Combination Attack cl.5A > C Can be used interchangeably with 6D+REV when feint cancelling in combos Obviously not very useful as a way to bait a response as it doesn't really resemble anything that an opponent might be looking out for.",
    "623bd": "Gato's anti air special and combo tool B version has invul frames D version and REV are now the same speed as of the season 2 opening patch D and Rev version can be braked on the first hit. They recover 4 frames slower when whiffed compared to on hit or block. Optimal way to go into 1EX combos. Has less forward movement than other DP brakes, making it worse for conversions but better for anti airs. This move can be linked into off counterhit D Wind Fang brake, and also combos from a very close range 2/clA. Get to know the ranges where you can combo into this vs having to spend EX off heavies.",
    "6a": "Hits overhead Links into 2A at really close range Can hit meaty to be upwards of +7 on hit, however, Gato will always be pushed out of the activation range of cl.C, so the reward remains more or less the same, only can convert into 623D/BD brake for more damage Startup animation is practically indistinguishable from Quaking Fang (236P) and Forward Feint (6D+REV) 50/50 setup after B super in the corner, full jump into either forward feint (6D+REV) into 2B or 6A",
    "6acafterquakingfang": "Can be delayed after Quaking Fang to frametrap REV Art version can also be delayed; this does not frametrap, but does delay HD timing Can be canceled into Lull by holding A or C. Holding AC cancels into EX Lull; but REV Art version must use REV Accel to cancel into EX Lull. Instead of holding buttons, you can also cancel into Lull by timing your button press to be during Piercing Fang's recovery. REV Art version does not knockdown Always cancel this into Lull on hit, then dash cancel Lull to maintain oki.",
    "6b": "Gato does a long reaching leaping kick capable of avoiding low pokes and grounded projectiles Has a very high hitbox, letting you hit people out of jumps and hops quite consistently With proper spacing it can be made plus on block Can link into 2A on normal hit and cl.B(or even cl.C) on counter hit/Wild Punish depending on spacing Gato's \"God button\". Absolutely paramount when playing in the midrange, as pretty much no character has an easy answer to it.",
    "6drev": "Mimics the startup of Quaking Fang Also resembles the startup of 6A, and to a lesser extent the startup of f.5C (Anything that thrusts his arm forward) Can be used interchangeably with 5D+REV when feint cancelling in combos Great feint for duping the opponent as it recovers fast and resembles a bunch of Gato's key moves, most crucially, his standing overhead 6A, which lets him exploit his opponents good hand-to-eye coordination by going low once Forward Feint finishes.",
    "aafterlull": "Exits Lull stance.",
    "aafterwindfang": "This is a weird poke, mostly useful in mid range to harass people into jumping in on you, which you excel at calling out. It can also be used on a read to punish fireballs, but you won't get much reward from that. Psuedo projectile that hits the ground diagonally across from Gato. Hits the area right in front of Gato when done from 214B and 214BD Hits around two character's lengths away from Gato when done from 214D The 214BD > A version can be cancelled into air REV Blow, creating a gapless blockstring that takes off 60% of the opponents guard gauge when they're overheated",
    "ab": "Hold A/C to enter Lull stance or AC to enter Rev Art Lull stance Instead of holding buttons, you can also cancel into Lull by timing your button press to be during Forward Throw's recovery. Entering Lull stance and immediately cancelling it into a forward dash leaves Gato point blank and +8",
    "bafterlull": "Counter that uniquely catches all strikes regardless of how they're normally guarded As of the Season 2 beginning patch, normal version nullifies projectiles, REV version reflects them Extremely high reward when catching REV Blows as it causes an unscaled counter hit launch Lengthy recovery where Gato can be thrown or Wild Punished should he fail to catch something Can quick cancel into Black Fang again or into White Fang anytime during this lengthy recovery Mostly sees use to make people scared to punish if they didn't mash on Quaking Fang's followup as, if it's spaced properly, you can cancel into Lull and then use this to counter their punish.",
    "bafterwindfang": "Normal version is a combo tool for certain juggles, specifically when the juggle limit of 214P ~ C has been reached The 214BD ~ B version grants a very high juggle that lets Gato follow-up with basically anything he wants, often completely refunding the REV spent on 214BD if no other EX move is used The 214BD ~ B version can not hit grounded opponents, though this rarely matters as 214BD launches them up for a juggle anyway",
    "cafterlull": "Multihit multitool (3 hits). Your best DP punish, and a very high damage DP punish starter in general. Can quick cancel and exit Lull stance immediately after the 3rd hit by pressing A. (Can press as early as the startup of the 2nd hit and as late as during the 3rd hit.) Can quick cancel into Black Fang or into White Fang anytime during recovery Both EX and normal version are used in all of his best juggles for optimal damage and meter efficiency. Frametraps off cl.5C and leaves you very safe. -2 on cancel, 0 without cancel. Comboing into this from cl.5C or 2C will net you a link into 5A to supers or Quaking Fang unless you are very far.",
    "cafterwindfang": "A divekick that's quite easy to make safe with large pushback. Mostly good for landing quickly after using wind fang or for frametrapping off a blocked wind fang. Wind Fang's angle makes this not really great for getting in. Can substitute Whirling Fang (214K ~ B) when in the corner to squeeze a little more damage out of a combo The EX version is +3 when done immediately, letting Gato threaten with another pressure extender after 214BD, which itself is +1 when braked. Do note that, if the opponent REV guards 214BD, immediate Arrow Fang will often whiff and leave Gato Wild Punishable.",
    "cd": "Hyper armor starts from frame 2 and lasts until recovery Hyper armor will absorb any strike except supers and High Dodge Attacks Moves forward and bulldozes through an opponent attempting to fight back, giving Gato corner carry and a nice knockdown on hit, while being very safe on block with huge pushback. Best used in unpredictable moderation.",
    "cla": "Obligatory 4f proximity normal Always in range for a cancel into 623K Chains into other lights or Combination Attack Not recommended as a defensive mash option as the activation range is very short, often resulting in getting f.A instead, which is 2 frames slower",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : Starter of the combination attack, deals some damage. cl.AC > C : Follow-up that adds damage and gives time to choose the ender. D ender : Very unsafe on block but causes a soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Overhead ender, same move as his Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV) but it's +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Low ender, same as his 2D, it's very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "Fast and plus, very good for pressure Goes into his Command Combo 1 Can occasionally link into cl.C on normal hit, most consistently after a cross-up j.B Can link into 2C on reaction to a counter hit",
    "clbdccd": "The final hit can only be done with S.P.G active. Second hit is -2 on block and can easily be hit-confirmed into the rest of the target combo Surprisingly solid and economical combo from a light starter, especially when ending it with CD",
    "clc": "Relatively short activation range for a cl.C, though it makes up for it in sheer speed Incredible normal for applying pressure",
    "cld": "Somewhat redundant button that functions as a low reward option for calling out a defensive jump/hop as it hits high above Gato's head - a relic from a more degenerate past Links into 2A or light DP on normal hit and microdash cl.C on counter hit As of the start of season 2, can convert into 2A, 214B>B",
    "dafterlull": "Command grab Gato can set up tick throws by cancelling his lights into White Fang EX version combos into rev accel Wind Fang into whatever you want.",
    "dafterwindfang": "A lot weaker than it usually is. Not a mixup as he doesn't have an overhead from demon flip anymore. Quite easy to make it hit very meaty after a hard knockdown, letting Gato be +3 and point blank to threaten with cl.B, which will combo if the opponent doesn't block Sliding Fang Most use out of this is to go over fireballs and catch someone walking backward - which is a very hard use case to justify as it needs to be timed far better than a jump-in to get an actual conversion off the normal version. The EX version sees use during specific combos and can work a little better as a way to get in due to Rev Accel, but don't rely on it.",
    "fa": "Longest special cancellable light for confirming into small combos Prone to whiffing when used in extended light chains, particularly from a 2B",
    "fb": "Fantastic range for pestering and fishing for stray hits Always in range to cancel into 236236A/C and Hidden Gear, possibly even one-hit confirmable if you're a genetic freak",
    "fc": "Moves Gato forward slightly This move has a decent amount of recovery on whiff so use with caution One-hit confirmable into 236P",
    "fd": "Moves Gato forward slightly Very reliable whiff punish tool due to it's range and speed, with amazing reward on Wild Punish",
    "ja": "Quick air-to-air jab",
    "jb": "Go to cross-up normal outside of Aerial Turn",
    "jbja": "Extended jump-in string for punishing fireballs Can not be performed from a hop Can not be used in double overhead/fuzzy guardbreak set-ups as the j.A hits mid",
    "jc": "Long and advantageous air button that also incurs a non-trivial amount of REV gauge build-up on block, making it suitable for hop pressure +5 on block when done in the apex of a hop on a standing opponent",
    "jcd": "Completely alters Gato's jump arc, briefly lifting him up in the air before he plummets down heel first Has quite a bit of forward momentum, potentially flying over the opponent and landing on the other side of them when performed up close - whiffing completely Very slow aerial REV Blow, though somewhat mitigated by just how dramatically it shifts Gato's position, letting him bait anti-airs even when he seems too low to the ground to do so.",
    "jd": "Can be used with Aerial Turn as a crossup alternative",
    "jrev": "Gato faces the other side while airborne, mainly used for cross-ups Jumping over with this properly lets you bait most reversals while maintaining offense by landing with j.D"
  },
  "hokutomaru": {
    "214ac": "An important projectile move for Hokutomaru to control space and use as an approach tool. C version throws out 3 Shuriken clustered together Opponent Normals can neutralize Shuriken if timed correctly. The Shuriken can be hit out of the air by attacks. As such, the C Version can blow through single hit fireballs as only one of the Shuriken gets neutralized. REV Art fireballs eliminate all Shuriken regardless of hits.",
    "214bd": "Diagonal version of 214P that throws the Shuriken(s) at an upward 45\u00b0 angle. Opponent Normals can neutralize Shuriken if timed correctly.",
    "22ac": "Teleports Hokutomaru either directly above where he stood (22A) or above and in front of the opponent while extending a leg to hit them (22C/AC) A version can be cancelled into Air Shuriken (j.214P) or Air Naname Shuriken (j.214K) Startup for the C and AC version is much slower in actuality as the move puts Hokutomaru on the top part of the screen before he descends with a heel kick. Can always get a combo if your opponent is hit crouching or if they get counter hit. Useful tool for punishing players who are zoning him out or as a surprise neutral check. Be mindful that a wary opponent can anti-air his teleport easily with High Dodge or a DP.",
    "236236a": "\"Fireball\" super where Hokutomaru charges his ki and sends it out in front of him. This does function as a projectile so projectile invincible moves can go through it. It is also able to cancel out weaker projectiles and other Ignition gear projectiles like Kain 236236B. Has invincible startup and is your preferred reversal option when only one bar is available. Can lose to a well timed High Dodge.",
    "236236b": "Hurricane anti-air super. This version only has frame 1 head invincibility so it should not be used as a reversal outside of anti-air situations. This super will hit on both sides so it is a good option to beat cross ups. JDing this super in the air will quickly pull you into it and often over Hokutomaru's head, potentially crossing yourself up in the process.",
    "236236c": "More damaging version of his 236236A with similar uses and properties. It can notably cancel out weaker projectiles and other Redline gear projectiles like Kain 236236D. It has the fastest startup of his non S.P.G supers so it is the easiest to link into after a tight air juggle. Can lose to a well timed High Dodge",
    "236236d": "His most damaging super outside of S.P.G state. Aim to end in this if you can for the most damaging combo ender. Unlike the B version this has full invincibility and can be used as a reversal option as well as an anti-air.",
    "236ac": "More time under Andy's tutelage has taught him Andy's famous rushing dash punch. It is a decent move for surprising your opponent with a quick attack from further ranges as a gap-closer. The hitbox will not come out unless Hokutomaru gets near his opponent. This means that from further ranges the animation will be shortened as the end recovery from performing the punch will not happen. The A version is notably his fastest special on the ground which makes it his fastest meterless guard cancel option. The C version is always -6 from any range. It should be used very carefully as he will be consistently punishable on block. The AC version has the same coverage as the C version but can be cancelled into other Rev Arts to be plus or get a full combo.",
    "236bd": "Hokutomaru executes his version of Andy's famous Kuuha Dan. This move is a staple combo piece but is very unsafe on block/whiff if not hit confirmed properly. Unlike most other brake moves, the REV Art version does not effect the juggle limit of the D version when used in the same combo. Most hits will whiff on a crouch blocking opponent. 236BD version: There is an interruptible <3f gap before the last, cancellable hit where the opponent can mash DP, super or REV Blow. Marco, B. Jenet, Hokutomaru and Mai's CDs are fast enough to punish. If Hokutomaru is in S.P.G. cancelling into CD will neutralize the opponents CD, after which he can do his CD > j.CD target combo to get a crumbling/launching counter hit punish.",
    "2a": "Mash option that comes out in 4 frames regardless of proximity, has strong chain cancel options in 2B and f.B, ensuring a knockdown.",
    "2b": "Threatening low with great range and speed with strong cancel options into c.B or f.B for good reward.",
    "2c": "Good far reaching button for whiff punishing with or checking grounded pokes. Can confirm easily into any of his heavy specials.",
    "2crev": "Beats Highs, most Mids, and REV Blows after the evasion is active. Very good startup and hitbox for anti-airing, and is Hokutomaru's main tool for that purpose. Special/super cancelable on hit only, so always inputting a cancel has no real downside.",
    "2d": "Universal sweep attack. Unsafe on block but decent enough for checking walk back attempts or counter poking in neutral.",
    "3b": "Slide kick that is punishable at point blank but becomes more plus when spaced out properly. Good option for catching people who are walking away from you or just as a way to approach on the ground. Unfortunately not plus enough on hit to get a consistent combo from any spacing or with counter hit/wild punishes. It is possible to combo off of this into 4f buttons if you time it perfectly as a point blank meaty.",
    "3d": "Cancellable into Air REV Arts and any version of j.22P Launches opponents upwards when it hits. Risky anti-air as it has no air move invuln but it hits higher than his High Dodge Attack and grants a combo regardless of counter hit/Wild Punish, essentially serving as a DP brake in lieu of his slow 236D.",
    "4ab": "Hokutomaru grabs his opponents and blows them up with his ninjitsu. Simply hold up forward to perform an autotimed 5f safejump when the opponent is thrown into the corner.",
    "526drev": "Mimics a shuriken toss. Use this to throw off your opponents who are expecting to respond to a shuriken toss or just for your cl.C/D feint cancel pressure.",
    "5crev": "Universal overhead attack that beats Lows and Throws. A good move to check your opponent's reactions and steal wins if they are low health. Can also link into his close normals after if it counter hits or if it is timed properly as a meaty.",
    "623bd": "B version is a command leap that can cancel into the REV Art versions of his air specials. A decent option for traversing over low attacks or some grounded fireballs. D version has a strike at the end. Can be combo'd off of on counter hit or wild punish. Rev Art version is the most useful as it is always plus on block and you can combo into his other Rev Arts on hit.",
    "ab": "Hokutomaru grabs his opponent and knocks them away with a flip into shuriken. Has more advantage than his back throw.",
    "cla": "Standard 4f proximity normal Chains into other lights or Combination Attack. Good for applying pressure, hit confirms, or as a quick anti-hop anti-air.",
    "clb": "Very advantageous normal for pressure and starting combos. Frame traps and auto confirms into cl.C if used to start a blockstring from point blank. Has enough hitstun to combo into 214AC, enabling strong 2-hit light confirms (ex. 2B cl.B 214AC).",
    "clc": "Largest activation range of his close normals. An important starter and good punish option for confirming into his heavy specials.",
    "cld": "Strong plus frames but very slow to start up. Ideal as a meaty option for it's high plus frames on feint cancel. Also your most rewarding button to punish DPs with.",
    "fa": "Standard far jab. Use it to check dash-ins or as a filler in block-strings/combos.",
    "fb": "Very long special cancellable light that moves Hokutomaru slightly forward, can combo into 236B for a knockdown even at tip range. Great for hitconfirming your crouching 2B lows.",
    "fc": "Hokutomaru takes a big step forward with an elbow check. Has some of the best reach out of his standing buttons and combos readily into any of his specials. Strong Wild Punish tool when feint cancelled as it leaves him very close and +22",
    "fd": "Kick that is aimed slightly at the air. Can occasionally catch people who are jumping but generally not a great normal to use as it is only super cancellable and has noticeable recovery.",
    "j214ac": "Throws the Shuriken straight ahead while keeping Hokutomaru suspended in the air. Regular versions have a height restriction and can only be done from a forward or neutral jump. Opponent Normals can neutralize Shuriken if timed correctly. REV Art versions have no height restriction and can be done while jumping backwards or even a backdash.",
    "j214bd": "Diagonal version of j.214P that throws the Shuriken(s) at a downward 45\u00b0 angle. Regular versions have a height restriction and can only be done from a forward or neutral jump. Opponent Normals can neutralize Shuriken if timed correctly. REV Art versions have no height restriction and can be done while jumping backwards or even a backdash.",
    "j22ac": "Aerial version of his teleport. The A/C versions can only be done from neutral or forward jumps. The Rev Art version can be done from any jump and backdashes as well. Can be done very low to the ground by inputting as [282P] rapidly Has less advantage compared to his grounded version.",
    "j789": "Hokutomaru's double jump. His most annoying option for mixing people's anti-air reactions or to traverse the screen. Hokutomaru will turn to reface the opponent if they got behind him while he's double jumping. He is able to perform any regular aerial option available to him like from his normal jump including: normals, specials, JD, feint, etc.",
    "ja": "Special cancel allows for a small combo on counter hit air-to-air",
    "jab": "Air throw that always has the same knockdown advantage regardless of how high you throw them from. Useful for punishing opponents who are expecting to JD your other anti-air options.",
    "jb": "Hits cross-up, follow up with c.B > c.C to take advantage of the vacuum effect",
    "jc": "Generic jumping heavy punch. You would usually prefer to use j.D instead as a jump in.",
    "jd": "His farthest reaching heavy attack in the air. Good choice for high reward jump-ins.",
    "jdrev": "Mimics a shuriken toss - in the air Cannot be cancelled into In patch 3.00, removed the ability to cancel into normals after feinting.",
    "spgactive236236rev": "Hokutomaru pays tribute to his teachers and performs a dash punch similar to his 236C. If he connects he will do an explosive combo. Has the fastest startup and does the most damage out of all of his supers. He is fully invincible for the duration he is dashing which makes this a strong option for beating out mid-range fireballs or punishing far normals on block (ex. Billy f.C). Will ALWAYS side-swap if it doesn't kill. Be mindful you can potentially corner yourself after the super finishes. Does 72 damage in chip unless JD'd/REV guarded",
    "spgactivecd": "Uniquely fast Rev Blow that forces the opponent to respect Hokutomaru's defense similar to a reversal, as even doing a counter Rev Blow or High Dodge is restrictive and difficult. On hit, can be followed up by air Rev Blow as a target combo, giving Hokutomaru added reward on regular hit or Wild Punish Rev Blow. Functional as an anti-air thanks to its upwards hitbox and inability to be JD'd by a jumping opponent. Comes at the cost of being -5 on block, which is universally punishable if blocked point blank, which makes low-commitment pressure options effective against it.",
    "spgactivecdjcd": "A follow-up to get more damage out of Rev Blows. Can only be cancelled into if Rev Blow hits the opponent and isn't blocked. Cannot be performed if the Rev Blow is the finisher for Combination Attack (cl.A > C > C > D > CD). Do not use this if the initial Rev Blow counter hits as Hokutomaru can use a full combo instead.",
    "spgactivejcd": "Aerial rev blow with usual guard point properties. This is safe on block and can even be plus if you manage to hit the opponent below their waist. A great tool for blowing up anti-air or air-to-air attempts."
  },
  "hotaru_futaba": {
    "214acac": "Hotaru's light chain ender and a general pressure tool. 214A combos from lights but is always unsafe, 214C and 214A+C can be braked to become safe or +3 respectively at the cost of a slower startup. The meterless and metered versions actually have a different amount of hits, which makes a Guard Cancel DP feint punish difficult to pull off consistently. On top of that, the A+C version can be cancelled into other Rev Arts before the brake, which makes it so the only consistent way to punish Hotaru for abusing this move on block is a Guard Cancel Super, as slower/non-super reversals can get hit by a cancel to Rev Blow for enormous damage.",
    "214bdbd": "214B/D reflects most standard projectiles, while 214BD can reflect Rev Art projectiles. In the season 2 major patch, the D version is no longer plus on block. Reflecting Mai's Rev Kachousen will deflect the first fan, causing the second fan to be destroyed.",
    "236236bd": "Side swaps after completion unless done in the corner",
    "236236rev": "Side swaps after completion. Leaves you at +1 but spaced out. If used against a corned enemy, it leaves you cornered but in range for a meaty or command grab. Goes stupidly far in 6 frames with no travel time, so if you know your frame data you can use this to pick up punishes most characters can't hope for. This also means it's easy to combo into in juggles or grounded confirms, and it can work as an anti air. After the super, crouch A will beat 4f+ normals if they choose to attack (or jump), but conversions are limited. Instant Air Rev Blow covers many options they can do, losing mostly only to High Dodge Attack and supers.",
    "236acac": "The slower speed of 236A Hakki Shou allows Hotaru to run behind it and maintain offensive pressure.",
    "236bdbd": "Command Grab 236BD can be Rev Accel'd into other special moves for a combo. Hotaru's premier guard cancel, the B+D version actually beats 4 frame light chains and all versions beat DP feint cancels. Another one of Hotaru's special moves that is arbitrarily faster than most other moves of its type, only surpassed by Tizoc's heavy SPD (6248C)",
    "2a": "Hotaru's other fastest normal, tied with close A. In situations where you could use either, close A gives better conversions on hit and better follow-ups on block. The only advantage of crouch A is the range.",
    "2b": "Quick crouching low, a nice starter for her light combos.",
    "2bcor2drev": "Mimics the startup of Kobi Kyaku Higher recovery than the other two feints.",
    "2c": "Hotaru's longest special-cancellable normal. A keystone of her neutral and pressure, it combos into 623C* at all ranges, and frametraps into 214D and 214C, although 214D cannot frametrap 4-frame buttons. It can even trade-combo into dp+C or 214C against 4-frames! Cancelling into 214BD or Late-cancelling into 214B are also an option to beat 4fs. Despite being -1 on feint cancel, it's still worth considering due to both the above frametraps and having a 4f crouch A. Effectively negates Rev Guard pushback when feint cancelled due to it's forward movement (and leaves you in command grab range).",
    "2crev": "Beats Highs and Rev Blows",
    "2d": "Hotaru's sweep, hits low and cause a soft knockdown.",
    "5bcor5drev": "Mimics the startup of Hakki Shou Can provoke jumps from opponents expecting a fireball, and doubles as a combo and pressure option.",
    "5crev": "Beats Lows and Throws",
    "623acac": "A version is extremely fast and fully invincible. C and AC is air invincible and brake-able. Hotaru's DP. A version : Fully invincible and fast DP. Why was this ever 4 frames. C version : Air invincible, faster than many 623C's, good as an anti air. If braked, the move becomes safe and launches the opponent, allowing Hotaru to combo them. This first hit however has a very short horizontal hitbox which alters or limits Hotaru's combo routes depending on range. AC version : Air-invincible, fast, and travels slightly forward, allowing the first hit to reach farther than her 623C.",
    "6bcor6drev": "Mimics the startup of Tenshin Shou The same duration as her 5D+REV feint, which lets Hotaru bait a punishable response to either special they mimmick without weakening her pressure at all.",
    "ab": "Moves both players backwards (meaning it can take them out of the corner for crossups).",
    "cd": "Armored palm strike with great horizontal range.",
    "cla": "Close C (with a followup combo) is a big payoff natural link / frametrap but loses to 4f normals. Frametrap with Crouch B instead to beat jumps or normals (at worst, trades with 4f normals). Allows command grab to be used after without any delays or movement needed to connect it.",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : Starter of the combination attack, deals some damage. cl.AC > C : Follow-up that adds damage and gives time to choose the ender. D ender : Very unsafe on block but causes a soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Overhead ender, same move as her Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV) but it's +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Low ender, same as her 2D, it's very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "Smaller payoff on hit than Close A due to no natural links into heavies on hit, but much more advantage on block. Crouch C can work well after specifically to beat fuzzy jumps after, but loses to 4f normals and immediate jump. Command grab can also be done immediately after. Potentially safe to slower reversals when performed meaty. Also allows close C to link after on hit.",
    "clc": "Has a target combo (Close C -> forward+D, forward+D), but avoid doing the final hit since the second hit is cancellable anyway. Being a 6f startup heavy, it will beat 4f normals in +2 situations without trading.",
    "clc6d6d6d": "Gives a hitconfirm window and extra damage, should always be used after landing cl.C, on hit or on block.\nA noob-trap move made by the mash police. Leaves Hotaru wide open for the opponent's juiciest party starter on block, and ends the combo on hit.",
    "cld": "When feint cancelled this move is +4 on block and +6 on hit (allowing links to close C). This move should almost always be feint cancelled when used. The only exception being if it gets guard cancelled into a non-invulnerable special. In that case you'd consider cancelling into dp+C. +4 on block means crouch C or dp+C will beat 4f+ normals after, as well as make it hard to fuzzy jump potential command grabs. Getting proper combos into close C from this out of jump normals usually requires a micro-dash (or lower damage routes such as far A/B or crouch A -> dp+C, etc.) Allows immediate run into command grab after.",
    "fb": "Good for checking dashes with a buffered qcb+A behind it.",
    "fc": "Very difficult to whiff punish if your opponent isn't actively baiting it out. A good button to throw out in neutral to proactively stuff offensive approaches.",
    "fd": "Hotaru's longest normal. Can combo into cl.C on Wild Punish unless it only lands on extended hurtboxes. A highly committal, but strong poke tool and whiff-punish button.",
    "j214bdbd": "Hotaru's divekick. Useful to alter your jump arc and mess with anti-airs. The B+D version can be done out of a backjump, which lets Hotaru skip neutral straight after retreating, sometimes whiff punishing grabs or ground normals. Can also be used as an alternative air Guard Cancel to Rev Blow, to more consistently punish single-hit anti-airs.",
    "j2b": "Chun-Li stomp. Causes a bounce off the enemy and grants air actions again on hit or block. Allows the normally unusable E air turn after if performed during a hop. But performing during a hop also restricts jB from crossing up. Must use fulljump to get the crossup version.",
    "ja": "Standard air-to-air normal. Has a target combo into jB. In air-to-air situations, this requires the opponent to JD twice. Good air-to-ground as well, as it can throw off many guard cancels. Leaves the opponent standing next to you on hit; the higher you hit them in their jump arc, sooner you reach the ground before them. Hitting them right as they start or are about to land leaves you slightly minus",
    "jab": "Air Grab Hotaru throws the opponent to the opposite side.",
    "jajb": "Cannot be used in a short hop",
    "jb": "A long-range air normal that's fantastic for poking and space control. Can not crossup during hops, only full jumps.",
    "jc": "Standard air-to-air normal. Soft knockdown on hit, allowing dp+C or other followups depending on distance. Also enables combo routes from counterhit launches starting with dp+C or 214K.",
    "jcd": "Potentially the best air Rev Blow in the game. Despite having a height restriction, can still basically be performed instantly from the air. In this situation, it's +2 on crouch block, -2 on stand block. 15f startup makes it very hard to High Dodge Attack (2CE) on reaction Extremely fast startup considering it's armored on frame 2. Decent pushback on block that will leave you out of close normal proximity (where most 4fs are usuable) Excellent reward on counter hit means you can throw it out in many situations, or use it to enforce strike/throw mixups if you are concerned about invulnerable reversals. Excellent combo ender that builds super meter and grants safejumps from almost any route you can use it.",
    "jd": "Standard jump-in normal.",
    "jrev": "Hotaru faces the other side while airborne, mainly used for crossups. Allows a corner steal with j.D"
  },
  "joe_higashi": {
    "214acac": "Joe adopts a pose and gains guard points against mid and high attacks. AC version also covers low attacks. Has a bevy of followups that hit high/low as well as a command grab. The guard points do not work against supers.",
    "214acac6d": "A stepping low-sweep Furthest range of all follow-ups Great option that is safe on block and knocks down on hit, can be used as a mixup option in block strings but doesn't have much reward unless REV version is used",
    "214acacab": "A side-swapping command grab with decent range Able to immediately tick-throw after 1 light normal, or a slight delay after far C REV version allows follow-ups as usual with REV enhanced command grabs",
    "214acacc": "A far range overhead elbow swing Not much reward unless counterhitting or spending meter Due to having 4 active frames, it can be used in meaty setups after hard knockdowns.",
    "214acacd": "An ignorant kick that blows through other moves Only follow-up that retains guard points +-0 on normal hit and +5 on counter hit. In theory Joe can get a follow up, but oftentimes he can't link a fast normal due to the pushback.",
    "214bdbd": "Joe performs an aerial spin kick. Has a bad air hitbox and oftens loses to jump ins and air to airs. Will fly above low attacks, which can counter opponents trying to break through Joe's Fearless Man stance. BD version is a great way to give Joe advantage on block. Can also REV accel out of failed stance mixups to make them safe.",
    "236236ac": "Joe does an uppercut with his outside fist, launching his opponent. He then uses his main arm to launch a massive tornado.Extremely combo-friendly and easy to end combos with. This is Joe's reversal super and is fully invincible and faster than his other super. Joe will be at close D range and +17 on a ground hit and +19 on a juggle after 2 dashes after the A version of this move. The C version of this super has a very interesting property that is hard to see at first. The 2nd hit actually launches a massive fullscreen tornado that is a true projectile. Using this when the opponent is in overheat and full screen can be a very cheeky way to get a chip kill or chip damage.",
    "236236bd": "Joe rears his arms back and spins it, gathering gales into his fist. He then unleashes it forwards in a torrent that blasts the opponent away fullscreen.This is Joe's best combo ender but is much less combo friendly as a trade off. This super also has much worse okizeme than screw upper The opponent ends up nearly at fullscreen distance and Joe gets no oki off of this. However, this may be work to his advantage because he can transition to his strong zoning game. The D version of this move sets wallbounces like a AA counter REV blow and sets up a juggle. Joe can also perform a free follow-up with D. Simply holding down D will also cause this follow-up to occur. Charging the follow-up prevents the opponent from leaving the corner from the wallbounce.",
    "236236rev": "VERY poor range, you won't be punishing any slighty spaced fireballs or moves with this. Can sometimes whiff after 3 Light normals if Joe is a bit too spaced out.",
    "236acac": "Joe's signature grounded projectile. As with Preecha, the tornadoes linger a bit before dissipating A version travels half screen C version launches two tornadoes that travel about 70% of the screen This version can be braked to launch only one tornado and be safer against jumps AC version launches a stationary 3-hit tornado which can be followed up with 6P to launch another 3-hit tornado that travels nearly full screen. The follow up can be delayed significantly and can be performed as long as the initial tornado hasn't disappeared. If the follow up is performed quickly, the follow up scales the combo as if were a separate EX move and massively reduces damage potential, especially in shorter combos. This may be a bug. Caution: This special move has a massive flaw in which it is both negative on block AND HIT.Even on counter hit, Joe is only +2 with alot of pushback. Despite being a safe and effective block string ender, opponents who understand this flaw can make Joe think twice before using this move to end block strings. But for the most part,this move is Joe's safest block string ender and should be used as needed.",
    "236bdbd": "A leaping forward kick, kind of like Terry's Burning Knuckle. All versions can be braked before they hit. This can be useful for baiting reversals or approaching for throws. B version can technically be safe on block when spaced but very difficult in practice. Joe will more often than not be point blank and -5 or worse at most ranges this move is attempted from. Avoid using the D and REV version for the most part as they doesn't serve much purpose in Joe's kit and gameplan. The REV version is quite situational, it should only be used when Joe is in SPG to make it safe, and even then its not that worth going for.",
    "2bcor2drev": "Mimics Slash Kick. This is Joe's slowest feint.",
    "2crev": "Standard high dodge attack Slightly less range than far D, but can buffer specials",
    "3d": "The long range and active frames can be used to preemptively stuff an opponent's pokes or forward movement. Safe-on-block sliding distance is a hair shorter than round-start spacing. Sadly, does not low-profile moves.",
    "4ab": "Sends opponent further away than Joe's forward throw. Still have some meaty options like H. Hurricane Upper or Dash, H. Golden Tsumasaki",
    "56bcor56drev": "Mimics Hurricane Upper. This is Joe's fastest feint and the one used in his framedata.",
    "5crev": "Beats lows and throws. Nice option if opponent is trying to low-check Fearless Man, as this is +7 on counter hit Hits crouching opponents Nice complement to Step Low Kick",
    "623ac6c": "Joe performs two uppercuts and leaps into the air A REV-only followup to Bakuretsu Ken and is the highest damage followup. If going for a REV Blow, perform it only after Joe is airborne.",
    "623acac": "Joe unleashes a flurry of punches. Does low damage by itself but is safe on block and can be followed up on whiff or block during any of the 6 punches. While manually delaying the follow-up on the last hit leads to optimal damage, the opponent will begin to fall out of the move after the 5th hit, which can lead to the follow-up whiffing. AC version makes Joe step forwards before punching. He only performs 4 punches but it can be followed up on the last hit. No manual delay is necessary. AC version also cannot be followed up on whiff. Regular versions combo from lights and AC version combos from heavies.",
    "623acac6a": "Joe finishes the Bakuretsu ken with a one-two punch. Does more damage than Boom Elbow and is safe on block. Has less range compared to Boom Elbow and tends to whiff in juggles if Bakuretsu Ken doesn't hit close.",
    "623bdbd": "A rising knee strike. BD version has Joe charge forward slightly with a tackle before doing the Tiger Kick. Good horizontal range for a DP and always hits as long as far A hits, which many other characters cannot do with their DPs. At longer ranges, the D version's brakeable frames end before it can hit. This happens if 3 lights are used before the DP or from a close D > far A link. Braked DPs have a lot of extra recovery on whiff (9 frames for D, 7 frames for BD).",
    "6b": "Safe on block low with good range. Has similar range and startup to his universal overhead. Doesn't leave Joe close enough on hit to link cl.A, but he can frametrap with cl.D / 2C or go for a pressure reset.",
    "ab": "Leaves opponent close enough for different meaty options. L. Hurricane Upper / H. Golden Tsumasaki / Dash, cl.D / etc.",
    "cd": "Joe does a lunging elbow uppercut.",
    "cla": "Joe's only 4 frame move.",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. cl.AC first hit is cancellable by any follow-ups. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender, during cl.AC only the second hit is REV Blow cancellable. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : First hit of the auto combo, cancellable by any follow-ups. cl.AC > C : A second hit cancellable by any ender, a nice way to increase the damage or your opponent REV meter. Unsafe on block and can be punished if the auto-combo was started too close to the opponent. D ender : Far D follow up, different frame data and damage, very unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Safest ender, Same move as his Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV) but +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Same move as his 2D, meaning it's very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "Plus on block and links into 2C on hit. On block into 2C frame traps 5F moves.",
    "clc": "An unusually slow close C that often loses in close scramble situations if you're used to other characters. Also has a short activation range. Links into far C on feint but only on standing opponents. Also links into 2C.",
    "cld": "Plus on block, has throw invincibility and forces stand. Always links into far A which can combo into D DP (no brake). Links into far C on counter hit.",
    "cra": "Joe's fastest normal outside of cl.A.",
    "crb": "Standard low light kick. Nice for chaining into far A.",
    "crc": "Great normal for combos, counterhit conversions, and frame traps.",
    "crd": "A standard sweep.",
    "fa": "Great combo tool at the end of light chains. At max range it can still combo into H/REV Tiger Kick or L. Slash Kick",
    "fb": "A fast and safe poke. Amazing option to use after 2B and will link consistently from all ranges. Overall great button",
    "fc": "Whiffs on crouching opponents unless very close. Joe's best neutral button as it is cancelable and has good range. Has amazing cancel options and can start Joe's offense easily. Extremely whiff punishable so use with care",
    "fd": "Poke that can be cancelled into supers or a target combo. Interesting button that can be used as a whiff punisher which also has an easy to confirm target combo which gives it multiple uses. Great button in neutral Hits crouchers.",
    "fddd": "Hit confirmable extension of Joe's poke for more damage and a knockdown.",
    "ja": "Joe's fastest air-normal and a good jump-in",
    "jb": "Crossup normal and a good jump-in",
    "jc": "A high elbow swing. Better as an air-to-air than a jump-in.",
    "jcd": "Joe does a diving knee in the air.",
    "jd": "Joe's furthest reaching jumping normal"
  },
  "kain_r_heinlein": {
    "214p": "Kain's new mine special compliments both Kain's pressure and space control, making it a versatile move. The B and D versions create a mine at 2 different distances, and the REV version creates both mines. All versions will automatically detonate with a delay, or can be input again to manually detonate. The REV version will detonate both mines at the same time automatically, but will stagger and combo if done manually. All versions can compliment Kain's Schwarze Flamme, putting another thing on the screen for the opponent to worry about. Most combos Kain scores, along with his throw, can lead to him placing a Schwarzes Rondo on top of the opponent. This gives Kain looping pressure if he desires, which usually leaves him majorly plus if the opponent blocks it successfully.",
    "236236a": "Decent reversal tool and combo ender. Usually outclassed by using 236236B during a combo, but useful to add damage in combos if needed. Most routes into 236236A require dashes or Schwarze Panzer feints to get the super to connect properly.",
    "236236b": "An incredible use of Kain's meter, and one of the best Ignition gears in the game. Starts up fast enough, covers a solid amount of the screen, and is +8 point blank on block, making it Kain's turn no matter where this connects on the opponent. Launches on hit, giving Kain extended juggles anywhere. This can cause Kain's damage to absolutely skyrocket with meter. Though expensive, this can be thrown out semi-randomly at mid range if desired, forcing a reaction or + frames from the opponent. Does considerable chip/REV damage, and slows down significantly on JD/HD, which can be worse for the opponent if they guess wrong after. Because of this, Kain can lead the opponent to checkmate scenarios where they have to DP/JD/REV guard for their life at low health on burnout/high REV gauge with enough meter.",
    "236236c": "Though useful as a high-damage reversal, it loses versatility in combos, as Kain can combo from his 236236B into his 236236A, which usually leads to more damage.",
    "236236d": "Though incredibly strong, it is generally overshadowed by using both Ignition Gears in combos, and two 236236Bs in pressure. Really funny for combo videos and pressure, though.",
    "236236rev": "Similar issue to Kain's Redline gears in that he usually gets better damage using his two ignition gears together. However, Kain's Hidden Gear still sees use as a way to reset his REV gauge and for easier, more stable confirms.",
    "236k": "Fairly reasonable combo tool. All versions are identically unsafe on block. B version starts traveling fastest, D version does the most damage and has a few starting hits, and the REV version can be cancelled into 28BD to make it + on block. All versions have a brake, but only the B version properly brakes at point blank. None of the brakes work after a connect. The REV version is most used as a combo extender off juggles or heavies, while the B and D version are used as combo enders and work on lights (though the D version can fall out at range). The D version is Kain's best meterless guard cancel option without down charge.",
    "236p": "Kain's iconic fireball returns as a zoning and combo tool. Has a feint that looks similar to the fireball and still backsteps. All versions cause Kain to backstep, which can get you out of sticky situations, but can lead Kain to making combos harder or cornering himself in neutral if used improperly. A version fires horizontally, C version fires diagonally upwards. REV version covers both angles. All versions are useful in corner combos, but the REV version is particularly useful for both midscreen and corner routes.",
    "2a": "Fairly average 5 frame crouching jab. Useful for abare and combos. Has less range than f.A, causing it to whiff on cl.D/distanced cl.C starters. Replace with f.A in these situations if possible.",
    "2ab2c": "Though not an amazing string on its own, it still sees use in certain Schwarzes Rondo setups as a solid low meaty.",
    "2b": "Fairly solid 5 frame low. Hitbox is smaller than the move shows. Combos into f.B, but will fail at further ranges.",
    "2c": "Excellent neutral tool with decent speed and frame advantage. Combos into itself/cl.C on CH depending on range, and dash-up cl.D on WP, making it a very rewarding whiff punisher.",
    "2crev": "Kain's anti air in a pinch when [2]8D isn't available. Very good range with reliable, if low-damage, combo options from it.",
    "2crevc": "An interesting string off Kain's 2CRev. Gives extra combo opportunities depending on where it is used. Does not come out on block, making it punishable. Limited by the usefulness of 2CRev",
    "2d": "Fairly standard 2D. Careful using this in neutral, as opponents can punish with their own 2D if poorly spaced. Can do an OTG 2D on WP. Not particularly useful, but it is good to know that it is possible.",
    "4ab": "Kain's forward and back throws leave the opponent the same distance and have the same frame advantage, making them functionally identical except for positioning.",
    "5crev": "Fairly average Low Dodge. Useful for overheads and low/throw baits. When combined with Schwarzes Rondo, this gives Kain a fantastic, yet slow overhead that can lead to massive damage.",
    "ab": "Though fairly average in the scope of COTW, it gains much more utility than other throws, as Kain can usually set up a Schwarzes Rondo after the throw. This can give Kain a rather oppressive half of his strike/throw game.",
    "after214p214p": "Manual detonation is great for opponents who try to slip past the mine or for staggering the REV version. On a grounded connect, manually detonating the opponent on the first mine of the REV version can lead Kain to dash back up and do a cl.D on hit, causing staggering damage. For meterless verions, Kain is usually +8, which gives him a f.C instead.",
    "cd": "Kain's SPG attack covers a large amount of screen space, making it a serious threat once Kain has it. It can be spaced to have more advantage than usual, even as far as becoming + on block. Like all SPG attacks, it loses to early throws and High Evade attacks.",
    "cla": "Rather standard 4 frame light. Combos into cl.C on close connect, is + on block, and can lead to autocombo.",
    "clb": "Usually overshadowed by other options, but is the best source of + frames on block of all of Kain's normals. Frametraps into cl.C on block and hit.",
    "clc": "A fast, 2 hit normal that is a staple in Kain's pressure. Is +2 on block when using Feint cancels, giving a solid way to run strike throw when conditioned. Combos into 2A/st.A on hit with feint cancels, and the 2 hits gives you time to gain down charge. Is +10 on ch with feint, giving a hard, yet rewarding microdash/microwalk cl.C. +25 on WP feint cancel, giving an easy dashup into a full combo.",
    "cld": "Great normal for max damage routes. Forces stand, guaranteeing f.A later in a combo. Always + enough to guarantee dash cl.D on CH/WP, albeit with varying difficulty.",
    "fa": "Essential normal for combos. Cancels into 28C if passed through during the input. Whiffs on crouching opponents, so either combos must be either started with cl.D, confirmed, or subbed with 2A.",
    "fb": "Long ranged light normal with special cancelling, usually used for Kain's light combos.",
    "fc": "Decent cancellable poke, though the range can be lacking in comparison to Kain's other heavies. Combos into itself on CH with feint cancel, and leads to a dash-up cl.D on WP.",
    "fd": "Kain's longest normal. Decent for neutral use. Does not get much on CH, but leads to dash-up cl.D/f.C on WP depending on range.",
    "hold28k": "Kain's flashkick is a versatile tool, usable as a combo starter, extender, ender, anti-air, pressure tool, and reversal. Since this move has a quicker-to-execute input than a DP motion, Kain has an advantage in reacting to certain moves with invincibility. He makes up for this with needing down charge to do reversals, which limits his movement in neutral and ability to block overheads if he wants to use this move. The B version is a decently fast reversal, the D version is a solid anti-air with a brake. The REV version is a combo extender, and can be braked to become + on block. If possible, the D version (and its respective brake) is usually Kain's best Guard Cancel after JD/HD, though could prove difficult to execute. When braked, the D version has 4 frames extra recovery on whiff, leading to potential consequences if you misjudge a jump-in. Even if the fireball connects on a grounded opponent, Kain is at major disadvantage, so only use it in situations where the first hit can connect. The fireball can hit OTG. This usually happens when the first hit connects on an extended limb or at its max range, keeping Kain safe on hit.",
    "ja": "Jumping normal that is only notable for being Kain's fastest jumping normal. Not very useful in most scenerios.",
    "jajb": "Usually overlooked, as the two hits add extra scaling to combos started with this.",
    "jb": "Solid air=to-air normal for Kain. Does its job well enough.",
    "jc": "Though the range is not amazing on j.C, it is Kain's highest damage jumping normal, making it useful in punish scenarios like jumping grabs or fireballs.",
    "jcd": "Similar to Kain's grounded SPG, but aimed downwards in the air. Can have slightly more frame advantage than 5CD depending on how deep it is connected. Finally, it can be used as an OTG at the end of certain combos (ex. corner combos ending in 236236A), letting you squeeze just a little more damage out in specific scenarios.",
    "jd": "Is a nice mix of damage and hitbox size for air-to-ground situations. Usually used in higher jump-in connects where j.C might not be applicable."
  },
  "ken_masters": {
    "214acac": "Runs with various follow-ups, can be stopped by pressing B (Emergency Stop follow-up).",
    "214acac2d": "Overhead follow-up, but with there being no low followup out of run this is generally very easy to block. The Rev run version can be cancelled into and out of very liberally, so it can occasionally work as mixup, and it's safer regardless.",
    "214acac4d": "A/C Quick Dash will always trigger the same version of the Tatsu. AC Quick Dash will trigger an EX version of this Tatsu. An enhanced Tatsu with flame effects where Ken performs 5 hits. The EX version doesn\u2019t launch like the regular EX Tatsu and stays almost the same as the A/C Quick Dash version, just stronger. The EX version has better juggle potential than the regular version and will allow further juggles if it hits airborne. E.g. EX Jinrai > 6D > 6D > 214AC > (wait) > 4D. Links into stand C on counter hit or wild punish, which gives it some utility in whiffed DP punishes for corner carry.",
    "214acac6ac": "A stronger version of Shoryuken with more hits and damage, and incredible horizontal reach. Meterless Quick Dash always results in a boosted heavy Shoryuken, Rev Quick Dash always results in EX. While no version is fully invincible, this can still be used as a long range anti-air in neutral for jumps done against distant fireballs.",
    "214acac6d": "A/C Quick Dash always results in an boosted heavy Dragonlash. AC Quick Dash always results in an boosted EX Dragonlash. All versions switches sides on an airborne opponent. A more powerful version of his Dragonlash.",
    "214acacb": "Allows you to end the Quick Run, generally only used to convert a Wild Punish 6C/3C or to tick throw people. Can be used in neutral thanks to it's short duration, but it's still pretty risky.",
    "214acacd": "A stepkick, meterless version is safer when spaced, Rev version is always safe. Generally a poke with reward on counterhit, but it's also a frametrap from heavy normals.",
    "214bdbd": "B version launches on either hit, but no followups are possible unless it hits close. While it has a 4-frame startup and is technically Ken's fastest move, this only applies to the first hit, with the second part hitting on frame 14~16. That first hit also has limited range and doesn't hit airborne opponents, so it can struggle as an abare option. D version has good corner carry and can combo into light DP in the corner. BD version isn't very rewarding, but it combos from all of Ken cancellable normals, even lights from tip range. DB Brake generally scales combos more than its worth, however it can combo into 623C/AC, so it can open up meter dump routes from weak hits.",
    "236236ac": "Both version are invincible. A version is a double Shoryuken and C version a triple. C version does an embarrassingly low amount of damage, barely more than a level 1 Shippu Jinrai-kyaku.",
    "236236bd": "236236B is Ken's highest damage Ignition Gear, mainly done after sideswitch Dragonlash or cancelled into from EX moves like DP, Jinrai, or Hadouken. 236236D has full invincibility and can be Braked for a combo extension. B version : Excellent damage but slightly harder to juggle into than Shoryureppa due to its slower startup. Ken generally has to spend an EX DP to super cancel into this from most routes to guarantee a hit, however it is much better in whiff punishes or general confirming. D version : Braking this move against grounded opponent will let you get a combo for potentially slightly higher damage and some of your meter back; +4 on grounded connect means you can link B tatsu, while against a launched opponent you can go into any DP or 214BD. Unlike the B version, this is fast enough to always combo off a DP brake, and it allows Ken the flexibility of going straight into this in a low-scaling combo for max damage, while still being able to cash out in a longer combo off an EX DP super cancel. As a general rule of thumb, if no EX moves (or REV blow counter starter) are used, going straight into this without a EX DP super cancel will do more damage.",
    "236236rev": "Costs 2 bars and only avaiable during S.P.G., displayed damage is with S.P.G. bonus. Resets the Rev meter to 0%, even during Overheat. Has the same animation as Ken's SF4 focus attack with some decent range. Standard Hidden Gear and mostly a combo ender, though it should be noted that Ken really likes to use level 1 supers, and might frequently lack the meter to use this one. In any case, a Rev reset is appreciated in a final round, as it allows Ken to freely spam Rev moves to dominate neutral and push strong pressure to close out wins.",
    "236acac": "The world famous fireball. All versions are gapless blockstrings when cancelled from 2D and all will combo on hit, even at tip range.",
    "236bd4d6k4d6k": "Only available during REV Jinrai. Additional stagger tool and a confirming hit for Jinrai followups. All versions are mostly identical with small differences in frame data and damage, and they all combo from the move they follow up.",
    "236bdbd": "Rekka with massive delay windows and high/low followups. Street Fighter 6 players' nightmare fuel. B version is best for pressure and combos from lights, 236D is a corner combo tool. BD version is Ken's preferred Rev combo starter.",
    "236bdbd4d": "Overhead follow-up. Always leaves a gap, but works as a functional mixup with 236K > 6B Identical on counterhit to Kazekama Shin Kick.",
    "236bdbd6b": "Low follow-up. A safe pressure ender with a delay window that can turn it into a frametrap. +6 on counterhit, can combo into 6B. By delaying the follow up off D Jinrai in the corner, the shin kick can hit meaty, which allows Ken to have anywhere between +5 to +8 of juggle potential. The +8 sweet spot allows him to link to a heavy DP brake for a powerful low-scaling combo into ignition Shoryureppa or Redline Shippu Jinrai-kyaku.",
    "236bdbd6d": "B and D version whiff against crouch, relagating them to combo tools. BD version is Ken's primary choice for grounded combos. Usually cancelled into Kasai Thrust Kick, then another EX move. Causes a massive launch on Counterhit, so if you can manage to frametrap with it, you can go wild.",
    "2a": "A fairly standard 2A, has a slightly shorter range and lower damage than 2B but can be Special Cancelled as a trade-off. Due to the fact that Ken lacks a 4F Normal, 2A will be one of your main abare tools.",
    "2b": "Ken's main low, has good range and can chain into any other light normal except 6B. Like 2A it has 5 frames of startup, making it one of your preferred option for abare and quick punishes.",
    "2c": "Beefy button that is naturally plus at all times and leads to an easy frame trap into itself. Normal hit leads to Command Combo 1, Counterhit leads to itself, Crouch D, or Command Combo 2. Uniquely LINKS into Shippu Jinrai-kyaku allowing for an easier confirm.",
    "2crev": "Beats High/Mid, can be used as an anti-air.",
    "2d": "Surprise, it's not a sweep; it's an actual low forward. Works great in footsies against opponents that backwalk a lot to block mid pokes, or to deter shimmies.",
    "3c": "An upwards hook and a pretty solid anti-air option. Ken's strongest DP punish starter and links into itself on counter hit feint. This can however be awkward to do as 3B+C or 3D+REV will not result in a feint. Also links to itself on wild punish at any range.",
    "3d": "Unlike the rest of the cast, Ken Sweep is tied to a command normal Good range but terrible on block. Ken will always be punishable on block even at max range but the opponent might require the use of meter to do so if the Sweep is spaced well.",
    "4ab": "Ken's trademark Hell Wheel, sending opponents up and over across the stage. Ken is too far away to maintain his offense outside of the corner, but the corner carry itself is still often valuable..",
    "562bcordrev": "Ken's only feint, mimic is Hadoken. Has a flash like Marco's feint so it can be mixed in with normal Hadoken.",
    "5a": "+1 with okay range. Since Ken has no proximity normals, this is kind of his version of most characters' c.B, a quick, plus light normal, but with chains available and more range.",
    "5a5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during 5AC or 5ACC. 5AC, 5ACC, 5ACCD are REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with 5A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. 5AC : First hit of the auto-combo, can only be canceled into a follow-up or an ender. 5AC > C : Follow-up that can be canceled into any ender or REV Blow. A simple way to deal more damage, increase the REV, or reduce the opponent\u2019s guard bar(Overheat). D ender : Just a mid ender, soft knockdown on hit and unsafe on block but it's REV Blow cancellable. 6D ender : Overhead ender, +2F on hit and block but has a gap beforehand where the opponent can mash or use a move with upper body invincibility to counter. 2D ender : Low ender and is the same move as his 3D, so it's very unsafe on block.",
    "5b": "Has a lot more range than it seems. Perfect for punishes and confirms, even works after 2B2B.",
    "5c": "6 frame Heavy Normal that is not tied to proximity. The range is pretty short however, even with Ken advancing a bit during startup. When in range, 5C is pretty great as a combo starter and occasional pressure tool.",
    "5crev": "Beats Lows and Throws. Welcome back UOH.",
    "5d": "A forward advancing high kick with a Command Combo attached to it. Whiffs on crouchers, and not great as an anti-air either, despite the animation. By itself 5D is a pretty unremarkable normal, the forward movement makes it so 5D has better range than 2D, but whiffing on crouching characters makes it inconsistent in neutral. Its inability to hit crouchers or jump-ins properly combined with its very poor 22 frames of recovery makes this very committal and liable to get you killed if misused.",
    "623acac": "A version is fully invincible. C and AC versions are air invincible. Just like with Marco, there's never a reason to not brake the C version either for safety or combo extensions. At particular longer distances where the C version hits on the later frames, the juggle height is improved and it might become possible to combo into Shippu Jinrai-kyaku. Don't count on it however, because there's no surefire way to tell if the DP hits late enough. Braked DPs have a whopping 10 extra recovery frames on whiff, making them much more punishable when whiffed than they appear. AC version is only super cancellable on the second hit, but attempting to SC on the first hit will cause the SC to happen on the second hit anyways.",
    "623bdbd": "The D and BD versions are +1 on block, a useful tool for both getting in on the opponent and pressure but also a risky one since the opponent can react to its long startup and hit Ken out of it. BD version switches sides if it hits an airborne opponent and groundbounces them for a juggle.",
    "6b": "Cancels into Shippu Jinrai-kyaku (236236K) A standing overhead that gets more advantage when hit on crouchers, though this is about as slow as Low Dodge Attack it is less telegraphed and it allows you to combo into Shippu Jinrai-kyaku on crouch confirm",
    "6c": "Ken's stand HP from SF6. Still long, still active, but now much more negative on block. However, Ken can feint cancel to be +1, this being his primary use of feint in general. Leads to massive damage on Wild Punish even without having to invest a lot of resources, so it's great for whiff punishing.",
    "6d": "Ken's stand HK from SF6 and his longest wild punish normal. Can be cancelled into Quick Step but not other specials. Unique among all heavies in this game, on wild punish, this move launches the opponent. Hitstop on wild punish makes it much easier to confirm into run or supers Best to either follow with normal Quick Step ~ Dragonlash for damage or EX Quick Step ~ Tatsumaki for corner carry.",
    "ab": "Ken's throw from Street Fighter 6, and here he'll never have to worry about losing his throw loop. Very plus, enough for a double dash into a meaty 2C, a run overhead, or a variety of setups in the corner.",
    "c46c": "Chin Buster in some regional dialects. Ken's primary confirm route from a 6 frame button. Can be delayed or done on whiff. Uniquely scales damage by 10% instead of 5%. Forces stand on hit, which is useful for confirming into Jinrai snap kick. Should always be cancelled into a special or feint regardless if it connects on block or hit, when done raw it's punishable on block and even on hit.",
    "cd": "Only available during S.P.G Ken's REV Blow, armors everything from frame 2, but loses to throws, supers and High Dodge Attack.",
    "ddd": "A situational but useful Command Combo starting from 5D. Uniquely scales damage by 10% instead of 5% on the second hit. 5D>D :Unlike it's Street Fighter 6 counterpart, the second hit of Ken Command Combo 2 is cancellable by Specials. Pretty generous cancel window make it fairly easy to hitconfirm on hit or starting pressure on block Does better damage in combos than Command Combo 1 but 5D whiffing on crouch and 8F of startup relegate that fact to some longer ranged counterhit standing confirm of 2C or of a Jump-in hitting the opponent standing. 5D>D>D : Final hit of the command combo, rarely seen because cancelling 5D>D into a special will always lead to better damage and oki. The main use of this 3rd hit lies in the fact that the hitconfirm window for it is longer than the window for a special cancel so if your not confident in your hitconfirms you could find some use for it.",
    "j214bdbd": "B version : Descends while keeping jump momentum. Can cross up, though this is unrewarding. D version : Flies straight horizontally. BD version : If used on ascent, increases upwards momentum momentum and keeps the same on descent. Increases forward momentum in any case, and it can crossup more effectively than j214B. Can be cancelled into from 623AC, which leads to Ken's most optimal combos into Hidden Gear.",
    "ja": "Always knocks down, so it can be blindly cancelled into B or BD tatsu on air-to-air connects to convert on hit but usually still land safe if the opponent JD'd.",
    "jcd": "Only available during S.P.G Stops any air momentum Ken has and goes into an air tatsu. If cancelled from EX DP > EX Air Tatsu > (delay, close to ground) > Rev Blow, Ken recovers fast enough for a light DP, Hidden Gear or Shoryureppa. Air REV Blow, same than the grounded one : Armors everything from frame 2 and loses to throws, supers and High Dodge Attack."
  },
  "kenshiro": {
    "214acac": "Kenshiro performs a short-ranged ki blast. All versions leave the opponent standing on hit The heavy and REV versions have an optional follow-up attack that is performed with 6P. It knocks the opponent down on hit Despite Kenshiro using channeled ki to perform it, it\u2019s still a strike and can be countered/parried as usual If you\u2019re familiar with Ryu\u2019s Hashogeki from SF6 , this move is similar to that. These short ranged ki blasts are simple to use, safe, and especially rewarding if they Counter Hit. The slower versions are even plus on block, so sneaking one into a blockstring will allow you to keep your turn. Although they don\u2019t make as great combo filler as 236K, they make up for it with their versatility. Light version (214A) : A safe ender that\u2019s optimal for damage from light confirms A very straightforward striking special Combos from Kenshiro\u2019s lights On Counter Hit or Wild Punish, links to far A Does more damage than 236B, but leaves Kenshiro further away on hit Heavy version (214C) : Kenshiro\u2019s plus-on-block special move +2 on block while being barely fast enough to combo from heavies Both the first hit and the follow-up launch on Counter Hit Links to 5AC on Wild Punish Not very combo-friendly due to its startup You can confirm the follow-up attack easily when you see the first part hit REV version (214AC) : The version most commonly used in combos Similar to the heavy version, but is MUCH faster to start up, letting you cancel heavies into it to start combos Both the first hit and the optional follow-up attack have REV Accel properties Kenshiro\u2019s only plus-on-block REV special. Use REV Accel into this on block to keep your offense going Debuff Trigger: The debuff is applied and triggered by the follow-up attack. When it does so, the knockback is slightly higher, improving Kenshiro\u2019s REV Accel routes and combo conversions. That said, the first hit avoiding the debuff trigger is situationally useful to either keep the debuff on the opponent or to save the trigger for later in the combo.",
    "214bdbd": "Kenshiro attempts to catch a projectile with his fingers and sling it back at the opponent. Projectile counter used to turn opponents\u2019 projectiles of equal or less strength into a fast, high-priority projectile of Kenshiro\u2019s own Kenshiro is Wild Punishable by strikes for as long as the projectile parry window is active This pseudo-reflect gives Kenshiro an option to fight back against the projectiles commonly deployed to keep him out. The higher-priority projectile that this move creates (when it parries a fireball) beats other projectiles clean, offering Kenshiro something to help hang in tough vs projectile users like Kain or Krauser. Regular version (214B/D) Counters regular (light and heavy) projectiles The projectile that Kenshiro throws upon successful counter is the highest-priority non-REV projectile in the game, beating other regular projectiles clean (including charged fireballs from Krauser and Mr. Karate) REV version (214BD) Counters light, heavy and REV projectiles The projectile from a successful counter is the highest-priority non-super projectile in the game Kenshiro can\u2019t REV Accel a successful counter unless the projectile makes contact with the opponent Debuff Trigger: If the enemy is debuffed by Channeling Points and the projectile from a successful counter hits them, they are very briefly popped up in place for a soft knockdown. Combos from it are very dependent on distance, but it\u2019s possible to juggle from it without REV Accel.",
    "236236ac": "Kenshiro winds up and delivers a powerful two-fisted punch. Kenshiro's faster super and the one that works in most of his combos For a super, the damage is very low at a mere 180 Triggers the Channeling Points debuff, but cannot apply it This super is used purely for combos. With its fast startup and ability to extend combos on debuffed opponents, there are a variety of ways you can hit it, but the most common one is mid-juggle to take advantage of its combo-extending properties, cranking out as much damage as possible. Without the debuff, the super is still very useful for its fast speed and hard knockdown. Kenshiro's other super is generally better to confirm into against grounded opponents due to the utility, but this super has better raw damage, especially with the debuff, so there's places to use both. The Redline version is pretty bad. Its damage is atrocious and its fireball invincibility is practically useless. It does, however, do more damage than his Redline kick super, making it better in confirms on debuffed opponents. If they're not debuffed, just use other supers instead. Debuff Trigger: What happens when this super hits a debuffed opponent depends on the strength of super, and the current state of the opponent: The basic version vs a grounded opponent stuns them on the spot; this is the only way to hit multiple of this super in a combo The basic version vs an airborne opponent will launch them high, just like Counter Hit launches The Redline version re-grounds opponents if they were airborne, then stuns them after the knockdown",
    "236236bd": "Kenshiro slams his thumbs into his opponent's temples, striking their channeling points. Both versions are fully invincible on startup Applies the Channeling Points debuff on hit if it's not already on the opponent Cannot hit airborne opponents , even though it can be blocked Lowest-damage super in the game The main draw of this super over the other is that this one applies the Channeling Points debuff and a hard knockdown without spending REV, ensuring that the opponent absolutely has to respect your wakeup pressure. With solid range, it works after various confirms, and is a surefire way to give Kenshiro all the momentum he could want. If it happens to hit a debuffed opponent, the ensuing stun means you'll get a ton of damage out of landing it under the right circumstances. Although it has invincibility on startup, it won't hit airborne opponents at all, making this super completely avoidable with a reactive backdash. This severely hurts its utility, both in combos and as a reversal. The Redline version of this super always stuns the opponent, which means it can lead to upwards of 500 damage on hit. Although it's still hindered by its inability to hit airborne opponents, opponents have to be wary of a Kenshiro with two bars, since Guard Cancelling into this super can completely turn an entire game around. Debuff Trigger: If this super hits a debuffed opponent, it will knock them down and stun them for a guaranteed follow-up attack. The Redline version ignores the debuff and always stuns the opponent.",
    "236236rev": "Kenshiro lunges forward with a high kick. On hit, triggers a cinematic featuring his iconic attack. Only available during S.P.G.; damage value factors in S.P.G. bonus Resets the REV meter to 0%, even during Overheat During the cinematic, there are 90 hits of 1 damage each; these hits are always unscaled Doesn\u2019t interact with the debuff, causing it to disappear during the cinematic Unsurprisingly, the Hokuto Hyakuretsu Ken is here too, and it\u2019s one of the best Hidden Gears in the game. Fantastic range, great damage, works in any combo you could think of, and is incredibly satisfying to land. Like nearly all Hidden Gears, this resets your REV meter, but comes at the cost of okizeme; although it\u2019s tough to push offense after the fact, having all of that REV meter available helps you find that remaining damage to close the round out. Although the damage when done raw is about average for a Hidden Gear, you may notice that its minimum damage, often seen at the end of longer combos, is absolutely massive, coming close to 300. This is because the 90 hits during the cinematic are all 1 damage each, meaning they can\u2019t be scaled, not even by Final Ratio S.P.G., making the Hidden Gear hit incredibly hard no matter the combo into it. Note: If this Hidden Gear will do enough damage to KO, Kenshiro rips his top off, a significantly different animation sequence plays, a custom music track from the 2026 anime overrides the current BGM and the Hidden Gear deals a lot more damage, as he declares that his opponent is already dead. Despite this bonus damage, the KO margin is solely determined by the damage of the regular non-KO Hidden Gear, as with Tizoc's Hidden Gear.",
    "236acac": "Kenshiro slides toward his opponent and snaps his finger in front of them. The finger snap part of the attack is not launched if Kenshiro does not make contact with the opponent by the end of the slide's active frames All versions are safe, but minus on block A mix-up tool that forces the opponent to guess between left or right. Technically you can brake the move before the hit, but rarely if ever is doing so useful. Not to mention, it\u2019s safe on block in most matchups, and on Counter Hit, you get a combo. Light version (236A) : Can be used to safely end blockstrings and fish for counterhits The only version that hits from the front of the opponent instead of behind Combos into Far A on Counter Hit / Wild Punish Heavy version (236C) : Used to cross up the opponent Crosses over to the other side of the opponent and delivers the hit from behind, even if the opponent is in the corner Can travel about 2/3-screen distance and still launch the attack if Kenshiro makes contact with the opponent. Combos into Far A on Counter Hit / Wild Punish REV version (236AC) : Used to start combos after the crossup. The only version of this move that can start combos on a normal hit, and this must be done by canceling it into another REV Special via REV Accel Can travel about half-screen distance and still launch the attack if Kenshiro makes contact with the opponent. Combos into AC on Counter Hit / Wild Punish Debuff Trigger: If the enemy is debuffed by Channeling Points, this move very briefly pops up the opponent in place for a soft knockdown. 623A, 236236C and 236236R are the only attacks that combo from this without REV Accel canceling into another REV special. If this move hits a debuffed opponent as a Counter Hit, adds exactly 1 extra frame to the tiny launch and allows 623C to hit (and can be braked to continue a combo). This will also add 1 extra frame to each of the soft knockdown advantages.",
    "236bdbd": "A rapid series of kicks delivered after a short step forward. All versions can be made safe on block when spaced, making them a solid poke against debuffed opponents Easily Hyper Defendable by simply pressing forward anytime while blocking the move A straightforward rapid-kicking attack, very similar to Chun-Li\u2019s Lightning Kicks. They make for a safe cancel option on block that can\u2019t be checked by mashing, unlike 214P. Each version has a much different purpose, but the Light version is the easiest one to use, owing to its many applications, both as a poke and as combo filler. Light version (236B) : Used as a combo extender and in light confirms Combos from lights On hit against an airborne opponent, can continue juggles and combo into other specials Combos into Far A on Counter Hit / Wild Punish against a grounded opponent Heavy version (236D) : Primarily used to safely end blockstrings when braked, or as a combo ender when not braked. AC and heavies can combo into this Cannot continue juggles, even if braked or against an airborne opponent REV version (236BD) : Primarily used in combos, particularly at the start of them AC and heavies can combo into this The last hit applies the Channeling Points debuff to the opponent. If this attack is braked on hit, or canceled into another REV special before the final hit, the Channeling Points debuff will not be applied or triggered Debuff Trigger: If the last hit connects against a debuffed opponent, they will be launched slightly higher and closer, allowing the hit to combo into 623A before the opponent hits the floor.",
    "2bcor2drev": "Mimics the startup for move ( 236K ). Moves Kenshiro forward by slightly less than the horizontal travel distance of a hop.",
    "2cd": "Rev Blow ender only available in S.P.G.",
    "2crev": "A high roundhouse kick. Primarily used as an anti-air and low-profile poke. Has about as much range as Far D and consistently hits hops at the apex of their jump arc [ Click here to read more about the High Dodge Attack. ]",
    "4ab": "Has the same setups as Forward Throw when you throw them into the corner. Unfortunately, a Back Throw midscreen chucks them too far away to push any strong okizeme.",
    "56bcor56drev": "Mimics the startup of his overhead ( 6A ). This is Kenshiro\u2019s fastest feint, use this whenever a combo requires a feint.",
    "5crev": "A spinning kick delivered during a short hop. Primarily used for its overhead property. Safer on block than 6A, but requires a counterhit / Wild Punish or a meaty hit to start a combo. [ Click here to read more about the Low Dodge Attack. ]",
    "623acac": "Kenshiro uppercuts, then does a flying kick through the opponent. All versions have invincibility to air attacks on startup If the initial uppercut whiffs, the follow-up flying kick has no active hitbox You can brake the heavy and REV versions to prevent the follow-up kick, turning them into safe, scaled launchers for combos The follow-up flying kick will not go through a cornered opponent, keeping Kenshiro on the same side All versions are Counter Hit punishable on recovery if you don't brake them This DP combines two of Kenshiro\u2019s specials from the 2005 ArcSys HnK game into one, though it works similarly to many of this game\u2019s DPs, including the true invincibility. The main difference is that the flying kick switches sides on hit, which is good for defense but bad for oki. Nonetheless, it is important to Kenshiro\u2019s defense and combos alike. Light version (623A) : Kenshiro\u2019s main reversal and his fastest special move overall. Fully invincible on startup Kenshiro's fastest special move at 5f Has slightly more range than the other versions The invincibility doesn't last all the way through the uppercut, which can make it trade with jump-ins if done early Heavy version (623C) : Kenshiro\u2019s main anti-air. The first hit launches on Counter Hit, causing a high launch if you brake it Unlike the light version, the air invincibility lasts all the way through the active frames, making it more generous as an anti-air Kenshiro's best Guard Cancel special, being a fast launcher When Braked, it suffers an additional 11 frames of recovery on whiff REV version (623AC) : Much like the heavy version, but great combo filler, as well. All the same features as the Heavy version, but is slightly faster and does more damage on its own The follow-up kick can always REV Accel into j.236AC Debuff Trigger : Only the follow-up kick can trigger the debuff. When it does so, it pops the opponent up slightly higher, but otherwise doesn't change Kenshiro's combos.",
    "623bdbd": "Kenshiro does a backflip. All versions move Kenshiro backward and give Kenshiro projectile invincibility while airborne Can be cancelled to j.236P upon reaching a certain point in the backflip A very niche special, but is great at dodging some reversal supers (Raging Storm, Haoh Shoukou Ken, some grab supers, etc). Against non-super projectiles, Two-Fingered Intercept is almost always better, but the reflect on the REV version is useful against fast projectiles, such as those from Ronaldo or Geese. Regular versions (623B/D) Invincible to throws and projectiles on 6~30f Nullifies basic projectiles while ignoring other projectile-type moves REV version (623BD) The invincibility to throws and projectiles starts 1f earlier (5~30f) Reflects all projectiles of normal or REV strength, but still ignores super-strength projectiles",
    "6a": "Kenshiro does an overhead chop. Links to 5A or 2A on hit and 5AC on Counter Hit A core piece of Kenshiro\u2019s offense, this overhead chop is mainly to catch opponents respecting Kenshiro\u2019s low presence, especially if they\u2019re debuffed. You don\u2019t get too much from this overhead normally, but you can combo into a super pretty easily on hit. If you get a Counter Hit or Wild Punish, it links to 5AC, giving you potentially round-winning damage off of an overhead. Unlike Low Dodge Attack, though, this overhead is unsafe on block, so use this move sparingly.",
    "ab": "Despite the blue spark effect in the animation, this doesn\u2019t interact with the debuff As a close-range character with no command grab, you\u2019ll find yourself using this throw a lot to open up opponents. Thanks to its generous knockdown time, you have lots of time for okizeme on the opponent\u2019s wakeup. Against a cornered opponent, you can backdash after the throw to setup an autotimed safe jump (vs 5f reversals). Video example here .",
    "ac": "Kenshiro does a series of three punches. Very fast for what\u2019s functionally a far heavy, linking from close B on hit Kenshiro is absolutely blessed with this 7-frame special cancellable normal that goes into all of his REV moves. It\u2019s your main way of going from various lower-commitment starters into heavy damage, thanks to its link from close B on hit. It also works from various Counter Hits, such as 236AC. Thanks to being three hits, it gives you more time to confirm a hit so you can choose your follow-up special appropriately.",
    "cd": "On its own, a fairly standard REV Blow. Kenshiro takes a big step forward on startup, which allows it to reach even REV Guarding opponents if that\u2019s what it takes to Overheat them. However, it\u2019s impossible to use it in a spacing trap. You can combo after the REV Blow if it hits a debuffed opponent, but it has to be a quick follow-up (like a DP) when done midscreen. The step forward leaves Kenshiro vulnerable to throws before the REV Blow connects. Debuff Trigger: Causes a wall bounce on hit, allowing a juggle to 623P or super. The debuff won\u2019t trigger on Counter Hit.",
    "cla": "Kenshiro\u2019s fastest attack at 4f startup Kenshiro\u2019s Combination Attack is accessed from this normal Chains into other lights, most importantly close B, which then goes into your Heavy/REV specials If you don\u2019t know what to press up close, just press this. It\u2019s mashable, chainable, and plus on block. You can also access close B > 5AC from this normal, which can give Kenshiro some absurd damage for a 4-frame starter.",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. cl.AC first hit is cancellable by any follow-ups. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender, during cl.AC only the second hit is REV Blow cancellable. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : First hit of the auto combo, cancellable by any follow-ups. cl.AC > C : A second hit cancellable by any ender, a nice way to increase the damage or your opponent REV meter. Unsafe on block and can be punished if the auto-combo was started too close to the opponent. D ender : Far D follow up, different frame data and damage, very unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Safest ender, Same move as his Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV) but +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Same move as his 2D, meaning it's very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "You can chain into this from other lights On hit, gives a link to 5AC, giving you a route from lights to your stronger specials. An extremely potent normal on offense. It\u2019s quite plus on hit and block, and gives you a combo to Kenshiro\u2019s 5AC command normal for massively upgraded damage.",
    "clc": "Wild Punish +19 (+24 when feint cancelled) Moves Kenshiro a bit forward on startup Kenshiro\u2019s close C is a bit unusual. Despite having no good linking options, not even after a feint cancel, it ends up being useful in jump-ins, sometimes even optimal, due to the extreme precision required to hit a feint cancel BnB from a jump-in. In addition, the forward movement ensures your special cancels will work if you Counter Hit an airborne opponent with it.",
    "cld": "Wild Punish +22 (+29 if feint cancelled) Forces stand on hit Annoyingly high pushback can situationally limit the combos you get from it On Counter Hit, you can dash up to link a second Close D and still get your full combo, but it\u2019s tight to time This is your main combo starter, as it goes into your far C or 2C after a successful feint cancel. Add to this the fact that it\u2019s a whopping +4 on block, and you\u2019ve got one seriously dangerous move when you\u2019re on the attack. It comes with the infuriating downside that because of close D\u2019s high pushback, hitting the full feint cancel BnB off of a jump-in is unusually precise. To squeeze out the most damage as Kenshiro, it\u2019s highly recommended to get a feel for this move\u2019s pushback so you know when to go for a feint cancel link, and when to simply special cancel it.",
    "cra": "Chains out of and into any light normals, unlike far A Available special cancel combos are the same as far A\u2019s Something of a jack-of-all-trades button. It\u2019s a similarly used move to far A, but at the expense of a little range and damage, you actually get to chain out of it. This gives Kenshiro more reliable routes to super from lights.",
    "crb": "Kenshiro\u2019s only low combo starter Chains into other light normals, including itself Chains into close B when up close, giving Kenshiro some of the highest damage from a low starter in the game An insanely good low normal. Not only can you chain repeated lows to stop opponents from walking out of your pressure, but its ability to combo into close B > 5AC up close gives your empty jump lows huge damage potential by CotW \u2019s standards. It\u2019s also set up by feinting your overhead, making for brutal guessing games for debuffed opponents.",
    "crc": "Wild Punish +19 (+22 when feint cancelled) Kenshiro\u2019s longest-ranged normal that still cancels to special moves This move picks up links from cl.D feint cancel decently well thanks to its good reach. It\u2019s also a strong tool for counterpoking or whiff punishing due to its longer range than far C. Like most other 2Cs, it\u2019s almost always minus when not cancelled, even on hit - so you will definitely want to cancel it so you can keep up your offense.",
    "crd": "On Wild Punish, puts opponents into a special OTG state where you can follow up with another sweep Your average sweep. Usually safe when spaced, this can get opponents to think twice about simply walking backward out of Kenshiro\u2019s reach and away from his mix. It outranges his 2B, giving it some potential value as a surprise extra-ranged low.",
    "fa": "Mashable and chains from other lights, but only chains to itself Combos to 623P are range-dependent, but 214A and 236B work at any range A good normal overall to mash, confirm, hop check, you name it. Its main downside is its inconvenient pushback on hit and inability to chain to far B, restricting its greater combo potential.",
    "fb": "Kenshiro\u2019s longest-ranged light Can be chained into from other lights This long-reaching normal has two uses. Besides its obvious utility as a fast poke, it also picks up chains from 2A and 2B, making it essential in super confirms when you\u2019re not close enough for close B. However, having it blocked almost certainly ends your turn.",
    "fc": "Wild Punish +17 (+22 when feint cancelled) Kenshiro\u2019s highest-damaging cancellable heavy Pretty fast for a far heavy, but its range isn\u2019t that great This backhand is your optimal link after close D feint cancel, but its mediocre range limits its reliability outside of Wild Punish or Stun combos. With that said, its speed makes it fantastic to punish whiffed buttons.",
    "fd": "Wild Punish +22 Kenshiro\u2019s longest-ranged normal, and whiffs surprisingly fast for its range Easily single-hit confirms your supers Loses to High Dodge Attacks This is an essential normal to Kenshiro\u2019s gameplan. Kenshiro doesn\u2019t have many normals with good range, so in mid-range, you\u2019ll want to leverage the threat of this normal to keep the opponent honest and work your way in. It\u2019s very difficult to directly contest this move without committing to a risky High Dodge Attack, since it whiffs quickly for a far heavy, and you can make your opponent pay dearly for getting Wild Punished by it.",
    "j236acac": "Kenshiro rapidly punches his opponent while airborne. Can be performed from a forward or neutral jump, or during Extreme Airflow, but its height makes hitting grounded opponents difficult Use a Tiger Knee input (2369P) to perform it as close to the ground as possible Halts Kenshiro\u2019s aerial momentum on startup All versions combo from j.A on Counter Hit / Wild Punish A decently damaging air-to-air special, but the most difficult special move to use overall. It\u2019s most effective as an air-to-air, or occasionally as combo filler (especially the latter, in the case of the REV version). Light version (j.236A) : The fastest but weakest version. Niche combo tool. Kenshiro gradually descends during the move, but his opponent doesn\u2019t if they\u2019re hit; this allows for a greater ability to juggle after it, particularly in the corner Possible, but very difficult, to juggle to Redline or Hidden Gear midscreen Heavy version (j.236C) : Mainly cancelled into from Counter Hit j.A to score extra damage and a knockdown. Kenshiro does not descend during the move Impractical to combo after, but some corner juggles exist REV version (j.236AC) : Most commonly used after 623AC to extend combos. Can be performed from a jump in any direction (including backward) as well as after 623AC If performed after 623AC, you will need to reverse your input direction to REV Accel to this move midscreen; however, you don\u2019t do this against a cornered opponent Debuff Trigger: Opponents hit by this move with their Channeling Points activated will get launched slightly higher, allowing you to land and keep juggling them. Cancelling this move into REV Blow will pop the opponent up even higher.",
    "ja": "Can be cancelled to j.236P and REV Blow, but on Counter Hit, only the j.236P will combo A fast air-to-air that can be cancelled to pick up extra damage from a successful air-to-air exchange, or to respond to another character\u2019s REV Blow with your own.",
    "jb": "Can cross up Has a Command Combo follow-up when done during a full jump The customary cross-up light given to most characters. However, Kenshiro\u2019s j.B comes with solid downward and forward reach, giving it uses as both a jump-in and a retreating aerial.",
    "jbja": "Only available from full jumps.",
    "jc": "This is your main jump-in normal for starting combos.",
    "jcd": "A downward lunge. If it hits a grounded opponent, or a launched opponent who\u2019s close to the ground, he\u2019ll follow up with a hitgrab where he knees them in the face. Doesn\u2019t go into the hitgrab if it hits an airborne opponent, but the full damage of the REV Blow will instead be on the first hit. Also doesn\u2019t go into the hitgrab on Counter Hit, but instead crumples as though it were the grounded REV Blow. REV Accel\u2019ing into this after j.236AC almost always gives a juggle on the ground, usually to a DP or super. Debuff Trigger: Launches a grounded opponent much like a Counter Hit special, or has slightly higher knockback vs an airborne opponent. The debuff won\u2019t trigger on Counter Hit.",
    "jd": "This big kick has a lot of reach, making distant jump-ins more threatening than they appear due to the normal\u2019s ability to skirt around less committal anti-airs like hop-checking jabs. You can also chase opponents\u2019 back jumps with this normal. You can start combos by jumping in with it, but j.C is usually preferred for that."
  },
  "kevin_rian": {
    "214acac": "Charge up (or abort) a fierce backfist strike, then launch the attack by releasing the attack button or after a maximum charge time is reached. Press any kick button during the charge to cancel without attacking (minimum total animation length is 17 frames, or 16 for REV version). This is referred to as a Rotor Cancel. All versions and charge levels cause a knockdown on normal hit / Wild Punish and cause a slow launch on counterhit, with only one exception (see 214A below) A Rotor Cancel can be used as a Guard Cancel to force any attack to inflict only 17 frames of blockstun, opening up dangerous new punish opportunities. Read more about it here. Light version (214A): Swing a long backfist strike with huge horizontal range. Used as a long-range poke, but can also be used as a light confirm and/or combo ender. All special-cancelable attacks can combo into this on a normal hit except for Far A, which can still combo into it if Far A hits as a counterhit / Wild Punish Good spacing makes this very difficult or downright impossible to punish despite being -6 on block for the uncharged version. As an exception to the above note on 214P granting knockdowns, an uncharged hit will leave the opponent grounded, even on counterhit / Wild Punish (where it becomes +9 instead of +4). An uncharged counterhit against an airborne enemy causes slow launch, and an uncharged airborne wild punish causes a knockdown. Heavy version (214C): Bring down a slow, heavy overhead fist. Used as a close-range mixup tool. Only combos from Close C/D, but also frametraps from these normals on block Knocks the opponent straight down instead of away from Kevin If fully charged, Kevin steps forward far enough to make this hit from a bit further than round start distance, in addition to becoming safe on block REV version (214AC): A 2-hit strike with an animation that looks a bit like a faster 214C followed by a shorter 214A. Used in combos, but can also be used to end blockstrings if braked. Despite the animation, the first hit is not an overhead Can be braked on the first hit to remain plus on block. Otherwise, punishable if not braked or REV Accel canceled into another REV special. A Rotor Cancel from this version is 1 frame faster than the normal versions of 214P",
    "214bdbd": "Kevin performs a quick combat roll toward the opponent. Invulnerable to projectiles. Normal version (214B/D) : Primarily used to move through projectiles. Will not switch sides with an opponent REV version (214BD) : Primarily used as a left/right mixup tool at close range. Faster roll with a shorter animation than the normal version Can roll past an opponent to quickly switch sides Can REV Accel into any other REV special to make the roll animation 1 frame shorter and spend less REV for subsequent REV specials",
    "22bd": "Kevin hums a tune to himself while slowly crawling toward the opponent. This is a stance that can be canceled into one of two attacks that are exclusive to this stance and are usually used on an opponent's wakeup as a mixup tool Can low profile some moves, including projectiles It takes 11 frames to enter Creeper, then Kevin crawls forward for 60 frames and spends 30 frames getting up",
    "22bdab": "While crawling, press any light button to exit the crawling stance early and get up This animation is faster than the one that automatically activates after 60 frames of crawling, taking only 20 frames to get up instead of 30 Can be used to bait reversals if Creeper is used while an opponent is knocked down",
    "22bdc": "One of two attacks available during Creeper, a sweeping low punch at the ankles. Punishable on block (and even on hit) if it is not canceled into a special attack or feint",
    "22bdd": "One of two attacks available during Creeper, a rolling overhead kick. Ordinarily does not start a combo on hit, but some setups can make this up to +9 on normal hit (or +4 on block), allowing it to combo into Close C for a full combo Will not high profile any low attacks, does not grant airborne status or throw immunity, and 2CR will low profile it for a Wild Punish",
    "236236ac": "Kevin leaps straight at the opponent with a series of punches before punching a shotgun shell with an uppercut to the opponent's face. Used as a reversal and combo ender. 1-bar version (236236A): Standard reversal / combo ender super Kevin's cheapest reversal without using a Zero Frame Super (see Gatling Freeze / 236236K below) Not very good against jumping opponents 2-bar version (236236C): Identical in function to 236236A, just deals more damage",
    "236236bd": "A fast, high-angled kick that causes an explosion while Kevin famously shouts \u201cBingo!\u201d. Primarily used to hit-confirm far heavies into high-damage combos 1-bar version (236236B): Launches the opponent, allowing a combo to continue (or start) with an additional 25% of combo scaling applied Does not have any invulnerability and cannot be used as a reversal unless it is used as a Zero Frame Super , but is exceptionally rewarding in this case as it still functions as a combo-starting reversal Any ground normal can be canceled into 236236K to start a combo except for 2D. 6B and 5CR cannot directly cancel into 236236K , but a counterhit / Wild Punish on either of these attacks allows them to combo into other normals, which can then be super-canceled. Not the most reliable anti-air despite the attack angle, especially when 2CR can combo into 236236K anyway 2-bar version (236236D): Unlike 236236B, this super is fully invulnerable and can be used as a reversal On grounded hit, opponent remains in a lengthy hitstun animation on the ground, giving Kevin enough time to move in and hit Close D to continue the combo. On airborne hit, launches even higher and slower than a counterhit launch Still not great at covering the air space above Kevin",
    "236236rev": "After delivering a short uppercut, Kevin underhand tosses a 40mm grenade in front of the opponent's face and detonates it with a big punch. Like any other Hidden Gear, this is a standard reversal / damage cashout tool that can only be accessed while in S.P.G. and costs 2 stocks of the Power Gauge. Resets Rev Meter to 0%, even if the user was overheated when the attack was activated Short horizontal range",
    "236acac": "Kevin leaps into the air and swings his fist down on the opponent. All versions: Can jump over attacks and potentially score a Wild Punish, starting a combo Very tall hitbox, capable of hitting full jumping and crouching opponents reliably Regardless of hit or block, gains an additional 3 frames of advantage against a crouching opponent Combos into 2A or Far A on grounded counterhit / Wild Punish, or Close C if the opponent is crouching. Causes big launch if it counterhits an airborne opponent Light version (236A): Used to safely end blockstrings while inflicting good damage to the opponent's REV meter. About the same jump angle as a forward hop Will never jump over an opponent, even if they are knocked down Heavy version (236C): Used to quickly approach an opponent or contest air space. About the same jump height as a forward jump, but travels a bit further Can jump over a crouching opponent and whiff if they are too close Can also be used as a combo ender following a braked 214AC hit REV version (236AC): Used to remain plus on block and inflict heavy damage to the opponent's REV meter. Identical animation and jump angle to the light version, but deals more damage and is always plus on block Strangely, more advantageous on block than on hit (+2 vs +1)",
    "236bdbd": "Kevin sweeps his heel at the opponent's ankles, then grabs them out of the air and hits them with an explosive knee. All versions: Not a low, despite the animation The grab animation does not actually have a hitbox, the second hit only activates if the first hit lands Light version (236B): Used as a light confirm and/or combo ender Kevin\u2019s fastest strike special, but very punishable on block and cannot be braked. Any special-cancelable normal will combo into this Heavy version (236D): Used to launch from heavy normals or safely end blockstrings Always brake this version. There is currently no benefit to letting the grab animation play, ever. The following information assumes this attack is braked. Slower, but safe on block and launches the opponent for a combo starter/extension on hit Causes big launch on counterhit Any special-cancelable heavy normal will combo into this REV version (236BD): Used as combo filler, or to continue close-range pressure on block Plus on block if braked, and the first hit pulls the opponent closer on block Launches on hit if braked. If not braked, the second hit can be canceled into uncharged 214AC In addition to heavies, Close A and 2A can also combo into this if they hit as a counterhit / Wild Punish",
    "2a": "A standard crouching jab. Used as a fast, low-risk poke at short range. Will combo into 214A or 236B on normal hit Kevin's only crouching normal that cannot be low/high profiled by any dodge attack, all others can be high-profiled by 5CR",
    "2b": "A low poke against the ground with Kevin\u2019s boot. Used as a fast, low poke and can start combos at very close range. Long range for a 2B Can be chained into any other light normal If Kevin is outside of Close B \u2019s activation range after hitting 2B , a super-cancel into 236236K will usually be required to continue a combo beyond a light confirm",
    "2bcor2drev": "Mimics the leaping animation of 236P, with a shorter jump height and animation but no hitbox at all. Moves Kevin forward by a little more than the distance traveled by 236A Can jump over throws, lows and even mids with sufficiently low hitboxes",
    "2c": "A downward punch to the ground from a crouching position. Primarily used to poke without the risk of being low-profiled by 2CR . Combos into any version of 214P or 236K on normal hit On counterhit, can combo into Far B or 6B if 2C is feint canceled. On Wild Punish, causes slowdown and gives enough time to dash in for a combo starter Not an overhead or a low, despite the animation.",
    "2crev": "Kevin sticks his boot up while laying back. Primarily used as an anti-air, but is also a reliable ground poke. Usually combos into 214P on counterhit or Wild Punish against an airborne opponent, but the low hitbox of these attacks can make these conversions inconsistent unless the attack hits late in the opponent\u2019s jump arc. Will reliably combo against a grounded opponent.",
    "2d": "A big poke against the floor with Kevin\u2019s back leg. Used as a mid-range low poke. A quick and fairly ordinary sweep that hits low for a soft knockdown (or a hard knockdown on Wild Punish).",
    "4ab": "Placeholder",
    "56bcor56drev": "Kevin's fastest feint and the one used in his normal framedata.",
    "5crev": "A very short hop followed by a downward kick. Primarily used on an opponent's wakeup. Like any other 5CR, this attack will jump over lows and has throw invulnerability Only starts a combo on counterhit / Wild Punish (+8 on hit) or if it hits as a meaty",
    "623acac": "Kevin quickly leaps forward before dropping straight down to the ground with an explosion opponents. Can be used to high-profile an attack and come back down for a Wild Punish. Light version (623A) : Used to jump over an attack and potentially score a Wild Punish. Safe on block and relatively fast, but has the shortest and lowest jump Cannot cross up an opponent Combos into Close C on counterhit / Wild Punish Light version (623C) : Used to cross up an opponent and continue offensive pressure on block. Slowest version, but jumps further and higher and is always plus on block Can cross up an opponent Combos into Close C on counterhit / Wild Punish REV version (623AC) : Used as combo filler and to continue offensive pressure on block. Fastest startup, longest horizontal jump distance and slightly shorter vertical jump height than the heavy version Can cross up a crouching opponent, but becomes (-2 on block / +3 on hit / +8 on counter hit or Wild Punish) and will never cross up a standing opponent Has a hitbox while in air, making it capable of extending a combo Combos into 2A on normal hit as long as it does not cross up, or Close C on counterhit / Wild Punish",
    "623bdbd": "Kevin grabs the opponent and delivers an explosive 2-punch combo Normal version (623B or 623D): A standard command grab. High damage and better knockdown compared to the normal grab Tied for Kevin's fastest special attack (other than 623BD), making it a good choice for Guard Cancels if the opponent is close enough to be grabbed REV version (623BD) Starts up 1 frame faster and can be canceled into 236BD to start a combo, but also applies a heavy amount of damage scaling that requires a significant Rev Meter or Power Gauge investment in order to deal more damage than the normal version",
    "6b": "A quick, sweeping kick at the ankles followed by a straight kick to the stomach. This is a forward-advancing, safe poke primarily used to fish for high-reward counterhits. Its long animation makes it dangerous to whiff, but it is safe on block Cannot be canceled into anything, and does not combo into anything on a normal hit On Counter Hit / Wild Punish, becomes +6 on hit and can combo into Far A , 2A or 2B after the animation ends The first hit is not a low, despite the animation. However, a 5CR will high-profile it for a Wild Punish. Conversely, the second hit can be low-profiled by 2CR .",
    "ab": "Placeholder",
    "cd": "Kevin rolls forward into the opponent Travels about 1/3 screen distance forward Due to a complete lack of reversals outside of super attacks, Kevin will be reliant on Guard Canceled Rev Blows on defense Hits opponents in OTG state, and is in fact Kevin\u2019s highest-damaging OTG when it\u2019s available While in SPG, ending combos with REV Blow will open up a wide variety of mixup options on the hard knockdown; see Kevin's [ Strategy page ] for more information",
    "cla": "A quicker, shorter jab than his Far A . Used in close-range pressure Kevin\u2019s fastest attack, just 1 frame faster than 2A and Close B Chains into other lights and can combo into 214A or 236B on normal hit, can additionally combo into 236BD on counterhit / Wild Punish",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Universal target combo that can be accessed on hit or block, starting by chaining Close A into C. Sometimes abbreviated as ACCD. Close A > C : Can be canceled into the third hit of the target combo, or can be skipped to go straight to a finisher or REV Blow. Close A > C > C : Third hit that can only be canceled into a finisher or REV Blow. This target combo has 3 finishers, each activated by pressing D while holding a direction: 5D : Soft knockdown on hit, uses the same animation as Far D . Not a mixup and unsafe on block if not canceled. The only finisher that can be canceled into REV Blow 6D : Overhead finisher. Uses the same animation as 5CR , except now it is +2 on hit or block. Can be low-profiled by 2CR or interrupted with a sufficiently fast attack. 2D : Low finisher. Identical to using 2D in any other situation, and is just as unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "A quick, short stomp at the ankle. Used to start close-range pressure on block and combos on hit. Very advantageous on block with low pushback, allowing for frame traps and links into Close C Will combo into Close C on normal hit, and Close B > Close C is one of Kevin\u2019s primary combo starters Not a low-hitting attack, and therefore can be blocked while standing despite the animation. A 5CR dodge attack will still jump over it for a Wild Punish.",
    "clc": "A dangerous, short hook with Kevin\u2019s back hand. Primarily used to start or continue combos. Unusually long activation range Can combo into any version of 214P or 236K on normal hit",
    "cld": "A heavy knee to the midsection. Used to set up effective close-range guard pressure, or to start Kevin's most damaging combos. Shorter activation range than Close C , but can set up more dangerous pressure and mixup situations on block Close D > Feint > Close C is Kevin's highest-damage combo starter (and a frame-trap on block). On counterhit, Close D (counterhit) > Feint > Close D > Feint > Close C is possible.",
    "fa": "A slightly longer jab than Close A . Used as a low-risk poke to contest both grounded and low-altitude air space directly in front of Kevin Kevin\u2019s only far normal that can be canceled into special moves Will hit a crouching opponent Chains into light normals and can combo into 236B or super attacks on normal hit. Can combo into 214A on counterhit / Wild Punish",
    "fb": "A quick, long side kick at waist level. Used to cover mid-range ground space with a fast poke Can only ever combo into super attacks (usually 236236K ), even on counterhit / Wild Punish Very unlikely to be punished on block, and it is possible at close range, but very disadvantageous nonetheless This move\u2019s low hitbox placement makes it susceptible to a well-timed hop. Despite this, it can still be low profiled by a 2CR dodge attack for a Wild Punish, much like all of Kevin\u2019s other far normals.",
    "fc": "A heavy, straight punch. Primarily used as a poke to stop a ground advance, whiff punish and/or super-cancel a hit into 236236K Generous super-cancel window on hit or block Can combo into Far B or 6B on counterhit unless Far C hits at absolute maximum range Causes slowdown on Wild Punish, granting enough time to dash in and continue the combo Will hit a crouching opponent. Can sometimes whiff on a hopping opponent and is therefore not reliable for stopping hops.",
    "fd": "A slow, high round kick. Used for poking, whiff punishing and stopping hops. Same combo and hit-confirm options as Far C Better at hitting airborne opponents than Far C , and can still hit crouching opponents but may whiff against them at longer ranges",
    "ja": "A quick jab angled downward at a 45 degree angle. An all-purpose jumping attack. Kevin's fastest air attack",
    "jb": "A mostly-flat, horizontal kick. Primarily used to cross up an opponent. Kevin\u2019s only aerial normal that is capable of crossing up an opponent Usually only combos into Close A or Close B after crossing up an opponent",
    "jc": "A downward swipe with Kevin\u2019s hand. Primarily used for jump-in situations. Kevin's slowest, but most damaging air normal.",
    "jcd": "Kevin swings both fists down at the opponent Cancels all midair momentum upon activation and briefly floats Kevin in place, making it difficult to time a 2CR in response to this move",
    "jd": "Kevin spins in the air and extends his leg for a heavy kick. Primarily used as a mid-air poke. Kevin's longest-range air normal While great for air-to-air, it is also reliable at hitting opponents slightly below Kevin and can be used to control ground space from a hop"
  },
  "kim_dong_hwan": {
    "214acac": "Moves him forward. Heavy and Rev versions can be braked. Rev version can be braked at 2 points during the move. Is +2 on block after either brake points. First point leaves you in throw range, second just outside of it Heavy version is +6 when braked on a counter hit. Becomes a launching counter if only the last hit counters. Very important move for his combos and pressure. 214A is his main light confirm and 214C lets him stay up close. The Rev version is a great combo tool and in pressure as you can change up the brake timing.",
    "214bdbd": "Can be done as a feint with 2DRev. Beats Lows. Light version hits mid and isn't a true blockstring. Heavy version is unsafe most of the time. All versions can be braked. Heavy, Rev and Feint versions give you enough height to do j214BD or Super Dong Kick. Amazing mix tool, key part of Dong's gameplan, be it the move on its own or by braking it. On counter he gets a full combo from any version. The main risk comes from the fact it can be mashed out of or DPed if the opponent knows what to do.",
    "236236b": "Fully Invul. Comboes from Lights. Ol' reliable, does great damage and is really easy to combo into. Gives him very easy safe jump set ups when used as a combo ender anywhere on screen",
    "236236d": "Fully Invul. Usually his best meter dump whenever you're not in SPG.",
    "236236rev": "Fully Invul. Available only in SPG. Big cinematic Super, useful when needed but he's often spending 2 bars on 2 Supers.",
    "236bdbd": "Heavy version is a low, only comboes after clC and Counter 2C/2CRev. Death on block. Only Rev version can be braked. Can be done on startup or after the first kick. Braking the first kick leaves you +1 Light Version causes a juggle on air hit. The stomp returns, and he can combo it now. This his most important combo tool, especially the heavy version. The EX version is a great combo and pressure tool.",
    "2a": "Longest Dong light normal that's also a low, no special cancel makes this a unrewarding button but its range is useful when confirming into a super from 2B",
    "2b": "Great button to confirm with, cancel into 5A for your light confirms",
    "2c": "The Kim Family's Sacred Art -1 on block after a feint Very stubby, special-cancelable normal. Good for intercepting runs/dashes and taking advantage of plus frames. Combos into 236D on counter hit",
    "2crev": "Beats Highs and Rev Blows Combos into 236D on counter hit Extremely good poke for Dong, it has less range than far D but is faster and special cancellable on hit meaning you can buffer a special and it won't come out on block or whiff. Unfortunately the scaling on confirms from this is high but this still is one of Dong's most useful neutral tools",
    "2d": "a sweep Don't whiff this because the hurtbox lasts a bit after the hit, making you a big fat target for Wild Punishes.",
    "4ab": "Leaves them midscreen, potentially setting up a cross-up",
    "5crev": "Beats Lows and Throws. On its own it's fairly standard but on Counter or Wild Punish it links into clC and 2C, making it a great tool.",
    "623acac": "Standard DP. Rev version can be canceled into another Rev Art before or with the last hit. Nothing much to say here, it's an ok DP. Main issue is the Heavy version's stubby range, making some things harder or impossible.",
    "ab": "Dong kicks the opponent full screen and taunts them. While they may seem too far away for a follow-up, immediately running after recovery can get Dong in their face at up to +5, allowing for meaty pressure.",
    "cd": "Slap your body the floor. Decently ranged Rev Blow.",
    "cla": "Dong's fastest Normal. Great for starting light strings, stopping hops, and that's about it.",
    "clb": "Links into clC. Starts his Command Combo One of your essential normals due to the fact it links into clC, has good range too.",
    "clbdd6d6dcd": "You die if you just do this, finish the Target Combo.\nA bit too risky to be of much use but its there if needed.\nOnly available in S.P.G, gives it a nice knockdown.",
    "clc": "The Button. Comboes into 236D. +2 on block after a feint. Links into 2C after a feint for even better hitconfirming. This is your best friend, cherish this button as it's what gets everything started. Easy to confirm with, starts the big routes without a need to use Rev.",
    "cld": "A button Links into far A & 2A Links into clC on a counter hit Generally less useful button than clC, however the link into A buttons and better advantage makes this a button a niche for easy block confirming, notably being better than clC against Rev Guard and GC Rev Blow Lack of special cancels means you have to resort to linking into an A button and cancelling with a super to do any substantial damage on normal hit.",
    "fa": "Your main light confirm.",
    "fb": "Good as a poke but no cancels.",
    "fc": "Great for Wild Punishes, very jank on normal Counter Hits however since it doesn't link into anything. Much better at catching jumps than far D but whiffs on crouchers at further ranges",
    "fd": "Great range and allows for great confirms on Counter. 1 frame slower than far C but always hits crouchers and has better horizontal range. Fantastic for whiff-punishes, leading to a high damage combo on Wild Punish",
    "j214bdbd": "All versions hit mid and can be very unsafe. All versions can be braked. You can do anything after a Brake, including another j214K, giving him a quadruple jump of sorts. You can only do each version once per jump. Rev version is +6 on hit after a 214D Brake. Only Rev version can be done as a TK input, but it must be slightly delayed else it doesn't come out in time. Very plus depending on height Dong may not be able to fly but he can jump good, this move is an amazing mobility tool. Rev version recovers the fastest so its best saved for last.",
    "j236236b": "10/10 name. Has no invul. Comboes off of jA and jC. If done very low to the opponent in a combo, it allows for combo extensions. You use this super to be optimal or to be a show off, high risk for a bit more reward. If done correctly however, he can do 2 Supers in a combo and extend after it. Usually after 236D or 623C/AC (Braked), however the easiest setup is to do 623AC > j214BD*, inputting the super during the Brake animation.",
    "j236236d": "Not even the name can save this. Still no invul but it lets him combo from back jump jA. Flashy and does more damage but it's generally a waste of meter. In pretty much every situation where you'd want to dump both bars on this, a double Level 1 Super Dong Hwan Kick setup (for the higher damage), or Hidden Gear if you're in SPG (for the Rev cooldown), are both better ways to spend the resources. Outside of securing kills in situations where a single Level 1 won't be enough (and you don't want to risk potentially dropping a double Level 1), or taking advantage of the invincibility in the air to occasionally surprise your opponent, there just isn't really much reason to go for this.",
    "j236bdbd": "The classic Kim dive kick, worse for wear. Heavy version will never land all hits and will often leave you minus ON HIT ...avoid the heavy version. Spacing the light divekick is the only way to get plus on hit, and it's typically safe on block. Has a tendency to drop hits on air contact. Rev version can be done while jumping back, which can be useful while running away. The Rev version can also be canceled into 214BD, which can be Brake Canceled for some tricky crossups, but it's fairly expensive meterwise.",
    "j46c": "Pulls in on hit. Is even when done at its highest on block. Tends to bug out when done above their head. Doesn't give him a third gun as the name implies. His best crossup, the taco. Fast, long reaching and starts combos thanks to its great hit stun and pull in on hit.",
    "ja": "Starts his airborn Target Combo. Comboes into 236K. Hits crouching opponents. Hits standing opponents if done at the start of a jump. Great air normal, useful for jump ins and pressure.",
    "jab": "Air throw, leaves you a full screen lenght away from them",
    "jajc": "j.C loses its juggle when doing this. On paper it lets you turn this jump in into a heavies tarter, but its not good at that job.",
    "jb": "Can hit as a crossup. Barely hits crouching opponents. Another great air normal, not a great crossup but its still a crossup.",
    "jc": "Causes a juggle on airborne opponents. Special cancelable. Whiffs crouching opponents. A very interesting normal, as it can start combos if it hits an opponent in the air, so it's best used for those rather than jump ins, where it whiffs on crouching opponents.",
    "jcd": "Whiffs on crouching opponents. Dong shows off his Football skills, great as an ender and an air to air, but not good as an overhead. Your most consistent way of converting off of j236BD and 214BD.",
    "jd": "His highest damage air normal, and also the slowest. A solid heavy jump-in and its large horizontal reach makes it great for controlling the air and walling people out."
  },
  "kim_jae_hoon": {
    "214acac": "Jae Hoon raises his leg up and launches a flurry of kicks. Heavy and REV versions could be held to enter a Guard Point stance, but loses to lows and throws. Armor starts from frame 11 up to frame 28 on both Heavy and REV versions Heavy and REV versions can be forward dash cancelled by holding the button and inputting a dash input at any point during the guard point. They can also be breaked before the hit comes out by pressing the REV button. Light version (214A) Potentially the worst move in the game, has 0 use cases in Jae Hoon's gameplan and will get you killed for using it. Unsafe on block with bad risk-reward. Avoid using at all costs. Heavy version (214C) Fully held version is safe on block, non charged or even partial charged version is punishable on block. Does significant block damage and REV guage damage Can be held to enter a guard point state starting at frame 11 Rev version (214AC) placeholder",
    "214bdbd": "The iconic lunging split kick makes its way into the game, Hangetsuzan now multi-hits on all versions and in REV version's case leads to some of his strongest combo routes. Unfortunately, Hangetsuzan is very unsafe at most ranges on all versions though it can be spaced to be safe at the very tip of its last hitbox. This classic neutral tool has its uses but beware as it is commonly death on whiff and block. It is not recommended to use this move as your typical \"Neutral skip\" as it doesn't have the safety or range. Using this move recklessly can be a very easy way to lose rounds. All versions launch on counterhit when only the last hit lands Light version (214B) 2 Hits Travels round start distance Best meterless combo ender for most of Jae Hoon's combos and optimal ender for niche -5 on block punishes In version 3.00, now combos from light enders. Heavy version (214D) 3 Hits Travels the farthest at around 3/4ths screen length Doesn't have much uses except in very specific combo routes as an optimal ender. Rev version (214BD) 3 Hits Travels just a little under Heavy version's distance Important for converting heavy starters into stronger REV cost routes Can be made safe with REV Accel, making this a somewhat useful neutral tool",
    "236236ac": "Classic Kim launching kick super which allows for a combo extension. Normally does less damage than 236236B unless extending the combo in the corner. Allows for an easy safejump if used as an ender (28D-AB > forward jump/forward jump x2). Can follow up with 214K midscreen Near corner allows for 28K brake extension Always possible to link into 236236B afterwards",
    "236236bd": "Jae Hoon runs forward a distance until he comes into contact with the opponent. When hit he converts into a flashy combo ending with a final phoenix somersault or an explosive axe kick depending on which level. His reversal and damage super that will be used for most of his metered combos. As Jae Hoon relies on charge for his reversal, this can see use over the Flashkick when it is more convenient to input.",
    "236236rev": "Jae Hoon does a thrusting side kick, if it connects, he lunges at the opponent and unleashes a series of high kicks. He then leaps back and does a slow-mo Hien Zan for the ages. A reference to his father's Houou Kyaku against Cyber Cheng in Fatal Fury: The Motion Picture.",
    "236acac": "Jumping flame kick Not an overhead, is a mid Does not knockdown All versions can be braked before the hit Light version (236A) Fastest version but punishable on block Heavy version (236C) More range, safe on block, but more start up Good to catch hops, back dashes, and low hitting moves Rev version (236AC) +1 on block AND hit Useful combo extender Always leaves a gap when used in a cancel chain. On airborne hit, does a ground bounce which allows follow-ups. One of Jae Hoon's weaker specials. Doesn't have much usage except as a combo extender in the REV version. All versions can be high-dodge attacked on reaction and lose hard to invincible reversals. Not recommended to use in neutral due to massive recovery frames and very small range. Despite all versions being brakable. All of them lose to the same counterplay to the non-braked versions.Pretty much used as a gimmick knowledge check to sneak in a throw or low.",
    "236bdbd": "Double-hitting kick special move. Both hits are mid despite looking otherwise. Light version (236B) Jae Hoon's best meterless light string ender Extremely unsafe on block Heavy version (236D) Great special that has alot of uses in Jae Hoon's gameplan Safest Blockstring ender using the break Great meterless combo extender Rev version (236BD) Extremely versatile move with a lot of use cases First hit can be canceled into grounded REV specials, while the 2nd hit can be canceled into air REV specials. Jae Hoon's best way to set up Dive kick mixups( if you can call them that lol)",
    "28bdbd": "placeholder Braked versions recover 10 frames slower on whiff, making them easily Wild Punishable if they miss placeholder Light version (28B) placeholder Heavy version (28D) placeholder Rev version (28BD) placeholder",
    "2crev": "Pretty good High dodge attack and one of the better ones in the game. Very similar to Dong's. Good horizontal and anti air hitbox Links into REV Shaka Shu( better for grounded hits) and REV Zangetsu Zan( better for aerial wild punishes) for a reliable buffer.",
    "446b": "One of the most unique moves in City Of The Wolves. Only usable after a back dash. Despite having very interesting properties and good frame data. This move is quite niche and doesn't have much uses or a place in Jae Hoon's gameplan. Safe on block and cancelable Good anti-air hitbox, but doesn't lead to a combo Fast recovery which makes it hard to punish, although can be telegraphed if the opponent is looking for it. On grounded hit starts a combo with REV Hangetsu Zan",
    "4ab": "Average back throw. Gives much less advantage as his forward throw, but still gives true strike-throw okizeme. Can set up a deep hitting heavy dive kick mixup but is not safe to wake up reversals.",
    "5bcor5drev": "Mimics the startup of Hangetsu Zan ( 214K ). Puts you in throw range even if the opponent walks backwards after blocking a close heavy. Gimmicky and doesn't have much usage, Avoid using except in niche setups or to bait reversals",
    "5crev": "Another Government mandated move. Very average in frame data and range. Very hard to set up a meaty hit which allows Jae hoon to combo after due to low active frame count. But overall, a strong and important tool to use.",
    "62bcor62drev": "Mimics the startup for Shakka Shuu ( 214P ). This is Jae Hoon\u2019s fastest feint, use this whenever a combo requires a feint.",
    "ab": "Jae Hoon's throw. Gives good oki but nothing spectacular. Can be followed up mid-screen with 3 dashes to be +2 on top of the opponent. Can set up a throw frame kill using either Close A or Close B. Or an a meaty Close D Can set up a cross up jump, which is safe to buffer reversals by doing the following: Dash--->Neutral Feint--->Jump forward",
    "cd": "Government Mandated Rev blow. Not much to say about it. Below average range, Significant start up, and not much utility in neutral. Best used defensively and after a guard cancel DOES NOT combo after the low follow up from REV dive kick ( for some odd reason)",
    "cla": "Great Light starter and good in chaining but has an extremely small activation range Sets up tick throws very well 2x cl.A --> 2A is a consistent 3 hit light string which is the only way Jae Hoon can land 3 light attacks and end with a meterless special. Possible bug ( or design decision): This button can't be canceled to Hien Zan (Flashkick) when using it in a light string chain despite being special cancelable.",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. cl.AC first hit is cancellable by any follow-ups. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender, during cl.AC only the second hit is REV Blow cancellable. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : First hit of the auto combo, cancellable by any follow-ups. cl.AC > C : A second hit cancellable by any ender, a nice way to increase the damage or your opponent REV meter. Unsafe on block and can be punished if the auto-combo was started too close to the opponent. D ender : Far D follow up, different frame data and damage, very unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Safest ender, Same move as his Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV) but +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Same move as his 2D, meaning it's very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "Standard close B pressure and combo starter with very fast start-up Identical to Dong Hwan's close B but with 1 less plus frame. In range to follow up with a close C, which frame traps and starts Jae Hoon's strong blockstring offense. Great all around button, Use this.",
    "clc": "Double hitting high kick. Very similar to to Dong Hwan's, but is neutral on block with feint. Has alot of cancel options and can start his offense and blockstring mixups but quite hard to meaty or hit the opponent on wake up with. One of Jae Hoon's best pressure normals Easily confirmable due to the double-hitting property and big cancel window Starts combos with REV Hangetsu Zan and if blocked, Jae Hoon can force a favorable RPS situation using REV Shaka Shuu ( which is probably Close C's best cancel option on block)",
    "cld": "The Party starter Jae Hoon's strongest pressure starter, meaty, and reversal punish. On feint hit combos into 2C and on block Jae Hoon can frame trap with 2C as well. 5 Active frames make it very easy to meaty with.",
    "cra": "Jae Hoon's best button to mash on defense.Cancels from this button are quite weak and all cancels lose to the opponent mashing. The only frame trap option from Crouch A is Light Ryuusei Raku ( 236LK), which is extremely risky to go for as it is extremely minus on block. Good opponents will mash their 2B every time they see you do a 2A chain blockstring. The only way to counter this 2B \"Option select\" is to do 236LK to go over lows, but that is also a hard call out and punishable on block Meterlessly combos into Hien Zan(flashkick) and and light Ryuusei Raku (236LK) On counter hit, only gains the additional ability to combo into 214LK. Great option to use after a blocked Far D",
    "crb": "Standard low hitting light kick. Jae Hoon's best way to catch backward's movement and can easily chain into Far A at most ranges. Could be used after a blocked Far D, but Jae Hoon can't convert anything after a hit. Better to use Crouching A instead",
    "crc": "Jae Hoon's fastest and only special cancellable heavy poke, commonly used as a frametrap and confirm hit after clD > down feint. Despite its stubby range it's essential for converting off of counterhit and Wild Punish pokes. Will always combo from a clD > down feint link. Has a strong buffer into Rev 214K to start combos and blockstrings. Despite being Jae Hoon's farthest cancelable poke. It is not very useful in neutral due to the abysmal range and recovery and should mainly be used in block strings.",
    "crd": "Average Sweep frame data wise with good range. Use this sparingly as it is very punishable on block.",
    "fa": "Standard Jab which has quite good range. it is not cancellable to specials. Reliable way to end 3 hit light strings,and confirm into super. Go to button after a 2B, although can whiff if the 2B hits at tip range.",
    "fb": "Short-range kick which has the same frame data as Far A but with 5 more damage and slightly worse range. Great option to use when ending 3 hit light strings as it is cancellable into a good knockdown using super or light hazanshu",
    "fc": "Jae Hoon does a far-reaching roundhouse kick that only slightly advances his body. Exceptional poke, Jae Hoon's main way of space trapping and whiff punishing opponents and walling them out. Cancelling into a super is the only way to convert off a hit but a Wild Punish can allow for a follow-up from any range, depending on the option. Prone to getting hopped over if done too late. Can be linked from Wild Punish with a walk > clD, walk > 2C or REV Hangetsuzan. Usually safe when spaced out, but is always -6 on block. Quite easily punished if not spaced well",
    "fd": "Jae Hoon advances while up kicking, hitting twice. Essential button for Jae Hoon as it uniquely leaves him slightly advantageous letting him continue offense without REV from a far heavy. Although far D has a massive weakness of hit not immediately hitting crouchers, it enforces Jae Hoon's rushdown and is one of his best ways of closing the gap and extending pressure.This button is also super cancelable. One of the strongest buttons in the game Links into 2A on hit and 2C on counterhit. Hits the highest out of his far pokes making it great for catching jumps early. Only hits crouching opponents on frame 23 as the first hit does not hit crouchers. This leaves Jae Hoon vulnerable to 2Cs from almost any character, often leading to a devastating close-range wild punish. WARNING: Despite the amazing upsides to this button, it has alot of counterplay and weaknesses that could lose you the game. The main one being being very suseptible to high dodge attacks getting hopped on and whiff-punished due to the high recovery. Being predictable with this button can be extremely costly.",
    "j236bd6b": "REV enhanced low Follow up that can be made safe another REV cancel. Extremely important part of Jae Hoon kit and servers as the low follow up of Hishou Kyaku REV Hien Zan ( flashkick) is a great cancel option after using this move both on hit and on block.",
    "j236bd6d": "placeholder placeholder",
    "j236bdbd": "placeholder placeholder Light version (j.236B) placeholder Heavy version (j.236D) placeholder Rev version (j.236BD) placeholder",
    "j236d6b": "Low attack follow-up after a heavy Earth flash Extremely unsafe and risky to use. Should be used only after heavy conditioning for the opponent to block standing using Phoenix Strike ( overhead follow-up)",
    "j236d6d": "placeholder placeholder",
    "ja": "Jae Hoon kicks downward at a very steep angle. There is not much reason to use this button. It has fairly low blockstun so it could be used to mix up your jump in offense. Not much reason to use it over Jae Hoon's other jumping normals",
    "jad": "Target combo from Hoon's j.A, fairly good Good air to air despite being 8 frame start up Can't be done out of a hop 2nd hit only combos from a counterhit or wild punish",
    "jb": "Jae Hoon does a fast horizontal kick in the air. Due to the hitbox angle and speed. This is Jae Hoon's best air to air normal Main air cross-up button",
    "jc": "Jae Hoon does an aerial Axe kick which hits very deep. Best jump in button Jae Hoon has",
    "jcd": "Armored flying kick with impressive range and a good hitbox. One of the better air rev blows in the game and is quite strong in neutral. Great to use in air to airs and very combo-friendly Very useful and during REV dive kick mixups and any airborne REV special move. CAUTION:This move has a MASSIVE tendency to completely whiff on crouching opponents but this interaction is rare as most opponents will stand block when Jae Hoon is airborne. This move can also still whiff on standing opponents if done at the Apex of the jump. A good rule of thumb is to do this move while Jae hoon is desending from his jump or hop",
    "jd": "Similar to jump C but with better horizontal range, great air to air button (despite the slow start up) and a good jump in button as well at ranges where jump C might whiff."
  },
  "kim_kaphwan": {
    "214bdbd": "Kaphwan does a somersaulting crescent kick. All versions hit from round start distance and go over certain lows. They also hit OTG Unlike Jae Hoon, Kaphwan cannot brake any version This versatile special move defines Kaphwan as a character. The Hangetsu Zan is a combo ender, punish tool, advancing move, and more all rolled into one, though each version has its own uses. Light version (214B) : A versatile tool that can be used in a bunch of different ways. Combos from lights Always knocks down on hit, unlike the other two versions Launches on Counter Hit, making it one of Kaphwan\u2019s strongest punishes on DPs and whiffed REV Blows Good as a poke if you space it properly; its pushback on block is much worse than other games Heavy version (214D) : High risk, but high return. Leaves standing on grounded hit, giving a combo to 2C on Wild Punish Kaphwan\u2019s optimal OTG after a sweep that Wild Punishes REV version (214BD) : The ultimate round-start jumpscare, reminiscent of KOF XIII. Gets Kaphwan in their face quickly and lets him push more offense with REV Accel. Retains almost all of 214D\u2019s properties, but has faster startup and is safer on block",
    "236236bd": "Kaphwan rushes at the opponent and delivers a rapid beatdown. Both versions have invincibility on startup Faster startup than your average Ignition Gear The Redline version works after his Command Combos due to its faster startup If the move passes under an airborne opponent, Kaphwan will immediately stop and transition to the recovery Kaphwan\u2019s famous Houou Kyaku is the ideal ender for most of his combos, offering solid damage, combos into it from range, and most importantly, a hard knockdown to strong, consistent okizeme should your opponent survive. It\u2019s also Kaphwan\u2019s cheapest invincible reversal without charge. All in all, this super is incredibly versatile in its usage. Though the Redline version is generally outclassed by the basic version due to its worse okizeme, there are some places where only it works, such as after his Command Combos. Generally speaking, though, it\u2019s better to do other combos that allow you to use the basic version, unless you need the extra damage to secure the kill. Lastly, a fun fact: The Redline version\u2019s animation changes if it KO\u2019s the opponent, referencing the Real Bout series.",
    "236236rev": "Another rushing beatdown, this one triggering a cinematic if it hits. Only available during S.P.G; damage value factors in S.P.G bonus Resets the Rev meter to 0%, even during Overheat Like Redline Gear, you can juggle into this from Kaphwan\u2019s Command Combos Kaphwan\u2019s Hidden Gear is the ultimate cash-out super. It does massive damage, on top of his already-huge optimal combo damage, and resets his REV meter on hit. With its big hitbox and fast speed, it\u2019s also an effective, albeit inefficient, reversal. This super\u2019s fatal flaw, however, is that it resets to neutral, with no time to get back in. You retain zero momentum after using this move. However, that\u2019s offset by the super\u2019s damage and REV reset, allowing Kaphwan to use his normals and REV specials to play the life lead.",
    "236bdbd": "Kaphwan firmly stomps the ground in front of him. When done meaty, all versions give links into lights on hit (even without stance) and are plus on block All versions hit OTG Can hit airborne opponents, and all versions allow for juggles into super or any flashkick on air hit This stomp is a potent tool that forms the crux of Kaphwan\u2019s close-range game, not only for their potency in frame traps, but also to access a special stance (see below). It\u2019s quite good on its own, as well, being safe on block, rewarding on Counter Hit, and useful in juggles. Light version (236B) : Reasonably quick and can be used in a lot of places. Combos from lights on hit, and frame traps from them on block Cannot go into stance, making it mostly for frame traps, juggles, and as a standing ender Pushes out just barely enough on block to avoid reversal throws unless Just Defended Heavy version (236D) : Used to access the stance and its follow-ups. Safer on block and with higher pushback than the Light version, potentially creating spacing traps Transitions to stance on frame 33 REV version (236BD) : Combine with the stance and follow-ups to juice your combos. Very similar to the Heavy version, but with faster startup and access to REV stance Transitions to REV stance on frame 30",
    "236dbd6a": "Kaphwan sways back, then rushes forward with a high kick. If Kaphwan dodges something during the forward movement, it triggers a slowdown that causes the kick to punish whatever it dodged, similar to a Dodge Attack Both versions are invincible to air attacks while the hitbox is active Both versions lose to High Dodge Attack done on reaction to the sway This backsway into a kick, despite appearances, is the dedicated stomp follow-up to beat mashing. If the opponent doesn\u2019t know to High Dodge Attack or backdash this move, they will get absolutely rocked by it. You can also use it to bait and punish opponents attempting to hit the stance itself. Regular version (236D > 6A) : Slow unless you trigger the slowdown, but tough to deal with. +7 on Counter Hit or Wild Punish, giving a link to close B You can do this follow-up multiple times in a row, and it will beat mashing every time REV version (236BD) : Still slow, but is more rewarding if you dodge something. On Counter Hit or Wild Punish, the frame advantage goes up to +8, allowing a link to 2C. Your close normals are too slow to link here, but if you spaced it, far D will link",
    "236dbd6b": "Kaphwan delivers a leaping axe kick. Both versions hit overhead and are plus on block. Kaphwan will also be in throw range if he does this move close enough Both versions will beat many crouching lights mashed by the opponent Both versions hit OTG This is the turn-stealing follow-up, great against passive opponents or those you\u2019ve forced to clam up. There are a couple of ways to beat it, such as a gap for the opponent to check it with standing normals, but if they get Counter Hit or Wild Punished, the reward is immense. Regular version (236D > 6B) : A greedy but resourceless plus-frame reset. Won't combo from 236D unless it Counter Hits Can link to 2C on Counter Hit or Wild Punish REV version (236BD > 6B) : Really strong combo filler. Combos from the stomp and allows for a link to 2A on grounded hit Still links to 2C on Counter Hit / Wild Punish Ground bounces on air hit, enabling juggles to super or any flashkick",
    "236dbd6c": "Kaphwan kicks upward twice. The only follow-up that combos on normal hit for both versions; also combos from other follow-ups For both versions, the second hit launches on Counter Hit, but whiffs on crouch Both versions have air invincibility on startup This is the \u201ccash-out\u201d follow-up, the one you use to convert your hits into a knockdown. It combos not only from the stomp itself, but also every other follow-up. Regular version (236D > 6C) : All-around good ender with the ability to anti-air. Generally ends your combo with a knockdown and the ability to tech-chase Unlike other follow-ups, this is really unsafe on block REV version (236BD > 6C) : Strong combo filler and a highly damaging anti-air. Adds a third kick that ground bounces on hit, enabling juggles to Kaphwan\u2019s other specials. You can avoid this third kick by braking the move Still punishable, but braking the move makes the punishes less severe",
    "236dbd6d": "Kaphwan stomps the ground again. All repeated stomps are identical to the Heavy version (236D) Regular version (236D > 6D) Combos from 236D on Counter Hit, making it situationally good as combo filler REV version (236BD > 6D) Identical to the regular version, meaning you cannot REV Accel out of it",
    "236dbdhold6": "Upon holding forward after Heavy or REV Haki Kyaku, Kaphwan goes into a stance with one leg raised, allowing him to do one of four follow-ups. The stance persists for a limited time as long as Kaphwan holds forward. Letting go of the forward input will cancel the stance immediately. Kaphwan can perform one of the follow-ups immediately on entering stance. Upon completing one of the follow-ups (except Kuusajin), keep holding forward to return to the stance, allowing you to do another follow-up. All follow-ups past the first will always be their non-REV versions. Kaphwan can not move, crouch, jump or block as long as he remains in stance.",
    "2crev": "Cannot be cancelled on block Wins against jumping attacks, standing normals and REV Blows Combos starting with it have additional scaling An alternative anti-air for when you don\u2019t have charge. It has alright range, about the same as his flashkicks. It has feint cancel links on the ground, giving you inherent charge time.",
    "4ab": "Kaphwan throws the opponent judo-style to the ground. Safe jumps off of this throw have to be manually timed One forward dash leaves you +20 and in throw range If you throw the opponent into the corner, you can set up a meaty Low Dodge Attack or 236D by frame-killing with feint or far A, respectively Deceptively good okizeme for a back throw, especially if you throw the opponent into the corner.",
    "562bcor562drev": "Mimics the startup of Haki Kyaku ( 236K ). This is Kaphwan\u2019s only feint, use this whenever a combo requires a feint. Buffering a move out of it moves Kaphwan very slightly forward on startup; if you don't buffer a move, Kaphwan moves very slightly backward instead You can buffer a feint during the first hit of cl.D if you input it with neutral or forward, but NOT down",
    "5crev": "Universal overhead, wins against lows and throws On Counter Hit or Wild Punish, links to close/far B; meaty setups that Counter Hit let you link heavies here Kaphwan\u2019s low dodge differs from 6B in that it\u2019s safe, but has no combo options without Counter Hit or a meaty setup. In spite of its low reward, it\u2019s still a great move to use when you\u2019ve conditioned your opponent to tech the throw on wakeup or mash lows against your shimmy.",
    "6b": "Kaphwan overheads the opponent with his heel. Combos to far A and 2A on hit Spacing it makes it safe on block but removes your ability to combo afterward One of those high-risk, high-reward overheads. If you\u2019ve conditioned your opponent to block low, surprise them with this. Its hitstun and link to 2A allow you to charge a flashkick on reaction to a successful hit.",
    "ab": "Kaphwan shoves the opponent and kicks them down. After frame-killing with far A, hop in for an autotimed safe jump vs 5f reversals Alternatively, two forward dashes leaves you point blank and +11 Although this throw appears to throw the opponent too far away, Kaphwan recovers quickly, allowing you to immediately dash back in and keep threatening offense, even midscreen.",
    "cla": "Kaphwan's fastest attack Used to stop hops, in light confirms, or to perform his Combination Attack Chains from both crouching normals point-blank The standard fast jab is the main move Kaphwan will mash or stop hops with, especially since far A is only a frame slower. Useful to go into his Combination Attack as well, as this is Kaphwan's only way of comboing to his REV Blow from lights without spending REV.",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. cl.AC first hit is cancellable by any follow-ups. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender, during cl.AC only the second hit is REV Blow cancellable. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : First hit of the auto combo, cancellable by any follow-ups. cl.AC > C : A second hit cancellable by any ender, a nice way to increase the damage or your opponent REV meter. Unsafe on block and can be punished if the auto-combo was started too close to the opponent. D ender : Far D follow up, different frame data and damage, very unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Safest ender, Same move as his Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV) but +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Same move as his 2D, meaning it's very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "Two-hitting attack with special and target combo cancel options from the second hit Long activation range for a light means you can chain into it from either crouching normal or his close A Links to far B on hit, or 2C on Counter Hit / Wild Punish Cannot be feint cancelled, as target combo has higher input priority Another two-hit attack with great advantage whether it hits or is blocked. This second hit gives you inherent time to charge, making it much better on hit than your other lights thanks to the linking routes to flashkick brake.",
    "clbd": "A confirmable follow-up from close B. On hit, you can juggle light flashkick or Redline/Hidden Gear An easy combo off of close B, good for when you don\u2019t want to use resources. Like the first command combo, you can tack on a flashkick at the end, but it\u2019s also not bad to go for the tech-chase.",
    "clc": "Wild Punish +26 (+29 when feint cancelled) Slightly slower than 2C, and shorter activation range than close D, but is your best heavy on hit and block Links to far B on hit, even without feint cancel Kaphwan's strongest heavy button by many metrics, although its use is limited by its proximity requirement. On hit when feint cancelled, links into far C, which leads into all your strongest combos, or even sweep for a surprise knockdown. It also frame traps to almost every other normal you have on block.",
    "cld": "Wild Punish +24 (+28 when feint cancelled) Forces stand on hit Long proximity range allows it to work just outside of throw range The first hit reaches VERY high, making it useful as a situational anti-air Cancels to supers on either hit, but to specials on the second hit only A pretty good offensive button, as they go, thanks to its two hits, high vertical hitbox, and consistent link on feint cancel giving you a route to 2C for charge-building. Thanks to reaching just beyond throw range, it's a powerful button in shimmies. On hit against airborne opponents, it will not cancel to special moves due to the second hit whiffing. This allows you to option-select special cancels, as you won't whiff a special move when tagging a hop or backdash. This utility along with its other strengths arguably make Kaphwan's cl.D his most reliable combo starter.",
    "cra": "Tied with far A for Kaphwan\u2019s fastest non-proximity attack, but has inherent charging and better chains at the expense of damage Mashable A jack-of-all-trades button. While having worse damage than far A, and worse range than far B, its built-in charge and chains to either far normal make it useful both as combo filler and to mash.",
    "crb": "Kaphwan\u2019s only low combo starter Chains into either close light when done point blank You can chain two lights into far B and still be in range for flashkick A shockingly abusable normal, owing to its range, inherent charge and combo potential. Use it after an empty jump, or to catch people walking back in neutral or pressure.",
    "crc": "Wild Punish +16 (+22 when feint cancelled) For a far heavy, great range and speed, but not great on block Looks like a low, but hits mid Great combo filler, great whiff punish, great at a lot of things due to its speed and reach. This is also your most reliable link from a feint-cancelled close heavy, as it\u2019s faster than far C while also hitting crouch (albeit with a smidge less damage and range). Having it blocked ends your turn unless you spend REV, however, so be mindful of your resources when using it in neutral.",
    "crd": "Kaphwan\u2019s longest-ranged attack to come out in less than 10 frames Moves Kaphwan forward on startup On Wild Punish, puts opponents in an OTG state. Your damage-optimal OTG is 214D, which will always reach in this scenario Like Kaphwan\u2019s other buttons, his sweep is also faster than average, which makes it great as a situational punish or surprise low. Unfortunately, also like his other buttons, it moves Kaphwan forward, making spacing it to avoid a punish more difficult. Use sparingly.",
    "fa": "Chains from other lights. Technically chains to itself, but this chain whiffs more often than not Unusually high damage for a far A Standard hop-checking punch attack with decent range and 5f startup, making it your optimal button to mash. At 40 damage, it's one of the highest-damaging A normals in the game despite its fast startup. The only downside is its lack of chaining options compared to 2A. You can't charge while pressing it, but once you've practiced charge buffering, you can get around that.",
    "fb": "Deceptively large range and fast for a far B Kaphwan's most consistent cancel to heavy flashkick brake Moves Kaphwan forward a bit on startup, but if you don't buffer anything during the recovery, he will move back to where he started the move This normal can do so much that it acts as Kaphwan's swiss army knife. Not only is it fast for a far B, its hitbox is huge, it chains easily from his crouching lights, it special cancels, and with charge buffering, it allows you to do three lights to flashkick, even from two 2Bs. At nearly all ranges, it creates a spacing trap into far C (although crouching normals can check it). There's almost nothing this normal can't do, really, though it's worse on whiff than your average light normal. On Counter Hit or Wild Punish, it combos to 214BD, making it incredible as a counterpoke if you have REV to spare.",
    "fc": "Wild Punish +19 (+23 when feint cancelled) Kaphwan's longest-ranged normal that still special cancels Loses some range vs crouching characters, causing the feint cancel link from close C to whiff on crouch A snappy, decently-ranged kick with good size and forward movement, great for whiff punishes, controlling space, and the like. It's kind of like Terry's far C. Most notable about this normal is its whopping 90 damage and ability to combo to flashkick brake at max range (unlike 2C), but with the caveat of whiffing on crouch past a certain range. As a result, you will need to forgo this normal in favor of 2C in combos on a crouching opponent.",
    "fd": "Wild Punish +22 Fast, two-hitting and with huge range on the second kick, featuring target combo and super cancel options The second kick always comes out, making it a good surprise poke, though it's awful on whiff Fast for a far heavy while also having great range, this double kick is your best heavy for scoring Wild Punishes. Tag them with this if you see them whiff something laggy. Take care not to use this button too recklessly, as it's pretty bad on whiff itself while also being vulnerable to hops. If both hits are blocked, Kaphwan will always be in range to get mixed up by the opponent, or outright punished if they have a grab super. If you\u2019re trying to space out an opponent, use his other normals instead.",
    "fd6d": "A confirmable follow-up from far D. On hit, you can juggle light flashkick or Redline/Hidden Gear This command combo gives you a way to stretch out your damage after a far D on normal hit. While you do get a flashkick at the end, you can alternatively forgo the flashkick and instead tech-chase off the knockdown to stay in.",
    "hold28bdbd": "Kaphwan\u2019s flashkick. ~40f charge time for all versions All versions have invincibility to air attacks on startup Like all anti-air specials, all versions are Counter Hit punishable on recovery if Kaphwan doesn\u2019t brake them One of Kaphwan\u2019s other defining specials, an anti-air kick. This is a charge move, which carries both benefits (like reaction punishes) and downsides (like having to charge it). Nonetheless, it is important to Kaphwan\u2019s defense and combos alike. Knowing which version to use, and when, is key to playing the character at his best. Light version ([2]8B) : Kaphwan\u2019s main invincible reversal, and his fastest special move overall. Fully invincible on startup Cannot be braked nor cancelled to the follow-up, making it extremely punishable Quick enough to tack on at the end of his Command Combos or various juggles Heavy version ([2]8D) : Kaphwan\u2019s main anti-air. Only the second hit launches on Counter Hit Can be followed up with a second kick if you don\u2019t brake it, input with 6D When braked, launches on hit and is safe on block, but recovers 12 frames slower on whiff REV version ([2]8BD) Nearly identical properties to the Heavy version, right down to the damage and optional follow-up Launches higher when braked than the Heavy version, enabling unique juggles Recovers 10 frames slower on whiff only",
    "j214bdbd": "Kaphwan performs a series of aerial kicks. All versions can be done from a neutral or forward jump All versions float in the air until recovery Due to the height restriction, no version can hit crouching characters unless they extend a hurtbox upward (i.e. Rock 2C) For all versions, the second hit launches on Counter Hit While Hishou Kyaku is mainly meant for grounded opponents, Kuusen Kyaku is used to attack airborne ones. This forward kicking series works seamlessly from his j.C, and while it's mostly combo filler, it is fast enough to be used as an air-to-air as well. The damage is pretty good, especially on the Heavy and REV versions. Light version (j.214B) : A quick two kicks. Only situationally useful. The fastest version, but does the least total damage Juggles from it into super or any version of flashkick are possible in the corner, but are height-dependent Heavy version (j.214D) : Cancel j.C into this for impressive damage. The highest-damage air special Kaphwan has without using REV You can brake this move after the second kick, comboing to 236K or air super if you're high enough It's very difficult to juggle after this, as you need the last kick to hit late and you need corner REV version (j.214BD) : Generally REV Accel\u2019d into for optimal damage. Unlike the other versions, you can do this one from a backjump, but not a backdash Launches the opponent higher than the Heavy version, allowing for juggles on the ground even if you don't brake it Very unsafe if blocked on the ground, and can be ducked to become even more unsafe",
    "j236236bd": "Kaphwan unleashes a series of kicks at the opponent. Only the Redline version is invincible on startup Better damage than his grounded super, but also harder to land Can be juggled into off a flashkick brake, but requires a tricky TK (i.e. 2362369K) Though the Redline version ground bounces after the last hit, you can\u2019t actually combo from it Kaphwan\u2019s air super in CotW is not an aerial Houou Kyaku, but rather a brand-new move. It\u2019s decently quick, offering solid damage and okizeme, and has lots of ways to combo into it. While it has higher damage and better knockdown advantage than Kaphwan\u2019s grounded super, combos into it tend to do less damage, so both supers end up being pretty equal as enders. The Redline version has much better okizeme than the basic version, but only slightly better damage for twice the cost. It\u2019s better to just use the basic version in combos unless the extra damage will win the round.",
    "j236bdbd": "A multi-hitting divekick that functions wholly differently depending on the version used. All versions can be done from a forward or neutral jump, and can be done surprisingly low to the ground Kaphwan briefly stalls in the air before divekicking, allowing them to bait and punish certain anti-airs (such as High Dodge Attacks) Frame advantages on Counter Hit / Wild Punish do not carry over past the first hit This divekick's main use is for baiting anti-airs or to punish the opponent from a neutral jump. It means Kaphwan doesn't appear to give up as much space by neutral jumping than he appears. Light version (j.236B) : Kaphwan kicks the opponent in the face repeatedly. The damage is pretty decent, but the okizeme is not Horrendous on block, even with the backward bounce Combos from j.A on Counter Hit, or j.214D brake on normal hit Heavy version (j.236D) : Kaphwan keeps kicking downward until he lands. Safe, but finnicky on hit and block On grounded hit or block, Kaphwan will always land in throw range If you Counter Hit or Wild Punish a grounded opponent with the first hit ONLY, Kaphwan can link 2A or far A to pick up a combo REV version (j.236BD) : On hit, Kaphwan kicks the opponent up and into a series of spinning kicks. On block, it functions just like the Heavy version. Unlike the other versions, you can do this one from a backjump, but not a backdash If you jump over the opponent then do this version, it will auto-correct in the opponent's direction on startup Impressive damage, but it does all of its damage on the final hit The only way to combo from this move is to REV Accel into j.214BD or REV Blow",
    "ja": "Kaphwan\u2019s fastest and most active air normal Struggles to hit grounded opponents A quick and simple air normal, your best option as a reactive air-to-air. You have combo options on Counter Hit thanks to its special cancel.",
    "jb": "Can cross up Has a target combo follow-up during a full jump The universal jumping normal that hits on both sides of Kaphwan. An essential tool for okizeme, as it can pressure multiple wakeup rolls simultaneously with proper setup.",
    "jba": "Can only do during a full jump Requires Counter Hit or Wild Punish to work on an airborne opponent Pretty niche use. Helps you convert from a higher-up j.B vs grounded opponents, or against an airborne opponent, pops them up slightly higher so you can do a left-right mix on the opponent\u2019s reset.",
    "jc": "A strong air normal that juggles and special cancels Worse startup and range than Kaphwan\u2019s other air normals Kaphwan\u2019s j.C is interesting. You can cancel and combo out of it like a grounded normal, or juggle from it to something on the ground, like a flashkick. Any way you land it and follow it up, is insanely rewarding. The problem is that it\u2019s much more difficult to hit than it looks, due to its poor reach and startup. You\u2019ll mainly look to hit it off a Counter Hit launch (such as from 214B), which leads to combos that do absurd damage, especially in the corner.",
    "jd": "Kaphwan\u2019s longest-ranged air normal Has a second hit that only hits cross-up Kaphwan\u2019s primary jump-in, with good range as well. On top of starting combos after jumping in, its reach also gives it use as a retreating air-to-air. There is also a second hit that only hits cross-up, similar to Dong Hwan\u2019s Mule Kick. While it\u2019s fantastic for that purpose, you\u2019ll want a proper setup for it due to taking 14 frames to come out. Without a hard knockdown or the corner, j.B is much easier to use a cross-up."
  },
  "mai_shiranui": {
    "214acac": "Light version won't normally combo. Causes a launch on Counter Hit. Heavy version comboes from Lights. Rev version combos from a counter hit light attack Only the Light version deletes projectiles. Rev version frame traps from lights into a full combo The heavy version is Mai's best meterless combo ender Mai's classic fiery move. The heavy version is her main light confirm and a great ender, whilst the Rev version starts all her combos. The light version has seen better days, not that good or safe, but still lets here delete projectiles, and fish for counterhits from a surprisingly long distance just make sure it's spaced, since at -5 it can still be punished. It can lead to a lot of damage midscreen on CH, so it's a good frametrap or DP punish.",
    "214bdbd": "Despite looks, they're all mids. Heavy version comboes only after clD. Links into 2A on hit. Light and Heavy become +11 on Counter Hit and link with farC. Heavy version and Rev versions are both +2 on block. Another great tool in her arsenal, mainly for pressure and for Counter Hit combos. On Counter, both versions become high damage starters, as she gets a link into far C or 2C. The heavy version frame traps from close D and leaves Mai plus, which can lead to looping pressure that requires a Guard Cancel or risky mashing to escape. Heavy Sayo Chidori in particular is one of Mai's strongest tools and one of the strongest specials in the game, as it is a strong cancel option that is plus +2 on block and adds to the mental stack of the opponent while blocking Mai. The counter play to this move is also quite niche as the best counterplay is to Just Defend the hit, but at 23 Frames of startup, most opponents will not be ready to react correctly. She can also mix it up with her Light Sayo Chidori to throw off their timing The Rev version frame traps from f.C and c.C, but on block it is fairly easy to just reaction Hyper Defense it. Due to this it's mostly reserved for combo extensions, but there's it's present in basically every metered combo Mai has.",
    "236236a": "Fully invulnerable, but with a rather terrible hitbox for a reversal. Doesn't hit behind her either. The highest damage an Ignition Gear can have. Mai's super reversal with a godawful hitbox. Do not use this if you can help it, but it is an option. For combos it's very easy to juggle into, easier than 236236B/D, but also less rewarding. Great move to use against air Rev Blows or special moves that approach from high jump arcs due to the long active frames and high hitbox.",
    "236236bj236236b": "Mai's best general use super. Has obscene damage, works in ranged confirms from light and has decent corner carry. Mai's best and highest damage combo ender, especially in the corner. Probably what most of your meter should go into. Has a tendency to not connect all hits on airborne opponents, needs to be manually delayed in some combos.\nA new, faster air version. Basically worthless against grounded opponents, but part of Mai's optimal routing in juggles as an instant air super, where its possible to combo after for either resource gain or a second super.",
    "236236c": "Fully invulnerable and as fast as her DP. Still doesn't hit behind her. Hits big and is very combo friendly, but there is no reason to use it when 236236D or 236236Rev are available. Still probably Mai's best reversal on wakeup.",
    "236236dj236236d": "Fully Invul. Can be braked on hit. Ranged reversal (sadly not great against fireballs) and more damaging combo tool than Redline Kagerou. Can be braked, when you have this as your starter (eg.: as a reversal) you get to do marginally higher damage than you otherwise would while building a bit of Power Gauge back by dumping your entire Rev gauge into a combo, so if you're going for a kill combo and have Rev to spare it's useful.\nFully Invul. A jumpscare waiting to happen. Unless you're trying to bait anti-airs in style this is probably a terrible usage of meter, as it does low damage and can't be followed up with a second super due to cost.",
    "236236rev": "Fully invulnerable. Available only in SPG. Potentially the worst Hidden gear in the game, the hitbox on the move is much, MUCH smaller than most, which can cause drops in baffling situations, like after two lights, and utterly kills any serious uses in neutral. Will usually connect off of DP breaks, but proceed with caution. When it connects it's as good as any but getting it to actually connect in a combo is very annoying",
    "236acac": "Light travels slow but has significant recovery; heavy travels fast with less recovery Rev version throws 2 fans. Acting as 2 EX projectiles. Mai's classic fan throw, as always a good zoning option, and the Rev version is great at keeping her plus. Not good as an approach projectile by itself, but the Rev version can be cancelled into [2]9AC, which works as a surprise approach occasionally.",
    "236bdbd": "Light version comboes from lights. Heavy and Rev versions can be braked. Light and Heavy do fewer hits on higher height hits. [Possible bug] Be aware that this move is not effected by the SNK \"longcut\" (2369X) and will often be interpreted as Hana Arashi(623K) by the input reader when done out of a forward dash. Counterhitting with the elbow hitbox will lead to a launch Another important move in Mai's kit, most of her combos require at least one of these. Can be made safe with a brake but never plus.The heavy version on counterhit leaves Mai at +6, which allows a link into close C. Important note about the light version: In 99% of the time when ending your combos meterlessly, using 214C is always the better option than 236B. 214C has better damage and better oki on hit, on top of being much safer if you miss your confirm. The only reason to use 236B is to get SLIGHTLY more corner carry which in most cases is not worth the damage and oki sacrifice.",
    "2a": "Hitbox is shorter than the animation. Links after 214D. 6 frame start up 2A which is uncommon, but has more range to compensate A decent poke held back by Mai's deceptive animations, it will look like it hit but won't.",
    "2b": "Super cancelable Her main low, needs to be chained into other lights to get a confirm. Crouching B will link into Far B very consistently on a standing hit. In Ver.1.7.2 allows Mai to link 2 hits of crouching B into a far A",
    "2c": "Seriously lacks range, meaning it's basically just a combo tool, where it's important for linking out of c.B. +1 on feint cancel but Mai has a hard time enforcing anything off of it outside of lights that lead to little damage. Commonly used in Mai's optimal damage combos for reversal punishes",
    "2crev": "Moves her forward. Decent ranged 2CRev, but the forward movement leaves Mai punishable 99% of the time if it isn't cancelled. The hitbox is also bad for anti-airing, honestly do not bother outside of desperate reads in neutral.",
    "2d": "A sweep with great range and fast start up.",
    "3d": "Long range 2 hitting command normal low Can be canceled into from close C but becomes a mid. Links into cl.C on Counter Hits but the link is extremely range dependent. Great option to use after REV Sayo Chidori and REV Kachosen to catch walkback Reworked after its last appearance. Safe on block, fast and overall a great move that can fish for Counters. Note: Despite being a great move in Mai's kit. This move is extremely weak to hops and jumps. Try using it only when you know the opponent will stay grounded.",
    "4ab": "Worse oki but she still has the advantage. Can wave dash to make said advantage better.",
    "5crev": "Beats Lows and Throws. Great range for an overhead but it will get blocked a majority of the time due to its slow speed, that being said Mai still appreciates any tool that can punish permanent crouchblocking in neutral. Can be plus enough as a meaty to link out of but good luck landing that against anyone that's not literally asleep.",
    "623bdbd": "Can hit behind her but tends to whiff crossups. Her classic DP, making its return after decades since the last SNK game with it. Gets her combos started from Lights and Heavies, and works as a combo extender/ender, Mai's best extender off lights if she can't afford c.B > 214AC. Its bad against crossups, so knowing how to crosscut DP is a fairly useful skill for using it properly. Besides that, brake hitbox is decent, and the full move goes quite high, but with comparatively poor horizontal reach.",
    "ab": "Gives her plenty oki to get her game going.Can set up a meaty +6 overhead anywhere on screen by doing a forward hop frame kill.In the corner you can do a neutral hop frame kill into light fireball to get a meaty +11 fireball, but this setup loses to reversals and most light DPs.",
    "cd": "Faster than average for a REV Blow, but stubby. It'd be a great anti air but crossups tend to beat it.",
    "cla": "Mai's fastest move. Chains into itself. One of her most important moves, especially for crossup confirms.",
    "clb": "Pretty much Mai's best pressure normal. +3 on block as standard and a whopping +8 on hit, meaning it can link to 2C and lead into all of Mai's highest damage routes. Has unusually high pushback on both hit and block. which can cause 2C to Whiff completely ( especially on standing opponents). Only light normal that combos into REV Ryuunbu, very strong option to chain into from lights but only point blank",
    "clc": "Starts her Command Combo. On counter hit, can be comboed into itself with a feint. Frame traps and combos after a blocked close D feint A cancel into REV Ryuunbu starts most of her combos and is a very safe ender Very generous proximity range.this is Mai's main heavy confirm; you will use it a lot. Keen opponents can hyper-defend the second hit on a hard read. Great cancel options with the best one being REV Sayo Chidori for a true frame trap and plus frames on block. Has another strong cancel option into heavy sayo chidori( For plus frames) and light ryuunbu( for catching back dashes or jumps)",
    "clc3d3d6dcd": "Last hit is an Overhead. Rarely used due to its high risk and low reward, but a good option to close out rounds as a knowledge check.\nOnly available during S.P.G., replaces the last hit with her Rev Blow.",
    "cld": "Mai's main normal for feint cancels, but it's so painfully slow it can't even connect after a jumpin and leaves a massive gap if you want to dash in and reset pressure with it.Close D is only +2 on feint cancel block, quite lower than other Close D buttons, but it leaves Mai in range for her 6 frame Close C. As such it's best as a meaty or a punish route filler when it's confirmed. Meterlessly frame traps if canceled to heavy Sayo Chidori and Mai's only way to frame trap 5-frame lights using heavy Chidori. Leaves Mai in range to connect a 2B which is very strong to catch walk backs or hops",
    "fa": "One of her best normals and potentially the best Far A in the game.Mai's main ender when chaining lights. Great button to use after Heavy Sayo Chidori or or the REV version to frame trap.On counter hit you can go into a full combo using REV ryunbuu, or buffer into a safe ender with light Sayo Chidori or a Fan fireball. Cancelling into REV ryuunbu is a very strong option that is low risk but high reward. Will always link from a 2B after any range after patch 3.00",
    "fb": "Whiffs on Crouching opponents from long ranges. Super cancelable Extremely strong and opressive neutral button Great range but the lack of cancels means you won't see it used often. This button is a great poke in neutral that is quite hard to deal with for the opponent due to the fast start up and low recovery frames. This move also always connects after any light attack if the opponent is standing. A tip range 2B will always link into Far B which helps her dash low confirms greatly. Due to the unique and elevated hitbox angle of this button. Far B can be an extremely strong option to catch forward dashes, backdashes, and most importantly, hops after ending block strings",
    "fc": "Whiffs on Crouching opponents from long ranges. Great range and starts her big combos with ease. Its speed and weakness to crouching are the drawbacks you need to remember when using it. Far C should always be canceled into something, as the frame data on both hit and block are very bad. Safe cancels can be into Light Sayo chidori or a Fireball. If the opponent is conditioned to block. Mai can start canceling Far C into Heavy Sayo chidori to start some offense and pressure. Overall a great button with alot of cancel options and uses.",
    "fd": "Super cancelable with a generous window for confirming Mai takes a step forward and goes for a long horizontal kick. Mai's longest normal and a great button in neutral, great for fishing for Counter Hits and Wild Punishes. However, it's -5 with little pushback and lots of forward movement, which means it's actually punishable more often than not by far lights (or Rock's 2C which can easily lose you the round). Be very careful with spacing it.",
    "hold2789acac": "Light, Heavy and Rev change trajectory and speed. 7 and 8 jump back whilst 9 jumps forward. Mai's command jump, gives her the unique ability to do an air special from a back jump without it being a Rev Art. Its charge time is slower than others, but its shortened for Rev Accel cancels into it.",
    "hold2789acacac": "Light and Heavy have different angles but same speed. If done after the Rev versions, it moves faster. The dive, can be death for you if used wrong. Aim for the legs to make it safe on block, or plus but highly pushed out on the EX version.",
    "j214acac": "Light and Heavy keep their angles from the follow up version. Rev versions act like the Heavy. Non-enhanced versions can never be plus. Launches on grounded and aerial counter hit, and knocks down on normal hit. Standalone versions of the dive, nothing different about them besides being cancellable into from j.A. This one is more immediately available compared to its alternative, so it's used to cover ground to escape the corner or similarly mess with anti-airs, and its launch on Counter Hit gives way to Mai's best corner combos. It can be used against jumping guard cancels or after a counter hit air-to-air j.A, but is fairly slow and unreliable depending on the oponent's height, so Rev Blow is better when available.",
    "j2b": "Active hitbox till landing Can be punished on hit. Can be canceled into j214AC and air rev blow. Can change air trajectory slightly Possibly the worst version of this move, there is no hiding it. Use with caution.",
    "j7nearthewall": "Can do air Normals and j214AC afterwards. Her classic walljump, same as usual. Mai jumps off the corner wall extremely high in the air( some times going off screen) at the opponent. The high jump arc can be quite difficult to anti-air if the opponent is not expecting it. A great way to get out of the corner, but don't overuse it much as it is very easy for the opponent to air to air if you become too predicatble.",
    "ja": "Air to air with great range,large active frame count, and speed. Can be followed up with a cancel to divekick, Rev Blow, or air super to bait JD or combo on counterhit/WP. Overall an extremely important Aerial normal for Mai and one of the best air to airs in the game.",
    "jab": "A great air throw that let's her start her pressure.A great option to counter Air rev blows.",
    "jb": "Can hit as a crossup. Her main crossup button, does its job well.",
    "jc": "Good for air to airs and jump ins.",
    "jcd": "Much better than her grounded Rev Blow. Has a decent air-to-air hitbox and is very easy to combo into off of Rev DP or Divekick. With precise cancel timing out of her Rev Specials, it's possible to only land the first hit, which sends the opponent absurdly high into the air, which enables some of Mai's strongest corner routing.",
    "jd": "More horizontal range than the other buttons. 5 active frames, making it great for safe jumps."
  },
  "marco_rodrigues": {
    "214p": "Counter window is active at frame 3 (needs testing) Does not work against projectiles. A and AC version counters highs and mids. C version counters lows. A dash that triggers a counter if Marco gets hit during it. Can be used to make your opponent second guess their buttons, but this is very risky as it leaves Marco wide open to an easy Wild Punish combo if it doesn't work. Particularly useful against Rev Blows; if you are doing a normal and you confirm that they are armoring it with a Rev Blow, cancelling into the A version will counter the Rev Blow with a counter hit, which allows for a juggle combo. Note that since different characters have different start-up times for their Rev Blow, the timing will always be slightly different. The AC version can be cancelled into other Rev Arts on a successful counter, notably 623AC > Brake, allowing for further damage. Note that on counter hit you do not get a special launch. It can also be cancelled into from 236AC to get juggles on hit and plus frames on block. This is an expensive but powerful option.",
    "236236a": "Hits around frame 19 at full screen Super fireball. Extremely easy to juggle into due to its range and speed. Beats any other non-super projectile in the game, giving Marco a much needed edge in fireball wars. Great for quick light conversions into a hard knockdown.",
    "236236b": "Super uppercut. Can be used as a reversal, which could be useful if it will kill; otherwise 623A is more effective since its faster. Harder to combo into than 236236A due to its lack of range but is slightly stronger and works well in juggles. Can lose hits if it hits too high.",
    "236236c": "Multi hitting super fireball. Basically, just stronger than the A version. The chip damage is about the same as the A version, so if you're going for a chip kill, just stick with that.",
    "236236d": "Double super uppercut. Although stronger than 236236C, the lack of horizontal range and the tendency to lose hits if it hits too high up makes this the worse option usually when you want to cash out. The second uppercut also doesn't come out on block so Just Defending it is not more difficult than the B version. You can brake after the first uppercut and get a juggle combo, but this is only good for disrespecting your opponent.",
    "236236rev": "Tackle that goes about 1/3 of the screen forward. Enters cinematic automatically on hit. Very fast, making it easy to combo into. Although linking two level 1 supers is usually stronger, it's still worth it to combo into this when given the opportunity to reset your REV guage. Oki situation afterwards is not very good, however.",
    "236k": "A series of flying kicks. The B version is a forward advancing flying kick that leads into a short combo on hit. Fast and combos reliably from all light normals except far A. Works well as a juggle ender as well. Can be used as a jump scare in neutral; works well against people who hop a lot, but is unsafe if blocked on the ground. This move can be braked before the kick connects, making the move act as a sort of dash. Note that if the opponent is too close (a little shorter than round start distance), the kick will automatically come out and it won't be able to be braked. The D version is an upward knee followed by a downwards kick. Combo filler mainly. When braked, Marco skips the downward kick and recovers faster, allowing for juggles. This combos from 2A at all ranges but only connects on standing opponents. 2C forces stand, so hit confirms from that normal are guaranteed. The BD version looks similar to the B version, but is faster and applies a hard knockdown. The corner carry and hard knockdown is very good for Marco, making this your preferred combo ender. Combos reliably from all lights, including far A. You can brake in the middle of the kicking animation, skipping the last kick. On block this leaves you at only -2, making this a useful neutral skip option. You can also use the brake version for some corner juggle extensions.",
    "236p": "Classic fireball. Important part of Marco's neutral game. Marco's travel relatively fast, making them primarily useful for zoning and checking movement; not so much starting offense. The fireball appears around Marco's hand, so if you want better advantage on hit or block than listed, the opponent must be spaced somewhere in front of Marco's hand. Because of this, it's not the best cancel on block, however, the A version frame traps from lights and the C version frame traps from heavies, which can be useful. Unfortunately, even if they counter hit, you are too far away to get a conversion. The AC version acts as a close range hit instead of a projectile, causing a launch. Its primary use is to be cancelled into 214AC to dash forward. On hit you get to continue the combo. On block Marco is left +3, allowing him to extend his pressure. Cancelling this from C normals is a frame trap, and if it counter hits, Marco is able to continue the juggle without cancelling into 214AC.",
    "2a": "Standard low jab. Combos from 6A consistently if close enough. Chains into all light normals. Combos into all 236K and 623P versions.",
    "2b": "Go-to normal for low mix-ups. Has a bit less range than most 2Bs, though that's somewhat mitigated by Marco's fast dash. Chains into all light normals, which you'll want to do to get a combo into 623P or 236K.",
    "2bcor2drev": "Feints 236B. There is a smear effect on this feint which can make it useful for baiting a response, but is still overall overshadowed by 6+Feint.",
    "2c": "Shoto-style Mexican uppercut. This normal sets Marco apart from the rest of the cast somewhat: while most characters have at least one fast heavy normal on their close buttons, Marco's fast heavy normal comes from this button, which allows him to get some heavy confirms at more ranges than others due to a lack of a proximity requirement, such as a spaced out hop or some punishes on block. The speed also allows for links from close B, meaty 6A, and cl.D xx 6+Feint. This is an important normal to get used to using. The recovery is quite high, so make sure to cancel it always. Avoid using this in neutral as it's also death on whiff. On counter hit, if you cancel into 6+Feint, you can link another 2C. Has some vertical range allowing it to be used as an anti-air, however this is almost always overshadowed by High Dodge or 623C. On hit this forces stand, which allows for guaranteed combos into 236D.",
    "2crev": "Average ranged High Dodge. Range is slightly shorter than Far C. At closer ranges, a cancel into 623C is reliable; at further ranges, you'll have to use 236AC or 236BD. When used as an anti air, cancelling into 623C~Brake is consistent at most ranges and safe when it isn't. 236D~Brake is consistent at all ranges but is unsafe if the High Dodge doesn't hit as a Wild Punish or counter hit.",
    "2d": "Your sweep. Range is just a bit larger than far C. Solid whiff punish tool, but unsafe.",
    "4ab": "Less advantage than his forward throw. Can still get oki, but cannot set up a safe jump.",
    "5bcor5drev": "Feints Hold/Release P. Hold/Release P is not a terribly scary move so this likely will see little use.",
    "5crev": "Has further range than most but one of the slowest low dodge attacks at 25 start up frames. Generally overshadowed by 6A as an offensive tool but is safer on block.",
    "623p": "() = damage value if the attack hits high up (outside of brake range) Classic uppercut. A version is fast and fully invincible. Use this as your meterless reversal. C version has upper body invincibility making it great as an anti-air. When braked (which you have no good reason not to, ever), this acts as a safe on block combo starter as well. leading into great damage anywhere on screen. Its speed makes it easy to combo into from any normal, although the short horizontal range may make it whiff at some ranges. The AC version acts almost identically to the C version but starts up one frame faster and can be used to extend juggles after 623C>Brake has already been used. It also launches slightly higher. When the C and AC versions counter hit, the opponent gets launched very high in the air, allowing Marco to dash up and use the same uppercut again. The C and AC versions, when braked, have an additional 4 frames of recovery on whiff.",
    "6a": "Overhead elbow. One of Marco's most important tools for opening up the opponent. Usually allows for links into 2A, range permitting. If done meaty, you can hit later in the active frames making the move up to +7, allowing for stronger links into 2C. While 6A is a very threatening normal for the opponent to deal with, it does not come without risk has 6A will always be punishable if it happen to be blocked due to the fact that it can only be -6 on block at best with little pushback therefore predictable use of this move can potentially backfire heavily.",
    "6bcor6drev": "Feints 236P. Has the least total duration of all of Marco's feints so this will be the one you primarily use. Notably, unlike a lot of other projectile feints, Marco's still has the flash of the projectile appear, which makes it, in theory, more effective at baiting a reaction from your opponent.",
    "abor6ab": "Marco has enough time to get up close after this throw for oki, even without wavedashing.",
    "cla": "Marco's fastest normal. Combos into 623C for good return.",
    "clb": "Your go-to light normal for pressure and hitconfirms. Frame traps and links into 2C.",
    "clc": "Two hitting heavy close normal. Like most multi-hitting normals, extra frame advantage from counter hit and Wild Punish carries over to the second hit, and also only counts as 1 hit for damage scaling purposes. Easy to hit confirm with. but somewhat slow making it hard to confirm off of hops. Make sure to cancel into a safe special on block. Cannot be made plus on block with feints.",
    "clcdd": "Marco combos into his Far D and Low Dodge Attack. Considering how much damage and block pressure Marco can normally get from cancelling his close C, this Target Combo is completely overshadowed by his other options. The Low Dodge portion of this Target Combo does not hit overhead.",
    "cld": "Party starter. 6+Feint makes this normal plus on block and hit (+3 and +6 respectively), allowing for strong combos and pressure. At 10f start-up however, it's a bit slower than most other party starter close normals, making it hard to confirm off of hops.",
    "db": "Marco cancels the active frames of his far D. Essentially an extra feint. Moves forward quickly which might catch some opponents off guard, but overall not a move that's very good on paper.",
    "fa": "Standard low commitment jab. Has more range than 2A, but has slightly less hitstun, meaning it cannot combo into 236B in any circumstance. Combos into your 623P specials and 236BD just fine however, which is usually more desired.",
    "fb": "Fantastic range for a light normal. Can cancel into super for some quick cash outs. In return for its range, has a bit more commitment than your A normals, having a total duration of 27 frames.",
    "fc": "Unlike some other Far Cs, Marco's is not special cancellable, but it makes up for it with its massive range (and it can still be used to raw confirm a super), so it's your best normal for poking and whiff punishing. It is also absurdly quick to recover, making it difficult to whiff punish even when the opponent is expecting it.",
    "fd": "High hitting kick. Can anti air hops pre-emptively but is overall very committal, especially since Marco moves forward doing this, making him more prone to whiff punishes. While it has more range than far C usually, it whiffs on crouchers at tip ranges making it worse at whiff punishing crouching moves. Leads to a full combo on counterhit, usually with 2C. Has a feint option to have some tricky movement (See Far Heavy Kick (Feint))",
    "holdkthenkhold": "Button must be held for at least 60 frames. Flying kick. This move is good at fishing for counter hits in neutral and is very rewarding if done so. It's unsafe if done up close but properly spaced you can be up to 0 on block. You run into the same problems with sacrificing some of your buttons if you're planning on using this however, especially if you're fishing with the BD version. It also doesn't grant anything significant on regular hit. BD version flies further and is faster, making it more effective as a jump scare option.",
    "holdpthenphold": "Button must be held for at least 60 frames. Often referred to as either \"TAP\" or \"Zonk\" by the community after the similar hold-then-release moves of Street Fighter's Boxer and Cody respectively. Lunge punch. In order to use this, you must hold one or both of the punch buttons to charge it, then release to execute the move, which means sacrificing a few of your most important buttons and universal options such as throw or dodge at any given time. The move itself is a straightforward strike with not that much reward, so this move is rarely used outside of some optimized combos. The AC version is notable for being plus on block. On its own, it doesn't leave Marco at a range where he can realistically threaten with much, however, Accel chaining it into 214AC retains the +4 block advantage and leaves Marco point blank.",
    "ja": "High hitting punch. Has the same hit and blockstun to a heavy normal (based on personal experience, further testing required). Useful as an air-to-air and for mixups against JD. Whiffs on crouchers. Guarantees a link into 2C on hit no matter how high you hit it.",
    "jb": "Short kick. Marco's cross-up button. On hit, guarantees a close A.",
    "jba": "Cancels j.B into j.A. Just like how j.A works normally, this is +6 at worst on hit, guaranteeing a 2C. Useful if your j.B hits too high up. Still whiffs on crouchers, but most opponents will instinctively stand block when they see you take to the air anyway. Cannot be performed out of hop.",
    "jc": "Marco's most reliable jump in. Use for offense.",
    "jd": "Far reaching kick. The slower start-up means the button must be committed to a bit earlier than usual. The further horizontal range makes it useful for tagging opponents a bit further way. Can also be used defensively when used during a retreating hop."
  },
  "mr_big": {
    "214aca": "While in the 214AC stance, press A to launch the same followup attack Mr Big would get on a successful parry of a non-low attack, without the invulnerability. The exact cancel window is from frame 11 to 20 of the 214AC stance Safe on block Can start a combo if canceled into another Rev special Scales combos by an extra 15%, for a total of 40% (5% move, 20% REV Art, 15% extra).",
    "214acac": "Mr. Big adopts a defensive posture and taunts the opponent into attacking him. The light version blocks any non-projectile attack that does not hit low, even super attacks The heavy version blocks lows, and will not block any other kind of attack The Rev version will block any non-projectile attack. If a mid/high attack is blocked, the mid counterattack will be used. If a low attack is blocked, the low counterattack will be used. Mr. Big automatically follows up with an attack if he successfully parries an attack. This special automatic attack has 34 frames of full invulnerability with no recovery frames, allowing Mr. Big to immediately block if it counters an invincible, multi-hitting super. If an attack does not match the correct version of 214P used, it will hit as a Wild Punish starting on frame 3 (before this, it is a counterhit) The counterattack follow-ups can be manually activated, but these attacks will not have invulnerability and come with 1 less frame of advantage on hit. See the section below for more details",
    "214acc": "While in the 214AC stance, press C to launch the same followup attack Mr Big would get on a successful parry of a low attack, without the invulnerability. The exact cancel window is from frame 11 to 20 of the 214AC stance Safe on block Hits low and can start a combo if canceled into another Rev special. Scales combos by an extra 15%, for a total of 40% (5% move, 20% REV Art, 15% extra).",
    "214ap": "Press a punch button while in the parry stance to launch the same follow-up attack Mr Big would get on a successful 214A parry, but without the invulnerability. The exact cancel window is from frame 11 to 15 during the 214A stance Unsafe on block at close range, good spacing will make punishes difficult or impossible",
    "214bdbd": "A rapid, 4-hit attack with short range. A versatile special attack with many different use cases. Light version (214B): Mr Big\u2019s fastest strike special. Used to end blockstrings safely and as combo filler, but can also be used as a light confirm. Mr Big\u2019s most reliable special attack for Guard Cancels at close range. Use 236B instead if the opponent is too far. Any special-cancelable normal can combo into this, if Mr Big is close enough. Can only start/extend a combo if it hits an airborne/juggled opponent, or as counterhit / Wild Punish. Heavy version (214D) Used to convert heavy attack hits into big damage. Slower and just barely unsafe on block, but launches the opponent just enough to start a combo on hit. Any special-cancelable heavy attack can combo into this, assuming the opponent is close enough. On hit, combos into 623C (brake). Rev version (214BD) Used as combo filler. Slower than the light version, but faster than the heavy version. Adds a fifth hit, but can be braked to stop on the fourth and remain safe on block. Although the final hit is unsafe on block, it can be canceled into another Rev special, keeping Mr Big safe at the cost of additional Rev meter.",
    "214cp": "Press a punch button during 214C to launch the same followup attack Mr Big would get on a successful 214C parry, but without the invulnerability. The exact cancel window is from frame 11 to 15 of the 214C stance Hits low. Unsafe at close range, good spacing will make punishes difficult or impossible",
    "236236ac": "Because this move spawns a projectile and is not a strike move \"connected\" to Mr. Big himself, JDing all of the hits of the move causes the extra JD stop frames to delay the blockstun of this move beyond normal amounts, resulting in a frame advantage for him to what would normally be about -30. This creates a win-win situation for Mr. Big if this is used as an anti-air: if it hits, it hits. If it gets JD'd, the opponent will have to JD all the remaining hits in order to not take damage, but doing so probably results in it being safe or even plus for Mr. Big.",
    "236236bd": "Spend super meter to launch a beefed-up command grab. Very high damage, fully invulnerable and can function as a reversal. Same range as 623K. Also great for use as a Guard Cancel during an opponent\u2019s blockstring, unless the opponent is using a long-range attack (e.g. Billy Far C blockstrings) The slower startup of the 1-bar version ( 236236B ) allows it to beat safejumps. For the extra meter spent, the 2-bar version ( 236236D ) is faster and deals more damage. Using it as a Guard Cancel on wakeup will scale the damage for no benefit at all (it is already active and invulnerable on frame 1), and should be avoided.",
    "236236rev": "Mr Big delivers a short, heavy kick. If it hits, a cinematic plays and the full damage is dealt. Like any other Hidden Gear, this costs 2 bars of super stock and is only accessible while in SPG, but deals massive damage and resets the user\u2019s Rev meter to 0% on hit, even if they were overheated at the time of activation. Short range Leaves Mr Big at approximately hop distance away from the opponent after the cinematic",
    "236acac": "Fire a projectile along the ground. Will not actually travel fullscreen unless Rev meter is spent. Light and heavy versions have a close-range hitbox that is only active for 2 frames with better damage and advantage on block than the projectile. It also knocks the opponent down on hit. The numbers in [brackets] represent values for the close-range hitbox (exact range is very slightly shorter than 1 forward hop). Light version (236A) Shortest range, will hit from slightly shorter than round start distance The short range hitbox will launch on counterhit, a projectile counterhit stumbles the opponent for 32 frames Heavy version (236C) Slower startup, but better damage, advantage on block/hit, and range (will hit from slightly further than round-start) The short range hitbox will launch on counterhit, a projectile counterhit stumbles the opponent for 35 frames Can be used as a frame-trap tool if canceled into from Close C Rev version (236AC) Fires a fullscreen projectile along the ground, but does not include the close-range hitbox that the normal versions have and will never launch on counterhit (stumbles opponent for 38 frames) Same startup as the light version with better damage on the projectile, and is always at least +2 on block",
    "236bdbd": "Soar straight forward into the opponent with a horizontal dive. Can be braked at any point after the first active frame to cancel the rest of the flight and immediately enter recovery frames Mostly very unsafe (can be mitigated with extremely accurate spacing, attempting to utilize this is not recommended), but has very long range and launches on counterhit. Launches on counterhit Can be used to bypass these projectiles by flying over/through it. Anything not listed here can hit Mr Big during the flight. Rock's Reppuken (All versions) Terry's Power Wave (A/C versions) B. Jenet's Baffles (All versions) Hokutomaru's Shuriken (Regular versions, the move's hitbox destroys the projectiles) Light version (236B) Fastest startup, but shortest range While 623K and 214K are better Guard Cancel options in many situations, 236B is a great option in its own right with powerful, niche applications where it can shine, such as to launch the counterhit recovery state of a braked anti-air (especially Kain flashkick) and Billy's Far C special cancels from long range Heavy version (236D) Slowest startup, better range than the light version Still fast enough to launch a braked anti-air if used as a guard cancel, but too slow to punish most other attacks Rev version (236BD) Same range as the heavy version, but slightly faster startup (still slower than the light version) Can be made safe on block (or start a combo on hit) by canceling into another Rev special, making it a solid option for approaching the opponent",
    "2bcor2drev": "Mimics the startup for Ground Blaster ( 236P ). This is Mr. Big's slowest feint.",
    "2crev": "Mr Big\u2019s crouching dodge attack, a universally available attack for low-profiling and anti-airing. Read more about the crouching dodge attack here. Has about as much horizontal range as Far D A grounded hit can combo into anything Far C would, a hit against an airborne opponent can be followed up with 623C",
    "4ab": "Mr Big quickly lifts the opponent over his head and throws them far behind him. Dashing twice will still leave Mr Big at point-blank range, but the advantage will be 0 on wakeup Throwing the opponent into the corner and hopping immediately after creates a +4 on wakeup situation, allowing 623K to hit an opponent on their first grabbable frame",
    "56bcor56drev": "Mimics the startup of California Romance ( 623P ). This is Mr Big\u2019s fastest feint, use this whenever a combo requires a feint.",
    "5crev": "Mr Big\u2019s standing dodge attack, a universally available overhead attack that jumps over lows and throws. Read more about the standing dodge attack here. Moves Mr Big forward by about half of the distance traveled by a forward hop Safe on block, but does not start a combo unless it hits as a meaty , counterhit or Wild Punish",
    "623acac": "Mr. Big leaps and swipes a baton upwards with his main arm. Used primarily as anti-air and combo filler. Does not launch on counterhit unless the first hit whiffs and the second hit lands as a counterhit, and 623A will not recover fast enough to start a combo in this situation. Light version (623A): This is not fully invulnerable during startup (or ever). Do not use this as a reversal. It is, however, immediately invulnerable to air attacks through all of its startup and first half of its active frames. Mr Big's fastest anti-air, and it hits twice Hits from slightly shorter than round-start distance, or the distance of a full jump Heavy version (623C): Adds a long dash before attacking. Despite retaining air-invulnerability, this is way too slow to be used as an anti-air, and so it is mostly used as combo filler. Can be braked at two different points: If braked during the startup dash on the ground, immediately enters recovery frames after 9 frames of air-invulnerable dashing Otherwise, can be braked right as the attack becomes active to stop Mr Big from going airborne and cancel the third hit. This is still barely punishable on block, unlike other braked anti-air specials, and has even more recovery on whiff. Rev version (623AC): Slightly slower than the light version, but with longer range. It is still fast enough to combo from any special-cancelable attack, making it an excellent tool for starting combos from lights. Has the same two brake points as the heavy version, but is actually safe on block Braked version launches the opponent higher than the braked heavy version, but is dangerous to whiff due to added recovery on whiff only",
    "623bdbd": "A fairly standard command grab with only two versions, normal and Rev. Only deals a little more damage than a normal grab, but still useful for the fact command grabs are inescapable once they hit. Has slightly more range compared to regular grabs. Mr Big\u2019s best guard cancel option in many situations, if the opponent is close enough Rev version starts a harshly-scaled combo on hit and can be canceled into other Rev specials. Can lead to big damage if Mr Big has super meter to spend.",
    "6a": "An overhead swing with one of the sticks. Used as a standing overhead to start a small combo. Unlike 5CR, does not require a counterhit / Wild Punish to start a combo Can be made safer (up to -4 and too far for a Close A to activate) by using a meaty setup. A counterhit, meaty 6A can combo into Far D.",
    "ab": "Mr Big quickly knocks the opponent down with a 2-hit strike, then kicks them across the floor. Dash twice after the animation to be +1 at point-blank range on the opponent's wakeup",
    "cd": "Mr Big\u2019s Rev Blow, an armored attack that is safe on block. Only accessible while in SPG. Read more about the Rev Blow here. Moves Mr Big forward by about 1/3 of the screen, and has enough range to hit from even further thanks to an extended hitbox.",
    "cla": "A jab with the back of front stick at close range. Mr Big's fastest normal. Used to start light chains. Similar to Far A, press A again for a unique follow-up. This second hit is the exact same as Far A > A , except it starts up 1 frame faster. Chains into other lights Combos into 623A, 623AC and 236B on normal hit, and additionally combos into 236A and 623C on counterhit / Wild Punish Mr Big's only close normal that can be low-profiled by 2CR",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. cl.AC first hit is cancellable by any follow-ups. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender, during cl.AC only the second hit is REV Blow cancellable. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : First hit of the auto combo, cancellable by any follow-ups. cl.AC > C : A second hit cancellable by any ender, a nice way to increase the damage or your opponent REV meter. Unsafe on block and can be punished if the auto-combo was started too close to the opponent. D ender : Far D follow up, different frame data and damage, very unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Safest ender, Same move as his Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV) but +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Same move as his 2D, meaning it's very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "Can be used to link into lights for small hit-confirms. Cannot chain into lights, but will link into 2B on hit Will always combo after hitting a cross-up j.A Close A/B > 2B > Far B is the go-to light combo starter. Close B will provide more time to hit-confirm and slightly better damage compared to Close A.",
    "clc": "A quick cross-slash with both sticks. Used to start big combos. Close C > Feint > Far D is the go-to combo starter, replace Far D with Far C > 214D if Close C lands as a counterhit. If Close C hits from max range, Far D may whiff and should be replaced with Far A. Close C > 236C is a frametrap that launches if 236C counterhits, and grants +1 on block",
    "cld": "A quick hit followed by short delay before initiating a rapid, 4-hit spinning attack with the sticks. Used primarily in guard pressure, but can also start a combo. Cannot be canceled into special attacks, unlike most close normals Links into lights on a normal hit, a counterhit can combo into Far C > 214D Susceptible to Hyper Defend due to the practically non-existent gaps between hits 2-5. Despite this, the extremely short recovery limits Guard Cancel options for the opponent.",
    "cra": "A short poke at waist-level that comes with similar chaining restrictions to Far A. Used in light chains. Just like Far / Close A, press A to get a second, slightly slower hit. Combos into 623A or 623AC on hit",
    "crb": "A quick tap at the ankle with the front stick. Used to start light combos from a low attack. Chains into lights Can be jumped over with 5CR",
    "crc": "Used for poking, and can start a combo with a good hit-confirm. Slightly longer range than Far D and only 1 frame slower, but otherwise does anything Far D would do Cannot be dodged with 2CR or 5CR",
    "crd": "A sweep with the sticks. Used as mid-range low poke. On a Wild Punish hit, another 2D will hit while the opponent is on the ground for +52 advantage, but a small amount of damage can be sacrificed by using 236A (+61), 2B (+62) or 236C (+65) instead to get slightly better advantage frames.",
    "fa": "A quick poke with the stick, followed by a jab with the other if A is pressed again. Used as a low-risk poke with unusually long range for a jab. Only the first hit chains into other lights, but both hits are special cancelable Second hit has slightly slower startup but is otherwise the same as the first Usually combos into 623A, but this can whiff at absolute max range. Will always combo into 623AC. All of Mr Big's far normals will hit a crouching opponent but can still be low-profiled by an opponent's 2CR",
    "fb": "A waist-level jab with the stick. Used at the end of light chains to hit-confirm into a special. Shorter range than Far A and cannot chain into lights, but better special-cancel options and consistency This will always combo into 623A, 236B and 623AC on normal hit. On counterhit / Wild Punish, can additionally combo into 623C",
    "fc": "A long-range, forward-advancing swipe with the stick. Used as a combo starter and a whiff punish tool. Very long range (can hit from round-start distance with a 2-frame microwalk) and moves Mr Big forward by a little less than the distance of a forward hop Combos into any special on normal hit, except 236C and 214A > P (but 214K may whiff at longer ranges). On counterhit, the two exceptions above no longer apply and Far C > Feint > Far B will combo except at max range where Far B must be replaced with Far A",
    "fcc": "After Far C connects with an opponent on hit or block, press C again to follow it up with a strong jab of the other stick. This follow-up is also special cancelable. Combos into anything Far C would, except 214D always whiffs Can be delayed by up to 2 frames after Far C connects",
    "fd": "A mid-level swing with both sticks, used in hit-confirms. Shorter range (just a tiny bit longer than Far B) and does not have a target combo follow-up, but faster startup compared to Far C. Otherwise, does anything Far C would",
    "ja": "A downward poke with the front stick while falling down. Hits on both sides of Mr Big, use this to cross up the opponent. Even shorter horizontal range than his command grab, not suitable for uses other than crossing up the opponent Reliably combos into close lights after a cross-up",
    "jb": "A forward poke with the front stick while in air. Use this for air-to-air situations Best horizontal reach of Mr Big's air normals, not including the second hit of j.C",
    "jc": "A 2-hit attack with two fast, consecutive swipes of the sticks. Good all-purpose aerial attack. The multi-hitting nature of this move makes it especially useful for filling the space in front of Mr Big with hitboxes, especially during a hop The first hit will combo into the second against an airborne opponent, even without a counterhit / Wild Punish",
    "jcd": "Mr Big\u2019s aerial Rev Blow. Kind of like 236K, but at a diagonal angle toward the ground. Upon activation, briefly floats Mr Big upward before diving to the ground. Mr Big will also roll forward a short distance after landing.",
    "jd": "A straight downward swing with both sticks. Good for air-to-ground situations, especially to start a combo from a jump-in. Hitbox reaches further down below Mr Big than any of his other air normals"
  },
  "mr_karate": {
    "214bdbd": "Mr. Karate lifts one leg up and enters his Drago Supremo stance. He has four different followups out of this stance, but when used by itself Drago Supremo functions as a parry that works on strikes and projectiles, with different properties depending on how soon after activating the stance Mr. Karate parries an attack. Drago Supremo has a bit of startup similar to Nightmare Geese's counters, 3 frames for the normal version and 2 frames for the REV version, so it will not work as a wakeup reversal against properly timed meaties. Drago Supremo will parry all Mid and High strike including supers and REV Blows as well as non-REV and non-super projectiles, but loses to Lows and Throws. Additionally Drago Supremo cannot counter attacks that are behind Mr. Karate such as cross-ups. When a parryable strike connects, Mr. Karate will automatically follow up with Dragon Roar. When a parryable projectile connects, Mr. Karate will destroy it and remain in Drago Supremo stance. REV Drago Supremo and its followups are mostly the same, but Mr. Karate gains the ability to Dragon Burst after Shouran Kyaku and can REV Accel after Dragon Roar and Dragon Howl . Mr. Karate also gains the ability to parry Lows and REV projectiles, though the stance will still lose to Throws, cross-ups, and super projectiles. If Mr. Karate parries a strike immediately after activating Drago Supremo, he will instead perform Dragon Howl , a stronger attack. Even if the opponent was airborne when they triggered Dragon Howl, they will be forced into a grounded state for the animation. REV Dragon Howl can be followed up with Dragon Burst or a REV Accel. This makes Drago Supremo a good option when attempting a Guard Cancel, as certain REV Accel blockstrings that blow up other Guard Cancel options like a REV Accel into a REV DP will be countered by Dragon Howl. If Mr. Karate parries a projectile immediately after activating Drago Supremo, he will reflect the projectile instead of destroying it.",
    "214kbd": "Mr. Karate swats away an incoming projectile. If done at any point during the stance following the initial parry frames, Mr. Karate will simply kick the projectile out of existence. However, to deal with REV projectiles, Mr. Karate will have to be in REV Drago Supremo . If a projectile makes contact with Mr. Karate during frames 5 to 7 of entering Drago Supremo (frames 3 to 5 for the REV version), he will instead reflect the projectile back at the opponent. Successful Dragon Dances can be cancelled to any of Drago Supremo\u2019s input follow-ups, as well as Dragon Burst. If Mr. Karate doesn\u2019t cancel the Dragon Dance, he will instead remain in Drago Supremo.",
    "214kbdb": "Mr. Karate exits Drago Supremo without attacking. OVERHEAT : Unchanged. Regular versions (214K > B) Mr. Karate is in Wild Punish state for the entire duration. Rev version (214BD > B) Identical to the regular versions.",
    "214kbdd": "Mr. Karate attacks the opponent with an overhead heel. Mr. Karate also performs this attack automatically if he parried a strike too late for Dragon Howl . OVERHEAT : Unchanged. All versions Hold either the B or D button to immediately return to Drago Supremo. If Mr. Karate does this, this move become safe against strikes even though it's -5 otherwise. You will return to the regular stance regardless of whether you did the regular or REV stance beforehand. Has an optional follow-up, Dragon Roar: Ren . The startup has brief invincibility to low attacks, and any low that is dodged within this window will trigger a slowdown, easily letting you confirm the follow-up. Slowdown triggers against quick low attacks like 2B do not guarantee a punish due to Dragon Roar's slow startup. Mr. Karate can also link into 2A at most ranges it counters a low/throw attack. Launches if they counter hit an airborne opponent, giving you big punishes against air REV Blows (or some moves like Terry\u2019s Power Dunk). Rev version (214BD > D) Becomes safe on block at -1, and you can REV Accel it. Special versions (Dragon Step / Counter) If Dragon Roar is done out of a Dragon Step counter, all versions gain full invincibility until the end of their active frames. If Dragon Roar automatically triggered via a Drago Supremo parry, all versions gain full invincibility until the end of their recovery frames. All versions otherwise retain their original properties and frame data.",
    "214kbdd6d": "Mr. Karate follows up Dragon Roar with a second kick. The only difference between regular and REV is that you can REV Accel it, not only giving you additional combo opportunities but potentially saving you from how egregiously unsafe it is on block.",
    "236236rev": "placeholder placeholder",
    "236263ac2363214ac": "Mr. Karate's iconic Ryuuko Ranbu combo super returns. Ryuuko Ranbu is less damaging than Super Haou Shoukou Ken but has faster startup, allowing it to work in combos where Super Haou Shoukou Ken won't However, Mr. Karate's can still combo into Super Haou Shoukou Ken anyways by utilizing a forced far D input and still have it land 1 frame faster on frame 8. . It's also Mr. Karate's invincible super, making it a good reversal option during Overheat. The Redline Gear version is slightly faster than the Ignition Gear version and does more damage. When Mr. Karate is in S.P.G. and has two bars of Power Gauge, he can instead input 2363214AC to perform True Ryuuko Ranbu . It has the same range and startup as the normal Redline Gear version but does slightly more damage and has a different finishing move. Though it is a neat nod to the traditional Ryuuko Ranbu input, it's largely redundant compared to his Hidden Gear due to having the same use conditions (requiring 2 bars of super meter and S.P.G.) but it does less damage, has one frame slower startup, and does not reset the REV Gauge back to 0%. The only thing it has over his Hidden Gear is its longer range.",
    "236263bd": "Mr. Karate performs another signature Kyokugen technique. He rears back and focuses energy into both hands before turning it forwards, unleashing a massive screen-high fireball that flies at a blistering speed. Ignition Gear version (236236B) Deals more damage than his Ignition Ryuko Ranbu and is the preferred 1 bar ender in combos. If it hits high enough in the corner, Mr. Karate can often tack on at least a light Ryuuga. Destroys other non-super projectiles while travelling across the screen very quickly. This is useful for shutting down opponents attempting to counter zone Mr. Karate with REV projectiles. If charged, Super Haou Shoukou Ken will deal more damage and have increased durability, allowing it to negate other uncharged Ignition projectiles but not Redline projectiles. Redline Gear version (236236D) Fires three fireballs instead of one, instantly dominating any fireball war. Can be cancelled into Dragon Burst on the second hit, which can be followed up with a Heavy Hien Shippuu Kyaku, even from full screen. Like the Ignition version, charging the Redline version will cause it to deal more damage and have increased durability. Each charged Redline Super Haou Shoukou Ken projectile negates all projectiles while clashing with other charged Redline projectiles (Kaiser Wave, Super Haou Shoukou Ken).",
    "236acac": "Mr. Karate's fireball. OVERHEAT : Regular versions deals less damage, no longer beats other regular projectiles and no longer knocks down on hit. Heavy version also cannot be charged. Light version (236A) Has poor startup compared to other fireballs but make up for it with the ability to destroy other projectiles without being destroyed itself, making it an excellent tool for countering other Projectiles. Will not destroy REV or super projectiles. Heavy version (236C) Functions largely like the A version but has an even slower startup, making it extremely vulnerable to being jumped. Travels faster than the A version. Can be cancelled into Dragon Burst on hit or block. Can be charged up to turn it into Haoh Shoukou Ken , making the projectile larger and more damaging. This is Mr. Karate's optimal DP punish starter for DPs -45 or worse. There is only one level of charge. Although Mr. Karate can charge the move past this point, it only delays the projectile and does not increase the damage further. Charging the fireball does not grant it the properties of a REV or a super projectile, despite its name and appearance. If Mr. Karate is roughly at full screen, he will recover before the Haoh Shoukou Ken hits the opponent and will thus be unable to cancel it into Dragon Burst. Cannot be done when overheat since Mr. Karate cannot charge this fireball when in that state. Rev version (236AC) A shorter ranged blast of ki that launches the opponent and can be followed up with Dragon Burst.",
    "236bdbd": "A flying kick with good range and is one of Mr. Karate's staple combo tools. OVERHEAT : Regular versions deals less damage and no longer knock down. This makes them punishable ON HIT if up close. Light version (236B) Light confirm combo ender and works at any range from 2A or f.A. It can also be used as an advancing poke in neutral provided Mr. Karate spaces it right. It is punishable when used up close but can be made up to -2 when spaced enough. Because Mr. Karate has outstanding launch juggle damage when compared to his lacklustre close/crouching heavies, this usually serves as his optimal DP punish starter. Heavy version (236D) Your go-to bread and butter combo ender. It can be followed up with Dragon Burst and comboed into Light Ryuuga. If the opponent is launched high enough before they are hit with this move, Mr. Karate can also follow up with Heavy Ryuuga or Ryuuko Ranbu. Rev version (236BD) Mostly the same as Heavy Shippuu Kyaku outside of being able to REV Accel it. Also launches the opponent slightly higher than the Heavy version, allowing a juggle into Heavy Ryuuga.",
    "623acac": "Mr. Karate's DP. Any version that is braked will suffer 4f additional recovery on whiff. OVERHEAT : Light version loses its invincibility (like other meterless invincible DPs). Heavy version cannot be charged. Light version (623A) As usual, this version has full body invincibility like most light DPs. Heavy version (623C) Only has air invincibility but can be Braked. Can be charged to turn it into Kyokugen Ryuuga , dealing more hits and more damage in exchange for longer startup. Mr. Karate maintains air invincibility for the entire duration of the charge up. When Braked, it launches the opponent higher than a normal Heavy Ryuuga Brake, allowing for additional combo opportunities. Rev version (623AC) Essentially the same as the C version, except faster and usable in more juggle sitations, such as from a REV Shouran Kyaku. Does an astounding 180 base damage when fully charged, making it the highest damage non-grab REV special in the game. Mr. Karate can easily combo into a fully-charged REV Ryuuga off of a REV Accel and, if in SPG, can combo out of it using an air REV Blow to quickly bring him back to the ground.",
    "646bdbd": "A flurry of rapid kicks. OVERHEAT : Regular versions deals less damage. Light version (236B) Can be used as a combo ender after a juggle from a braked Heavy Ryuuga. It has less corner carry than Heavy Hien Shippuu Kyaku but does more damage in certain combos. Heavy version (236D) Has slower startup but can be Dragon Bursted out of. Mr. Karate can only combo into a light Ryuuga from this move as it vacuums the opponent down into a fixed height on hit. Rev version (236BD) Mr. Karate's main REV Accel combo starter. Can also be Dragon Bursted out, giving Mr. Karate the uncommon flexibility of spending just a single REV art for a decent combo.",
    "abc": "Every character can taunt by pressing A, B, and C together, but Mr. Karate's taunt actually serves a practical gameplay use. Taking its name and use from the Art of Fighting series, Razz will increase the opponent's REV Gauge by 17% when used. This can be used to force a high REV opponent into Overheat. Mr. Karate can generally find an opportunity to Razz his opponent after a Heavy Hien Shippuu Kyaku if he doesn't Dragon Burst it, though some opponents with fast, long range supers might be able to punish him in time. Begins increasing opponent's REV gauge at frame 22 and reaches 17% at frame 65. The REV Gauge increase will stop if Mr. Karate is hit during the taunt. The opponent's REV Gauge build up is unaffected by what S.P.G. Ratio they are currently in. No matter what, their REV Gauge will always increase by 17% as long as Mr. Karate can perform the taunt uninterrupted.",
    "cla": "Mr. Karate\u2019s fastest attack Chains to all of his other light normals or his Combination Attack Can be chained into from a point-blank 2B A core move of Mr. Karate\u2019s defense-minded moveset, being fast, mashable, chainable, plus on block, and with Mr. Karate\u2019s tall stature, a great hop check as well. On grounded hit, you can go into your Combination Attack, mash out a confirm into 236B, or cancel to DP brake to launch your opponent for a full combo, but if you\u2019re close enough, you can chain a close B, pushing your damage from lights even further.",
    "clb": "Links to 2C on hit and frame traps into it on block Combos to DP brake very reliably; on Counter Hit/Wild Punish, you can even link to the DP A powerful normal in Mr. Karate\u2019s arsenal, great for pushing your advantage at close range. Its link to 2C on hit gives you reliable light-to-heavy combo routes, and on block, this same sequence shuts down reversal lights and throws from your opponent.",
    "clc": "The faster of Mr. Karate\u2019s close heavies but has short proximity detection A feint cancel lets this move link to 2C and gives Mr. Karate massive reward on Counter Hit, and makes this move his most advantageous normal on block Fantastic on both hit and block if feint cancelled, otherwise very punishable This gut punch is a great close-range button\u2026 as long as you remember to feint cancel it. Even though it\u2019s a frame slower than 2C, it links to said button on normal hit when you feint cancel it, and on block that same feint cancel is plus enough to allow a follow-up close/far B or 2C to beat any reversal normals from your opponent. Counter Hitting with this normal gives you a triple-heavy combo, maximizing your damage. To do this, microdash after the first close C, then do a close C/D > feint cancel > 2C > special. It\u2019s tough to time, but you can use your Rev button\u2019s dash macro here.",
    "cld": "Hits twice, but only the second hit cancels, easily letting you confirm hit or block but potentially leaving you vulnerable to Guard Cancels Forces stand on hit, like all close D\u2019s Links to 2C on normal hit when feint cancelled This here\u2019s your main combo starter. Landing it should be one of your goals as Mr. Karate, because from there you easily get this character\u2019s big combos. Like with close C, you can feint cancel it on block and it will be plus, but unlike close C, the opponent can trade their way out of most follow-up pressure (unless you frame trap them with something like charged DP brake).",
    "cra": "This and far A have nearly identical frame data Lower damage and range than far A, but you can chain out of it For the most part, this normal exists as pure combo filler as you can chain it into any other light normal, making it essential for many confirms. Outside of combos, it is largely outclassed by far A, but it does combo to DP more consistently.",
    "crb": "Great range and frame data for a 2B Chains to both close lights point-blank, itself and 2A for most of its range, and far B at any range The only non-proximity normal Mr. Karate has that\u2019s plus on hit without feint cancel One of the best 2B\u2019s in the game. In addition to all the benefits 2B\u2019s usually get, it has good reach, chains to Mr. Karate\u2019s close normals very well, enables all of his offense by preventing the opponent from walking back out, and he can chain multiple 2B\u2019s and still convert (to a super). It is very abusable, and especially effective when you\u2019ve conditioned your opponent with your other moves.",
    "crc": "Hits twice, but only the second hit cancels Second hit reaches REALLY high, making it great as an anti-air This normal does two things really well. In neutral, it\u2019s a wonderful anti-air due to its high reach, stuffing jump-ins or even some would-be cross-up attempts. Thus it\u2019s your main move to stop opponents from jumping out of the corner, and it\u2019s cancellable. Just be mindful of its poor horizontal range. This normal is also a great combo tool, as it links from close B and either of your feint-cancelled close heavies. The two hits give you an easier time to confirm hit or block and choose your cancel appropriately. You should always cancel it, since it\u2019s awful on block if you don\u2019t.",
    "crd": "On Wild Punish, puts an opponents into an OTG state, letting you follow up with another 2D or a 236236B On its own, it\u2019s just an average sweep. The range is about the same as far C, which means it outreaches 2B, if only by a little bit. It\u2019s best used at max range, where it becomes safe in many matchups. At this range, it\u2019s also great at tagging opponents walking backward, which is common when they anticipate a stance mix or surprise 236K.",
    "fa": "Above-average reach for a far A Only chains to itself Combos to 236B and supers at any range A low-commitment jab with above-average range, making it great as a hop check, in confirms, or as a quick punish. Its great range allows you to combo from chains of 2+ lights, even two 2Bs. However, beware that nothing that it combos to is safe on block \u2014 so avoid raw cancelling this normal in neutral.",
    "fb": "Mr. Karate\u2019s longest-ranged light normal Fast startup for its range A fast, decently-ranged straight kick that can be chained into and combos to all of his supers. This does two things: it makes your light chains into super very reliable, and if you have meter, it becomes quite the scary counterpoke since you can buffer your super behind it.",
    "fc": "Longer range than it looks due to the forward step during startup On Counter Hit/Wild Punish, a Feint Cancel allows for links to far B or D A step punch with good reach that special cancels. While using it in neutral is tough due its slow startup and being awful on block or whiff, it reaches far enough to pick up combos after distant Wild Punishes, or to punish a blocked sweep. In these scenarios, it\u2019s very easy to buffer a cancel to 646BD for a full combo. If you use this move outside of punishes, you can single-hit confirm from it, or feint cancel it for matchup-specific spacing traps.",
    "fd": "A decent long-ranged kick with largely the same range as far C, except that it is 2 frames faster and can\u2019t be cancelled to every special. It has a deceptive disjointed hitbox that does not miss crouchers even at a distance. However, several quirks exist with this move that no other normals in the game possess. It is able to cancel into its command combo followups, but it is able to do so even on whiff If it hits an airborne opponent, they are left in a resetting juggle state. Furthermore, it has enhanced juggle capabilities normally only given to specials and supers and can hit opponents mid-juggle without a counter hit it cancels to Drago Supremo (but no other specials), allowing you to use the juggle properties to push a strike-throw 50/50 even at the end of a long combo It can be selectively performed even at close distances by pressing 5B+D",
    "fddfdd6dfdd6b": "placeholder placeholder",
    "holdaholdc": "By holding down either Punch button when he has any amount of REV Gauge built up, Mr. Karate will begin focusing his ki. A mechanic taken from his home series Art of Fighting , Spirit Charge reduces the REV Gauge as long as it is held down. Mr. Karate can generally find an opportunity to Spirit Charge after a Heavy Hien Shippuu Kyaku if he doesn't use Dragon Burst, after a back throw, or after his command grab. Spirit Charge can't be used if Mr. Karate has 0% REV.",
    "j4d": "placeholder placeholder",
    "j4dnearwall": "placeholder placeholder",
    "j4holddnearwall": "placeholder placeholder",
    "ja": "Combos to air REV Blow on Counter Hit or Wild Punish Mr. Karate\u2019s fastest air normal. Great as an air-to-air, even if a little stubby.",
    "jb": "Can cross up Mr. Karate\u2019s j.B is a bit slow for an air light, but makes up for it with its range, both in front of and behind him.",
    "jc": "An obnoxious air normal with a deceptive hitbox, this is Mr. Karate\u2019s preferred jump-in normal. It\u2019s lacking in horizontal range, though.",
    "jd": "The hitbox\u2019s downward angle and good forward reach make this normal great for more distant jump-ins. However, combos from it can be somewhat picky due to its pushback on hit.",
    "jdd": "placeholder placeholder"
  },
  "nightmare_geese": {
    "1632143acac": "The pretzel input returns in all its glory. By performing the original Raging Storm input, Nightmare Geese performs a stronger version of the move. This is a secret variation of Raging Storm and is not displayed on the command list in-game. True Raging Storm does slightly more damage than the double quarter circle version of Raging Storm. It also has a slightly different visual and sound effect so you can tell the difference. True Raging Storm can be comboed out of if it connects at a high enough height, around the apex of the opponent's jump height. This can usually be achieved by landing it as a precise anti-air or landing a Counter Hit special move that causes a launch state like Counter Hit Heavy Fudou Ken. After True Raging Storm connects in these contexts you can follow up with (REV) Raimei Gouha Nage or (REV) Raimei Shin and get some more out of them with REV Accel. This applies to the Redline Gear version as well, though as usual the only marginally better damage increase isn't worth spending a second bar in most cases. None of this will work on a normal Raging Storm, only the pretzel input version. With all of that said, True Raging Storm is better than Raging Storm in pretty much every single way. There are a few use cases for the easy input version though; it's faster and easier of course, it might be better as a wakeup reversal since in that situation you would care more about getting the opponent off of you over getting slightly higher damage with a more complex input, you'd probably want to do the easier input version if the damage will end the round regardless of which input you use, and it's easier to use in Hyper Defense because of the forward inputs. Still, at least in combos you should always use True Raging Storm unless the input is too hard for you. It's only a very slight increase in damage and the followup combos require very specific situations. Don't worry if you can't pull it off.",
    "2141236bd": "Just like with Raging Storm, Nightmare Geese's Deadly Rave also has a stronger secret input version. In addition to the different motion input, the player has to manually input every hit in the ensuing combo after the initial lunge. The True Deadly Rave button squence is as follows: 2141236B > A > A > B > B > B > C > C > C > 214C. If you're having trouble memorizing all of that, think of it as just two A presses, three B presses, three C presses, and then a quarter circle back + C press at the end. You can get away with just mashing the correct buttons as long as you know which hits in the animation are which. True Deadly Rave does slightly more damage than the normal Deadly Rave. The fact that every hit is a manual input means that the player can choose to stop the combo early, usually right before the 214C ender. Unlike prior games such as Garou: Mark of the Wolves or Capcom vs SNK 2 where stopping early leaves you with enough frame advantage to link a normal into a special move to get back some of your meter, here the only reason to end the combo before the final hit is to be +3 and right in front of the opponent, which compliments Nightmare Geese's strong offense. Losing out on more damage in the short term in exchange for the potential of more damage out of an ensuing mixup is usually worth it in most high level fighting game player's minds unless that short term damage is enough to win the round. Still, you get enough okizeme anyway that you can end it with the cool ki blast if you really want to. As usual, there's no real reason to use the Redline Gear version unless the damage will win the round. It doesn't do much more damage than the Ignition Gear version to justify spending a valuable bar of super meter, on top of the fact that the True Deadly Rave combo does the same damage on both the Ignition and Redline versions before the ending hit, meaning there is no reason to use the Redline Gear version if you want to end the super early.",
    "214aaca": "A second palm strike. Unlike the other Fudou Ken followups, this one can't be charged. When using Fudou Ken to end combos, this is Nightmare Geese's most damaging uncharged followup. Use this in situations where juggling into the charged versions of his followups is impossible. Do not use this followup on block, as it is very unsafe and highly punishable. The REV version isn't very different from the normal version aside from doing slightly more damage and being able to REV Accel out of it.",
    "214aacb": "A low hitting sweep. This is the only uncharged followup to Fudou Ken that is safe on block, being -3. There is no gap between Fudou Ken and Fudou Ken: Ba, keeping Nightmare Geese safe from Guard Cancels barring a Hyper Defense. Numbers in [square brakets] denote values for fully-charged versions. When charged, this move changes from being -3 to -1 on block, does slightly more damage, and allows Nightmare Geese to follow up with Raimei Gouha Nage on hit. The only difference between the normal version and the REV version is that the REV version becomes +2 on block when charged and can be REV Acceled into REV Raimei Gouha Nage or REV Raimei Shin.",
    "214aacc": "A dual palm strike similar to Nightmare Geese's Ha command normal. While this move might seem redundant compared to Fudou Ken: O since it also hits mid, does less damage, and is also punishable on block, its true strength comes from when it is charged. It also keeps the opponent standing and Nightmare Geese will be +2 on a grounded hit, so he can choose to sacrifice the higher damage and soft knockdown of the A followup in exchange for more pressure. Numbers in [square brakets] denote values for fully-charged versions. The charged version changes from -5 on block to -3. IT can be used as a frame trap to dissuade the opponent from mashing out of Nightmare Geese's charged followups on block. Nightmare Geese can vary his charge timing and keep the opponent guessing, though he will only be safer on block if he fully charges the attack. If Nightmare Geese very briefly charges this attack, he can frame trap 5F normals from Fudou Ken. On hit, the charged version will leave Nightmare Geese +5, just enough to link into 2A. On Counter Hit the uncharged version is +7, enough to link into Far B. On Counter Hit the charged version is +10, allowing Nightmare Geese to follow up with 2C and start his heavier combos. If Nightmare Geese hits an airborne opponent with the charged version, he can follow up with Raimei Gouha Nage, though midscreen he will usually have to whiff cancel Far D first. The REV version is only -2 on block when uncharged and +3 when charged. Its frame data on normal hit and Counter Hit for both the uncharged and charged versions are the same as the normal versions.",
    "214aacd": "An overhead axe kick. There will always be a gap between Fudou Ken and Fudou Ken: Un regardless of whether Nightmare Geese charges it or not. It's -5 on block so the opponent can get a small punish, but there's a decent chance they might not block it considering it only has 19 frames of startup. Numbers in [square brakets] denote values for fully-charged versions. When charged this move goes from being -5 on block to -3 on block, does slightly more damage, and can be followed up with Raimei Gouha Nage. The uncharged REV version is unpunishable at -2 and only has one extra frame of startup, allowing Nightmare Geese to keep his fast overhead while also being safe on block, though there is still a gap to worry about. The charged REV version becomes +3 on block.",
    "214acac": "Nightmare Geese strikes with his palm. The light and REV versions have follow-ups on hit, block and even whiff. Using Fudou Ken on block on its own without using its follow-ups is ill-advised, as the Light, Heavy, and REV versions are all punishable on block. Only the Light version can be comboed into from Light normals. The Heavy version acts very differently. Instead of a palm strike that can be followed up by a variety of different options, Nightmare Geese performs an upwards palm strike that launches the opponent, allowing for juggles. A staple part of Nightmare Geese's combos, though it only combos from Heavy normals and Counter Hit Light normals.",
    "214b": "Nightmare Geese's Upper Body counter. At 3 frame startup and 12 active frames it can be a very useful defensive tool, though if Nightmare Geese fails to counter anything he has 29 frames of recovery to deal with before he can act again, which is more than enough time to get punished severely for guessing wrong. Its fast 3F startup makes it a great option for Guard Canceling against multi-hit attacks or tight REV Accel sequences, even things that usually beat Guard Cancels like a cancel into a Heavy/REV DP Brake. In addition to the long recovery, Nightmare Geese is in a Wild Punish state for the entirety of the move's duration, whether he is hit during the recovery or is hit by something this move cannot counter like a move aimed below his waist or a projectile. Nightmare Geese can special cancel into this move. This can be used to make a hard read and call out a Guard Cancel if the opponent Just Defended the previous normal attack.",
    "214bd": "Essentially a REV version of the Upper Body counter. Shares much in common with the other two; 3F startup, 29F recovery, Wild Punishable, tosses the opponent behind him like the normal Upper Body counter. The only difference is that it can be REV Acceled into and out of. Meterlesly, Nightmare Geese can either stay on the side he tossed the opponent to but do less damage with Heavy Raimei Shin, or swap sides again to end up on the same side he was when he performed the counter and do more damage by doing dash/Far D into Raimei Gouha Nage. In REV Accel blockstrings Nightmare Geese can REV Accel into this move to call out Guard Cancels.",
    "214d": "Nightmare Geese's Lower Body counter. 3F startup and 29F recovery, just like the Upper Body counter. Does slightly more damage than the Upper Body counter and tosses the opponent the entire screen's length away from Geese. Although it might seem like this move can only counter attacks classified as Lows, it can also counter Mid attacks that are aimed at the lower body like Terry's 2C, hence why it is a Lower Body counter and not strictly a Low counter. Just like with the Upper Body counter, Nightmare Geese is in a Wild Punish state throughout the entire move's duration.",
    "236236ac": "Nightmare Geese's iconic Raging Storm, now repackaged as a double quarter circle input instead of the nightmarish pretzel input it was back in the day. Since Nightmare Geese lacks a meterless invincible reversal, Raging Storm is his go-to wakeup reversal option, though it's obviously very risky and costs meter. Also a good anti-air and combo ender. Tends to do slightly more damage than Deadly Rave but has less range, so use that in situations where Raging Storm will whiff.",
    "236236bd": "Nightmare Geese's iconic combo super. It's been simplified compared to prior iterations though, its input having changed to a double quarter circle and the ensuing combo being automatic rather than every hit having to be inputted by the player. While it does slightly less damage than Raging Storm and the Ignition Gear (level 1) version isn't invincible, Deadly Rave is still useful for its long horizontal range and corner carry. Because of this you should usually use Deadly Rave in midscreen combos over Raging Storm unless you're in a situation where Raging Storm is the better option (ie. in the corner or if you need the extra damage to KO).",
    "236236rev": "A powerful attack with surprising range, especially for a 6-frame move. It deals massive damage on hit, but is only available during SPG. Resets the REV meter to 0%, even during Overheat. Swaps sides with the opponent on hit and gives very little okizeme. If you want momentum after the super, just use your Ignition Gear instead unless you need the REV meter reset. Due to its fast startup and long range, there are some combos where Hidden Gear is the only super you can end the combo with, and some moves where this attack is the only punish.",
    "236acac": "Nightmare Geese's trademark Reppuu Ken. Each version of Reppuu Ken has different properties, making them each useful for different situations. Light Version (236A) Nightmare Geese tosses a grounded projectile. The Light version of Reppuu Ken is as standard as projectiles come, used for attacking from a distance. Heavy Version (236C) Nightmare Geese tosses out two Reppuu Kens, a short range one that nullifies non-REV or super projectiles followed by a full screen projectile. Similar in function to Rock's Heavy Reppuu Ken, this move can give Nightmare Geese an edge in fireball wars by eating the opponent's projectile and launching one right back, thus creating an opportunity for him to get in closer. REV Version (236AC) Nightmare Geese tosses out a supercharged, fast, multihitting Reppuu Ken. While it has the slowest startup among his Reppuu Ken versions, the projectile will blow through regular projectiles and cancel out with REV projectiles. Its fast speed lets it snipe opponents from half or even full screen, especially if they are still recovering from throwing their own projectile.",
    "236bdbd": "Nightmare Geese delivers a heavy foot stomp directed at the opponent's feet. Despite what the low hitting animation might suggest, all versions of this move are mids and can be blocked standing or crouching. Numbers in [square brakets] denote values for OTG hits. Light Version (236B) A safe blockstring ender at -2, providing a less risky but also less rewarding alternative to Fudou Ken mixup attempts. This move can be used as a decent frame trap if done as a delayed cancel from a Light normal, allowing Nightmare Geese to follow up with 2A if up close or Far B at range on Counter Hit. Heavy Version (236D) A very slow but +3 on block pressure reset. Its 27 frame startup means that the opponent has more than enough time to react and punish, but if they are conditioned properly than Heavy Raimei Shin can catch them off guard and score Nightmare Geese some plus frames. While Nightmare Geese isn't plus enough on hit to combo afterward normally, he can make himself around +5 or +6 on a meaty hit and link into his 2A. If it Counter Hits, Nightmare Geese can link into 2C. Also hits OTG, though situations where you can actually connect OTG with this move are rare. Not even a Wild Punish 2D gives Nightmare Geese enough time to link into this move. If the move hits the opponent right as they're about to hit the ground, a brief cutscene will play where Geese smirks before punting his opponent, giving extra damage and a hard knockdown. It\u2019s possible to combo into this. REV Version (236BD) A middle ground between the Light and Heavy versions. It has the faster startup of the Light version and is plus on block like the Heavy version, though it's +2 instead of +3, making it a good choice to REV Accel into in REV Art blockstrings. If this move hits the opponent right as they\u2019re downed from certain attacks, Geese will smirk and punt the opponent, just like the Heavy version, and you can REV Accel this punt into EX OTG Grab. You can combo into this special from various EX Fudou Ken followups depending on the state of the opponent. If done after a landing a Wild Punish 2D, this move cannot be REV Acceled.",
    "256bcor256drev": "Mimics the startup of Reppuu Ken. As Nightmare Geese's only grounded Feint it's used for Feint cancel links and faking a projectile in neutral.",
    "2crev": "Cannot be cancelled on block. Wins against jumping attacks, standing normals and REV Blows. Triggers a slowdown if it meets any of these. Horizontal range is so bad that it doesn't even match the move's animation. Nightmare Geese's most reliable anti-air option. It's got great vertical reach but awful horizontal range. When you know the move will Counter Hit or Wild Punish - such as after triggering a slowdown - you can always combo it into 214C for a launch into a full, albeit scaled, combo.",
    "4ab": "Geese tosses the opponent over his shoulder, similarly to his command grab from the older games. When used midscreen, throws the opponent almost an entire screen's length away. You can meaty the opponent's wakeup with a 236A. In the corner, any safe jump has to be manually timed, but two dashes or an empty fullhop gives you a meaty close heavy, 2C or 5AC. Geese's back throw switches sides, though the okizeme isn\u2019t quite as good as his forward throw. You can safely meaty the opponent's wakeup with a Reppuken, which effortlessly sets up your zoning or potentially forces the opponent into a checkmate.",
    "5crev": "Universal overhead, wins against lows and throws. Triggers a slowdown if it meets either of these. On Counter Hit or Wild Punish, links to 2C. Second hit\u2019s hitbox reaches so high that it ironically has limited anti-airing capabilities. Dodges lows and throws, and in doing so gives Geese a combo afterward. It hits twice, making it easier to confirm an opportunity to link something. It's also good to use on opponents whom you've conditioned to tech the throw or mash against your shimmy.",
    "623acac": "Nightmare Geese leaps up into the air and slashes downward. Despite its DP input and its similarity to DP type special moves, this move is NOT invincible nor a good anti-air; rather, Hishou Nichirin Zan is another tool in Nightmare Geese's bag of mixup tricks. All versions can be Braked on startup, which will make Nightmare Geese leap into the air without performing the slash. This can be useful for empty jump low mixups, especially when done after knockdowns and setups. Light Version (623A) Numbers in [square brakets] denote values when it hits an opponent in the air. The Light version is a safe blockstring ender, though there is a decently-sized gap between any normal cancelled into this move and when the move hits. It cannot hit crossup. The frame advantage for this move varies depending on if the opponent blocked it standing or crouching; on standing block it's -2, on crouching block it's 0. Additionally, if spaced properly (about two backdashes away from the opponent) it can become +2 on both standing and crouching block. The Light version can hit airborne opponents on the way up and knock them down with the slash hit. The first hit does not connect on grounded opponents and can make for an okay anti-air, though since it has no invincibility of any kind it can be stuffed or trade with the opponent's air buttons. It can be used as a combo ender in situations where Fudou Ken will not work due to having been used earlier in the combo. As the startup of all versions of this move can be Braked, this means that Nightmare Geese can get a psuedo-DP Brake combo if he connects with the airborne-only first hit and Brakes. At most heights he won't be able to actually juggle into anything outside of his Hidden Gear, but at higher juggle heights (like those granted by a Counter Hit Heavy Fudou Ken for example) he has just enough time to juggle a Light Hishou Nichiran Zan Brake into Raging Storm, Deadly Rave, or even Light Fudou Ken at the highest juggle. Heavy Version (623C) A swiss army knife of a special move that can act as an overhead, a pressure reset, a crossup, a meaty, and a hard projectile callout in just one move. At 35 frames of startup this move can unfortunately be reacted to very easily, whether by the opponent blocking correctly or by an anti-air. To compensate, Nightmare Geese travels about half the screen and it is always plus on block and able to continue his pressure, though how plus he is differs depending on spacing and if it hit meaty or not. =3 on block if done at close range/as a crossup, around +5-6 on block if spaced from two backwards hops away, and +9 on block if spaced to hit at the very tip. +5 on standing hit, allowing for a link into Far A. If Nightmare Geese does manage to hit a crouching (read: sleeping) opponent with this move, he'll be +9 (+8 on crossup) and be able to link into 2C. On Counter Hit/Wild Punish Nightmare Geese will be +10 and can link into 2C. Nightmare Geese can put himself in this situation if he preemptively leaps over a projectile with this move and hits the opponent during their projectile recovery. With a healthy amount of REV to spend, at least one bar of super meter and S.P.G. state, Nightmare Geese can get immensely damaging combos out of one good read like this. REV Version (623AC) Similar to the Heavy version in that it's an overhead and is plus on block. Starts up slightly faster but is still very reactable at 32 frames of startup. Has slightly more horizontal range as well. +2 on block if done at close range/as a crossup, +9 on block if spaced to hit at the very tip. +3 on standing hit, +7 on crouching hit. Nightmare Geese doesn't get much in the way of combos outside of Far B. REV Hishou Nichirin Zan is a good combo tool, as it ground bounces airborne opponents, usually after being REV Acceled from REV Reppuu Ken.",
    "632146acac": "Nightmare Geese's OTG grab. This move is very specific on when it can grab the opponent off the ground. Most soft knockdowns and even hard knockdowns like those given by supers, throws, and REV Blows cannot be picked up by any version of this move. Rather, you should treat it more like a built in followup to some of Geese's special moves, such as the charged followups to Fudou Ken, Chuudan Atemi Nage, and REV Raimei Shin, rather than something you can do every time you knock the opponent down. This is Geese's highest damage follow-up after Wild Punish 2D, but it switches sides. To keep the same side, OTG with Double Reppuken instead. Sideswaps with the opponent, so there might be instances where you would choose not to use this move in the situations where you can in order to not put yourself in the corner. The REV version has two versions, an uncharged and charged version. The uncharged version works similarly to the normal versions outside of being able to be REV Acceled into. The charged version has a bit more startup but causes a ground bounce that can be comboed out of with a REV Accel. It is impossible to follow up the ground bounce meterlessly with the exception of a Heavy Raimei Shin in the corner. The numbers in [square brakets] denote values for the fully-charged REV version.",
    "632146bdbd": "Nightmare Geese's command grab. Rounds out his wealth of mixup options by allowing Nightmare Geese to show the opponent the hard way that they can't just sit and turtle out his strong offense forever. Causes a hard knockdown. No version of this move can be comboed into no matter what, not on Counter Hit or close range or Wild Punish or REV Accel. Pushes Nightmare Geese back substantially in the corner, though he still has plenty of time to dash back in and apply okizeme. The REV version can be REV Acceled out of, giving Nightmare Geese a way to combo out of a command grab though it will be heavily scaled. Your best REV Accel option is generally Reppuu Ken (236AC).",
    "ab": "Geese grabs the opponent by the neck and launches them off with a palm strike. When used midscreen, throws the opponent 3/4 of the screen away. You can meaty their wakeup with a 236C. In the corner, any safe jump has to be manually timed, but a Reppuu Ken feint allows you to time a meaty, reversal-safe 236A on their wakeup, locking them down for more pressure. Despite how far away this appears to throw them, you have a generous window to push okizeme after the throw. Not only can you easily meaty the opponent with a fireball, but two dashes sets the opponent up perfectly for a mixup using far D whiff cancel. In the corner, while the knockdown time is the same as Explosion Ball, the okizeme is even better due to the distance Geese is left afterward. You basically get to do whatever you want as a mixup there.",
    "ac": "Wild Punish +21f. Has an identical animation to Fudou Ken \u2022 A (214P~5C). Can\u2019t be cancelled into anything, not even super. Moves Geese forward on startup. When it hits meaty, can be up to +5 on hit, enabling a link to 2A. On Counter Hit, becomes +8 on hit, linking to 2C for a full combo. A fast, advancing palm strike. It isn't cancelable at all, but where this move shines is in whiff punishing. At 8 frames startup it's almost twice as fast as Far D and works as a great counter poke against long reaching attacks or as a spacing trap tool. Getting Wild Punished by this move is absolutely soul-crushing.",
    "cd": "A pretty standard REV blow with decent reach. Geese gets safe jump setups if he juggles into this move from EX moves like his rekkas.",
    "cla": "A short, upper elbow strike. Primarily used to pressure an opponent on block at close range. Geese's fastest attack Also works as a hop stopper or to start the universal Close A > C > C > D Combination Attack",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. cl.AC first hit is cancellable by any follow-ups. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender, during cl.AC only the second hit is REV Blow cancellable. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : First hit of the auto combo, cancellable by any follow-ups. cl.AC > C : A second hit cancellable by any ender, a nice way to increase the damage or your opponent REV meter. Unsafe on block and can be punished if the auto-combo was started too close to the opponent. D ender : Far D follow up, different frame data and damage, very unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Safest ender, Same move as his Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV) but +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Same move as his 2D, meaning it's very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "A short, fast kick at thigh level. Primarily used to pressure an opponent on block at close range. Cannot chain into anything else, but combos into his other lights (except Far B ) on hit Slower startup, but better advantage on block compared to Far A as a frametrap tool",
    "clc": "An open-hand strike to the ribs. Primarily used to start high-damage combos and guard pressure. Combos into 2C with Close C > Feint > 2C",
    "cld": "A double kick with Geese\u2019s front foot. Primarily used to start high-damage combos. Forces stand on hit Can be feint-canceled to combo into Far C . Combos into itself on Counter Hit / Wild Punish (requires micro-dash)",
    "cra": "A standard crouch jab. Used to start light combos. Reliably chains into itself or Far B Combos into 214A , 236B and 236C Cannot be dodged by either dodge attack",
    "crb": "A quick poke against the floor with Geese's front foot. Primarily used to start light combos with a low attack. Chains into itself and other light normals 2B > 2B > Far B > Special / Super is one of Geese's primary light confirm strings",
    "crc": "An open-hand strike at knee level. Primarily used for poking, but can also start combos. Combos into most specials on hit, but max-range hits may cause some of them to whiff. Tied for Geese's fastest heavy normal, but does not have a proximity requirement unlike Close D",
    "crd": "A long range sweep against the ground with Geese's back leg. Used as a mid-range low poke. Very unsafe on block On Wild Punish midscreen, dash up and hit 236C or 6246P to deal extra damage. Both do more damage than hitting a second sweep and 6246P will switch sides. If it Wild Punishes an opponent in the corner, 6246[AC] will grab the opponent off the ground and start a combo",
    "fa": "A short jab with Geese\u2019s front hand. Primarily used as a short-range poke. High hitbox placement gives it secondary utility as a hop stopper Combos into 214A , 236B and 236C on hit",
    "fb": "A fast, waist-high kick with Geese\u2019s back foot. Primarily used in light confirms and as a low-risk poke. Geese's longest-ranged light normal, capable of hitting even after comboing into 2 other chained light attacks before it Will always combo into 214A . 236B and 236C can combo if the opponent is close enough.",
    "fc": "Geese does a spinning step forward and strikes his fist at chest level. Primarily used during combos. Moves Geese forward, making special cancels from Far C much more reliable compared to special canceling from other normals in combos. Slow startup and small hitbox make it unsuitable for poking, especially compared to 2C",
    "fd": "Geese takes a big, spinning step forward to deliver a kick from long range. Primarily used for whiff punishing and counter-poking. Moves Geese forward even further than Far C Normal hits do not combo into anything unless super-canceled Combos into any light punch or 2B on counterhit. On Wild Punish, starts a full combo. Exclusively on whiff, can be canceled into 6246K (command grab) or 6246P (OTG grab), utilizing the forward movement of Far D to extend the range of these grabs",
    "j214acac": "An aerial version of Nightmare Geese's Reppuu Ken. Useful for controlling space from a distance. Can only be performed during a neutral or forwards jump or hop except for the REV version, which can be performed from a backwards jump or hop. All versions briefly halt Nightmare Geese's jump momentum, allowing him to mess with the opponent's anti-air timing. The Light and Heavy versions push him backwards and let Nightmare Geese keep his distance while the REV version keeps him moving forward with the exception of the backwards jump/hop version. The Light version travels slower and has a very slightly higher angle than the Heavy version. The Heavy version travels faster and has a very slightly lower angle than the Light version. The REV version fires two Shippuu Kens and generally locks down the opponent at mid range, giving Nightmare Geese an opportunity to close the gap with a dash or Far D. Can be Feinted, which halts Nightmare Geese's jump momentum and moves him backward without throwing a projectile. See the above section on his Air Feint for more detailed information.",
    "ja": "Can be cancelled to Shippuu Ken and REV Blow on hit or JD. Since you can cancel this to Shippuu Ken, this is undoubtedly your best best air-to-air; Counter Hits cause the Shippuu Ken to combo and knock down, while resets cause them to deal with the meaty Shippuu Ken when they land. The cancel to REV Blow is purely to respond to other REV Blows.",
    "jb": "Can cross up. A multi-purpose air normal that you can land with, use to cross up, and even contest other characters in the air from a neutral or back jump thanks to its forward reach.",
    "jbcorjdrev": "Mimics the startup of Shippuu Ken. Geese still halts his air momentum and sends him backward. If done high enough, Geese can still do a real Shippuu Ken before landing. This Feint halts Nightmare Geese's jump and moves him backwards a bit, allowing him to bait anti-airs. Geese will be locked out of his normals until he lands, but he can still do a real Shippuu Ken before landing. If you\u2019re in S.P.G., Geese can even do an aerial REV Blow after the air feint. Combine this with a backwards jump, and Geese have a way to lure opponents into thinking he is retreating, only to surprise them by moving back in.",
    "jc": "A deep-hitting jump-in normal. It's a bit slower than his other aerials, but it does its job well.",
    "jcd": "A an armored divebomb at an angle. Used to end combos after EX Shippuu Ken or EX Nichirin Zan, or to contest DPs. Like grounded REV Blow, it gets beaten by High Dodge Attack. If it hits the opponent low enough to the ground, the second hit won't come out and the REV blow will end instantly, giving Geese extra okizeme or even a combo into Hidden Gear.",
    "jd": "An all-purpose jumping heavy normal. You can use it to jump in, or retreat against grounded opponents with its downward hitbox."
  },
  "preecha": {
    "214acac": "Preecha steps back, can be followed up with a variety of moves. On its own, can be used to keep yourself safe, or bait a button or reversal. Can be used as a sort of backdash that can be done as a guard cancel to escape weaker pressure sequences or avoid getting locked down by a projectile. Best used as a meaty, but Rev Circular Step takes noticably less time before its followups come out, which makes it better in blockstrings.",
    "214acac46ac": "Overhead elbow strike out of Circular Step Useful option to open up an opponent that crouchblocks constantly. Can start a combo if you link with 2A, but some setups can make it up to +6 on hit, which links into c.C. Rev version is +2 at worst on block, but on hit it's identical to the meterless version. Can be made more plus as a meaty. One of Preecha's most powerful moves as it is a meterless safe overhead that gives a combo on hit",
    "214acac46b": "Low kick out of Circular Step, goes into knee strikes on hit Normal version can't be combo'd out of, Rev version can be followed after with any rev special for a combo even after the knee strike animation. Sometimes starting a combo from the rev version is harder than expected so be sure to practice combos after the rev version connects in training mode. The followup animation only plays out on grounded opponents, on air hits Preecha whiffs a throwing animation. It's still possible to cancel the Rev version before this comes out. Faster than the overhead but still unlikely to ever be fuzzy blocked. The normal version is sometimes punishable and the Rev one is safe but minus, both can still be hit meaty to improve frame advantage.",
    "214acac46d": "After Circular Step, Preecha jumps forward with a strong knee strike. Used mainly for combos, as it's unsafe on block and doesn't offer any mix, unlike Preecha's other follow-ups In midscreen combos, it can connect only by REV cancelling REV Screw Leg (236BD) On counter hit launches the opponent high for a full combo",
    "214bdbd": "Preecha jumps forward with a kick that covers a lot of ground Can be used to jump over grounded projectiles, but not traditional fireballs. Has to be preemptive as it doesn't have projectile invulnerability on start up. All versions are safe, B+D version is actually plus and a combo starter/extender against airborne opponents or on CH/WP. B version doesn't have much uses in Preecha's gameplay. Stick to using the D and BD versions instead. D version can crossup, while the B and B+D versions are always same side, but this is pretty gimmicky outside of Overheat because of Rev Guard. Additionally, the D version is 0 on hit and leaves Preecha outside the reach of her 4 frame 2A, so in reality, even a successful usage of 214D achieves very little beyond its damage. D version starts a small combo on both counter hit and wild punish(+5 advantage which links to Far A). The B+D version is Preecha's best \"Neutral Skip\" but it doesn't have much range and has alot of recovery on whiff.",
    "236236ac": "Throw out a few bruising uppercuts, then finish off with a tornado. A version : Preecha's 1 bar metered reversal option and combo ender Good as an anti-air but painfully unreliable as a reversal, it has a random chance to forego the tornados on the first two uppercuts, which increases the damage but basically halves its range and makes it drop against extended hurtboxes. Airborne opponents sometimes drop out of the tornado in specific angles, so even as an anti-air it can be annoying to use. Sometimes Preecha's highest damage one-bar ender, when it feels like it. On top of the lower frame advantage in comparison to 236236B and just how much more consistent that super is, you're probably better off not using this one outside of reversaling through blockstrings and some anti-airing. C version : The most damaging fully invincible option preecha has outside of Hidden Gear. Similar to 236236A, has a random chance to do higher damage for no reason. Thankfully its hitboxes remain intact, and are significantly larger, so it's much better for defense. The randomly stronger version also has slightly better frame advantage (+34 from +33 against grounded opponents), which is variable in the first place against airborne opponents. It's hard to tell the versions apart as well, so don't use this for setups. Regardless of the varied advantage, it keeps the corner and leaves you +33 at the very least, which makes it preferable to 236236D as a cashout that won't necessarily kill.",
    "236236bd": "Preecha's horizontal super. B Version : A far-reaching super that sideswitches unless the opponent is backed against the corner. Is NOT projectile invulnerable. Still a useful punish or conversion super with its high range. Very easy to confirm, will connect from basically any normal cancel, and its reach can help pick up normally unrewarding hits like a distant fireball. While less damaging than 236236A can be, it can be used as an ender in combos where 236236A would miss, which generally net more damage on top of actually being consistent. +40 on most hits but +42 if combo'd after a 623D break. Both are consistent and useful for setting up a powerful meaty. Should use this super in 99% of Preecha's combos as it is very combo-friendly and is her highest damage 1 bar ender. D Version : Nearly full-screen invincible super. Preecha's best reversal and a useful combo ender. A rewarding punish if you react to an opponent throwing a fireball or whiffing something with long recovery. Can be braked by pressing A+B right after the first hit connects. The following combo will do lower damage than the full super, but you can secure oki from it or keep the corner. Unlike 236236B, it ALWAYS sideswitches (even in the corner) provided you don't brake it.",
    "236236rev": "An invincible projectile. Can only be executed with S.P.G active and two bars, but is your most damaging combo ender with the highest minimum damage. While its initial range is somewhat limited, it's still pretty easy to juggle into off of almost any special thanks to its speed. Does eventually go further but this is mostly irrelevant, if this is fast enough to punish something at range you probably have better options. Actually very active and technically possible to be made plus, which is completely useless but you can occasionally be funny with it and find meaty setups.",
    "236acac": "Rekka style move that hits up to five times An indispensable move, used in combos, pressure, setups and guard crushes. The meterless version is -5 on block with no delay window on its followups, a good opponent will punish this a majority of the time. The first block of the Rev version is the only one that grants you frame advantage (+3). Very useful pressure reset as it's unreactable and leaves you decently close to the opponent. A skillful opponent can Hyper Defend and punish the follow-ups, so be careful when using them for pressure. In patch 1.7.2, allowed this move to combo from lights making it a great ender for 3 hit light strings. Combo ender, always a true block string when canceled from, not a good blockstring ender.",
    "236acacp": "First Preecha's Combination follow-up.",
    "236acacpp": "Second Preecha's Combination follow-up.",
    "236acacppp": "Third Preecha's Combination follow-up.",
    "236acacpppp": "Final Preecha's Combination follow-up, really unsafe on block. Never end a blockstring with this move",
    "236bdbd": "Preecha kicks a tornado forward in an arc All versions stay on screen for a very long time, good for getting in or for oki, but quite bad at actually zoning since it does not go full screen. The windup of the D and B+D versions already form a projectile hitbox that eats others without stopping Preecha's tornado from coming out, which can potentially cover your approach for up to two fireballs from the opponent. D version is combo-friendly and allows consistent links into Preecha's P = mc2 super D and B+D versions can be braked after the first tornado appears. This is useful point-blank to squeeze in a throw when the opponent is expecting the second tornado, or to make the first hit plus with spacing or a meaty timing. Avoid using D and B+D versions in neutral as the start-up and recovery are extremely long. Use the B version for screen control and zoning. The D and B+D versions could be used in fireball wars, but use them only to absorb an opponent's fireball as doing them raw is very risky. When executing this move after a forward dash, input a Tiger Knee motion (2369) to avoid accidentally doing a DP. The D version is Preecha's best blockstring ender. Due to being safe and frame trapping off lights,and even setting up a small RPS situation on block with breaking the second hit. The BD version is great to set up Circular step mixups on block.",
    "2a": "Preecha's fastest move along with Close A, but with pitiful range. Seriously, it's awful. Only useful as a way to combo after 214A/C > 6C Avoid this move outside of combos, use 5A instead.",
    "2b": "Preecha's fastest low. Deceptive hitbox, it doesn't reach as far as it may appear. Can combo into 2A or 5A, making it your primary light starter.",
    "2c": "Links from close.D and close.C feints Okay poke, slightly longer reaching than Far A but still prone to whiffing in footsies. Very fast whiff punish tool for its reward, it leads to a full combo on Wild Punish. Safe on block at all times and only -1 on feint cancel, a decent pressure tool when out of close C and D's range. Great defensive button to check forward movement and a strong frame trap after a blocked close.D and close. C",
    "2crev": "Anti-air and panic button against Rev Blow. Mostly really bad, outdone as an anti-air by 623D feint, and its disappointing range leaves it exclusively as a counterpoke against grounded opponents, where it's very whiff punishable despite its low reward. Can still be useful in scrambles or against hops. On anti air counter hit or wild punish, your best cancel option is 214BC. All other options have a big tendency to whiff.",
    "2d": "8 frame start up making it one of the fastest sweeps in the game.Unfourtunatly it has less than average range. Not very commonly used.",
    "4ab": "Back throw that leaves the opponent at a fairly close range. The range allows for Preecha to cross-up with a meaty Heavy Hyperbolic Kick.",
    "562bcordrev": "Preecha's only feint. Primarily used in combos and pressure, grants Preecha fantastic frames on most of her cancellable heavies. Looks somewhat similar to her fireballs' startup animations which are very weak to jumpins, so you can bait a jump and punish with an anti-air.",
    "5crev": "Low and Throw invul overhead. Can be timed to be +1 on block or comboable on hit. One of the better low dodge attacks in the game with great range, start up, active, and recovery.",
    "623b46k": "Follow up to Sine-Cosine, sends the opponent back to the ground . Adds a bit of damage to your DP's, After 623BD you can do a rev blow to get a hard knockdown Worse knockdown than 623B/D midscreen, but near the corner they're about the same so there's little reason not to use this. In patch 1.7.2, added a ground bounce property on the final hit of 623BD >4/6k which allows combo extensions.",
    "623bdbd": "Preecha's anti air special and combo tool. B version has full invulnerability and can go through most fireballs, except for a few slower ones. (like Mai\u2019s 236A or Kain\u2019s 236236B) D/REV versions can be cancelled using brake. Braked D version is the ideal anti-air since it allows you to follow-up and remain safer if it's Just Defended. Braked D version is used to combo off of light confirms or extend juggles. It has 5f extra recovery on whiff, however Preecha's DP break is one of the best in the game with great range, even allowing her to connect it from a 3 light attack string",
    "ab": "It sends the opponent about 3/4 of the screen away, but plus enough to still get oki. In midscreen,Light Screw Leg can be used right after the throw to make the opponent wake up to a very meaty(+26) projectile. In the corner Preecha's meaty fireball set up becomes weaker, but she gains a scary high-low mix-up by using her circular step stance. After a forward throw in the corner preecha can do a forward hop then do circular step into 3 powerful options: A very meaty arc elbow(+6 overhead), a slightly delayed low angle kick(-4 on block low with pushback), and a very meaty parabola knee, which is 0 on block and gives a massive launch on counterhit. Preecha's forward throw is an important part of her gameplan and complements her high low gameplan. Preecha gets extremely strong okizeme from her forward throw at any part of the screen and can snowball the rest of the round by one throw.",
    "cd": "Slow, but far-reaching and safe Rev Blow Can be used as a way to close in, especially against Billy or characters throwing fireballs mid-range. Staggers the enemy on counter-hit, allowing for a combo after. Quite situational to use with not many uses in neutral. Avoid using it too much as opponents can high dodge attack or grab on reaction Combos only from 236AB(LAST HIT) and 236BD( FIRST HIT) Preecha travels a distance along the ground and does a big uppercut. This rev blow is not very combo-friendly, usually requiring Preecha to do it very early in the combo, or spend a lot of Rev meter to end the combo with it. It however has extremely big corner carry when used at the end of the combo and gives a hard knockdown",
    "cla": "Tied for Preecha's fastest move, but its range is limited. Will be used in light confirms, usually to link after a Cr. B, and to then cancel into a braked 623D. Better than 2A in most abare situations by virtue of its input; 2A is so likely to miss that pressing 5A for an OS between this and f.A is better almost every time.",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC and cl.ACC is the same move as close C and far C but the framedata and damage is different. D ender : Unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Overhead ender, +2F advantage on hit and block. 2D ender : Low ender, very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "Quick light normal and +3 on block, sets up frametraps with f.A, f.B and 2C. Great combo opportunities off this normal, links into c.C and your only light that can combo into 236A/C. Very good after a jumpin or dash and not fully outshined by c.C, as this is more plus.",
    "clc": "Your main close combo starter, cancellable and combos into any special you'd want. +2 on block and +7 on hit if feint cancelled, great for frametraps and its link into 2C is a core route for Preecha.",
    "cld": "+4 on block if feint cancelled, your best meaty normal if you can get it out in time. Can be confirmed into a combo with Cr.C if it's feint-cancelled. Great for punishes and setting up a rewarding frametrap. In patch 1.7.2, allowed this move to combo to Far C (+8) which deals more damage than Cr.C and opens more damaging routes.",
    "fa": "A standard 5 frame poke with decent range, the farthest of your 5 framers. Works as a consistent frametrap from c.B (2f gap), c.D (3f) and c.C > Feint (1f, beats Rev Blow) You'll often cancel this move into braked 623D (provided you're close enough for it to connect), as it can start a combo on hit, and leaves you safe if blocked.",
    "fb": "A quick poke with good range Only super cancellable and it's not a confirm. Conversions off this are pretty limited as a result. Leads into her B>D command combo. Not many uses for this move over all. Use Far C or Far D instead in neutral.",
    "fbfd": "Two hit target combo that's safe on block with no combo options. Improves far B as a poke, if you don't have a confirm this is completely safe to go for unless the opponent is able to hyper defense through it. Pretty unrewarding outside of that, but still better than a standalone far B.",
    "fc": "Lunges forward a bit. Has good range as a result, especially by Preecha's standards. Preecha's longest special cancellable poke, very commonly cancelled into 236AC for combos or plus frames, though it's single hit confirmable so you don't need to spend bar if you're looking only to convert. Also very useful when converting from Wild Punish stagger hits, and it's also a Wild Punish stagger normal itself. One of the strongest Far C attacks in the game that boasts extremely good properties and frame data. This button will be one of your main buttons to use in neutral",
    "fd": "Long range kick with a small amount of forward movement Preecha's longest normal and very fast for its kind, your main poking button and the only reason Preecha can even play footsies in certain matchups. While not special-cancellable, it can lead to big damage as it staggers on Wild Punish or if you buffer a Super. Does good rev damage on block while being mostly safe (Hotaru is fine guys).",
    "ja": "Fast jump normal with a bad hitbox for jumpins but a better one for air to airs. Can hit standing opponents on the way up for a minimal reward throw bait that also beats jumps and hops. Leads into Preecha's jA > jB command combo, which is only really useful for mixing up guard cancels or air-to-air Just Defense.",
    "jajb": "Only available during jump A, hop A is not cancellable. j.B follow-up is not an overhead. 2nd hit does not combo on normal hit. Needs an aerial counter hit or wild punish to connect Fairly good and fast air to air option, gives alot of plus frames on hit and allows her to start her offense",
    "jb": "Is able to cross-up, which is naturally very useful. Easily combos into your faster normals, so it's an overall strong jumpin. Has a good air-to-air hitbox as well.",
    "jc": "Good for air-to-airs and surprisingly for jump-ins as well. Generally your best jump normal besides j.B Has the highest base damage of Preecha's air normals, so it's a good combo starter.",
    "jcd": "A mid, despite looking like an overhead. Available after 214BD, 214AC > 6D, and 623BD (in place of 623BD > 6B/D) to secure a stronger knockdown with added damage.Note that in long juggles, 623BD into Air rev blow has a HIGH tendency to not connect. Preecha's only jumping Guard Cancel, but not particularly good at beating anti-airs or hitting grounded opponents because of its poor hitbox and halted downwards momentum. Preecha does a flying downward's angled axe kick. This move is very combo friendly and important for Preecha's oki and overhead/low setplay.",
    "jd": "Good jump-in hitbox but a terrible air-to-air one on top of its slow startup. Lower damage than j.C as well, it's usually a better idea to throw that one out instead."
  },
  "rick_strowd": {
    "214acac": "Rick sways back and forth in place, dodging incoming projectiles. Hold the punch button(s) to keep swaying indefinitely, retaining projectile invincibility all the while If you merely tap the button(s), you get only one frame of the invincibility, not enough to dodge anything placeholder placeholder Regular version (214A/C) placeholder REV version (214A/C) placeholder",
    "214bdbd": "Rick dashes backward a short distance. Light version moves back the furthest, heavy moves back the least, REV is in between Which strength of dash you input determines which Divine Blast you\u2019ll get Not invincible to throws, unlike a regular backdash placeholder",
    "214bdbdp": "Rick comes back with a rushing straight punch. placeholder placeholder Light version (214B > P) placeholder Heavy version (214D > P) placeholder REV version (214BD > P) placeholder",
    "236acac": "Rick winds up and unleashes a haymaker. Nearly every version is indentical on hit, a soft knockdown On block, both versions cause absolutely massive REV buildup for a special move placeholder Regular version (236A/C) placeholder REV version (236AC) placeholder",
    "236bdbd": "Rick ducks and dashes forward. placeholder placeholder",
    "236bdc": "Rick does a series of punches ending in a leaping uppercut. placeholder placeholder",
    "236bp236da": "Rick does a series of four rapid punches. Cancels to Aggressor Stance on hit or block Neither version does chip damage until the last hit placeholder Regular version (236B/D > A) placeholder REV version (236BD > A) placeholder",
    "236dc": "Rick does a series of four heavy punches. Cancels to Aggressor Stance on hit or block placeholder placeholder",
    "2crev": "Cannot be cancelled on block. Wins against jumping attacks, standing normals and REV Blows. Triggers a slowdown if it meets any of these.",
    "46ac": "Stance with multiple follow-ups. A jab, overhead, low, counter and a combo finisher. Can be canceled out of his 236K follow-ups. The cancel is slow so it can't be used to reversals, so Rick has to hard commit to counter to beat them.",
    "46aca": "Quick jab mainly used for frame trapping the opponent and extending some combos. Best way to deter people mashing out of your mix by frame trapping into desired follow up or just doing more jabs to catch their escape attempt. Overall low reward but is the best way to get any sort of offense started from stance without HKD.",
    "46acac": "Automatically exits stance on hit, block or whiff. Pretty punishable on block so be sure to confirm it, also a nice ender from routes that go into stance mid combo.",
    "46acb": "Counter from stance that can be used to hard call out reversals, however it loses to projectiles and lows. Not the most rewarding option either since midscreen it gets very little on hit.",
    "46acc": "Overhead follow-up. High risk for pretty low reward, even from a stance jab it can be mashed out with 5 framers, throws and jumped over for a full punish. Pretty fast overhead at frame 20 though so if you make your opponent respect you enough you can open them up with it.",
    "46acd": "Low follow-up. Catches mashes with buttons from stance jab as a frame trap and also combos if the first stance jab was a CH. Other than that, similar risk reward to Starfall as it is still throwable on the gap and jumpable for a full punish.",
    "56bcor56drev": "Mimics Blazing Sunburst, Rick's only Feint.",
    "5crev": "Universal overhead, wins against lows and throws. Triggers a slowdown if it meets either of these.",
    "623acac": "Rick hooks then uppercuts, forming a cross of light. All versions are invincible to air attacks on startup Unlike most other anti-air specials, Rick isn\u2019t Counter Hit punishable in recovery placeholder placeholder Light version (623A) placeholder Heavy version (623C) placeholder REV version (623AC) placeholder",
    "cla": "Rick\u2019s fastest attack Used to stop hops, in light confirms, or to perform his Combination Attack Chains from both crouching normals point-blank, on hit only",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. cl.AC first hit is cancellable by any follow-ups. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : First hit of the auto combo, cancellable by any follow-ups. cl.AC > C : A second hit cancellable by any ender. -2 on block, safe unless guard cancelled. D ender : Far D follow up, different frame data and damage, very unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Safest ender, Same move as his Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV) but +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Same move as his 2D, meaning it's very unsafe on block.",
    "fa": "Chains from other lights. Technically chains to itself, but this chain whiffs more often than not Good damage for a far A, but slower startup than average",
    "fb": "Very similar to far A, including range, speed and special cancel Does great damage for a light normal, but you can\u2019t chain out of it, making it worse vs guard point attacks",
    "fc": "Wild Punish +19~+24 Huge range, hitting from round start distance after a microwalk The hitbox advances while it\u2019s active, making it much better when spaced Up to +9 on Counter Hit",
    "fd": "Wild Punish +19 (+23 when feint cancelled) Rick\u2019s longest-ranged normal that still cancels to special moves"
  },
  "rock_howard": {
    "214acac": "Every versions are Brake-able before they connect with the opponent. Rock's lariat, a nice combo ender because it can connect from any range and travel half of the screen(~40% for the A version). A version : Can be safe if spaced. It\u2019s a good tool to end a combo from lights because it works at any range. As of Season 3, this recovers 2 to 9 frames slower on hit only (depending on when it hits), severely hindering its okizeme if the opponent wakeup rolls. C version : Unsafe on block at any range because a follow-up comes out when Rock reaches the opponent on hit and on block. Good heavy combo ender because it works at any range and does some decent damage. AC version : Same as C version, a follow-up comes out when Rock reaches opponent on hit and on block, but this version is REV cancellable so it can be safe in exchange of REV. With some REV, we can call this one the neutral skip version.",
    "214bdbd": "REV Rage Run turns all follow-ups into their REV versions at no additional REV cost. Command run with a variety of options.",
    "214bdbda": "A slow leaping slash that can cross up and is plus on block, though Rock is usually left too far away to get any meaningful pressure afterward. How plus Rock is on hit and block is dependent on whether the opponent blocked it standing or crouching and/or how late into the animation Rock hit them with it. The REV version is not much different aside from being slightly faster and doing a bit more damage; it mainly sees use as a combo tool in Rock's bread and butter REV Accel combo, being the harder but more damaging counterpart of REV Type: Dunk.",
    "214bdbdb": "REV version can combo into light if close enough. Low follow-up that is safe with a minimum of spacing.",
    "214bdbdc": "REV version is plus on block, meaning it's a good ender during a REV Accel blockstring. Safe follow-up, faster than the A follow-up and cannot be punished on block, and the REV version is positif.",
    "214bdbdd": "Swaps sides with the opponent when done up close, or simply stops Rock in his tracks when done further away. This move can phase through the opponent(even in the corner) but the recover is longer in this case.",
    "214bdbddacac": "Identical to the standard (632146P) version. B/D Rage Run can use the A, C, or AC versions. BD Rage Run always uses the REV version of Shinkuu Nage, regardless of input, with no extra REV cost. This move can be performed regardless of whether or not Rock switches sides with the opponent.",
    "236236ac": "Both version are invincible Big vertical super. High amount of active frames can make it awkward to punish on whiff. A version : One hit big damage super, can be used as a reversal but it's slower than a DP. C version : Deal a lot of damage and multi-hit, because of that it's harder for the opponent to air just defend it.",
    "236236bd": "D version is invincible and is brake-able at the third hit. Rock's horizontal super, not a really good reversal because it's a bit slow and only the D version is invincible. B version : Not invincible and loose against projectile, it can be used as an ender in a light combo, giving some nice damage and a hard knockdown. D version : This version invincibility combined with its fast travel time, makes it a good way to blow through fireballs in neutral. And you can brake it, meaning you can extend your combo or choose your ender.",
    "236236rev": "Resets the Rev meter to 0%, even during Overheat. Only available during S.P.G.",
    "236acac": "C and AC version are two hit, the first hit can nullify a projectile and the second can go through the projectile. Rock's grounded fireball that travels fullscreen. A version : Standard one hit fireball. Longer startup than the other version, but the fireball comes out faster and has less recovery. This makes it ideal for neutral and zoning situations compared to the other two. C version : The Double Reppuken. Rock leaves a small hitbox in front of him before firing a fireball. The first hit can destroy other fireballs, allowing the second to go through. Extremely slow compared to the A version, meaning you should save this for counter-zoning in fireball wars. Worth noting that Rock can also use this after a juggle into DP Brake in the corner to do 623C Brake > 236C > 623A/AC or 236236C/REV. While the timing is tight, and the follow-ups are limited due to the specific juggle, it's a good way to tack on extra damage without spending REV gauge. AC version : An enhanced version of the C Double Reppuken, sharing its initial hitbox into fireball. Plus on block, allowing you to pressure reset with it. This version is very strong for REV chains, allowing you to use it before something like 214AC for a hit confirm that leaves you plus (at the cost of some REV) or in an extended juggle sequence, like 214BD~C, 236AC > 214BD~A. Note that in that juggle, you are linking 236AC, so your REV gauge cost will not be reduced.",
    "236bdbd": "B and BD version counter mid and high D version counter low The follow-up is 1F and triggers automatically when Rock successfully counters a move. Is able to counter Rev blows Rock's counter stance, you have to choose between Mid/High and Low. It's not a real reversal, it as some startup before it's able to counter a move, it cause a counter hit if hit during start-up and wild punish during recovery. It can be used against frametrap if the gap between the two hit is long enough and the REV version allow Rock to start a combo by REV cancelling.",
    "2a": "Rock does a crouching jab. Simple jab, good for mashing out and to apply pressure due to its chain cancel. The special cancel allows for cancels like 2B > 2A > 214A on hit, making 2A valuable in that aspect. 2A is also an alternative normal used for linking due to its horizontal range compared to 2C. This makes it more useful after something like 214BD~B, where you're left +5 on hit, but not close enough to link into 2C.",
    "2b": "Rock kicks at their ankles. Rock's go-to low, and a strong one at that. Chain cancels allow for a hit confirm into f.B, which can grant a knockdown or combo depending on range. Outside of that, it sports long range, allowing Rock to catch people walking back in neutral or pressure by simply running and hitting 2B. A very abusable normal for Rock.",
    "2bcor2drev": "Mimics Hard Edge. 30 total frames.",
    "2c": "Rock does a crouching uppercut. Somewhat subpar for anti-airing due to its tendency to trade, but it's Rock's main tool for linking. The special cancel allows Rock to cancel into 623C brake, 214AC, or other various party starters. Notably can be linked after c.A and c.D feint.",
    "2crev": "Cannot be cancelled on block Wins against jump attack, standing normals and REV Blows. Easier to execute than a DP, but upper body invulnerability doesn\u2019t start on frame 1, so it can lose to jump-ins and is punishable on block.",
    "2d": "Rock does a sweep with his leg. Standard sweep, not much to write home about in this aspect. Can be used as an alternative Wild Punisher over f.D, though the reward is immensely weaker. Good to catch opponents walking back out of your pressure or in neutral, but you're not in range to hit 2B.",
    "4ab": "Hard knockdown, end closer than his forward throw but with less frame advantage.",
    "5bcor5drev": "Mimics Rising Tackle. 24 total frames.",
    "5crev": "Rock's universal overhead. Unlike other 5CR attacks, this one is not granted airborne status and can be grabbed at any point in the animation. Cannot start a combo unless it hits as a Counter Hit / Wild Punish, or as a meaty.",
    "623acac": "C and AC versions are Brakable after the first hit. Rock's dragon punch with a decent speed, a good reversal or anti-air tool. A version : Rock fastest reversal, fully invincible but really unsafe on block. Can be nice to use in a guard cancel situation to punish recover or any cancel because it's fast and invincible but for the reward is low. C version : Still fast and air invincible, by far is best choice to anti-air and the Brake version allows Rock to start a combo. It can be comboed from lights if close enough (cl.A > cl.B > DP+C or 2B > far B > DP+C for example). Also the Brake version is safe on block at -2, meaning it's a good tool to use in confirms and as a Guard Cancel. As of Season 3, the Braked version recovers 9 frames slower on whiff. AC version : Nearly the same than the C version exept it's faster and it hit further, so it's easier to combo from light. Like the C version, Braking this makes it safe on block, and it's a faster Guard Cancel option than the C version, but it costs a lot of REV. As of Season 3, the Braked version recovers 7 frames slower on whiff.",
    "632146acac": "Rock's special throw, can be used manually at any by doing the 632146+P input or as a Type: Shift follow-up by pressing any P buttons. A version : Is kinda fast and throw the opponent really far away without any way to cancel Rock's animation. C version : Slower, also throw the opponent far away but you can brake it and be able to move before the opponent fall on the ground. In brake version, the throw deal less damage but you have a better advantage and also you can choose to combo(214C, 236236B for exemple). The issue with that is the damage reduction is really high. AC version : Same speed than the A version and have an automatically follow-up that allow Rock to combo with less damage reduction than the C version.",
    "6bcor6drev": "Mimics Reppuu Ken. 20 total frames. Rock's fastest feint and the one used for his normal frame data. Use this when canceling a normal to gain more advantage.",
    "6ja": "Can only be performed from a forward jump. Jump version is special cancelable and by j.B (Command Combo 2). Can be used air-to-air, cancelling this move will combo in the air on counter hit.",
    "6jajb": "Cannot be performed out of hop.",
    "ab": "Hard knockdown with a good amount of advantage and the opponent is always at jump distance for Rock.",
    "cla": "+5 on hit, can link into 2C. A strong standing jab and Rock's fastest normal. Can be chained into other lights like cl.B for better frame advantage or 2B for a low option.",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : Starter of the combination attack, deals some damage. cl.AC > C : Follow-up that adds damage and gives time to choose the ender. D ender : Very unsafe on block but causes a soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Overhead ender, same move as his Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV) but it's +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Low ender, same as his 2D, it's very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "Quick knee with fewer cancel options than cl.A, but better range and frame advantage. On block, it's more pressure-oriented than cl.A, letting Rock go for a throw, a spacing trap, or a shimmy.",
    "clc": "Rock performs a left hook. Weaker than far C, but more reliable than cl.D, which is less effective without proper spacing. If out of range, f.C comes out instead \u2014 still solid, and safer than cl.D but with lower reward. +2 on block with forward feint cancel but only +3 on hit, leaving you no ability to link for a combo, so avoid autopiloting. Other Feint framedata : Neutral Feint(5BC) : -1 on hit, -2 on block. Down Feint(2BC) : -7 on hit, -8 on block.",
    "cld": "Can be cancelled into 6D for a target combo. On hit, link to 2C if cancelled by forward feint(+5). Force the opponent to stand on hit. Rock raises his leg for a devastating kick. A haymaker normal with great versatility, positive on block when cancelled into forward feint. Its high REV gauge damage and frame advantage make it a strong close-range normal that can easily be autopiloted on hit or block to maintain pressure. Other Feint framedata: Neutral Feint : +1 on hit, -2 on block Down Feint : -5 on hit, -8 on block",
    "cld6d": "cl.D > 6D is blockstring. An easy confirm tool that is safe on block if canceled with a forward feint.",
    "fa": "Rock\u2019s standing jab is his fastest far normal, can be chained into itself and is special cancellable on hit and block, allowing a knockdown with 214A on hit. Its range is decent for a Light normal, but lacking a cancel into f.B makes long-range confirms harder.",
    "fb": "Rock does a kick to the shins. Despite the kick being aimed towards the opponent's legs, it is not a Low. A strong poke and easy hit confirm. Rock can chain into this after 2A or 2B, making it an easy autopilot option when up close. It's special cancelable, so Rock can use f.B to end his light chains with a cancel into Light Hard Edge. It's also a strong counter-poke; Rock can throw out f.B in neutral and buffer REV Hard Edge so that if he Counter Hits a poke, he has enough frame advantage to combo into REV Hard Edge and transition into his bread and butter combo.",
    "fc": "A strong mid-rage poke that moves Rock forward a bit, has good range and is special cancelable. Good for neutral and for catching opponents pressing buttons in spacing traps. Use it with caution though, as its recovery can lead to a Wild Punish if whiff punished.",
    "fd": "+22 after Wild Punish, meaning Rock can run in and do f.C for a follow-up combo. The Wild Punisher. Rock does a somewhat slow side kick, It\u2019s a decent long-range poke but can only be super cancelled, with practice you can hit-confirm into a buffered Shining Knuckle. On block this move deals good REV damage, but its main use is for Wild Punishing. Combined with f.C\u2019s range and special cancel, it\u2019s easy to follow up with a combo at mid-range after a Wild Punish by running in and doing f.C \u2014 however, at farther ranges you may need to dash more in order to connect with f.C and you might not be able to do so if you're too far away. In these situations, Rock can instead opt to just link into REV Hard Edge and forgo the run into f.C.",
    "j214acac": "A angle is a bit upward, C downward and AC is between both of them. A and C version are only available during neutral and forward jump. REV version fires two projectiles and can be used in any jump direction and also backdash. Rock's air projectile, cancel his jump momentum to do a small back hop while launching the projectile.",
    "ja": "Only available during neutral and back hop/jump. A really fast(3F) vertical air to air move.",
    "jb": "Can cross-up Rock's jump attack with the longest horizontal range, can be used as an air-to-air.",
    "jc": "Jump-in attack with the highest damage and better horizontal reach than j.D.",
    "jd": "Slower and deals less damage than j.C but has a better vertical hitbox."
  },
  "salvatore_ganacci": {
    "214acac": "No differences between 214A and 214D A stance that gives Salvatore access to a true 50/50 on block. Only 214AC gets used due to the unsafe and committal nature of the regular versions and their lack of any meaningful reward.",
    "214acaca": "Cancels the stance Fast recovery Used to exit out of groove. Useful if opponent is attempting to reversal in the gap of Gain.",
    "214acacb": "Three hits Can be cancelled into any follow up Technically the fastest move in the game that is not a reversal at 2 frames. Used to make both options on the 50/50 gapless, but the reactable hit pattern makes it weak to Hyper Defense if the opponent is ready, in which case going straight into the mixup options is preferable.",
    "214acacc": "Slightly faster than the low option Can be delayed The overhead option off Groove. Will frametrap any attempts to punish if slightly delayed. Also leads to a Hard Knockdown on OTG hit, which Salvatore appreciates when available.",
    "214acacddd": "Slightly slower than overhead Can be delayed Safe on REV guard The low option off Groove. Requires Scratch beforehand to true string. Will only true string into Rev Blow on first hit.",
    "214bdbd": "214BD runs faster than normal versions No notable differences between 214B and 214D Will automatically do Stop follow up after a certain amount of time Salvatore's command run. It's faster than his regular dash and gives him access to oki he would otherwise struggle to get. It has some evasive properties allowing him to run under some moves his dash would get clipped by like Rock's Air Fireballs.",
    "214bdbdab": "Cancels Amazing Run Fast on recovery Used to halt Amazing Run to avoid committing. Can be used to setup strike throw from 236BD cancel.",
    "214bdbdc": "Safe On 214BD, the two hits will always come out even on whiff True strings into 214AC-B on 214BD Rev version is the tends to be the only version used Can be used as a frame trap and set up into his 50/50 while still having access to stomp for plus frames. A really good neutral skip as well if the opponent starts respecting 214BD-D",
    "214bdbdd": "Can be made safe if spaced or meatied on normal version Rev Version is always safe, or occasionally plus when it hits late enough. Low-hitting slide ender, at least the meterless versions, which can be harder to space. The Rev Version is an unreactable half screen low that, while not very rewarding, is always safe on block, with Rev Accel options in tow. Very strong for obvious reasons.",
    "236236ac": "1F counter super, win against any attack exept cross-up and throw. Because it's 1 frame, it can't be safe-jumped. The C version isn\u2019t much different from the A version, aside from dealing more damage and having more active frame.",
    "236236bd": "Both version are full invincible D version will always play an unskippable dance animation on K.O B version has more range while the D version is faster Can be used has a reversal or a combo ender.",
    "236236rev": "Resets the Rev meter to 0%, even during Overheat. Only available in S.P.G.",
    "236acac": "Frame 1 air invulnerable Gives full combo on counter hit 236C is the slowest, 236AC is the fastest, 236A is the middle ground Salvatore's best anti air option. It is fast and can be hit confirmed if it counter hit or not into one of the follows. It serves as his best guard cancel option as well due to the speed of the attack and easy input. Each one has their different merits to using as a guard cancel. 236A is the fastest meterless guard cancel option 236C gives the most reward off a successful guard cancel 236AC is the overall fastest and safest with S.P.G. but the most expensive and scales the hardest",
    "236acaca": "Can stall Sky's beat Combo follow up that can allow links into Air Dive on situational hits. Serves as Salvatore's main way of countering Just Defends on his anti air.",
    "236acacb": "Fast recovery On 236A, links only into 236236rev on the lowest hit and the others if hit high enough On 236C, links into 236236D and 236236rev on the lowest hit and will link into 236236B if hit high enough. If close enough also links into 236AC. This makes 236C the most rewarding and preferred anti air option On 236AC, links into every super regardless of height but the scaling is significantly worse Used to get better oki, land back on the ground for follow ups on counter hit or hit high enough, or land to link into supers",
    "236acacc": "Brings you straight to the ground Combo follow up if Sky's Beat connects. Very inconsistent in frame data and can very often leave you negative on hit if the opponent rolls towards you.",
    "236bdbd": "Regular versions are both safe All versions lose to Rev Blow For the anti projectile properties, all versions have to be timed with the projectile B Version Combos from lights Frame traps from lights Gives small combo on counter hit Nullify projectiles A safe true string block ender from heavies and the go to frame trap from lights. Due to long active frames, can easily setup up meaty stomps that can be up to +4 on block and +8 on hit. It is your go to to nullify projectiles on reaction. D Version Most optimal DP punish starter Frame traps and combos from cl.C Nullify projectiles On hit, can link into 2C Mainly used as damaging combo filler and his most damaging punish starter on DP reversals. It is pretty slow to nullify projectiles so it can only be used from full screen. BD Version First hit can break to be +4 True strings into Groove stance 50/50 or Amazing Run strike throw situation Can reflect projectiles True strings from heavies Salvatore's most important pressure tool and combo extender. On hit it leads to all his optimal Rev combos. On block it gets his offense going even when not cancelled into anything. Break is the preferred pressure choice due to the strength of being able to catch mashing and jumps/backdash with 2C. However letting it rip is also good to setup all the potential mixup scenarios and be +2 but right up the opponent's face. It is his strongest tool against ex fireballs being able to stop most of opponents attempts to skip neutral moving behind them.",
    "2a": "Best light button Combos into 236B on hit / 236BD on counterhit His main light confirm and mash button. Can be also used to apply pressure.",
    "2b": "Hits low Moves slightly forward His low poke that serves as his main low option for his Magical Step setups.",
    "2c": "Short range -1 on block after feint It is solid to frame trap with and to setup gapless 236BD pressure with. Can also be used as a close ranged whiff punish.",
    "2crev": "Beats Highs and Rev Blows Combos into 236D on grounded counter hit Short range evasive poke and anti air for Salvatore. Has no forward movement, is on the slower side for high dodge attacks, and due to the high recovery very risky overall.",
    "2d": "Sweep Below average sweep with shorter range than most in the game.",
    "46ac": "Can be dash canceled Can go from Forward Magical Step to Backward Magical Step and vice versa All follow ups except Magical Kick can go back into stance One of his best oki tools and the cornerstone to what makes him scary on knockdown. This stances gives him access to multiple overheads, a fast special cancellable mid, and a slow rekka series of attacks. It can be used in pressure too if the opponent gives enough respect to your other options.",
    "46ac6auptox3": "Hits mid on every attack Highly unsafe A slow rekka series that doesn't lead to much reward on hit and is always unsafe on block no matter the follow up. Doesn't serve much of a purpose at all.",
    "46ac6c": "Fastest overhead Requires a +7 meaty hit to link into stance dash cancel cl.C Can be plus on block from stance cancel depending on the meaty His main overhead to mix in from Magical Step.",
    "46acb": "Hits overhead despite its unusual animation. Requires a +7 meaty hit to link into stance dash cancel cl.C Can be plus on block from stance cancel depending on the meaty A tricky slow overhead that can catch the opponent off guard to its animation.",
    "46acd": "Special cancellable +1 on block after feint This move is his main way to combo off his overheads in stance. It can be used as a meaty due to it being plus on feint along with being albeit a very risky pressure reset.",
    "4ab": "Dashing twice will leave you at 0, while wavedashing will grant you some advantage, up to +5.",
    "562bcordrev": "Stomp feint Moves him forward slightly Salvatore's only feint attack",
    "5crev": "One of the quickest low dodge attacks in the game at 21 frames that is good at throw baits or getting a quick kill Can link into 2A on meaty setups Combos into 2C on counter hit",
    "ab": "Dashing twice leaves you at +1 right next to the opponent, while wavedashing gives you significantly more frame advantage, up to +7.",
    "cd": "Travels 1/4 of the screen Standard ground Rev Blow that is really susceptible to jumps. One of his better guard cancel options.",
    "cla": "Fastest normal Starts his Combination attack Combos into 236B on hit / 236BD on counterhit Can link into 236C and 236AC after two in a row on hit in the corner A quick anti air and solid pressure tool being able to frame trap with 236BD from it. It's vital for Sal to get reward from his lows to link into Combination attack from this move.",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Seem to be a bug : REV Blow dont deal any guard damage during Combination Attack(Ver 1.1.7-76419). Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC and cl.ACC is the same move as close C and far C but the framedata and damage is different. D ender : Unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Overhead ender, +2F advantage on hit and block. 2D ender : Low ender, very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "Has a target combo Links into cl.C on CH His go to pressure starter. Can frame trap with 2C against 5f and 2A against 4f.",
    "clbb": "Links into 2C on hit His light confirm into heavy attack. 2C will whiff at some ranges.",
    "clc": "Main heavy starter +4 on block after feint with huge pushback Can link into 2C on hit after feint Punish starter that combos into 236D on regular hit. Pushback makes it very vulnerable to REV Guard limiting its offensive capabilities.",
    "cld": "Cannot be feinted Three hits Multi-hitting normal that can be used as a meaty and as a deterrent for guard cancel happy opponents. Very vulnerable to REV Guard as well.",
    "fa": "Combos into itself and Supers / 236B on counterhit A situational anti air and not much else despite being plus on block",
    "fb": "Good range With target combo can hit confirm into super Good light poke and his longest ranged light button.",
    "fbb": "Follow up for f.B used to confirm into super.",
    "fc": "First hit whiffs on crouchers Combos both on regular and counter hit Longest range of his buttons This two hitting forward advancing move serves as Salvatore's best overall button. It is a Swiss army knife that can be used as an approach, a whiff punish, to continue pressure, and even catch opponents trying to jump.",
    "fd": "Fantastic range Links into lights on CH if close Salvatore's less committal long range poke. It has great range and is one of his best whiff punish options.",
    "ja": "Fastest jump normal Crosses up A quick air to air when you are right below the opponent and the first of Salvatore's wopping three crossup normals, this one being the fastest",
    "jb": "Longest reaching air normal Crosses up Salvatore's second crossup normal, slower than j.A but with better reach. It is his best option for horizontal air-to-airs along with being his longest ranged jump in attack.",
    "jc": "Third crossup; his go to air normal with a lot of active frames, this move is basically half the character. Salvatore's model doesn't visually match his collision box, which can make it seem like he's actually rubber banding when attempting an ambiguous crossup. Doesn't cross up on hop. Salvatore's most infamous move and for good reason. It is a massive jump-in that's active until landing, leads to huge damage, is very plus and can crossup. With this, Salvatore has easy access to ambiguous, visually disorienting and inconsistent crossups and a midrange approach strategy that's very annoying to consistently anti-air.",
    "jcd": "Cancels air momentum Hits on frame 28 Very slow as the hitbox doesn't come out until he reaches the ground. Has very niche use.",
    "jd": "Combos into lights on hit / close C on counter hit Changes air movement on a set arc depending on jump direction and momentum. Can hit up to 7 times Salvatore's most unorthodox air normal. The stall of his air momentum allows him to avoid projectiles, bait out any anti air attempts, jump over some normal and specials, and even just use it as a form of mobility. Additionally, the set arc it puts Salvatore on can even be used to create an intentionally shallow jump to have j.D hit early and attempt to reset pressure this way."
  },
  "terry_bogard": {
    "214acac": "Terry rushes straight at the opponent fist-first. You can combo into the light version from 2A or Power Dunk Brake, and REV versions from heavies. Heavy version is too slow to combo from anything that isn\u2019t a Wild Punish. All versions launch on Counter Hit. You can Brake the move to stop it short. More advantageous when properly spaced, use this farther out to avoid taking a big punish. Terry\u2019s signature move. In neutral, it\u2019s best used to approach behind fireballs, Brake to intimidate your opponent and possibly force reactions, and punish whiffs from a further distance than your normals allow. The light version combos from 2A and is your best meterless juggle when it\u2019s available. The REV version is Terry\u2019s go-to REV starter, since it combos from heavies and picks up combos nicely from Power Dunk brake, but some REV Accel sequences that combo on hit (namely to REV Crack Shoot) have gaps on block that can be mashed out of or worse, High Dodge Attacked, so it\u2019s not as mindless a starter as it may seem. The heavy version is too slow to combo from anything reliably, but it makes up for it with massive damage and being the only version that can be made plus on block. If you manage to land a Counter Hit with it, such as when punishing a DP, it will hit like a freight train and launch into a full combo with no scaling, meaning you can easily take half their life or more with the ensuing combo.",
    "214bdbd": "Terry performs a somersault heel drop. More advantageous on block when crouched. D and BD versions launch on Counter Hit. In the corner, if the D version hits a standing opponent, Terry can combo into light Power Dunk. Against a crouching opponent, Terry can go into heavy Power Dunk Brake. In certain juggles, it\u2019s possible to combo Power Geyser afterward depending on the opponent\u2019s height. In the corner, you can Power Dunk Brake for a big combo. Another advancing special, one that sees Terry hurtling through the air. It\u2019s one of the safest moves Terry can throw out without having to spend meter, but if your opponent knows it\u2019s coming, it\u2019s an easy High Dodge Attack for them. To that end, mix it up with your other approach options to keep them honest in neutral. Where this special shines is in its ability to go over certain pokes or fireballs to either close the gap on the opponent or even punish them outright. As a result, it\u2019s better done reactively than proactively, but if they\u2019re too slow to react and beat it, the Counter Hit launch combined with its short recovery will make them regret ever trying to stop it.",
    "236236ac": "Both versiion are fully invincible. A version : Fast and fully invincible with a massive hitbox, one of the most effective anti-airs in the game. C version : If braked from a raw hit and followed up with the entire suite of corner REV Accels, it does a minuscule amount of extra damage (381 vs 363).",
    "236236bd": "Terry's horizontal super. B version : Not invincible and loose against projectile. D version : Can work as a fireball punish at a pinch, but is less effective compared to ignition Power Geyser and REV Blow as it doesn't have many invulnerability frames. Unlike Ignition Buster Wolf, this vacuums the opponent down to the ground and kills any follow-up combo possibilities.",
    "236236rev": "Terry summons swirling winds and does a spinning side kick. If it connects, Terry does a high kick with his other leg, sending his opponent into the eye of a large tornado. Terry then leaps into the air and slams his fist into the ground, igniting and infusing the tornado with energy. Terry then shouts, channeling more energy into the tornado and causes it to explode. Has the fastest startup among his supers and reliably combos from almost any juggle, which can be useful if you messed up a corner route. Leaves Terry at near fullscreen distance with poor oki. Best course of action is generally a REV Power Wave followed with a run.",
    "236acac": "A projectile that travels along the ground. The button pressed for it determines its startup and travel speed. REV version is instead a big wave that travels slowly along the ground, letting Terry approach. Holding C/AC transforms the move into a short-range energy blast similar to Round Wave from other games. Numbers in [brackets] indicate values that change when you hold C/AC. A pretty alright projectile. Though you won\u2019t be zoning too many people, it\u2019s great for closing the gap, either of your own accord or by forcing the opponent to approach you instead. These are especially true in the case of the REV version, which is a big, slow fireball that locks the opponent down if they block it. If you hold the C or REV versions, this move becomes Burning Wave , which is a short, stationary blast that is much better on block. When you do the REV version, it becomes a whopping +4, making it a great pressure reset. It\u2019s good in corner juggles, too!",
    "236bdbd": "Terry hits with a hooking elbow. B version is only special move Terry has that combos from far A. As of the Season 2 patch, the D version is no longer plus on block (but the REV version is still plus). Your most consistent special cancel; the B version is the only cancel that consistently combos from far A. Like Garou and unlike his KOF and Street Fighter incarnations, Terry\u2019s Power Charge is a rekka series. They\u2019re highly juggle-friendly, the first hit is very difficult to punish if not outright safe, and they work cleanly from your lights, making them great to use in confirms and various other combos.",
    "236k6bdbd": "Terry hits with a second hooking elbow. Characters with an invincible reversal (for example a DP) can always punish this move. This hit is a frame trap after the first one, potentially catching opponents trying to punish you. Continuation of the rekka chain. On block, the meterless versions are outright punishable by certain reversals even if you commit to the third hit. For this reason, it\u2019s generally inadvisable to try to frame trap the opponent on block, except with the REV version, which is safe and can REV Accel.",
    "236k6k6bdbd": "Terry shoulder charges his opponent. B and D versions are very unsafe on block. The REV version at least has REV Accel to confirm whether it hits. The rekka ender. Knocks down on hit, but is very punishable on block. It\u2019s pretty difficult to push okizeme from this midscreen, so it\u2019s better to end combos with light Burn Knuckle instead when it\u2019s available instead of Power Charge. However, if you want corner carry, this is your best option.",
    "2a": "Tied with far A for Terry's fastest non-proximity attack, but has better cancel options at the expense of range Mashable and chainable. Reliably chains into itself or far B depending on range Combos to 214A, 236B, 623K (range dependent). Cancelling to 623K when in range to do is Terry's only safe blockstring ender that still combos on hit A great button for mashing, confirming, and applying pressure. Simply a jack-of-all-trades button.",
    "2b": "Terry's only low combo starter Needs to hit up close for a combo; Terry can't combo out of it at all from max range except for raw cancel to Ignition Gears Chains to close A when hit point blank; chains to the rest of his light normals otherwise Terry's only low combo starter and his only safe low. Unfortunately, unless you hit it point blank, the returns from it tend to be very underwhelming; from beyond close A range, all you get is 2A xx 214A or an Ignition Gear. Still, this low is quite valuable to Terry as it prevents the opponent from walking away from his pressure and strike-throw.",
    "2bcor2drev": "Mimics heavy Burning Knuckle. It looks funny, but is otherwise mostly useless.",
    "2c": "Wild Punish +20 (+24 if feint cancelled) Worse range than far C, but doesn't whiff on crouch. This is important for completing combos from your cl.C Command Combo No way to make this move plus on block without cancelling to certain REV moves This is Terry's multi-purpose heavy, being as fast and almost as far reaching without the caveat of whiffing on crouch. It\u2019s also very combo-friendly, easily working from his cl.C Command Combo. At 0 on hit and -6 on block, however, you always want to cancel it, so beware of any Hyper Defense Guard Cancels your opponent may try.",
    "2crev": "Cannot be cancelled on block Wins against jumping attacks, standing normals and REV Blows While it\u2019s largely outclassed as an anti-air by heavy Power Dunk Brake, Terry\u2019s High Dodge Attack is still very useful for calling out certain moves, beating REV Blows, and breaking certain REV Accel sequences on block. Due to its short range, it\u2019s almost always punishable on block, so it should be used very sparingly.",
    "2d": "Terry's longest-ranged normal Above-average startup speed for a sweep, giving it solid punishing power. On Wild Punish, puts opponents into a special state where you can follow up with another sweep, Power Wave or a scaled Power Geyser After being notoriously terrible in Garou, Terry's sweep is quite good in this game, at least in comparison to the rest of the cast, and it complements his gameplan well, stopping opponents from simply walking out of his spacing traps, not to mention doing so may make them lose their ability to punish it. Technically, it's fast enough to combo from cl.C command combo for a sudden knockdown that can potentially mess up the opponent expecting a different combo route. Of course, you lose out on a lot of damage in doing so.",
    "4ab": "Has the same setups as from forward throw.",
    "5bcor5drev": "Mimics Crack Shoot, Terry's slowest Feint. Really slow and will rarely be used in combos except when a cancellable normal Wild Punishes. However, it does have some niche use as a surprise throw setup after a blocked heavy normal.",
    "5crev": "Universal overhead, wins against lows and throws On Counter Hit, Wild Punish or meaty hit, links to close C Terry\u2019s Low Dodge Attack, also his only grounded overhead, is REALLY slow, and your reward from it isn\u2019t great even when you dodge something, unless you\u2019re close enough for your close heavies. What Terry\u2019s LDA does have going for it, however, is its above-average range. You can potentially surprise opponents by using it as a counterpoke. Still, it\u2019s your overhead, and it\u2019s also great to press when you\u2019ve conditioned your opponent to tech the throw on wakeup or mash lows against your shimmy.",
    "623bdbd": "Terry launches the opponent with a shoulder and spikes them to the ground. B version is fully invincible on startup. D and REV versions both Brake after the first hit, making them safe, and launch on Counter Hit. They make great Guard Cancels, but they suffer extra recovery when whiffed. D version has more reach and combos from two lights. All versions are Counter Hit punishable on block and whiff if not Braked. Just like in Garou, it\u2019s Power Dunk that serves as Terry\u2019s DP in this game. The B version is your most efficient reversal, being fast and fully invincible on startup, while the D and REV versions can be Braked after the first hit to launch into a combo. With all versions having air invincibility, it means one can\u2019t simply jump over your fireballs, or else they risk eating a combo. The REV version can even be comboed out of if you don\u2019t Brake it. Unfortunately, the range on the hit that Brakes isn\u2019t great, so you\u2019re somewhat limited in where you can throw it out. It is confirmable after a light normal or two, however, allowing you to launch into a full combo from your light buttons.",
    "6bcor6drev": "Terry's fastest feint and the one used in his framedata. Mimics Power Wave. This feint is the one you\u2019ll be using the most. It\u2019s fast, it fakes a projectile potentially baiting something committal from your opponent, and works seamlessly in Terry\u2019s combos.",
    "ab": "By doing a microstep forward you get a meaty crossup j.B that passes over reversal uppercuts.",
    "cd": "Terry does a sliding Power Charge ender and finishes with a Power Dunk. On counter hit, Terry stops and doesn't continue with the Power Dunk. Travels very far and can hit from slightly more than half screen distance, allowing Terry to shut down many fireballs on reaction. Due to the far forward movement, it can sometimes evade correctly-timed air REV blows which would have beaten typical ground REV blows with less movement.",
    "cla": "Terry's fastest attack. Terry's combination attack is accessed from this normal, and is his optimal meterless combo from it by damage Alternatively, you cancel this move into Power Dunk Brake to use your REV moves, greatly enhancing your combo This move is very important to Terry's gameplan, being fast, mashable, chainable, plus on block, and easily comboing into a launch via Power Dunk brake. If you're ever uncertain of what to do up close, just press this.",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. cl.AC first hit is cancellable by any follow-ups. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender, during cl.AC only the second hit is REV Blow cancellable. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : First hit of the auto combo, cancellable by any follow-ups. cl.AC > C : A second hit cancellable by any ender, a nice way to increase the damage or your opponent REV meter. Unsafe on block and can be punished if the auto-combo was started too close to the opponent. D ender : Far D follow up, different frame data and damage, very unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Safest ender, Same move as his Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV) but +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Same move as his 2D, meaning it's very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "A goofy looking kick that you probably won't use very much, but that doesn't mean it's bad; in fact, its plus frames combined with high reach make it an excellent button for locking down opponents in the corner. On Counter Hit or Wild Punish, it's easily confirmable into your close C for big damage.",
    "clc": "Wild Punish +19 (+26 if feint cancelled) Goes into his Command Combo on hit and block When used on its own, gives massive reward on Counter Hit and Wild Punish The moneymaker. This is a fast, powerful move that goes into your Command Combo and from there, all of your biggest damage combos. It's also a good button to press on its own, but in many situations, going for the Command Combo is better since it's even more plus on block and gives better returns on hit.",
    "clc46c6dcd": "This follow up from your close C is amazing\u2026 if you only do the 6C, since feint cancelling it gives you a link to most of Terry\u2019s heavies. This extension is important for all of Terry\u2019s high-damage combos. Any part of this Command Combo beyond the 6C is outclassed by REV Accel and feint cancelling. You\u2019re better off forgetting they exist.",
    "cld": "Wild Punish +25 Hits twice, easily confirming whether the opponent is blocking or getting hit You can link 2A from this move on normal hit and frame trap into it on block Forces stand on hit, allowing links into far C on counter hit on crouching opponents A two-hit upkick that is crucial to Terry's offense. On hit, you can easily confirm a knockdown, and on block, you can pour on the pressure. With its low pushback and surprising REV gauge buildup, it's great at the latter especially. A tricky microwalk allows Terry to link a close C on a Counter Hit, but you otherwise get your far C just fine.",
    "fa": "Terry's fastest far normal, tied with 2A, but has slightly better range You can chain into and out of this normal via light attacks (including itself) It can't reliably combo to anything on hit except Power Charge and Ignition Gears This straight jab is good for comboing from other lights into a knockdown via Power Charge, pre-emptively hitting opponents out of hops without committing to Power Dunk, and mashing on defense against an opponent's assault. It has slightly longer range than 2A, which is beneficial for light confirms since you can chain 2A into it.",
    "fb": "Terry's longest ranged light normal Can be chained into from other lights This shin kick is your best button for confirming into your Ignition Gears, as you can chain it just like your far A. However, you can't cancel it to special moves. It has good utility in neutral, as well; being only -1 on block with high pushback, it has a devious ability to set up spacing traps using your far heavies and Low Dodge Attack, which can devastate opponents trying to take their turn back.",
    "fbd": "Terry\u2019s only meterless followup from his far B Good for tacking on extra damage when counterpoking with far B, but not much else.",
    "fc": "Wild Punish +17 (+24 when feint cancelled) Terry's farthest-reaching normal that still cancels to special moves Whiffs on crouching opponents A big, beefy straight punch that reaches a decent distance forward while allowing special and feint cancels. It doesn't move Terry forward at all, much to his benefit via setting up spacing traps and pressure. It's decently fast for a far heavy, making it good for whiff punishing as well, but its lengthy recovery on whiff combined with its whiff on crouch mean you would probably rather use far D for whiff punishing instead.",
    "fd": "Wild Punish +22 A big kick with impressive range while being only one frame slower than far C, you can use this to pick up distant punishes, such as after blocked sweeps, and in footsies to tag opponents recklessly hitting buttons. A much better move to fish for Wild Punishes with than far C. Beware of using this move too close to the opponent, as the move being -5 on block means many characters can punish it.",
    "j9a": "Can be cancelled to REV Blow upon making contact with the opponent Terry's jump A when done during a forward jump instead becomes his short chop from the older games. It's just as fast as his neutral jump A, but has way less range; instead, when you're in S.P.G, you get a cancel to REV Blow, letting you respond to other characters' REV Blows.",
    "j9b": "Can cross up This is the air normal that you'll probably be using the most with Terry due to its cross-up properties and generous hitbox. By extension, it is Terry's best normal at setting up tick throws.",
    "ja": "Only comes out when done during a neutral or a back jump; see below for forward jump Insanely fast and reaches as far as his jump heavies Struggles to hit characters on the ground, especially crouching ones This move comes out faster than any other of Terry's air attacks, at a blistering 4 frames. Its far-reaching horizontal hitbox will strike out of the air almost anything at heights equivalent to that of Terry's own jump.",
    "jb": "Only comes out when done during a neutral or a back jump; see below for forward jump Terry's most active air normal This weird-looking air kick can only be done from a neutral or back jump, but has fantastic range and is his most active normal by far. Good for walling out opponents approaching you from the air.",
    "jc": "Really good damage and startup. It's mainly used as a close jump-in or hop-in attack, but it can also work as an air-to-air if the opponent is below Terry.",
    "jcd": "Terry does a jump D into a Crack Shoot in mid air. If done very low to the ground, Terry will land before he continues into the Crack Shoot, allowing for juggles. If cancelled into from REV Power Dunk (623BD) very close to the ground, it allows for juggles normally impossible, such as light Burning Knuckle or Buster Wolf.",
    "jd": "Terry's longest-ranged jumping normal by far during a forward jump Terry's slowest normal, but the damage and range make up for it; it's without a doubt his best button for jumping in. Sadly, the cross-up hitbox is gone. That said, this move can also work as an air-to-air, and it's actually great as one when jumping backwards."
  },
  "tizoc": {
    "214acac": "A running lariat. Unusually nuanced in braking options, and therefore quite useful in many oki setups for its fast forward movement. Launches on counterhit. Light version : Used for meterless light confirms. Any special-cancelable attack will combo into this. The chest has a hitbox during the slide, but this is -6 on block at best and -9 at worst, making it unsafe. If the hand hitbox is blocked, it is safe at -2. Can be braked during the travel to slide Tizoc forward by about half-screen distance without attacking the opponent Heavy version : Used to approach the opponent with +2 advantage on block at minimum Tizoc backsteps before performing his lariat, making this significantly slower than the other versions. Can be braked during the backstep to cancel the lariat entirely, but can also be braked during the travel to slide forward by about half to fullscreen distance, depending on brake timing. EX/REV version : This is the go-to move to cancel into from heavies. Slides the furthest (nearly fullscreen distance) and recovers fastest when braked, making this a useful (and costly) tool for oki setups. Similar on-block situation to the light version, but can be canceled into Rev Blow if Tizoc is in SPG, making this safe on block unless the opponent Guard Cancels into a grab or parry when the 214AC is blocked. Guard Canceled Rev Blows with 22F or faster startup will lose against Tizoc's Rev Blow, and the opponent does not exit blockstun after 214AC in time to counter Tizoc's Rev Blow.",
    "214acacabdrev": "All versions can be feints during the advance; Tizoc stops short before the lariat connects. The C version (214C) can be braked at the backstep preventing Tizoc from advancing. Since the move is unsafe on block at close range, feinting this move at close range may trick opponents into waiting for block impact. The feint for this move may be less likely to be useful at far range since opponents will be ready to whiff punish or recognize when this move is spaced to be safe on block. For the Rev version feint, Tizoc recovers faster than the light version and moves much further forward. This lets him close the gap with quick recovery and apply strike/throw pressure on the opponent\u2019s wakeup, or even at neutral.",
    "214bdbd": "A 2-hit attack. Tizoc leaps into the air and comes back down with an elbow strike. Can be used as an anti-air (mind the lack of invulnerability) or to close the gap on the opponent. Both hits can launch if they counterhit. Light version (214B) : Longest range Heavy version (214D) : Shortest range and slowest startup, but more damage than light version Tizoc backdashes before jumping into the air. This version can be braked during the backdash, canceling the jump entirely. Has a few air-invulnerable frames as soon as the first hitbox becomes active EX/REV version (214BD) : Mostly used in combos (especially 214AC > 214BD ) and as a Rev Accel after many of Tizoc's grabs to grant better knockdowns. Second hit ground bounces the opponent on hit. Can always combo into 214B on the ground bounce, or Rev Accel the second hit into a Rev Blow (better oki than j.236AC) or j.236AC (better damage than Rev Blow) for a hard knockdown. If the cancel from 214AC into 214BD is manually delayed, this can also combo into 214A for better corner carry (and worse oki) than 214B, or j.236A for superior damage and a hard knockdown compared to the other meterless enders. Second hit can hit an opponent as an OTG attack. This leaves Tizoc at +44 advantage, and two forward dashes after will place him at point-blank range from the opponent with +2 advantage. Rev Accel into this after Tizoc's EX grabs to keep the pressure on the opponent.",
    "236236bd": "A series of strikes with Tizoc's fists. Used to end combos and in super cancels. 1-bar version ( 236236B ): This is not a reversal, it is only invulnerable up to frame 3. Can become a reversal and, unlike the grab super, will even beat safejumps if used as a guard cancel. At max range, the second and third hit may not combo, even if the first hit lands. This makes it ineffective as a standalone punish tool. On hit, dash 3 times to be +5 at point blank range. 214C (do not brake) will leave Tizoc +1 at point blank range, allowing 360C / 360AC to grab the opponent on their earliest grabbable frame. 2-bar version ( 236236D ): Fully invulnerable and can function as a reversal, unlike the 1-bar version. Switches sides with the opponent, but also leaves Tizoc +36 at point blank range with no movement needed. This opens up numerous options for oki: An immediate dash followed by feint will reduce the advantage to 0, allowing a 5F grab to hit on the opponent's first grabbable frame. Alternatively, whiff any 2 of the following moves twice: 2A, 2B, Close C Empty hop forward is -1 after landing (can block a reversal) Whiff Close D (+9), a Close B launched immediately after will be +10 on hit and +8 on block Whiff 2C or 2D (+2), a 360A will hit on the first grabbable frame",
    "236236rev": "Tizoc's Hidden Gear. Can be used as a reversal in theory, but is woefully unreliable for it because of how easy it is to make this whiff. It is a strike and can be blocked or Just Defended. Long range for a hidden gear, can counter slow startup moves at range. Can hit airborne opponents, though the hitbox does not go very high. Can whiff if an opponent is moving forward or crouching at point-blank range in front of Tizoc Only accessible while in SPG and costs 2 bars. Resets the Rev Meter to 0% on hit, even if the player is overheated when this attack is launched. Leaves Tizoc on the other side of the screen at +8, there is no meaningful advantage to be pressed after the animation",
    "236acac": "Tizoc throws the opponent behind him and delivers a running kick. This is a command grab that is primarily used to switch sides with the opponent and throw them closer to the corner behind Tizoc. The only true grab that Tizoc is allowed to combo into, and its range is extended if used during a combo Like 360P/AC, this can be a powerful option for guard cancels. It is much easier, but slower and less rewarding compared to 360P. Useful for throwing the opponent into the corner. The EX version starts a combo instead of playing a cinematic. A single Rev Accel into 214BD > 214B alone can make this version deal more damage than the normal versions. Supers can easily push this damage to around 300, and a hidden gear scores around 423 if opponent isn't in SPG.",
    "236bdbd": "Tizoc glides forward and grabs the opponent, slipping behind them to perform a German suplex. Used to grab opponents from long range. Does not switch sides with the opponent. Light version (236B): Shortest travel distance and slowest travel speed Can be braked at any point before the grab hitbox becomes active Dashing twice after the animation leaves Tizoc at point blank distance at -1, or spend a 214AC (brake) to be +6 at close enough distance to hit Close C In the corner, grants a safejump after the animation: 1-frame microwalk, then jump forward. Either hit an air normal on the way down, or immediately use a grab on landing. Can be performed midscreen, but your followup on a hit is limited to Far A > 214A or Far B > super Heavy version (236D): Slower startup, but travels further and faster than the light version and recovers faster Otherwise identical to the light version REV version (236BD): Unlike the other versions, this is not a true grab and can be blocked. Despite this, it still does not hit an airborne or juggled opponent Any special cancelable attack will combo into this, and this can combo into 214BD with a Rev Accel on hit. Fastest travel speed and longest travel distance Can only be braked after the first active frame, not before. In the corner, feint and hop forward immediately after the animation for a safejump Dash twice after the animation to be +8 at point blank range ( Close B > Close C will combo in this situation)",
    "236bdbdabdrev": "Tizoc halts before the grab lands, allowing you to catch opponents trying to jump out with an anti-air. The C version can be feinted a little later (still before active) The Rev Art version is unusual as it is active (and blockable) before it can be feinted. Unless cancelled into from max range far A, doesn't allow enough space/time to stop it before it is blocked or hits (it is very unsafe too). Otherwise only functions as a true feint at neutral from further away since the opponent is out of range. After the minimum distance, can be stopped at any position before its maximum range. If immediately feinted, recovers one frame slower than 214AC (immediate feint).",
    "2a": "A short, horizontal elbow strike at waist-level. Primarily used to chain into far lights. Very short range, and is therefore not a very good close-range defensive tool Can chain into Far A or Far B, especially with Close A > 2A > Far A . Can also be canceled into 236B/D or 623K to fish for grabs. Combos into 236P, 214A or 236BD on hit",
    "2b": "Tizoc extends his front foot against the ground. Used to start combos from a low. Faster and longer range than 2A, and can be chained into other lights (usually Far A) An opponent's 5CR will jump over this for a Wild Punish.",
    "2c": "A diagonal poke with an open hand. Used to stop hops at long range. Only combos into supers on normal hit. On counterhit, combos into Far A Not reliable for stopping hops or jumps at shorter ranges.",
    "2crev": "Tizoc's crouching dodge attack, a universally available attack for low-profiling and anti-airing. Read more about the crouching dodge attack here. Advances Tizoc forward, and can therefore be spaced to become safer on block (up to -1) A hit on an airborne opponent will require a counterhit or Wild Punish for any special cancel followups to hit, otherwise they will whiff",
    "2d": "A back kick against the floor. Fairly standard sweep. On Wild Punish, a second sweep will hit for 157 total damage, or a 214BD can hit for 195 total damage at the cost of 25% rev meter (not including SPG reduction).",
    "4ab": "Press 4C or 6C to follow this up with an extra hit for a total of 140 damage and maintain screen side. If Tizoc initiated the back throw when backed up to the corner, the 4C/6C follow-up will put Tizoc back in the corner, so consider the positioning tradeoff for the extra 40 damage.",
    "4holdc": "A charged version of Far C, used by holding back and holding the punch button. Don't forget to feint for additional advantage frames, same as the uncharged version. If not fully charged, will be identical to Far C with no partial bonuses given If it hits an airborne opponent, this can usually be followed up with 214A or 214AC, even on normal hit. This does not make it a good anti-air, but it is certainly a rewarding one. If feint cancelled against a opponent on the ground, can follow up with Far C at most ranges (will require Far A at max range).",
    "4holdd": "A charged version of Far D, used by holding back and holding the kick button. Moves Tizoc forward when the kick is launched and fully charged If spaced properly, can become even more advantageous on hit/block (up to +4 on block, +12 on normal hit and up to +17 on counterhit)",
    "562bcor562drev": "Tizoc's only feint. Mimics the startup of 214P, but without the back step of a 214C (break) or the slide forward of the light/EX versions. Mostly used to frame kill in a safejump setup.",
    "5crev": "Tizoc's standing dodge attack, a universally available overhead attack that jumps over lows and throws. Read more about the standing dodge attack here. Unlike other 5CR attacks, this one starts a juggle. Without a meaty setup, 236236R will always hit. In the corner, 236236K will always hit. At max range, 236236K may whiff if not in the corner. With a meaty setup in the corner, an immediate backdash canceled into j.236AC can combo after this, but the timing is very tight. The meaty 5CR can be set up with the following sequence in the corner: Forward throw > Dash forward > 5CR > 44 > j.236AC (235 damage) Unlike other 5CR attacks, there is no additional frame advantage granted for a counterhit or Wild Punish",
    "623bdbd": "Tizoc flips into the air and lands on the opponent's shoulders to perform a Frankensteiner, switching sides with the opponent. Can be used to jump over attacks and punish with a grab, or as a special cancel to grab someone out of blockstun. Light and heavy versions are mostly identical, but the heavy version goes further and recovers 2 frames faster. However, the heavy version can whiff on a crouching opponent if they are too close. After the normal versions, immediately press 214AC (brake) to move up to point-blank distance at +4 advantage. EX version is slightly faster and does just a little more damage, but whiffs on crouching opponents. Rev Accel into 214BD after the animation to deal a total of 247 damage and remain close to the opponent at +44 advantage All versions have invulnerability to aerial attacks, but make no mistake, this is not an anti-air. At best, it makes this move a situational response to incoming safejumps.",
    "62486248ac": "A beefier version of Tizoc's 360AC grab. Active on frame 1, making it a true reversal. Do not use in guard cancels on wakeup, this will apply damage scaling to the super attack for no benefit at all because it is already a 1F attack (contrary to its name, Zero Frame Supers are actually active on frame 1, not 0), and it will never beat a safejump because it is not a strike attack. Slightly shorter range than 360A/360AC. 1-bar version deals 10 more damage than 360AC, but its 1F startup gives it powerful defensive utility 2-bar version is identical to the 1-bar version, but deals more damage. Dashing twice or jumping forward after the 1-bar version leaves Tizoc +10 at point blank range. 2-bar version leaves Tizoc too far away with insufficient advantage frames for any meaningful oki setups.",
    "6248acac": "Tizoc has a lot of grabs, but this is THE grab. For all of his shortcomings as a character, this is a grab that allows him to cash out for big damage on offense while also being a stellar defensive tool when used as a guard cancel. The exact requirement for the 360 input is as follows: At least 3 consecutive cardinal (straight up, down, left or right) directions must be hit. If this requirement is met, the fourth one can be substituted with a diagonal input (for example, 4269 is valid) The direction of rotation does not matter. Additionally, diagonal inputs are not necessary for the input (but their inclusion will not invalidate the input sequence). For example, 4268 is valid, but 4862 is also valid. The starting point can be any of the four cardinal directions. For example, 2684, 6842, 8426 and 4268 are all valid inputs. Keep these input requirements in mind, as multiple different versions of the 360 input may be more comfortable for different situations (buffering during a dash, guard cancels, buffering at the end of a jump, etc.) Additionally, this grab can cancel the first 5 frames of Tizoc's jump, even if that jump is done during the early frames of a dash. This can be used to artificially extend the range of this grab. Light version : Tied with EX/REV for longest range version Slowest at 7F, which can actually be advantageous when using as a guard cancel vs deep jump attacks. After the animation, immediately press 214AC (brake) to move up to approximately hop distance away from the opponent at +10. Tizoc will be much closer to the opponent after the animation if he is backed up to the corner when using this Heavy version : More damage and faster startup than the light grab, but shorter range Great for guard cancels due to its high damage and extremely fast startup at no additional REV gauge cost. This grab is fast enough to even beat some light chains if used as a guard cancel, assuming the opponent is close enough to get grabbed. Leaves Tizoc a little closer to the opponent after the animation compared to the light version. 214AC (brake) will leave you at point-blank range at +6. When used while backed up against the corner, one dash leaves Tizoc at point-blank range at +19. REV version : Spend some Rev meter to get the range of the light version and the speed of the heavy version in one package, plus massively increased damage (nearly as much as his super grab). Rev Accel into 214BD to inflict a total of 377 damage and stay at point-blank range from the opponent with +44 advantage Dash twice immediately after the animation ends to be +11 at point blank range, setting up a meaty Close D that will be +5 on block and +9 on hit (enough to link into Close C) without canceling into feint (and feinting will not improve this) If used while backed up to the corner, 360C > hop > feint leaves Tizoc at +1 and just close enough to hit 236P or 360A / 360AC, or press 214A (do not brake) to be +11 at point-blank range, similar to the midscreen setup",
    "ab": "Bear hug the opponent and kick them forward. Immediately press 214AC~AB after the animation ends to slide up to point-blank range at +14 advantage. If the opponent is backed to the corner, dash once and immediately press 5CR for a meaty overhead that can be followed up with an immediate backdash > j.236AC for a combo",
    "after4ab46c": "An extension of his back throw that adds damage and does not side-switch the opponent. Do not use this if you want to keep your opponent behind you.",
    "cla": "An open-handed upward jab. Used for light confirms and stopping hops at close range. Can be chained into itself or other lights, otherwise combos into anything that Far A would. Additionally, this can reliably combo into 236P if the side-switch is desired Can be low profiled by an opponent's 2CR",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. cl.AC first hit is cancellable by any follow-ups. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender, during cl.AC only the second hit is REV Blow cancellable. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : Same move as close C but the frame data and damage are different and the first hit can be cancelled by any follow-up. cl.AC > C : Far C follow up, different frame data and damage. D ender : Far D follow up, different frame data and damage, very unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Same move as his Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV) except it's faster, +2F on block, and can't be followed up with super. 2D ender : Same move as his 2D and nearly the same framedata, meaning it's very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "A quick stomp to the shin. Despite the animation, this move is not a low and can be blocked while standing. Primarily used as a pressure reset and to set up strike/throw mix. Links into 2B/2A on hit for a combo, and this same sequence is a frametrap on a blocked Close B. Counterhit / Wild Punish does not open any additional combo routing. Without special canceling or delaying the input, the normal versions of 236P will grab an opponent on the earliest frame possible after a blocked Close B. 236AC and any version of the 360 grab must be manually delayed by at least 2-4 frames after a blocked Close B, or the grab will whiff. Cannot be dodged with either 2CR or 5CR",
    "clc": "A downward chop with an open hand.Tizoc's fastest heavy attack. Mostly used to start high-damage combos, but can also set up strike/throw mix. At very close range, will combo into itself with Close C > Feint> Close C on normal hit. On counterhit, can combo into Far C after the feint. On Wild Punish, can combo into 4[C] after the feint. After a blocked Close C > Feint , the EX 360 grab must be delayed by at least 1 frame or it will whiff. Any other grab will not require a delay. At the edge of Close C's activation range, some grabs may whiff if used after a blocked Close C > Feint . At very close range, Tizoc will remain close enough for any grab to hit. Cannot be dodged with either 2CR or 5CR",
    "cld": "A heavy knee strike. Used for starting combos and getting huge plus frames. Forces opponent to stand on hit. Always combos into Close C with Close D > Feint > Close C More pushback on block than Close C, which may cause some grabs to whiff after Close D is blocked Can be low profiled by an opponent's 2CR",
    "fa": "An open hand jab that compensates for its slow speed with huge range. Used for poking, but can score decent damage with a good hit-confirm or a cancel into a slow grab. Excellent horizontal range, but the tip of the hitbox will whiff on a crouching opponent Combos into 236BD and 214A on normal hit. Can combo into 214AC on Counter Hit / Wild Punish One of the only lights in the game that gets better advantage on hit/block by canceling into feint",
    "fb": "A fast, plus-on-block side kick at waist-level, mostly used for poking and ending light chains on block. Unlike Far A, never whiffs on crouching opponents Can only ever combo into 236236K or 236236R with a super cancel, a counterhit will not reliably open any new routing Despite having the lowest hitbox of all his far normals, can still be low-profiled by crouch dodge attacks ( 2CR ) like all of his other far normals",
    "fc": "A special-cancelable, far-reaching backhand chop. Used to score big damage. Unusually long special cancel window Combos into 214AC on normal hit. For an easier hit-confirm, Far C > Feint > Far A > 214A is an option. On counterhit, combos into itself at shorter ranges with Far C > Feint > Far C . On a Wild Punish, will usually combo into itself except at absolute max range where 214AC becomes the best follow-up.",
    "fd": "A big, straight kick forward. Used to fish for counterhits and whiff punishes. Shorter range than Far C, but slightly faster and conversions on counterhit / Wild Punish are much more stable Normal hits can only combo into supers. Counterhits will always combo into Far A, and a Wild Punish will always combo into Far C with no microwalk required.",
    "holdduringspgcd": "Tizoc's Rev Blow, an armored attack that is safe on block. Only accessible while in SPG. Read more about the Rev Blow here. Long range, will hit from just a little further than round start distance. Sets up a safejump on hit. After the hit, immediately dash forward once, then forward jump with no delays.",
    "holdduringspgjcd": "Tizoc's aerial Rev Blow. Only accessible while in SPG. Can be canceled into immediately after the second hit of 214BD",
    "j236acac": "Tizoc grabs the opponent in the air and slams them to the ground underneath him. Can be used as an air-to-air attack, or as a combo ender to get a hard knockdown. Heavy version has shorter range than the other versions. Rev version can be performed after a backdash. Additionally, it can combo into 214BD for 247 damage and +44 advantage. Rev version has anywhere juggle property. This means that any juggle restrictions are bypassed. While Tizoc's aerials are not special cancellable, landing after hitting with an aerial can be followed up with j.236AC if you're fast enough. 214A (do not brake) leaves Tizoc point blank at +1 for normal versions and +3 for the EX version",
    "ja": "Far A, but now it's airborne. Used for air-to-air situations. Fastest startup, highest hitbox and longest horizontal range of out of all of Tizoc's air normals Can whiff against grounded opponents Special-cancelable if used during a full jump (not a hop)",
    "jb": "A leaping kick at a slightly downward angle. Primarily used to cross up opponents. Fairly low hitbox, making it good for hitting opponents at a slightly lower elevation",
    "jc": "A jumping frog splash. Primarily used to cross up an opponent.",
    "jd": "A flying dropkick. Primarily used for air-to air situations. Slightly more horizontal range and damage than j.C, but not as good for starting a combo after the hit and does not cross up."
  },
  "vox_reaper": {
    "214acac": "Vox's \"fireball function\" Light servers as a great and hard to contest poke in neutral . its also a good blockstring ender from heavies keeping Vox relatively safe while also punishing mashers as if functions as a 4F frametrap Heavy is the go to combo starter from heavy confirms. while its a 5f frametrap from heavy punches(trades with 4f) its unsafe on block Landing a counter hit with heavy will launch the opponent in a special state which allows another 214C to connect and relaunch EX is the combo filler usually cancelled into from 236AC Braking before the second hit of the EX version connects will grant Vox frame advantage on block (+3).",
    "236236ac": "A version : does less damage and have worse oki and has no invul compared than the kick counterpart. don't use this. can be used to OTG after a WP 2D. This single hit deals the full damage of the super for some reason. C version : an actually decent super compared to its lvl 1 version. has invul and can be used to punish fireballs. like all of Vox lvl1 super it side switches and it has worse oki than lvl2 kick super. best used in mid screen routes or when it can guarantee a KO otherwise use the kick super.",
    "236236bd": "B version : the ideal lvl1 metered finisher. can be used as a reversal. sideswitches but has enough oki to dash 3 times and meaty with cl.D and continue your pressure. When used in the corner, it sets up an auto-timed cross-up by doing a hop then a jump and pressing j.B D version : while it does less damage than the lvl2 punch super, its doesn't sideswitch and has amazing oki. always use this in corner routes to keep the corner. Sets up an easy safe jump in the corner by whiffing a cr.C and doing a forward jump",
    "236236rev": "Only available during S.P.G, resets the Rev meter to 0%, even during Overheat. Cinematic super. has absolutely no oki and after the animation it sends the other character to the other side of the screen. Best used to guarantee a KO or when Vox Rev meter is high and at risk of overheat. Situational fireball punish. Can punish fireballs on reaction at half screen away",
    "236acac": "Every version is brake-able during the dash part. Light version can be spaced to be safe (-3 from round start) Counter hitting with light and heavy give a high launch which starts a very damaging combo Heavy is always plus but can be 5f jabbed when cancelled into from heavy punches. EX is your go to juggle catcher and combo filler also has good corner carry Braking after the first hit of the EX version keeps Vox safe on block",
    "236bdbd": "Command grab that nets decent hard knockdown. EX can be special cancelled into other EX moves to lead to a bigger (scaled) combo. good to catch overly defensive opponents. Will grab opponents after a blocked cl.D feint or a cl.B without need to microwalk or dash.",
    "2a": "Vox's go to Abare/Mash button as it is cancelable and can link easily to Far B Your average 2A. be careful cancelling to DP as it whiffs from certain ranges. If starting from 2B and the goal is to cancel to DP its better to do standing A, if 2B chains to cl.A DP will connect making it a safer string.",
    "2b": "Vox's go-to low. good range. At close range can link into cl.A and go to a DP break to start a small combo Cancelable into super. Chains into Far B fairly consistently for a small combo",
    "2bcor2drev": "1F slower than the standing feint; mimics Vox's DP. Really no reason to ever use it over neutral feint",
    "2c": "Links naturally from cl.B Punishable on block, can cancel to 214A or into a DP brake to stay safe, or into ex DP cancelled into divekick to be plus and continue pressure. Can be used to anti-air due to 5 active frames but it doesn't have anti-air invulnerability Extremely potent button to use after a blocked cl.D as it frame traps into a huge combo and has safe cancels even if blocked",
    "2crev": "Cannot be cancelled on block Sometimes wins against jump attacks, standing normals and REV Blows. Not a very good anti air due to the horizontal hitbox and low active frames. On counter hit can combo into DP break to start a combo or a 236A Unfourtunatly this might be the worst high dodge attack in the game. With an abbhorent hitbox for both anti airing and dodging horizontal attacks. This move can lose you the round easily if you are not on point with your timing. The best usage for this move is to counter rev blows. Try anti airing with crouching C, DP, or air to air with j.C for a juggle.",
    "2d": "Has a good range, can be good to punish spaced blocked sweeps.",
    "4ab": "Great oki, can dash twice and meaty with cl.D.",
    "56bcor56drev": "Fastest feint and the one used in his framedata. Mimics his 214C Due to Vox's scary strike throw game, can be used after hard knockdowns to bait reversals.",
    "5crev": "Universal overhead +8 on counter hit and wild punish. Can link into cr.C for a combo Dodges low and throws. Not a very good low dodge attack when compared to the rest of the cast. With lower range and active frames than other ones. On top of a high recovery on whiff. Use this move sparingly or to close out rounds.",
    "623acac": "Vox's DP. All versions can be cancelled into divekick (2K) on hit but only heavy and EX cancel on block. 623AC~2K leaves Vox +3 and in range to use close heavy normals The C and REV versions can be braked to become safe launchers, but if they are braked and whiff, they suffer 5 frames additional recovery.",
    "ab": "Vox is +3 if he dashes after.",
    "cd": "Only available during S.P.G Full armor on the 2nd frame and like other REV Blows, it loses to meaty, throws, high dodge attacks, or if the opponent reacts with their own REV Blow. A below-average rev blow that is one of the weaker ones in the game. It has 25 frames of start up and no vertical hitboxes along with considerable recovery. Use with caution and limit usage for ending combos or block strings with it",
    "cla": "Always in range to cancel to DP. Great way to set up tik throws Extremely important button for Vox as he likes to dash in the opponent's face often. This button is Vox's main way to his access DP break launcher and start his offense.",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. cl.AC first hit is cancellable by any follow-ups. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender, during cl.AC only the second hit is REV Blow cancellable. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : Same move as close C but the frame data and damage are different and the first hit can be cancelled by any follow-up. cl.AC > C : Far C follow up, different frame data and damage. D ender : Far D follow up, different frame data and damage, very unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Same move as his Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV), +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Same move as his 2D and nearly the same framedata, meaning it's very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "Plus enough to link 2C after. Good button to setup a tick throw situation. Combos after 2B at point blank range after version 1.7.2",
    "clc": "Close range button which is negative on block even with feint Doesn't have many use cases except as a situational combo filler after counter hits. Can link into from a counter hit cl.B and counter hit cl.D for optimal combos. Does have plenty of cancel options which allow Vox to start his pressure even if the button is negative on block even when feinted. Your best frame trap is 214C, but it is a high risk high reward frame trap as it is quite negative on block. Another option vox can use ( if the opponent respects the cancel options) is to do 236C for a reset Niche tech *: Due to the higher than usual cancel window, vox can delay a 214A cancel to make it a true frame trap which is a much safer option than 214C. If the 214A is not delayed, then it becomes a true block string.",
    "cld": "Vox's main and best combo starter Go to button to start pressure as its +3 on block after feint cancel and has alot of pressure routes vox can go with. On hit feint cancel allows to link cl.B which naturally links to 2C.",
    "fa": "Decent button but outclassed by far B. No reason to use this button except in some -5 punish situations",
    "fb": "Farthest reaching light. Chains from 2B, 2A making it a good reliable 3 hit hitconfirm into 236A or super.",
    "fc": "Special cancelable and opens up massive damage if confirmed into 214C. Can link into itself on Wild Punish for an easy whiff punish. Dashing in and doing cl.D is a more rewarding option. Goes into many different options on block and starts offense. In neutral Vox's best safe buffer is 214A Significant recovery on whiff so use with caution",
    "fd": "Great all around neutral button Faster and longer than far C. Fantastic reward on Wild Punish, allowing for a reliable dash in cl.D(fc), clB, 2C combo. Similar to Far C, this button is very suseptible to being hopped over, but it is safer on whiff.",
    "j2bdbd": "Vox's divekick. good approach and fireball punish tool. Does not knock down even on counter hit EX can be done from any jump, normal versions only from neutral and forward jump. non EX can never be + on block and not rewarding on normal hit. on counter hit and wild punish normal versions can be plus enough to link a light after, or 2C if they hit deep.",
    "ja": "Great air to air button but not Vox's best.Has generous hit advantage allowing for close heavy punch to connect even when not landed deep. Cancelable, but only combos into dive kick on counterhit and wild punish.",
    "jb": "Crosses up.",
    "jc": "Potent Air-to-air that puts the opponent into a combo OK state which allows a pick up with DP break or a combo into super. Can't hit crouching opponents so don't use this as a jump in",
    "jcd": "Only available during S.P.G Could be used as anti air bait, starts a full combo on counter hit One of Vox's best midscreen combo enders as it gives him hard knockdown oki Solid air rev blow that is combo friendly when used after a REV dive kick.",
    "jd": "Vox's best jump in button. Use this after safe jump set ups"
  },
  "wolfgang_krauser": {
    "214acachold214holdaholdcholdac": "Krauser underhands a fireball forward. Light version is the high fireball; faster to startup and recover, but whiffs on crouch. Heavy version is the low fireball; can hit crouching characters but is slow to recover, being minus on hit point blank. It also hits OTG. The meterless versions have near-identical startup animations, making telling them apart extremely difficult until they come out. REV version is the slowest to startup, and is the height of your average fireball. It travels slowly at first but accelerates over time. It also launches on Counter Hit. Every version can be held; if held long enough, they do extra damage, are more plus on hit/block, and out-prioritize other basic projectiles. REV/Super projectiles will still beat them. A highly variable fireball that forms the crux of Krauser's long-range game, Blitz Ball is a very daunting projectile for the opponent to navigate around in neutral. The projectiles are fast, can go high or low, and can be held to mix up the timing and make predicting them much harder. Each version has its own weaknesses; the light version whiffs on crouching opponents, while the heavy version is highly vulnerable to jumps. Since each version of Blitz Ball can cover the other's flaws, you will always want to alternate unpredictably between high and low, instant and held, so that it becomes harder for the opponent to predict and avoid/punish. Mixing feints into your zoning can also be quite powerful.",
    "214bd46k": "Brakeable on first hit only 214BD > 4/6D.BR is only -3 on block. THIS is why Krauser does the damage he does. 214BD > 4/6D is most likely Krauser's best way to start REV combos, reliably cancelling into every REV special except 632146BD. Even without REV cancels, it juggles into every super as well as 623A. It is also Krauser's only Brake with a hitbox. While not damage-optimal to brake on hit, 214BD > 4/6D.BR is -3 on block, making it safe.",
    "214bdbd": "Krauser lunges forward with a knee. All versions are Brakeable before the hit. Unsafe up close, but can be made plus when spaced Light version is his most consistent special from lights Light version leaves a grounded opponent standing, heavy and REV versions knock down on hit and launch on Counter Hit. Heavy version is great combo filler in juggles. Krauser's rekka and main combo special. Notably, 214B/D/BD.BR itself lacks a hitbox much like 236B/D/BD.BR and thus means he has only one Brake combo and it isn't even damage-optimal. In REV combos, the heavy version can follow up 214AC for juggles into Gigantic Cyclone or Kaiser Claw.",
    "236236ac": "Krauser shoots up a massive projectile, in a very similar way to Marco's fireball super. Extrtemely tall hitbox, very difficult to jump unless very close Neither version is fully invincible on startup, not even the Redline version All versions hit OTG Worse damage than Gigantic Cyclone, but better okizeme This super has a lot of utility and is potentially one of the strongest supers in the game. Extremely combo-friendly and easy to link to. Despite looking quite gimmicky, this super is EXTREMELY HARD to deal with even outside of combos, due to its ability to be held to avoid Just Defense. Oftentimes, the opponent will have to choose between blocking the hit and suffering the REV buildup, simply taking the hit to protect their REV, or try to challenge it and potentially get hit. However, this super is extremely minus on block at all ranges and has more recovery the longer it is charged. Punishing Kaiser Wave on block depends on the range and character-specific tools. Marco's Haou Shoukou Ken, Rock's Shining Knuckle, Terry's Buster Wolf, and Preecha's P=Mc^2 are all great and fairly consistent punish options if the projectile was not blocked from completely full screen. Notably, this super can be held to boost its properties. After being held for 45 frames, the super becomes purple, and after being held for 120 frames, it will become red. It can be held for up to 225 frames (Ignition) / 229 frames (Redline) before Krauser releases it automatically. When held for at least 45 frames, Kaiser Wave can no longer be Just Defended, gains increased damage depending on charge length, has massively increased REV buildup (70%/85% for purple, 75%/95% for red) and completely beats out other supers of the same strength (Rock's Raging Storm, Marco's Haoh Shoukou Ken, etc.) unless they have projectile invincibility. Almost all of Krauser's hard knockdowns give him time to charge Kaiser Wave this long, forcing the opponent to potentially make a difficult decision between getting hit or getting Overheated.",
    "236236bd": "Krauser takes the opponent for a spin and tosses them away. Krauser's best invincible reversal. Despite the animation, it\u2019s a hitgrab that only hits front. On block, pulls the opponent in without dealing any chip damage or REV buildup. Krauser\u2019s highest-damage ender, but needs specific routes to juggle it due to the small hitbox. Cannot hit opponents in grounded, throw-invincible states (such as during hitstun, or if the opponent backdashes or does a Low Dodge Attack). Thus the only way to combo into it is with a juggle. Redline version has slightly more range, allowing it to combo from slightly more optimal routes ending with a REV Leg Tomahawk. The more damaging of Krauser's supers, Gigantic Cyclone is also used as a reversal. You can combo into it from some juggles as well, and is generally your preferred ender when available due to its damage and okizeme. Notably, though, it is no longer a grab in contrast to its past appearances. In the current version of the game, Gigantic Cyclone comes with massive caveats. You cannot combo into it on the ground, and when used as a reversal, it loses to grounded, throw-invincible moves such as most Low Dodge Attacks. However, it can still hit airborne opponents just fine.",
    "236bdbd": "Krauser does a flying axe kick that has massive range. All versions are breakable before the hit, stopping Krauser short. Light version becomes active faster but covers less range and is slightly minus. Heavy version is slow and reactable but covers a wider range while being up to +3 on crouching block. It can also go over most standard projectiles and punish them. REV version is a happy medium, being only one frame slower than the light version but has the heavy version's plus frames. Heavy and REV versions leave Krauser outside of close heavies' range, but surprisingly, Krauser is still left in throw range. All versions don't hit grounded opponents until the fourth active frame, making them 26/28/27 frames startup against them respectively. The infamous Leg Tomahawk is an extremely potent neutral tool, especially after making the opponent block a fireball. Its huge range and anti-projectile capabilities mean the opponent always has to be watching out for it. In advantage, it's also a good option for ending blockstrings to catch movement, backdashes, and some buttons. They can all be jabbed or High Dodge Attacked on reaction, however, so be careful not to overuse them.",
    "26bcor26drev": "Krauser's fastest feint and the one used in his framedata. Mimics Blitzball.",
    "2crev": "Cannot be cancelled on block. Wins against jumping attacks, standing normals and REV Blows. Triggers a slowdown if it meets any of these. Small hitbox and slow startup compared to most High Dodge Attacks. Lackluster high dodge attack. The hitbox is smaller than it looks, and it's rather slow to start up in comparison to other characters, but despite all these flaws, it still has a place in Krauser's game plan. When Counter Hitting or Wild Punishing an airborne opponent, it always combos to Kaiser Claw, no matter how high it hits. If it doesn't connect too high, you can instead cancel to heavy Knee Halberd for a full, albeit scaled, combo.",
    "4ab": "Though the throws have similar animations at first, the voice clip tells you which direction you\u2019re about to be tossed. During a back throw, Krauser will shout \u201cI will\u2026 crush you!\u201d",
    "5bcor5drev": "Mimics Leg Tomahawk, Krauser's slowest Feint.",
    "5crev": "Universal overhead, wins against lows and throws. Triggers a slowdown if it meets either of these. On Counter Hit or Wild Punish, links to close D up close, or 2C if too far away for the former. Meaty setups for this move do exist for Krauser, making it up to +6 on hit and +1 on block. Average universal overhead and low crush. Great hitbox and range.",
    "623acac": "Krauser reaches up for an airborne opponent and slams them back down behind him. Heavy and REV versions are air invuln frame 1 to recovery. Cannot be Just Defended. A meterless hard knockdown that you can combo into. Krauser's anti-air grab is one of his best tools, providing a hard knockdown on all versions. The Heavy and REV versions have air invuln on frame 1, shutting down most air approaches or attempts to evade Blitzball. Even in most REV combos, 623A is still the highest-damaging non-metered ender. By design, however, Kaiser Claw cannot hit grounded opponents. In addition, it does sideswitch on hit, making the move risky should you put yourself in the corner, even if it gives great, consistent okizeme. The REV version is almost identical to the Heavy version, but has REV Accel properties, allowing you to combo afterward.",
    "632146bdbd": "Krauser lunges forward to grab the opponent, slamming them back down to the ground. Heavy and REV versions have guard point from frame 3 until recovery REV version instead lifts the opponent up and strikes them in the back, switching sides before allowing REV Accel. Krauser's grounded command grab. While slow on startup, 632146K is still great for a lot of reasons. 632146D/BD both have near-instant guard point and reliably catch backdashes in the corner. 63214B, while lacking the guard point, is also the fastest at 22 frames and good for cancelling from lights. An important thing to note is that Krauser has strings and normals that are +3 on block, making 632146B/BD a good option against abare buttons, especially with the guard point absorbing infinite hits. The REV version allows for combos as well, although they scale worse than cancels, around 10~30 less damage than usual. It is committal, though, being jumpable and very punishable in recovery. Even with that, it's still a great tool and integral to pressure.",
    "6a": "Krauser's strongest and scariest overhead. Has MASSIVE range and fast start-up. will always combo into 2A on hit. Has quite distinct start up which makes it slightly easier to react to when compared to other command overhead moves and only 2 active frames, so it is hard to get strong conversions using it.",
    "6c": "Super cancellable Long reach While it is very unsafe at point-blank range, 6C is very long-reaching, hits crouchers, and is super cancellable on reaction.",
    "ab": "Important tool in Krauser's arsenal, as an easy to follow-up-on throw. When an opponent is in the corner, Krauser can follow up forward throw with meaty 214[AC], dash to be +8 and in their face. Krauser could charge Kaiser Wave to try hitting the opponent on their wake up, but it is not a strong option as Krauser will be punished universally by the cast and the opponent can do a hyper jump on wake up and avoid the projectile if Krauser tries to charge it.",
    "cla": "Special cancellable Krauser's fastest normal. Starts Combination Attack. It is worth nothing that the cl.A cl.B chain can be linked into cr. C at point-blank range, providing access to REV combos. It will fall out at further range, however. From crossup, though, cl.A is Krauser's most consistent combo starter, allowing for the cl.A > cl.B, cr.C link from a jump-in. In addition to this, it can hop check due to its height.",
    "cla5ccd6d2d": "Every ender can be done during cl.AC or cl.ACC. cl.AC first hit is cancellable by any follow-ups. During S.P.G, REV Blow can be used as an ender. D ender is REV Blow cancellable. Auto combo that can only be used by starting with close A > 5C(And no other direction), none of these moves are special or super cancellable. cl.AC : First hit of the auto combo, cancellable by any follow-ups. cl.AC > C : A second hit cancellable by any ender, a nice way to increase the damage or your opponent REV meter. Unsafe on block and can be punished if the auto-combo was started too close to the opponent. D ender : Far D follow up, different frame data and damage, very unsafe on block but soft knockdown on hit. 6D ender : Safest ender, Same move as his Low Dodge Attack(5C+REV) but +2F on hit and block. 2D ender : Same move as his 2D, meaning it's very unsafe on block.",
    "clb": "Cancellable Links into cr.C Links into cl.C and cl.D on crossup Probably Krauser's best light normal. Close B allows for access to REV combos due to being linkable into cr.C. Its 6-frame startup also makes it ideal for punishing specials that cl.C can't punish. In addition, it can be linked into cl.D on crossup. This provides a higher-damaging, albeit less consistent crossup conversion than cl.A. It is also +3 on block, making it great for light chain blockstrings and command grab tick throws.",
    "clc": "Wild Punish +23 (+30 when Feint Cancelled) Cancellable Hits twice but only cancels on the second hit; special cancels buffered during the first hit will not come out Krauser's other combo starter from jump-ins. A pretty lackluster button on its own, being -6 on block. One saving grace of regular cl.C is its link from cl.B on crossup. On regular jump-ins or particularly deep crossups, however, it's always best to do the 4/6C followup to enable more damage. Just use cl.D.",
    "clc46c": "Krauser throws a quick upwards hook using his other arm. Scales combos by an extra 5% on hit, which makes this feint route into a 2C do less damage than close D > feint, far C in combos.",
    "cld": "Wild Punish +22 (+30 when Feint Cancelled) Cancellable Forces stand on hit Despite being Krauser's slowest close normal, it boasts the highest raw damage of Krauser's close normals. Close D also shines as his main starter with cl.D > feint, f.C, providing better scaling than cl.C > 4/6C. It also has other benefits. First, crossup j.C, cl.B, cl.D > feint, 2C is Krauser's highest-damaging combo starter, with conversions doing on average having 1-2% more damage over cl.C > 4/6C > feint, 2C conversions from any jump-in. Second, it is a fantastic punish button, being +15 on CH and +22 on WP when grounded. This allows for maximized routing with CH cl.D > feint, 66 cl.C. Lastly, it is Krauser's safest normal when feinted, sporting a +3 on block with Feint Blitzball, enabling his command grab game to a scary degree.",
    "cra": "Special cancellable Krauser's safest crouching normal, being only -1 on block.",
    "crb": "Krauser's fastest crouching normal Cannot chain into itself Good at stuffing dashes and grabs, plus it has good range for a 2B. While it is super cancellable and can chain into 2A, f.A and f.B for more cancels, only 2A and f.B are special cancellable. Even then, there are plenty of ranges where 2B > 2A and 2B > f.B fall out. All of this compounds into a central weakness of Krauser; he lacks consistent rewards from lows. Sorry y'all, he can't have it all.",
    "crc": "Cancellable Wild Punish +18 (+23 when Feint Cancelled) One of Krauser's long-reaching cancellable heavies. It is also his fastest at 9 frames. Deceptively long, as it hits a little less that a full character length away.",
    "crd": "Forward-advancing On Wild Punish, puts opponents into a state where they can be OTG\u2019d with another sweep, low Blitz Ball, or Kaiser Wave Krauser's sweep boasts good range and it moves him forward. It also chains from 2A on hit, despite not being mentioned in-game.",
    "fa": "Krauser stabs forward. Has massive range and an all around great button to use in confirms and in neutral as a poke Not special cancelable Best 3 hit light string ender due to range and great chain option after a spaced 2B",
    "fb": "Amazing far B, one of Krauser's best buttons and has alot of uses in his game plan. Special cancelable Great 3 hit light string ender. Combos meterlessly to 214A at any range.",
    "fc": "Double handed slap with average range that also hits crouchers. Good button in neutral to check dash/run ins or to start blockstrings. Wild Punish +25 (+24 when Feint Cancelled) Special cancelable Massive cancel window Links after both feinted close D and close C",
    "fd": "Wild Punish +23 Super cancelable Use with caution Massive roundhouse kick with good range. Has significant recovery and very weak to jump ins. The hitbox is quite good at catching hops and can be used as a situational anti-air at ranges where Krauser's anti-air grab whiffs.",
    "ja": "Krauser's fastest air normal Active for eight frames, the longest of all his air normals Cancels to REV Blow on hit, block or air JD, but will not combo Krauser's lil' slap is his fastest air-to-air, if a bit more specific in application. It hits pretty high, but works well against full jumps.",
    "jb": "Long and strong Krauser's primary air-to-air. It boasts good range and hits lower, stuffing hops early. Has a cross-up hitbox on Krasuer's right knee and another hitbox on his extended left leg. The cross up hitbox is quite small so it is recommended to use jump C as Krasuer's main crossup tool",
    "jc": "Krauser swipes the area in front of him. Great jump in button but this button suprisingly has a very strong cross up hitbox that is very easy to land and hard to react to. Massive cross-up hitbox Go to Jump in button",
    "jd": "Launches on air hit, allowing for followup combos Massive flying knee that is another good option for jump ins. Extremely strong hitboxes all around.This button puts the opponent in a juggle state on hit which allows Krauser to get follow-ups, theoretically making it his best air to air move."
  }
}
